/* Dialectica thinkers: one per person, grouped by tradition, in English and Chinese. Edit freely, then run `node scripts/check-data.mjs`. */
window.DIALECTICA = window.DIALECTICA || {};
window.DIALECTICA.thinkers = [
  {"id":"laozi","tradition":"eastasia","born":-571,"died":-471,"life":{"en":"traditionally 6th c. BCE","zh":"传为公元前6世纪"},"name":{"en":"Laozi","zh":"老子"},"role":{"en":"Legendary sage credited with the Daodejing","zh":"传说中的圣人，相传著有《道德经》"},"idea":{"en":"The Daodejing teaches that the Dao is nameless and acts by not acting, and that the sage ruler stays low like water and lets things take their course. Whether a single author named Laozi existed is doubted; the text was probably written down from the late 5th century BCE and was fairly stable by the mid-3rd.","zh":"《道德经》教导：道无名，以无为而无不为；圣人治国当如水一般处下，顺任万物自然。是否真有一位名叫老子的作者颇受怀疑；此书大约自公元前5世纪末开始成文，到前3世纪中叶已大体定型。"},"detail":{"en":"According to Sima Qian’s Records of the Grand Historian (c. 100 BCE), Laozi, “the Old Master”, was Li Er, a keeper of archives at the Zhou court whom Confucius once consulted on ritual; weary of the declining dynasty, he left for the west and, at a border pass, wrote a book of five thousand characters. Sima Qian himself records rival identifications, and many modern scholars treat Laozi as a legend that gathered several figures. Bamboo slips from a Guodian tomb of about 300 BCE hold the oldest known parts of the text.\n\nThe Daodejing’s 81 short chapters argue that the Dao, the way all things come about, cannot be captured in names. Its favourite image is water: soft and low, yet it wears away stone. Striving, display and multiplying rules breed their opposites, so the sage ruler “acts without acting” (wuwei), keeps desires few and lets the people settle themselves. Read as political advice, spiritual guidance and cosmology, it became the founding text of Daoism, shaped Legalists such as Han Fei, and is among the most translated books in the world.","zh":"据司马迁《史记》，老子名李耳，曾任周王室的守藏史，孔子向他问过礼；他见周室衰微，便西行而去，在关口应关令之请写下五千言。司马迁自己也记下了别的说法，许多现代学者认为老子是糅合多人的传说人物。郭店一座约公元前300年的楚墓出土的竹简，是现存最早的部分文本。\n\n《道德经》八十一章反复说明：道是万物生成的方式，无法用名言把握。它最爱用的意象是水——柔弱处下，却能攻坚。刻意的作为、炫耀与繁多的法令，反而招来它们的反面，所以圣人“无为”，少私寡欲，让百姓自化。此书既被读作治国之术，也被读作修身之道与宇宙论，成为道家的根本经典，影响了韩非等法家，也是世界上译本最多的书之一。"},"schools":["daoism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Daodejing","zh":"《道德经》"},"y":-300}],"wiki":"Laozi","c":"Zhang Lu-Laozi Riding an Ox.jpg","portrait":{"w":600,"h":1094,"v":"daba4508cb","author":"Zhang Lu","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":null,"page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zhang_Lu-Laozi_Riding_an_Ox.jpg"},"focus":"50% 40%"},
  {"id":"confucius","tradition":"eastasia","born":-551,"died":-479,"life":{"en":"551–479 BCE (traditional)","zh":"公元前551–前479年（传统纪年）"},"name":{"en":"Confucius","zh":"孔子"},"role":{"en":"Teacher from Lu who sought to revive the Zhou way","zh":"鲁国教师，致力于复兴周代之道"},"idea":{"en":"Taught that anyone can become an exemplary person (junzi) by cultivating humaneness and practising ritual, beginning with respect within the family. He wrote no treatise; his sayings were collected by his disciples’ circles in the Analects.","zh":"主张人人都能通过修养仁德、践行礼仪成为君子，而这要从家庭中的孝悌开始。他没有留下专著，其言论由弟子及再传弟子辑为《论语》。"},"detail":{"en":"Kong Qiu, later honoured as Kongzi or Master Kong, was born in the small state of Lu, traditionally in 551 BCE, to a family of minor gentry; his father died when he was a child. He worked as a keeper of granaries and herds, rose to office in Lu, and then spent about fourteen years travelling between states in search of a ruler who would put his ideas into practice. None did. He returned home to teach, and his disciples, drawn from every rank, carried his sayings on.\n\nHe lived as the Zhou order was falling apart, and he believed it could be restored not by force but by character. The key is ren, humaneness, summed up in the rule “do not impose on others what you yourself do not desire”, and li, the rituals and courtesies that train feeling into right conduct, beginning with respect for parents. An exemplary person (junzi) is made by virtue, not birth, and a ruler who governs by virtue draws people as the stars turn around the Pole Star. The Analects, compiled by later disciples, became the core of Chinese education until 1905.","zh":"孔子名丘，传统上说生于公元前551年，是鲁国一个没落士族家庭的孩子，幼年丧父。他做过管理仓库和牲畜的小吏，后来在鲁国做官，又带着弟子周游列国约十四年，想找一位肯推行他主张的君主，却始终没有遇到。晚年回到鲁国授徒，弟子来自各个阶层，把他的言论传了下来。\n\n他身处周代礼制崩坏之时，相信秩序可以靠人格而非武力重建。关键在“仁”，一言以蔽之即“己所不欲，勿施于人”；还有“礼”，即把情感训练成恰当行为的礼仪规矩，而这一切从孝敬父母开始。君子由德行而非出身造就；为政以德，就像北极星安居其所而众星环绕。由后学编成的《论语》，直到1905年都是中国教育的核心。"},"schools":["confucianism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Analects (compiled by disciples)","zh":"《论语》（弟子辑录）"},"y":-400}],"wiki":"Confucius","c":"Confucius Tang Dynasty.jpg","portrait":{"w":350,"h":640,"v":"c4a73d0dd5","author":"Wu Daozi, 685-758, Tang Dynasty.","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":null,"page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Confucius_Tang_Dynasty.jpg"}},
  {"id":"mozi","tradition":"eastasia","born":-470,"died":-391,"life":{"en":"c. 470–391 BCE","zh":"约公元前470–前391年"},"name":{"en":"Mozi","zh":"墨子"},"role":{"en":"Founder of Mohism and critic of Confucian partiality","zh":"墨家创始人，批评儒家的差等之爱"},"idea":{"en":"Argued for impartial care and against offensive war and costly rituals, judging every doctrine by its benefit to the people; he is often called China’s first consequentialist.","zh":"主张兼爱，反对侵略战争与奢靡礼制，以是否有利于百姓衡量一切学说；常被称为中国最早的后果主义者。"},"detail":{"en":"Mo Di flourished around 430 BCE, probably in Song or Lu; Sima Qian calls him an official of Song, and later tradition links him with craftsmen. He led a disciplined band of followers who studied argument and also the defence of cities: the Mozi tells how he walked ten days and nights to dissuade Chu from attacking Song, out-manoeuvring the engineer Gongshu Ban in a model siege. His teachings survive in chapters written down by his followers.\n\nMozi asked a simple question of every custom: does it benefit the people? Wars, theft and feuds arise because people care for their own family or state and not others’. If everyone regarded others’ states as their own, who would attack them? Hence impartial care (jian’ai), the condemnation of offensive war, and attacks on costly funerals and court music. He proposed three tests for any doctrine: its basis in the sage kings, the evidence of ordinary people’s eyes and ears, and its benefit in use. For two centuries Mohism rivalled Confucianism; then it vanished, to be rediscovered in the Qing.","zh":"墨子名翟，活跃于约公元前430年，大概是宋国或鲁国人；司马迁说他做过宋国的大夫，后世传说则把他与工匠联系在一起。他率领一支纪律严明的团体，既研究论辩，也精通守城之术。《墨子》记载他走了十天十夜去劝阻楚国攻宋，在模拟攻守中胜过了工匠公输般。他的学说保存在后学记录的篇章中。\n\n墨子对一切习俗只问一句：它是否利民？战争、盗窃与仇杀，都源于人们只爱自己的家与国而不爱别人的。若人人“视人之国若视其国”，谁还会去攻打呢？于是有“兼爱”“非攻”，有对厚葬与礼乐的批评。他还提出检验学说的“三表”：本之于古圣王之事，原察百姓耳目之实，看它付诸实施后是否有利。墨家与儒家并称显学约两百年，此后湮没无闻，直到清代才被重新发现。"},"schools":["mohism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Mozi (compiled by his followers)","zh":"《墨子》（门人编纂）"},"y":-350}],"wiki":"Mozi"},
  {"id":"shang-yang","tradition":"eastasia","born":-390,"died":-338,"life":{"en":"c. 390–338 BCE","zh":"约公元前390–前338年"},"name":{"en":"Shang Yang","zh":"商鞅"},"role":{"en":"Qin minister whose reforms built Qin’s power","zh":"秦国重臣，其变法奠定了秦的强盛"},"idea":{"en":"Reorganised Qin around farming and war: ranks earned by military merit, households made mutually responsible, and strict laws applied to all. When his patron Duke Xiao died he fell from power; Qin troops killed him, and the new king had his body torn apart by chariots.","zh":"以耕战重组秦国：按军功授爵，令民户连坐，严法一体施行。靠山秦孝公死后他失势，被秦军所杀，新即位的秦惠王又将他的尸体车裂示众。"},"detail":{"en":"Born a noble of the small state of Wey around 390 BCE, Gongsun Yang served the chancellor of Wei before answering Duke Xiao of Qin’s call for able men in 361. With the duke’s backing he remade Qin in two waves of reform: households were grouped in fives and tens and made responsible for each other’s crimes, noble rank was given for heads taken in battle, land and measures were standardised, and counties run by appointed officials replaced hereditary domains. When Duke Xiao died in 338 his enemies accused him of rebellion; Qin troops killed him, and his body was torn apart by chariots.\n\nThe Book of Lord Shang, compiled by his followers, starts from the view that people pursue profit and fear punishment, and that a state is strong only through farming and war. Laws must be published, rewards certain and punishments heavy, and they must bind the powerful too: when the crown prince broke the law, Shang Yang punished his tutors. His reforms made Qin the state that would unify China in 221 BCE, and his name became a byword for harsh government.","zh":"商鞅本是卫国公族，约生于公元前390年，名公孙鞅，先在魏国相国门下任事，公元前361年应秦孝公求贤之令入秦。在孝公支持下，他分两轮变法改造秦国：民户编为什伍，相互告发、连坐；按斩首军功授爵；统一田亩与度量衡；以官吏治理的县取代世袭封地。前338年孝公去世，政敌诬告他谋反，他被秦军所杀，尸体又被车裂示众。\n\n由其后学编成的《商君书》认为：人趋利避害，国家只有靠农耕与战争才能强大。法令必须公布，赏必信、罚必重，并且对权贵一体适用——太子犯法时，商鞅便处罚了太子的师傅。他的变法使秦国成为日后在公元前221年统一中国的国家，他的名字也成了严刑峻法的代名词。"},"schools":["legalism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"The Book of Lord Shang (compiled by followers)","zh":"《商君书》（后学编纂）"},"y":-300}],"wiki":"Shang Yang"},
  {"id":"mencius","tradition":"eastasia","born":-372,"died":-289,"life":{"en":"c. 372–289 BCE","zh":"约公元前372–前289年"},"name":{"en":"Mencius","zh":"孟子"},"role":{"en":"Confucian who argued that human nature is good","zh":"主张人性本善的儒家思想家"},"idea":{"en":"Everyone has four “sprouts” of benevolence, righteousness, propriety and wisdom, shown by the alarm anyone feels on seeing a child about to fall into a well; cultivation makes them grow. A ruler who oppresses the people loses Heaven’s mandate.","zh":"人人皆有仁、义、礼、智“四端”，正如任何人见到孩子将要跌入井中都会怵惕恻隐；扩充培养便能使之成长。暴虐百姓的君主将失去天命。"},"detail":{"en":"Meng Ke was born around 372 BCE in Zou, a small state next to Confucius’s Lu. Sima Qian says he studied with disciples of Confucius’s grandson Zisi. Like Confucius he travelled from court to court, debating with King Hui of Liang and King Xuan of Qi, and like Confucius he found no ruler willing to adopt his programme. The book that bears his name records these conversations and his debates with rival thinkers.\n\nHis central claim is that human nature is good. Anyone who suddenly sees a child about to fall into a well feels alarm and compassion, not to win the parents’ favour or praise, but spontaneously. Such feelings are four “sprouts” of humaneness, rightness, propriety and wisdom; like sprouts they need care, and they wither when neglected, as the hills near the city were stripped bare. Politically he put the people first and the ruler last: a tyrant has forfeited Heaven’s mandate, and killing one is not regicide. Zhu Xi made the Mencius one of the Four Books, and he is honoured as the “Second Sage”.","zh":"孟子名轲，约公元前372年生于邹国，紧邻孔子的鲁国。司马迁说他受业于孔子之孙子思的门人。他也像孔子一样周游列国，与梁惠王、齐宣王等人论辩，也同样没有找到肯采纳其主张的君主。以他命名的《孟子》一书，记录了这些对话以及他与其他学派的论争。\n\n他的核心主张是性善。任何人忽然看见孩子将要掉进井里，都会生出惊惧与恻隐之心，不是为了结交孩子的父母或博取名声，而是油然而生。这样的心是仁、义、礼、智的“四端”；它们像幼苗一样需要培养，荒废了便会枯萎，就像牛山之木被砍伐殆尽。在政治上，他主张“民为贵，社稷次之，君为轻”：暴君已失天命，诛杀他不算弑君。朱熹将《孟子》列入“四书”，孟子被尊为“亚圣”。"},"schools":["confucianism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Mencius","zh":"《孟子》"},"y":-300}],"wiki":"Mencius","c":"Half Portraits of the Great Sage and Virtuous Men of Old - Meng Ke (孟軻).jpg","portrait":{"w":600,"h":813,"v":"1efa3cc9ec","author":"anonymous","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":null,"page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Half_Portraits_of_the_Great_Sage_and_Virtuous_Men_of_Old_-_Meng_Ke_(%E5%AD%9F%E8%BB%BB).jpg"}},
  {"id":"hui-shi","tradition":"eastasia","born":-370,"died":-310,"life":{"en":"c. 370–310 BCE","zh":"约公元前370–前310年"},"name":{"en":"Hui Shi","zh":"惠施"},"role":{"en":"Wei statesman, logician and Zhuangzi’s debating partner","zh":"魏国相，名辩家，庄子的论辩对手"},"idea":{"en":"His ten paradoxes, preserved only in the Zhuangzi, argue that size, place and time are relative (“I set off for Yue today and arrived yesterday”), ending in “love all things; heaven and earth are one body”. His own writings are lost.","zh":"他的“历物十事”仅保存于《庄子》中，论证大小、方位与时间的相对性（如“今日适越而昔来”），最终归结为“泛爱万物，天地一体也”。其本人著作已佚。"},"detail":{"en":"Hui Shi served King Hui of Wei as chancellor around the 310s BCE and was a celebrated disputer; the Zhuangzi says his books would have filled five carts. None survive. We know him chiefly through his friend and rival Zhuangzi, who records their arguments, and through a list of ten theses in the “All Under Heaven” chapter, written by an early Han author.\n\nThe theses play with the relativity of measures and places: “The largest has nothing outside it; the smallest has nothing inside it”; “the sun at noon is the sun setting”; “the south has no limit and yet has a limit”. If size, place and time depend on the standpoint from which they are judged, the distinctions people fight over are not absolute, and the list ends: “love all things; heaven and earth are one body”. Zhuangzi teased him for clever words, yet mourned him: after Hui Shi died, he said, he had no one left to talk with.","zh":"惠施在公元前310年代前后担任魏惠王的相国，是著名的辩者；《庄子》说“惠施多方，其书五车”，但这些著作全都失传了。我们主要通过他的朋友兼论敌庄子来认识他——《庄子》记录了两人的论辩——以及汉初人所作《天下》篇所列的“历物十事”。\n\n这十条命题探讨尺度与方位的相对性：“至大无外，谓之大一；至小无内，谓之小一”；“日方中方睨”；“南方无穷而有穷”。如果大小、方位与时间都取决于判断的立足点，人们争执不休的区分就并非绝对，于是结论是：“泛爱万物，天地一体也。”庄子常讥他巧于言辞，却在他死后感叹：从此再没有可以与之谈论的人了。"},"schools":["school-of-names"],"works":[],"wiki":"Hui Shi"},
  {"id":"zhuangzi","tradition":"eastasia","born":-369,"died":-286,"life":{"en":"c. 369–286 BCE","zh":"约公元前369–前286年"},"name":{"en":"Zhuangzi","zh":"庄子"},"role":{"en":"Daoist writer of parables, paradoxes and freedom","zh":"以寓言与悖论书写自由的道家思想家"},"idea":{"en":"Through stories such as the butterfly dream and Cook Ding carving an ox, he showed that every perspective is partial and that freedom comes from “free and easy wandering” with change rather than clinging to fixed views.","zh":"他借庄周梦蝶、庖丁解牛等故事表明，一切视角都是片面的；真正的自由在于随变化而“逍遥游”，而非执着于固定的见解。"},"detail":{"en":"Zhuang Zhou came from Meng in the state of Song and flourished in the late 4th century BCE. Sima Qian says he was once a minor official in a lacquer grove and turned down an offer to become chancellor of Chu, preferring, in a story the Zhuangzi tells, to be a tortoise dragging its tail in the mud rather than a sacred shell kept in a temple. Of the 33 chapters edited by Guo Xiang, only the seven Inner Chapters are usually linked to Zhuangzi himself.\n\nHis writing works by stories and paradoxes rather than argument. Every viewpoint, he suggests, makes its own “this” and “that”: the debaters of his day each called right what the other called wrong, and no neutral judge is available. He dreams he is a butterfly and wakes unsure which is dreaming. Cook Ding carves an ox for nineteen years without dulling his blade because he follows its natural joints. Freedom lies in moving with change, even death, instead of clinging to fixed views. His book shaped Chan Buddhism, Chinese poetry and painting, and every later Daoist.","zh":"庄子名周，宋国蒙人，活跃于公元前4世纪后半叶。司马迁说他做过蒙地的漆园吏，楚王请他做相，他谢绝了——《庄子》里的故事说，他宁愿做一只在泥里拖着尾巴的乌龟，也不愿做被供在庙堂上的神龟甲。郭象编定的三十三篇中，通常只有七篇内篇被认为与庄子本人有关。\n\n他的写作不靠论证，而靠寓言与悖论。他指出每种视角都有自己的“此”与“彼”：当时的辩者“以是其所非而非其所是”，却没有中立的裁判。他梦见自己是蝴蝶，醒来后不知是庄周梦蝶还是蝶梦庄周。庖丁解牛十九年而刀刃如新，是因为他顺着牛身的天然肌理。自由在于随顺变化——包括死亡——而不执守成见。此书深刻影响了禅宗、中国的诗歌与绘画，以及此后所有的道家。"},"schools":["daoism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Zhuangzi (the Inner Chapters)","zh":"《庄子》（内篇）"},"y":-290}],"wiki":"Zhuang Zhou","c":"玄門十子圖 莊子.jpg","portrait":{"w":600,"h":864,"v":"88958eda40","author":"Hua Zili (華祖立)","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":null,"page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E7%8E%84%E9%96%80%E5%8D%81%E5%AD%90%E5%9C%96_%E8%8E%8A%E5%AD%90.jpg"}},
  {"id":"gongsun-long","tradition":"eastasia","born":-320,"died":-250,"life":{"en":"c. 320–250 BCE","zh":"约公元前320–前250年"},"name":{"en":"Gongsun Long","zh":"公孙龙"},"role":{"en":"Disputer famous for “a white horse is not a horse”","zh":"以“白马非马”闻名的辩者"},"idea":{"en":"Argued that a term for a compound (“white horse”) is not the same as a term for a kind (“horse”), probing how names pick out attributes and things.","zh":"论证指称复合物的名（“白马”）不同于指称类别的名（“马”），由此探讨名如何指称属性与事物。"},"detail":{"en":"Gongsun Long, from the state of Zhao, lived roughly 320–250 BCE and was a retainer of Lord Pingyuan, a powerful Zhao prince. Early sources record him urging kings to lay down their arms, an echo of Mohist ideas. Of the book under his name, a handful of chapters survive, and scholars debate how many are genuinely his.\n\nHe is famous for the thesis that “a white horse is not a horse”. If you ask for a horse, a yellow or a black one will do; if you ask for a white horse, they will not. So what the term “white horse” picks out differs from what “horse” picks out, and the two terms cannot be identical. In another dialogue he argues that hardness and whiteness in a stone are separate, since sight gives the white but not the hard, and touch the hard but not the white. Xunzi and the Zhuangzi dismissed such arguments as sophistry; twentieth-century scholars read them anew as early Chinese reflection on reference, kinds and logic.","zh":"公孙龙是赵国人，大约生活于公元前320至前250年，是赵国权贵平原君的门客。早期文献记载他曾劝说君主“偃兵”，与墨家的主张遥相呼应。署他名字的《公孙龙子》只存下几篇，其中有多少确实出自他手，学者仍有争论。\n\n他以“白马非马”闻名。求马，黄马、黑马都可以应求；求白马，黄马、黑马就不行。可见“白马”所指与“马”所指不同，两个名称不能等同。在另一篇对话里，他论证石头的“坚”与“白”是相离的：用眼睛看只得其白而不得其坚，用手摸只得其坚而不得其白。荀子与《庄子》都斥之为诡辩；二十世纪的学者则重新把它读作中国早期关于指称、类与逻辑的思考。"},"schools":["school-of-names"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Gongsun Longzi (partly surviving)","zh":"《公孙龙子》（部分存世）"},"y":-270}],"wiki":"Gongsun Long"},
  {"id":"xunzi","tradition":"eastasia","born":-310,"died":-235,"life":{"en":"c. 310 – after 238 BCE","zh":"约公元前310年–前238年后"},"name":{"en":"Xunzi","zh":"荀子"},"role":{"en":"Confucian realist who held human nature to be bad","zh":"主张人性本恶的儒家现实主义者"},"idea":{"en":"Human nature left to itself leads to strife; goodness is the product of deliberate effort (wei) through ritual and learning, as a warped board is straightened by steaming and bending.","zh":"人性顺其自然会导致争夺；善是通过礼义与学习的“伪”（人为努力）造就的，正如弯曲的木材须经蒸烤矫正才能变直。"},"detail":{"en":"Xun Kuang was born in Zhao, probably around 310 BCE. He studied and taught at the Jixia academy in Qi, where Sima Qian says he was three times its senior master, and later served as magistrate of Lanling in Chu under Lord Chunshen, still alive after 238 BCE. Among his students were Han Fei and Li Si, the minister who helped the First Emperor unify China, and their fame later damaged his own.\n\nAgainst Mencius he argued that human nature is bad: we are born wanting gain, envying others and craving pleasure, and if we simply follow these impulses the result is strife. Goodness is the product of deliberate effort (wei). The sage kings devised ritual and rightness to shape and satisfy desires, as a warped board is straightened by steaming or metal sharpened on a whetstone. Heaven is no moral judge but a constant natural order: “it does not persist for the sake of Yao, nor perish for the sake of Jie”. His Confucianism dominated Han scholarship before later tradition preferred Mencius.","zh":"荀子名况，赵国人，大约生于公元前310年。他曾在齐国稷下学宫讲学，司马迁说他“三为祭酒”；后来在楚国春申君门下任兰陵令，前238年后仍在世。他的学生中有韩非，还有帮助秦始皇统一天下的李斯；这两人的名声后来反倒连累了他。\n\n他针对孟子提出性恶：人生来好利、嫉妒、贪求耳目之欲，若顺着这些本能，结果就是争夺与混乱。善是“伪”，即人为努力的产物。古代圣王制定礼义来引导和满足欲望，就像弯木要经过熏蒸矫正才能变直，钝金要在磨刀石上磨砺才能锋利。天不是道德裁判，而是恒常的自然秩序：“天行有常，不为尧存，不为桀亡。”他的儒学在汉代学术中影响最大，后来的传统才转而推崇孟子。"},"schools":["confucianism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Xunzi","zh":"《荀子》"},"y":-250}],"wiki":"Xunzi (philosopher)"},
  {"id":"han-feizi","tradition":"eastasia","born":-280,"died":-233,"life":{"en":"c. 280–233 BCE","zh":"约公元前280–前233年"},"name":{"en":"Han Feizi","zh":"韩非"},"role":{"en":"Prince of Han who synthesised Legalist thought","zh":"韩国公子，法家思想的集大成者"},"idea":{"en":"Combined law (fa), positional power (shi) and administrative technique (shu) into a theory of rule that relies on self-interest rather than virtue. A student of Xunzi, he died in a Qin prison.","zh":"将“法”“势”“术”合为一体，构建了一种依靠人的自利而非德行的统治理论。他曾师从荀子，最终死于秦国狱中。"},"detail":{"en":"Han Fei was a prince of Han, the weakest of the warring states. Sima Qian says he stammered and so wrote rather than spoke, and that he and Li Si studied together under Xunzi. His essays reached the King of Qin, the future First Emperor, who admired them. When Han sent Han Fei to Qin as an envoy in 233 BCE, Li Si, perhaps jealous, persuaded the king that a prince of Han could never be loyal to Qin; Han Fei was imprisoned and forced to take poison.\n\nHe fused earlier Legalist ideas into one theory of rule. A ruler needs fa, clear published laws backed by the “two handles” of reward and punishment; shi, the authority of his position; and shu, techniques for controlling ministers, such as holding them to account for matching their words with results. Rule must rest on self-interest, not hoped-for virtue, and must change with the times: clinging to ancient ways is like the farmer of Song who waited by a tree stump for another hare to run into it. Qin applied his ideas; later dynasties wrapped Legalist methods in Confucian language.","zh":"韩非是韩国的公子，而韩是战国中最弱的国家。司马迁说他口吃，不善言说而善著书，曾与李斯一同师事荀子。他的文章传到秦王（后来的秦始皇）手中，大受赏识。公元前233年韩国派他出使秦国，李斯大约出于嫉妒，劝秦王说韩国公子终究不会忠于秦；韩非被下狱，被迫服毒而死。\n\n他把此前法家的思想融为一套统治理论。君主需要“法”，即公开明确、以赏罚“二柄”为后盾的法令；需要“势”，即君位的权威；还需要“术”，即驾驭臣下的方法，例如要求他们言与事相符、名实相合。统治要建立在人的自利之上，而不是寄望于德行，还要随时代而变：死守古法就像那个守着树桩等兔子再撞上来的宋国农夫。秦国推行了他的主张，后世王朝则往往以儒家言辞包装法家手段。"},"schools":["legalism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Han Feizi","zh":"《韩非子》"},"y":-240}],"wiki":"Han Fei"},
  {"id":"dong-zhongshu","tradition":"eastasia","born":-179,"died":-104,"life":{"en":"c. 179–104 BCE","zh":"约公元前179–前104年"},"name":{"en":"Dong Zhongshu","zh":"董仲舒"},"role":{"en":"Han scholar who helped make Confucianism the state learning","zh":"推动儒学成为国家官学的汉代学者"},"idea":{"en":"Joined Confucian ethics to yin–yang and five-phase cosmology: Heaven and humanity resonate, so natural portents warn a ruler of his faults. His advice to Emperor Wu helped place Confucian classics at the centre of court learning.","zh":"将儒家伦理与阴阳五行宇宙论相结合，提出“天人感应”：自然灾异是上天对君主过失的警示。他对汉武帝的建言推动了儒家经典成为朝廷学术的中心。"},"detail":{"en":"Dong Zhongshu, from Guangchuan in the north China plain, was a specialist in the Gongyang commentary on the Spring and Autumn Annals and an erudite at the Han court. Around 134 BCE he answered Emperor Wu’s questions on government in three famous memorials, later served as chancellor to two quarrelsome princes, and retired to teach and write. Later tradition credited him with persuading the emperor to make Confucian learning the state’s orthodoxy; historians now think his role was smaller than that, and much of the book attributed to him was compiled later.\n\nHe read the Spring and Autumn Annals as Confucius’s coded judgments on history and joined Confucian ethics to the cosmology of yin and yang and the five phases. Heaven, earth and humanity form one system that resonates like strings tuned together. So when a ruler goes wrong, Heaven responds with floods, eclipses and strange portents as warnings; reading them is the scholar’s duty. This correlative vision gave Han Confucianism its cosmic frame and its scholars a lever on emperors.","zh":"董仲舒是广川人，专治《春秋》公羊学，在汉廷任博士。约公元前134年，他以三篇著名的对策回答汉武帝关于治道的策问，后来先后做过两位骄横诸侯王的国相，晚年去官，著书授徒。后世传统认为是他说服武帝“罢黜百家，独尊儒术”；现代史学家则认为他的作用没有那么大，而署他名字的书很多部分是后人汇编的。\n\n他把《春秋》读作孔子对历史的“微言大义”，又把儒家伦理与阴阳五行的宇宙论结合起来。天、地、人构成一个整体，彼此感应，如同调好音的琴弦同声相应。因此君主有过失，天就以水旱、日食和种种怪异示警，解读这些灾异是儒者的职责。这种“天人感应”的图景为汉代儒学提供了宇宙框架，也给了儒者制约皇帝的一根杠杆。"},"schools":["confucianism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Luxuriant Dew of the Spring and Autumn Annals (attributed; a later compilation)","zh":"《春秋繁露》（托名董仲舒，实为后人汇编）"},"y":null}],"wiki":"Dong Zhongshu"},
  {"id":"wang-bi","tradition":"eastasia","born":226,"died":249,"life":{"en":"226–249","zh":"226–249年"},"name":{"en":"Wang Bi","zh":"王弼"},"role":{"en":"Prodigy commentator who founded Xuanxue metaphysics","zh":"早慧的注释家，玄学“贵无”论的奠基者"},"idea":{"en":"Read the Laozi and the Book of Changes as pointing to non-being (wu) as the root of all beings, and taught that once meaning is grasped, words and images can be forgotten. He died at twenty-three.","zh":"把《老子》和《周易》解读为指向“以无为本”，并主张“得意忘言”。他年仅二十三岁便去世。"},"detail":{"en":"Wang Bi was a prodigy of the Wei kingdom in the decades after the Han collapsed. Still in his teens he impressed the leading minister He Yan in the salons of “pure conversation”, where scholars debated the Laozi, the Zhuangzi and the Book of Changes. Before he died of illness in 249, at twenty-three, he had written commentaries on the Laozi and the Changes that would be read for more than a thousand years.\n\nHe asked what must lie at the root of all things. Every determinate thing is this and not that, so it cannot be the source of everything else; the root must be what has no determinate form, which the Laozi calls non-being (wu). A good ruler likewise governs by simplicity, not by multiplying measures. For reading he gave a famous rule: words serve images and images serve meaning, so once the meaning is grasped the images, and then the words, can be forgotten. He also said Confucius embodied non-being and therefore did not talk about it, while Laozi, who had not, talked of nothing else. His Laozi commentary became the standard text.","zh":"王弼是汉朝崩溃之后曹魏时期的少年天才。十几岁时，他就在清谈的场合令重臣何晏折服；当时的士人在那里辩论《老子》《庄子》和《周易》。249年他病逝，年仅二十三岁，留下的《老子注》与《周易注》此后被研读了一千多年。\n\n他追问万物的根本是什么。一切确定之物都是“此”而非“彼”，所以不能成为其他一切的来源；根本必须是没有确定形态者，即《老子》所说的“无”。好的君主也应以简约治国，而不是增设措施。对于读经，他提出一条名言：言以明象，象以尽意，“得意而忘象，得象而忘言”。他还说孔子体现了“无”，所以不谈“无”；老子尚未体现它，所以总在谈它。他的《老子注》后来成为通行的标准本。"},"schools":["xuanxue"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Commentary on the Laozi","zh":"《老子注》"},"y":245},{"t":{"en":"Commentary on the Book of Changes","zh":"《周易注》"},"y":247}],"wiki":"Wang Bi"},
  {"id":"guo-xiang","tradition":"eastasia","born":252,"died":312,"life":{"en":"c. 252–312","zh":"约252–312年"},"name":{"en":"Guo Xiang","zh":"郭象"},"role":{"en":"Editor and commentator who shaped the Zhuangzi we read","zh":"编订并注释今本《庄子》的学者"},"idea":{"en":"Denied any creator or “non-being” behind things: each thing generates itself and is fulfilled by following its own nature, so the sage can serve in government while remaining inwardly free. He drew heavily on Xiang Xiu’s earlier commentary.","zh":"否认万物背后有造物者或“无”：万物自生独化，各顺其性便得圆满，因此圣人可以身处庙堂而心游山林。他大量吸收了向秀的旧注。"},"detail":{"en":"Guo Xiang was an official of the Western Jin dynasty, admired for his eloquence in the salons of “pure conversation”. He edited the sprawling Zhuangzi of his day into the 33 chapters we still read and wrote the commentary that shaped how it was understood. An anecdote of the period accused him of passing off Xiang Xiu’s unfinished commentary as his own; how much he borrowed is debated, but the finished interpretation bears his name.\n\nAgainst Wang Bi he denied that non-being could produce anything, since nothing comes from nothing, and he saw no need for a creator either. Each thing “is so of itself” and transforms on its own; its nature sets its allotment. The great Peng bird soaring ninety thousand li and the little dove flitting between trees are equally free if each acts according to its own nature. Freedom therefore does not require leaving society: the sage can hold office and attend to affairs while remaining inwardly at ease. This reading reconciled Daoist freedom with Confucian duty for the educated elite.","zh":"郭象是西晋官员，以善于清谈著称。他把当时篇幅庞杂的《庄子》删定为我们今天读到的三十三篇，并作注，从此决定了后人对《庄子》的理解。当时有一则轶事说他把向秀未完成的注据为己有；他究竟借用了多少尚有争议，但最终成形的诠释署的是他的名字。\n\n他反对王弼的说法，认为“无”不能生“有”，无中不能生有，同时也不需要造物主。万物“自然”“独化”，各自生成变化，其本性规定了各自的分限。大鹏高飞九万里，小鸟在树间跳跃，只要各适其性，就同样逍遥。因此自由不必离开社会：圣人可以身居庙堂、处理政务，而内心依然自在，即“游外以弘内”。这种解读为士人调和了道家的逍遥与儒家的责任。"},"schools":["xuanxue"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Commentary on the Zhuangzi","zh":"《庄子注》"},"y":300}],"wiki":"Guo Xiang"},
  {"id":"huineng","tradition":"eastasia","born":638,"died":713,"life":{"en":"638–713","zh":"638–713年"},"name":{"en":"Huineng","zh":"慧能"},"role":{"en":"Sixth Patriarch of Chan, emblem of sudden awakening","zh":"禅宗六祖，顿悟法门的象征"},"idea":{"en":"Taught that everyone’s original nature is pure and that seeing it directly is enlightenment, not the result of gradually polishing the mind. Much of his traditional story comes from the later Platform Sutra and the polemics of his follower Shenhui.","zh":"主张人人本性清净，直见本性即是觉悟，而非逐渐“拂拭”心镜的结果。他的传统生平很大程度上来自后出的《坛经》以及弟子神会的论争。"},"detail":{"en":"In the traditional story, recorded in the Platform Sutra and later lineage histories, Huineng was an illiterate woodcutter from the far south who heard the Diamond Sutra recited in the market and set off to study with the Fifth Patriarch Hongren. Put to work pounding rice, he answered the senior monk Shenxiu’s verse about keeping the mind like a polished mirror with a verse of his own, and Hongren secretly passed him the robe of succession. He later taught at Caoxi in Guangdong. Historians note that the earliest Platform Sutra dates from about 780 and that the story grew out of Shenhui’s campaign against Shenxiu’s line.\n\nThe teaching attributed to him is that every person’s original nature is already pure and complete, and that seeing it directly is buddhahood. Awakening is therefore sudden: it is not the end of a long process of wiping dust from a mirror, because in the end there is no separate mirror to wipe. Meditation and wisdom are one, and practice belongs in ordinary life. All later Chan and Zen lines trace themselves to him, and the Platform Sutra is the only Chinese work honoured with the title of sutra.","zh":"按《坛经》及后来灯史的传统说法，慧能是岭南一个不识字的樵夫，在市集上听人诵《金刚经》而有所悟，于是北上投奔五祖弘忍。他被派去碓房舂米；首座神秀作偈说心要像明镜一样时时拂拭，慧能也作了一首偈作答，弘忍便暗中把衣钵传给了他。后来他在广东曹溪弘法。史学家指出，最早的《坛经》约成于780年，这个故事是在自称其弟子的神会攻击神秀一系的过程中逐渐成形的。\n\n归在他名下的教法是：每个人的本性本来清净圆满，直接见到它就是成佛。所以觉悟是顿悟，而不是长期拂拭镜上尘埃的结果，因为归根到底并没有一面另外的镜子可拭。定慧一体，修行就在日常生活之中。后来所有的禅宗派系都追溯到他，《坛经》也是唯一一部被尊称为“经”的中国人著作。"},"schools":["chan-zen"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Platform Sutra (attributed; compiled by followers)","zh":"《六祖坛经》（门人记录）"},"y":780}],"wiki":"Huineng"},
  {"id":"zhou-dunyi","tradition":"eastasia","born":1017,"died":1073,"life":{"en":"1017–1073","zh":"1017–1073年"},"name":{"en":"Zhou Dunyi","zh":"周敦颐"},"role":{"en":"Song scholar honoured as the pioneer of Neo-Confucianism","zh":"被尊为理学开山的宋代学者"},"idea":{"en":"His Diagram of the Supreme Ultimate traced how the Ultimate of Non-being and the Supreme Ultimate give rise to yin and yang, the five phases and all things, and he grounded sagehood in sincerity (cheng).","zh":"其《太极图说》描述了“无极而太极”如何化生阴阳、五行与万物，并以“诚”为圣人之本。"},"detail":{"en":"Zhou Dunyi spent his career as a conscientious local official in south and central China and was little known as a thinker in his lifetime. He is remembered for a short essay on loving the lotus, which rises clean from the mud, and for having briefly taught the teenage Cheng brothers in 1046–47. A century later Zhu Xi placed him at the head of the line of the “Learning of the Way”, and his two short works became classics.\n\nHis Explanation of the Diagram of the Supreme Ultimate tells how the world unfolds: “the Ultimate of Non-being, and yet the Supreme Ultimate”. Its movement produces yang and its rest yin; their interplay yields the five phases, and from these come all things, humans receiving the finest share. The sage establishes the human standard through centrality, correctness, humaneness and rightness, “making stillness primary”. His Penetrating the Book of Changes makes sincerity (cheng) the foundation of sagehood. The diagram may derive from Daoist charts, a point Lu Jiuyuan later used against Zhu Xi in a famous debate.","zh":"周敦颐一生在南方和中部地区做勤勉的地方官，生前并不以思想家著称。人们记得他那篇《爱莲说》——莲“出淤泥而不染”——也记得他在1046至1047年间短暂教过少年时的程颢、程颐兄弟。一百多年后，朱熹把他尊为道学的开山之祖，他的两篇短文由此成为经典。\n\n《太极图说》讲述世界如何展开：“无极而太极。”太极动而生阳，静而生阴，阴阳交感化生五行，再化生万物，而人得其秀而最灵。圣人以中正仁义确立人道的标准，“主静立人极”。《通书》则以“诚”为圣人之本。太极图可能源于道教的图式，陆九渊后来在与朱熹的著名辩论中正以此为据发难。"},"schools":["neo-confucianism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Explanation of the Diagram of the Supreme Ultimate","zh":"《太极图说》"},"y":1070},{"t":{"en":"Penetrating the Book of Changes","zh":"《通书》"},"y":1070}],"wiki":"Zhou Dunyi"},
  {"id":"zhang-zai","tradition":"eastasia","born":1020,"died":1077,"life":{"en":"1020–1077","zh":"1020–1077年"},"name":{"en":"Zhang Zai","zh":"张载"},"role":{"en":"Neo-Confucian who saw all things as condensations of qi","zh":"以“气”解释万物聚散的理学家"},"idea":{"en":"Everything is qi condensing and dispersing in the Great Void; because all share one qi, “Heaven is my father and Earth my mother… all people are my siblings.”","zh":"万物都是“太虚”之中气的聚散；因为同出一气，所以“乾称父，坤称母……民吾同胞，物吾与也”。"},"detail":{"en":"Zhang Zai grew up in Hengqu in the northwest and as a young man dreamed of military glory; the statesman Fan Zhongyan, the story goes, told him to read the Doctrine of the Mean instead. He searched through Buddhist and Daoist books before returning to the Confucian classics, held modest offices, and taught at Hengqu, where his school was known as the Learning of Guanzhong. The Cheng brothers, a generation younger, were his relatives and constant discussion partners. He completed Correcting the Unenlightened in 1076 and died the following year.\n\nAgainst Buddhist emptiness and Daoist non-being he argued that there is no void: what looks empty is qi, the vital stuff of the world, in its dispersed state, and all things are qi condensed. Because everything shares this one qi, his Western Inscription draws an ethic of kinship: “Heaven is my father and Earth my mother… all people are my siblings, all things my companions”, while each still has a particular place and duty. Zhu Xi treasured the Western Inscription; six centuries later Wang Fuzhi revived his philosophy of qi.","zh":"张载在关中横渠长大，少年时喜谈兵事；相传名臣范仲淹劝他去读《中庸》。他遍读佛、道之书，终又回到儒家经典，做过一些不大的官，在横渠讲学，其学被称为“关学”。比他晚一辈的程颢、程颐是他的亲戚，也是常相论学的伙伴。他在1076年撰成《正蒙》，次年去世。\n\n他针对佛教的“空”与道家的“无”提出：并不存在真正的虚空，看似空无的“太虚”只是气的散开状态，万物则是气的凝聚。正因为万物同此一气，《西铭》提出了一种亲缘伦理：“乾称父，坤称母……民吾同胞，物吾与也”，同时每个人仍有各自的位置与职分。朱熹极其推重《西铭》；六百年后，王夫之复兴了他的气论。"},"schools":["neo-confucianism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Western Inscription","zh":"《西铭》"},"y":1070},{"t":{"en":"Correcting the Unenlightened","zh":"《正蒙》"},"y":1076}],"wiki":"Zhang Zai"},
  {"id":"cheng-hao","tradition":"eastasia","born":1032,"died":1085,"life":{"en":"1032–1085","zh":"1032–1085年"},"name":{"en":"Cheng Hao","zh":"程颢"},"role":{"en":"Elder Cheng brother, teacher of humaneness as oneness","zh":"二程中的兄长，以“万物一体”论仁"},"idea":{"en":"The humane person forms one body with heaven, earth and the myriad things; principle is realised by quietly recognising this unity rather than by piling up book learning.","zh":"仁者以天地万物为一体；体认天理在于默识这种一体，而非堆积书本知识。"},"detail":{"en":"Cheng Hao, called Mingdao (“Clarifier of the Way”), passed the highest examination in 1057 and served as a local official, known for gentle and effective government. He opposed Wang Anshi’s sweeping New Policies, and from the 1070s he and his younger brother Cheng Yi taught in and around Luoyang. Students remembered his warmth: sitting with him, one said, was like sitting in a spring breeze. He died in 1085.\n\nHis key idea is that the humane person forms one body with heaven, earth and all things. He drew on medical language: Chinese doctors call numbness in the limbs “not humane” (buren), because the patient no longer feels his own hand as part of himself. Selfishness is that kind of numbness towards others. So the learner must first “recognise humaneness” in this lived sense and then preserve it with sincerity and reverence, without forcing it or searching outside. His intuitive emphasis on the mind’s unity with the world was later taken up by Lu Jiuyuan and Wang Yangming, while Zhu Xi built more on his brother.","zh":"程颢号明道，1057年中进士，做过地方官，以宽和而有成效的治理著称。他反对王安石雷厉风行的新法；1070年代起，他和弟弟程颐在洛阳一带讲学。学生们记得他的温厚：有人说，与他相处如坐春风之中。他于1085年去世。\n\n他的核心思想是：仁者与天地万物为一体。他借用医家的说法：手足麻痹被称为“不仁”，因为病人已感觉不到那是自己的手足。自私就是对他人的这种麻木。所以学者须先“识仁”，即在切身的体会中认识这种一体之感，然后以诚敬存养它，“不须防检，不须穷索”。他对心与万物一体的直观强调，后来为陆九渊、王阳明所继承；朱熹则更多地承接了他的弟弟。"},"schools":["neo-confucianism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"On Understanding Humaneness","zh":"《识仁篇》"},"y":1079}],"wiki":"Cheng Hao"},
  {"id":"cheng-yi","tradition":"eastasia","born":1033,"died":1107,"life":{"en":"1033–1107","zh":"1033–1107年"},"name":{"en":"Cheng Yi","zh":"程颐"},"role":{"en":"Younger Cheng brother who made “principle” central","zh":"二程中的弟弟，使“理”成为核心概念"},"idea":{"en":"Taught that “human nature is principle” and that one approaches principle by investigating things one by one while holding reverent seriousness (jing).","zh":"提出“性即理”，主张通过逐一格物并持守“敬”来接近天理。"},"detail":{"en":"Cheng Yi, called Yichuan, made his name as a student at the Imperial University with an essay on what Confucius’s best disciple loved to learn. He long declined office, briefly tutored the boy emperor Zhezong from 1086, and was caught up in the factional fights of the late Northern Song: his teachings were banned and he was exiled before his death in 1107. His sternness became legendary; two disciples, the story goes, waited outside his door in the snow until he finished meditating.\n\nHe made principle (li) the central idea of Confucian thought. Every thing and every relation has its principle, the reason it is so and the way it ought to be, and human nature is that principle in us, which is why it is good. Principle is one, yet its manifestations are many, as one family love differs towards father, brother and stranger. His method has two parts: “cultivation requires reverence; advancing in learning lies in extending knowledge” by investigating things one by one, until understanding breaks through. Zhu Xi built his synthesis on these foundations, and the school is called Cheng–Zhu after them.","zh":"程颐号伊川，在太学读书时以一篇《颜子所好何学论》成名。他长期辞官不就，1086年起曾短暂担任少年哲宗的经筵讲官，又卷入北宋末年的党争：他的学说被禁，本人遭贬，1107年去世。他的严毅成了传说：相传两位弟子去见他，见他正在瞑目静坐，便侍立门外，直到门外积雪一尺。\n\n他把“理”立为儒学的核心概念。每一事物、每一种关系都有其理，即它之所以然与所当然；人性就是在我们身上的理，所以性是善的。“理一而分殊”：理是一，表现却各不相同，就像同一份爱，对父亲、兄弟与路人各有分寸。他的工夫分两面：“涵养须用敬，进学则在致知”，即逐一格物，积累到一定程度便豁然贯通。朱熹在此基础上完成了他的综合，这一学派因而称为“程朱理学”。"},"schools":["neo-confucianism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Commentary on the Book of Changes","zh":"《伊川易传》"},"y":1099}],"wiki":"Cheng Yi (philosopher)"},
  {"id":"zhu-xi","tradition":"eastasia","born":1130,"died":1200,"life":{"en":"1130–1200","zh":"1130–1200年"},"name":{"en":"Zhu Xi","zh":"朱熹"},"role":{"en":"Great synthesiser of Song Neo-Confucianism","zh":"宋代理学的集大成者"},"idea":{"en":"Systematised the theory of principle and qi and put the Four Books at the centre of learning; by investigating things patiently, one finally reaches a sudden, comprehensive understanding of principle.","zh":"系统化了理气论，并将《四书》置于学问的中心；耐心格物日久，终能“豁然贯通”，通晓天理。"},"detail":{"en":"Zhu Xi was born in 1130 in Fujian, passed the highest examination at eighteen, and found his direction under the teacher Li Tong. He preferred temple sinecures that left him time to teach and write, and rebuilt the White Deer Grotto Academy as a model school. His few years at court ended badly: in his last years his teaching was condemned as “false learning” and his followers purged, and he died in 1200 under that cloud. Within decades he was honoured, and from the 14th century until 1905 his commentaries on the Four Books were the basis of the civil service examinations.\n\nEverything, he taught, is made of principle (li), what makes a thing what it is and how it ought to be, and qi, the vital stuff that gives it form and can be clear or turbid. Human nature is principle and therefore good; wrongdoing comes from turbid qi and selfish desire. The remedy is to “investigate things and extend knowledge”, studying the classics, history and the world while holding the mind in reverence, until one day understanding becomes comprehensive. His synthesis became orthodoxy in China, Korea, Japan and Vietnam.","zh":"朱熹1130年生于福建，十八岁中进士，后来师从李侗，确立了为学方向。他宁愿担任管理道观的闲职，好有时间讲学著述，并重建白鹿洞书院，使之成为书院的典范。他在朝中的短暂岁月结局不佳：晚年他的学说被斥为“伪学”，门人遭到打压，1200年他在这片阴影中去世。数十年后他便获得褒崇；从14世纪直到1905年，他的《四书章句集注》一直是科举考试的标准。\n\n他认为一切事物都由“理”与“气”构成：理是事物之所以为此物以及它应当如何的根据，气是赋予它形体、有清有浊的质料。人性即理，所以本善；恶来自浊气与私欲。补救之道是“格物致知”：在持敬中研读经史、考察事物，直到有一天“豁然贯通”。他的综合成为中国、朝鲜、日本与越南的正统学说。"},"schools":["neo-confucianism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Reflections on Things at Hand (with Lü Zuqian)","zh":"《近思录》（与吕祖谦合编）"},"y":1175},{"t":{"en":"Collected Commentaries on the Four Books","zh":"《四书章句集注》"},"y":1190}],"wiki":"Zhu Xi","c":"唯一可靠的朱熹画像.jpg","portrait":{"w":600,"h":675,"v":"71faa10080","author":"向史公哲曰","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%94%AF%E4%B8%80%E5%8F%AF%E9%9D%A0%E7%9A%84%E6%9C%B1%E7%86%B9%E7%94%BB%E5%83%8F.jpg"}},
  {"id":"lu-jiuyuan","tradition":"eastasia","born":1139,"died":1193,"life":{"en":"1139–1193","zh":"1139–1193年"},"name":{"en":"Lu Jiuyuan (Lu Xiangshan)","zh":"陆九渊（象山）"},"role":{"en":"Song scholar who taught that the mind is principle","zh":"主张“心即理”的宋代学者"},"idea":{"en":"“The universe is my mind, and my mind is the universe”: moral principle is complete in the original mind, so learning means first “establishing the great root” rather than fragmented study. He left mainly letters and recorded sayings.","zh":"“宇宙便是吾心，吾心即是宇宙”：道德之理完具于本心，所以为学应“先立乎其大者”，而非支离地考索。他主要留下书信与语录。"},"detail":{"en":"Lu Jiuyuan, from a large and learned family in Jiangxi, passed the highest examination in 1172 and served in several posts, but is remembered as a teacher, above all at Elephant Mountain, which gave him his name Xiangshan. In 1175 the scholar Lü Zuqian brought him and Zhu Xi together at Goose Lake Temple to settle their differences; instead the meeting fixed them. Lu mocked Zhu’s method in a poem as “fragmented”, against his own “simple and easy” way. He died in 1193 while serving as magistrate of Jingmen.\n\nAs a boy, his chronology says, he wrote: “The universe is my mind, and my mind is the universe.” Moral principle is not something to be gathered from outside, book by book and thing by thing; it is already complete in the original mind that every person shares with the sages. Learning therefore means first “establishing what is great”, a phrase from Mencius, by turning back to that mind; once one knows the root, he said, “the Six Classics are my footnotes”. Wang Yangming revived his teaching three centuries later, and the school is called Lu–Wang after them.","zh":"陆九渊出身江西一个人丁众多、世代读书的家族，1172年中进士，做过几任官，但人们记得的是他的讲学，尤其是在象山，他因此号“象山”。1175年，吕祖谦邀他与朱熹在鹅湖寺相会，想调和双方的分歧，结果反而使分歧定型。陆九渊在诗中讥讽朱熹的方法是“支离事业”，而标举自己的“易简工夫”。1193年他在荆门知军任上去世。\n\n据其年谱，他少年时就写道：“宇宙便是吾心，吾心即是宇宙。”道德之理并不需要从外面一书一物地积累而来，它本已完具于人人与圣人相同的本心之中。所以为学首先要“先立乎其大者”（语出《孟子》），回到这本心；他说，学苟知本，“六经皆我注脚”。三百年后王阳明复兴了他的学说，这一学派因此称为“陆王心学”。"},"schools":["xinxue"],"works":[],"wiki":"Lu Jiuyuan"},
  {"id":"dogen","tradition":"eastasia","born":1200,"died":1253,"life":{"en":"1200–1253","zh":"1200–1253年"},"name":{"en":"Dōgen","zh":"道元"},"role":{"en":"Japanese monk who founded Sōtō Zen","zh":"日本曹洞宗的开创者"},"idea":{"en":"Taught “just sitting” (shikantaza): practice is not a means to enlightenment but enlightenment itself, and being is time (uji).","zh":"倡导“只管打坐”：修行不是通往觉悟的手段，修行本身就是觉悟；并提出“有时”，即存在即时间。"},"detail":{"en":"Dōgen was born in Kyoto in 1200 into the aristocracy and lost both parents young. Ordained a Tendai monk on Mount Hiei at thirteen, he was troubled, tradition says, by a question: if all beings already have Buddha-nature, why practise at all? He studied Rinzai Zen in Kyoto and in 1223 sailed to China, where under the master Rujing he experienced the “dropping off of body and mind”. Returning in 1227, he founded a monastery near Kyoto and in 1244 moved to the remote mountains of Echizen, where he built Eiheiji. He died in 1253.\n\nHis answer to his early question is that practice and realisation are one: we do not sit in meditation in order to become enlightened; “just sitting” (shikantaza) is enlightenment expressing itself. In the essays of the Shōbōgenzō, written in Japanese rather than Chinese, he bends language to show this, writing that being is time, so that a pine tree or a moment of practice is time itself, and that impermanence is Buddha-nature. Sōtō Zen, which he founded, became one of Japan’s largest Buddhist schools, and modern philosophers rediscovered him as a thinker.","zh":"道元1200年生于京都的贵族家庭，幼年父母双亡，十三岁在比叡山出家为天台宗僧人。相传一个问题困扰着他：若众生本具佛性，为何还要修行？他在京都学习临济禅，1223年渡海入宋，在天童山如净门下体验到“身心脱落”。1227年回国后，他先在京都附近建寺，1244年移居偏远的越前山中，开创永平寺，1253年去世。\n\n他对早年疑问的回答是“修证一等”：我们不是为了开悟而坐禅，“只管打坐”本身就是觉悟的显现。在以日文而非汉文写成的《正法眼藏》诸篇中，他改造语言来表达这一点：“有时”，即存在就是时间，一棵松、一刻修行都是时间本身；无常即是佛性。他开创的曹洞宗成为日本最大的佛教宗派之一，近代哲学家又把他重新发现为一位思想家。"},"schools":["chan-zen"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Universal Recommendation for Zazen","zh":"《普劝坐禅仪》"},"y":1227},{"t":{"en":"Shōbōgenzō","zh":"《正法眼藏》"},"y":1240}],"wiki":"Dōgen"},
  {"id":"wang-yangming","tradition":"eastasia","born":1472,"died":1529,"life":{"en":"1472–1529","zh":"1472–1529年"},"name":{"en":"Wang Yangming","zh":"王阳明（王守仁）"},"role":{"en":"Ming official, general and master of the School of Mind","zh":"明代官员、军事家，心学的集大成者"},"idea":{"en":"Principle is in the mind; everyone has innate good knowing (liangzhi), and knowing and acting are one, so moral learning means extending that knowing in action.","zh":"理在心中；人人皆有良知，知行合一，因此道德修养就是在行动中“致良知”。"},"detail":{"en":"Wang Shouren, called Yangming, was born in Zhejiang in 1472 and passed the highest examination in 1499. As a young man he tried Zhu Xi’s method literally, contemplating the bamboo in a courtyard for seven days to find its principle, and fell ill. In 1506 he angered the powerful eunuch Liu Jin, was flogged and banished to Longchang in the mountains of Guizhou; there, in 1508, he realised that his own nature was sufficient and that he had been seeking principle in the wrong place. He went on to suppress rebellions, capture the rebel Prince of Ning in 1519, and died in 1529 returning from a campaign.\n\nPrinciple, he taught, is not in things but in the mind. Everyone has innate good knowing (liangzhi), the capacity to tell right from wrong, and knowing and acting are one: seeing a beautiful colour already belongs to knowing and liking it to acting, yet the liking is there the moment one sees. Someone who “knows” filial piety but does not practise it does not yet know it. Moral life means extending innate knowing in every situation. His school spread through Ming China and later Japan.","zh":"王守仁号阳明，1472年生于浙江，1499年中进士。年轻时他照字面实践朱熹的方法，对着庭前竹子“格”了七天，想格出竹子之理，结果病倒了。1506年他得罪权宦刘瑾，被廷杖后贬到贵州深山的龙场驿；1508年在那里他领悟到“吾性自足”，此前是向外求理求错了地方。此后他巡抚地方、平定叛乱，1519年擒获起兵的宁王，1529年在征战归途中去世。\n\n他认为理不在物而在心。人人都有“良知”，即分辨是非的能力，而且“知行合一”：见好色属知，好好色属行，但见那好色时便已自好了。一个“知道”孝却不去行孝的人，其实还不算真知孝。道德生活就是在每一处境中“致良知”。他的学派风行明代中国，后来又传到日本。"},"schools":["xinxue"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Instructions for Practical Living","zh":"《传习录》"},"y":1518},{"t":{"en":"Inquiry on the Great Learning","zh":"《大学问》"},"y":1527}],"wiki":"Wang Yangming","c":"王守仁.jpg","portrait":{"w":600,"h":719,"v":"41cb7287fc","author":"Shen Junhui (沈俊繪)","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":null,"page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E7%8E%8B%E5%AE%88%E4%BB%81.jpg"}},
  {"id":"yi-hwang","tradition":"eastasia","born":1501,"died":1570,"life":{"en":"1501–1570","zh":"1501–1570年"},"name":{"en":"Yi Hwang (Toegye)","zh":"李滉（退溪）"},"role":{"en":"Leading Neo-Confucian scholar of Joseon Korea","zh":"朝鲜王朝最重要的理学家之一"},"idea":{"en":"In the Four–Seven debate with Ki Taesŭng he argued that the four moral sprouts issue from principle and the seven emotions from qi, giving principle an active role in moral life.","zh":"在与奇大升的“四七之辩”中主张四端发于理、七情发于气，赋予“理”在道德生活中的能动作用。"},"detail":{"en":"Yi Hwang, known by his pen name Toegye, was born in 1501 near Andong in southeastern Korea. He passed the state examination in 1534 and served the Joseon court, but repeatedly resigned to return home and study; he founded the school that became the Dosan Academy. From 1559 he exchanged letters for years with a younger scholar, Ki Taesŭng, in what became Korea’s most famous philosophical debate. In 1568 he presented the young King Sŏnjo with his Ten Diagrams on Sage Learning, and he died two years later.\n\nThe debate asked where feelings come from. Mencius spoke of four moral “sprouts” such as compassion; the Book of Rites lists seven ordinary emotions such as joy and anger. In Zhu Xi’s terms of principle and qi, Toegye concluded that the four issue from principle with qi following, while the seven issue from qi with principle riding on it. The point was that moral feeling has a source deeper than ordinary emotion, and that principle is active, not inert. His stress on reverence shaped Korean scholarship and was admired by Japanese Confucians such as Yamazaki Ansai.","zh":"李滉号退溪，1501年生于朝鲜东南部的安东一带，1534年科举及第，在朝鲜王朝任官，却多次辞官回乡治学，创办了后来的陶山书院。1559年起，他与后辈学者奇大升书信往来多年，展开了朝鲜最著名的哲学论辩。1568年他向年轻的宣祖进呈《圣学十图》，两年后去世。\n\n这场论辩追问情感从何而来。孟子讲恻隐等“四端”，《礼记》讲喜怒等“七情”。用朱熹理与气的概念，退溪最后的表述是：四端是“理发而气随之”，七情是“气发而理乘之”。要点在于：道德情感有比一般情绪更深的来源，而理是能动的，不是僵死的。他对“敬”的强调塑造了朝鲜的学术，也受到山崎闇斋等日本儒者的推崇。"},"schools":["neo-confucianism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Ten Diagrams on Sage Learning","zh":"《圣学十图》"},"y":1568}],"wiki":"Yi Hwang"},
  {"id":"yi-i","tradition":"eastasia","born":1536,"died":1584,"life":{"en":"1536–1584","zh":"1536–1584年"},"name":{"en":"Yi I (Yulgok)","zh":"李珥（栗谷）"},"role":{"en":"Korean statesman-scholar and Toegye’s younger rival","zh":"朝鲜政治家兼学者，退溪的后辈论敌"},"idea":{"en":"Held that only qi issues and principle “rides” on it, so the four sprouts are a subset of the seven emotions; he also pressed for practical reforms of government.","zh":"主张“气发理乘”，只有气能发动，理乘于其上，因此四端包含在七情之中；他还力主务实的政治改革。"},"detail":{"en":"Yi I, known as Yulgok, was born in 1536 in Gangneung; his mother, Sin Saimdang, was a celebrated painter and poet. She died when he was fifteen; after three years of mourning he spent about a year in a Buddhist monastery in the Diamond Mountains before returning to Confucian learning. He is said to have come first in the state examinations nine times, rose to be minister of personnel and of war, and pressed the court for practical reforms in taxation, education and defence. He died in 1584.\n\nIn the Four–Seven debate he sided with Ki Taesŭng against Toegye. Only qi issues, he argued, and principle “rides” on it; there is no second route by which principle issues on its own. The four moral sprouts are simply the good portion of the seven emotions. His formula “principle pervades, qi delimits” explains why one universal principle appears in limited, varied things. He also wrote a primer for students and the Essentials of Sage Learning for the king. The rival schools of Toegye and Yulgok shaped Korean thought for three centuries.","zh":"李珥号栗谷，1536年生于江陵，母亲申师任堂是著名的书画家和诗人。他十五岁时母亲去世，守孝三年后，他在金刚山的佛寺里待了大约一年，才回到儒学。据说他在科举中九次夺魁，官至吏曹判书、兵曹判书，屡次向朝廷力陈赋税、教育与国防的实际改革。他于1584年去世。\n\n在四七之辩中，他站在奇大升一边反对退溪。他认为只有气能“发”，理“乘”在气上，并不存在理单独发动的第二条路，即“气发理乘一途说”；四端不过是七情中善的那一部分。他的“理通气局”之说，解释了同一个普遍之理为何显现在有限而各异的事物之中。他还为初学者写了《击蒙要诀》，为君主写了《圣学辑要》。退溪与栗谷两派的对立，塑造了此后三百年的朝鲜思想。"},"schools":["neo-confucianism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Essentials of Sage Learning","zh":"《圣学辑要》"},"y":1575}],"wiki":"Yi I"},
  {"id":"nishida","tradition":"eastasia","born":1870,"died":1945,"life":{"en":"1870–1945","zh":"1870–1945年"},"name":{"en":"Nishida Kitarō","zh":"西田几多郎"},"role":{"en":"Founder of the Kyoto School","zh":"京都学派的创始人"},"idea":{"en":"Began from “pure experience” prior to the split between subject and object, and later developed a “logic of place” (basho) whose ultimate ground is absolute nothingness.","zh":"从主客未分的“纯粹经验”出发，后来发展出“场所的逻辑”，其最终根基是“绝对无”。"},"detail":{"en":"Nishida Kitarō was born in 1870 in Ishikawa prefecture on the Sea of Japan. Because he had not finished the standard course he could enrol at Tokyo Imperial University only as a special student, and for years he taught in provincial schools while practising Zen intensively. An Inquiry into the Good (1911) made him famous; he taught at Kyoto Imperial University from 1910 to 1928, and his students and colleagues became the Kyoto School. His late writings on the nation and the war remain controversial. He died on 7 June 1945, about two months before Japan’s surrender.\n\nHe began from “pure experience”: when we are absorbed in music, say, there is not yet an “I” hearing and a sound heard, only the experience itself; subject and object are later abstractions from it. He later recast this as a “logic of place” (basho): every judgment and every self exists within a field that contains it, and the final field is not a thing at all but “absolute nothingness”, a notion with roots in Zen. It was the first major attempt to state an East Asian insight in the vocabulary of Western philosophy.","zh":"西田几多郎1870年生于日本海沿岸的石川县。因为没有读完正规课程，他只能以选科生身份进入东京帝国大学，此后多年在地方学校教书，同时刻苦参禅。1911年的《善的研究》使他一举成名；1910至1928年他任教于京都帝国大学，他的学生与同事形成了京都学派。他晚年关于国家与战争的文字至今仍有争议。1945年6月7日他去世，距日本投降约两个月。\n\n他从“纯粹经验”出发：比如沉浸在音乐中时，还没有一个“我”在听、一个声音被听，只有经验本身；主体与客体是后来从中抽象出来的。后来他把这一思想改造为“场所的逻辑”：每个判断、每个自我都存在于包含它的“场所”之中，而最终的场所根本不是一个东西，而是“绝对无”，这一观念有其禅学根源。这是用西方哲学的语汇表达东亚洞见的第一次重大尝试。"},"schools":["kyoto"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"An Inquiry into the Good","zh":"《善的研究》"},"y":1911},{"t":{"en":"From That Which Acts to That Which Sees","zh":"《从动者到见者》"},"y":1927}],"wiki":"Kitaro Nishida"},
  {"id":"tanabe","tradition":"eastasia","born":1885,"died":1962,"life":{"en":"1885–1962","zh":"1885–1962年"},"name":{"en":"Tanabe Hajime","zh":"田边元"},"role":{"en":"Kyoto School philosopher and Nishida’s critic","zh":"京都学派哲学家，西田的批评者"},"idea":{"en":"Criticised Nishida’s “place of nothingness” as too contemplative and proposed a “logic of species” centred on social mediation; after the war he called for “metanoetics”, philosophy as repentance.","zh":"批评西田的“无的场所”过于静观，提出以社会中介为中心的“种的逻辑”；战后则倡导“忏悔道”，即作为忏悔的哲学。"},"detail":{"en":"Tanabe Hajime, born in Tokyo in 1885, studied mathematics and then philosophy at Tokyo Imperial University and wrote on the philosophy of science. Nishida brought him to Kyoto in 1919; in 1922–24 he studied in Germany, where he met Husserl and the young Heidegger. He succeeded Nishida in Kyoto and from 1930 openly criticised him. During the war his philosophy was used to support the state, and in 1945 he retired to a mountain village, where he wrote a book of self-criticism. He died in 1962.\n\nHis “logic of species” argued that individuals and humanity are always mediated by a “species”, a concrete community such as a nation; he meant it as social philosophy, but it lent itself to wartime nationalism. In Philosophy as Metanoetics (1946) he concluded that reason, pushed to its limits, breaks down, and that philosophy must pass through repentance (zange), relying on an “Other-power” in the spirit of Shinran’s Pure Land Buddhism, to be reborn. This turn made him a partner in later Buddhist–Christian dialogue and a model of a philosopher confronting his own complicity.","zh":"田边元1885年生于东京，在东京帝大先学数学、后学哲学，最初研究科学哲学。1919年西田把他请到京都；1922至1924年他留学德国，结识胡塞尔和年轻的海德格尔。他后来接替西田的讲席，并从1930年起公开批评西田。战时他的哲学被用来为国家张目；1945年他退隐山村，写下自我批判之作，1962年去世。\n\n他的“种的逻辑”认为，个人与人类之间总有一个“种”作为中介，即民族、国家这样具体的共同体；他本意是建立社会哲学，但这一理论却容易被战时的国家主义所利用。在1946年的《作为忏悔道的哲学》中，他断言理性被推到极限便会崩溃，哲学必须经过忏悔，依凭类似亲鸾净土思想中的“他力”而获得新生。这一转向使他后来成为佛教与基督教对话的伙伴，也成为一位直面自身责任的哲学家的范例。"},"schools":["kyoto"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Philosophy as Metanoetics","zh":"《作为忏悔道的哲学》"},"y":1946}],"wiki":"Hajime Tanabe"},
  {"id":"xiong-shili","tradition":"eastasia","born":1885,"died":1968,"life":{"en":"1885–1968","zh":"1885–1968年"},"name":{"en":"Xiong Shili","zh":"熊十力"},"role":{"en":"Founder of New Confucian metaphysics","zh":"现代新儒家形上学的奠基者"},"idea":{"en":"Trained in Yogācāra, he broke with it: original reality (benti) and its functioning (yong) are not two, but one ceaselessly creative process grasped in the moral mind.","zh":"他出身唯识学，后又与之决裂，主张“体用不二”：本体与其作用并非两物，而是在道德本心中体认到的生生不息的创造过程。"},"detail":{"en":"Xiong Shili, born in 1885 to a poor family in Huanggang, Hubei, joined the revolutionary army that brought down the Qing dynasty before turning to scholarship. In 1920 he went to study Yogācāra Buddhism under Ouyang Jingwu at the China Inner Learning Institute in Nanjing, and in 1922 Cai Yuanpei invited him to teach it at Peking University. His New Treatise on Consciousness-Only (1932) broke with his teacher’s school and provoked a fierce Buddhist rebuttal. He stayed in mainland China after 1949, wrote on despite growing isolation, and died in 1968 during the Cultural Revolution.\n\nHe rejected the Yogācāra picture of an unchanging true reality behind the changing world: substance and function are not two, and reality is ceaseless creative transformation, unfolding through contracting and expanding movements. His image is the ocean and its waves: the ocean is not something hidden behind the waves, it is the waves. This reality is known not by analytic reasoning but by the original mind, the moral intuition Confucians call humaneness, which made the Book of Changes, not Buddhism, his final authority. His students Mou Zongsan, Tang Junyi and Xu Fuguan carried New Confucianism on.","zh":"熊十力1885年生于湖北黄冈的贫寒之家，曾投身反清革命军，后来转向学问。1920年他到南京支那内学院随欧阳竟无学唯识，1922年蔡元培请他到北大讲授唯识。1932年的《新唯识论》与师门决裂，引来佛学界激烈驳斥。1949年后他留在大陆，在孤立中坚持著述，1968年在“文革”中去世。\n\n他反对唯识学在变动世界背后另立不变真如的图景，主张“体用不二”：实在就是生生不息的创造性转化，通过“翕”“辟”两种动势展开。他的比喻是大海水与众沤：大海并不藏在波浪背后，大海就是那些波浪。这一实在不能靠分析的“量智”，只能靠本心的“性智”把握，所以他最终归宗《周易》而非佛学。弟子牟宗三、唐君毅、徐复观把新儒学推向前去。"},"schools":["new-confucianism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"New Treatise on Consciousness-Only","zh":"《新唯识论》"},"y":1932}],"wiki":"Xiong Shili"},
  {"id":"hu-shi","tradition":"eastasia","born":1891,"died":1962,"life":{"en":"1891–1962","zh":"1891–1962年"},"name":{"en":"Hu Shi","zh":"胡适"},"role":{"en":"May Fourth reformer who brought pragmatism to China","zh":"把实用主义带入中国的五四运动领袖"},"idea":{"en":"A student of John Dewey at Columbia, he preached “bold hypotheses, careful verification”, applied scientific method to the study of Chinese tradition, and championed writing in the vernacular.","zh":"他在哥伦比亚大学师从杜威，提倡“大胆的假设，小心的求证”，以科学方法整理国故，并倡导白话文。"},"detail":{"en":"Hu Shi was born in Shanghai in 1891 to a family from Jixi, Anhui. A Boxer Indemnity scholarship took him to Cornell and then Columbia, where he studied under John Dewey and finished his dissertation in 1917 (the degree came in 1927). At Peking University he called for literature in the vernacular and became a leading voice of the New Culture and May Fourth movements, interpreting for Dewey on his 1919–21 tour. He was later ambassador to the United States, president of Peking University and head of the Academia Sinica in Taiwan, where he died in 1962.\n\nHe took from Dewey the view that ideas are tools for solving problems and must be tested by their consequences, and summed up method as “bold hypotheses, careful verification”. He urged “more study of problems, less talk of isms”, against grand ideological solutions. He turned critical method on China’s past: his Outline of the History of Chinese Philosophy began with Laozi and Confucius, not legendary sage kings, and his 1926 discovery of Shenhui manuscripts from Dunhuang rewrote the history of Chan.","zh":"胡适1891年生于上海，祖籍安徽绩溪。他考取庚款留美，先入康奈尔，后在哥伦比亚大学师从杜威，1917年完成博士论文（学位1927年授予）。回国后任教北大，提倡白话文学，成为新文化运动与五四运动的代表人物，并在杜威1919至1921年访华期间担任翻译。后来他出任驻美大使、北大校长和台湾“中央研究院”院长，1962年在台北去世。\n\n他从杜威那里接受了这样的观点：观念是解决问题的工具，要用后果来检验，并把方法概括为“大胆的假设，小心的求证”。他主张“多研究些问题，少谈些主义”。他把批判方法用于中国自己的过去：《中国哲学史大纲》从老子、孔子讲起，而不从传说中的上古圣王讲起；1926年他在敦煌写本中发现神会的著作，改写了禅宗的历史。"},"schools":["pragmatism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Some Modest Proposals for the Reform of Literature","zh":"《文学改良刍议》"},"y":1917},{"t":{"en":"Outline of the History of Chinese Philosophy, vol. 1","zh":"《中国哲学史大纲》（卷上）"},"y":1919}],"wiki":"Hu Shih"},
  {"id":"liang-shuming","tradition":"eastasia","born":1893,"died":1988,"life":{"en":"1893–1988","zh":"1893–1988年"},"name":{"en":"Liang Shuming","zh":"梁漱溟"},"role":{"en":"Philosopher and rural reformer, called “the last Confucian”","zh":"哲学家与乡村建设者，被称为“最后的儒家”"},"idea":{"en":"Argued that Western, Chinese and Indian cultures express three different orientations of the will, and that the world would turn from the Western path to the Chinese one; he later led the rural reconstruction movement.","zh":"认为西方、中国、印度文化体现了意欲的三种不同路向，世界将由西方路向转向中国路向；后来他投身乡村建设运动。"},"detail":{"en":"Liang Shuming was born in Beijing in 1893 into a scholar-official family. Largely self-taught and for a time drawn to Buddhism, he was hired by Cai Yuanpei in 1917 to teach Indian philosophy at Peking University. His Eastern and Western Cultures and Their Philosophies (1921) answered the New Culture movement’s rejection of tradition. He led a rural reconstruction experiment in Zouping, Shandong (1931–37), helped found the Democratic League, and in 1953 openly contradicted Mao Zedong and was publicly rebuked. He died in 1988.\n\nHe argued that cultures are shaped by the basic direction of the will. The West strives forward to satisfy desires, producing science and democracy; China seeks harmony and balance between desire and circumstance; India turns back and seeks to quiet desire altogether. Each fits a stage of human life, and once the West’s problems of material want were solved, he predicted, the world would need China’s attitude, and later India’s. In his later work he described China as a society organised around ethical relationships rather than individuals or classes. The historian Guy Alitto called him “the last Confucian”.","zh":"梁漱溟1893年生于北京一个士大夫家庭，基本自学成才，一度倾心佛学，1917年被蔡元培聘到北大讲授印度哲学。1921年的《东西文化及其哲学》回应了新文化运动对传统的否定。他在山东邹平主持乡村建设实验（1931–1937），参与创建中国民主同盟；1953年当面顶撞毛泽东，遭到公开批判。1988年去世。\n\n他认为文化取决于意欲的根本方向：西方意欲向前，力求满足欲望，产生了科学与民主；中国意欲自为调和、持中；印度意欲反身向后，平息欲望本身。三者各适合人类生活的一个阶段；他预言西方解决物质匮乏之后，世界将需要中国的态度，再往后是印度的。后来他把中国描述为“伦理本位”的社会。史学家艾恺称他为“最后的儒家”。"},"schools":["new-confucianism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Eastern and Western Cultures and Their Philosophies","zh":"《东西文化及其哲学》"},"y":1921},{"t":{"en":"The Essential Meaning of Chinese Culture","zh":"《中国文化要义》"},"y":1949}],"wiki":"Liang Shuming"},
  {"id":"feng-youlan","tradition":"eastasia","born":1895,"died":1990,"life":{"en":"1895–1990","zh":"1895–1990年"},"name":{"en":"Feng Youlan","zh":"冯友兰"},"role":{"en":"Historian of Chinese philosophy and builder of a “new principle learning”","zh":"中国哲学史家，“新理学”的构建者"},"idea":{"en":"Used Western logical analysis to rebuild Cheng–Zhu principle learning and wrote a landmark modern history of Chinese philosophy; he described four realms of life, rising to the “realm of heaven and earth”.","zh":"用西方逻辑分析方法重建程朱理学，写出具有里程碑意义的现代中国哲学史；他提出人生四境界，最高为“天地境界”。"},"detail":{"en":"Feng Youlan, born in Henan in 1895, studied at Peking University and took a doctorate at Columbia. As a professor at Tsinghua he published his two-volume History of Chinese Philosophy (1931–34), the first full modern account of the subject. During the war, teaching in exile in Kunming, he wrote six books building his own system, beginning with the New Treatise on Principle (1939). After 1949 he repeatedly renounced his earlier views, joined the campaign against Confucius in the 1970s, and in his last years rewrote his history once more. He died in 1990.\n\nHis “new principle learning” used Western logical analysis to rebuild Cheng–Zhu thought. For anything square there is the principle of squareness, a purely formal concept that “is” even if nothing square exists; principle, qi, the Great Whole and the Dao are reached by analysis, not found in the world. Human life has four realms, depending on how much one understands: the natural, the utilitarian, the moral and the “realm of heaven and earth”, where one sees oneself as part of the whole. His Short History of Chinese Philosophy (1948) shaped how generations abroad met the subject.","zh":"冯友兰1895年生于河南，先后就读于北京大学和哥伦比亚大学并获博士学位。任教清华期间，他出版了两卷本《中国哲学史》（1931–1934），这是第一部完整的现代中国哲学史。抗战时在昆明西南联大，他写成“贞元六书”建立自己的体系，首部是1939年的《新理学》。1949年后他多次否定旧说，1970年代参与“批孔”，晚年又重写哲学史，1990年去世。\n\n他的“新理学”用逻辑分析重建程朱理学：凡方的东西都有“方之所以为方”的理，这是纯形式的概念，即使没有方的东西它也“有”；理、气、大全、道都是分析所得的概念，而非世界中的事物。人生依觉解多少分为四种境界：自然、功利、道德，以及自觉为宇宙整体一部分的“天地境界”。他的英文《中国哲学简史》（1948）影响了几代海外读者。"},"schools":["new-confucianism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"A History of Chinese Philosophy","zh":"《中国哲学史》"},"y":1934},{"t":{"en":"New Treatise on Principle","zh":"《新理学》"},"y":1939}],"wiki":"Feng Youlan"},
  {"id":"nishitani","tradition":"eastasia","born":1900,"died":1990,"life":{"en":"1900–1990","zh":"1900–1990年"},"name":{"en":"Nishitani Keiji","zh":"西谷启治"},"role":{"en":"Kyoto School thinker of nihilism and emptiness","zh":"思考虚无主义与“空”的京都学派哲学家"},"idea":{"en":"Argued that modern nihilism can only be overcome by passing through nothingness to the standpoint of emptiness (śūnyatā). He attended Heidegger’s lectures in Freiburg in 1937–39.","zh":"主张现代虚无主义只能通过穿越“虚无”而达到“空”（śūnyatā）的立场来克服。1937至1939年，他在弗赖堡听过海德格尔的课。"},"detail":{"en":"Nishitani Keiji was born in 1900 in Ishikawa prefecture. Illness and his father’s early death marked his youth, and he found his questions in Nietzsche, Dostoevsky and Zen. He studied under Nishida in Kyoto and attended Heidegger’s lectures in Freiburg from 1937 to 1939. His part in wartime discussions of Japan’s “world-historical” mission led the occupation authorities to bar him from teaching for several years. Reinstated, he taught the philosophy of religion at Kyoto and became the school’s leading voice abroad; he died in 1990.\n\nHis theme was nihilism. Modern science and the loss of faith in God leave a “field of nihility” beneath everything, an abyss that most people avoid looking into. Nietzsche showed the way partway, by facing it; but Nishitani held that one must go through nihility to the standpoint of emptiness (śūnyatā), where a thing is no longer an object set over against a self but shows itself as what it is, on its own ground. Religion, on this view, is a transformation of how the self stands in this world. His Religion and Nothingness opened a long dialogue with Christian theology.","zh":"西谷启治1900年生于石川县，少年时疾病与丧父给他留下深刻印记，他在尼采、陀思妥耶夫斯基和禅中找到了自己的问题。他在京都师从西田，1937至1939年在弗赖堡听海德格尔讲课。因参与战时关于日本“世界史使命”的讨论，战后他被占领当局禁止执教数年。复职后他在京都讲授宗教哲学，成为京都学派在海外的主要代言人，1990年去世。\n\n他的主题是虚无主义。现代科学与上帝信仰的丧失，在万物之下留下一片“虚无之场”，多数人避而不视。尼采正视了它，走了一半的路；西谷认为必须穿过虚无，抵达“空”的立场：事物不再是与自我相对的对象，而在自身的根基上如其所是地显现。宗教因此是自我在此世存在方式的转变。他的《宗教是什么》开启了与基督教神学的长期对话。"},"schools":["kyoto"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Nihilism (Nihirizumu; in English as The Self-Overcoming of Nihilism)","zh":"《虚无主义》（日文原题《ニヒリズム》）"},"y":1949},{"t":{"en":"What Is Religion? (in English as Religion and Nothingness)","zh":"《宗教是什么》（英译本名《宗教与虚无》）"},"y":1961}],"wiki":"Keiji Nishitani"},
  {"id":"mou-zongsan","tradition":"eastasia","born":1909,"died":1995,"life":{"en":"1909–1995","zh":"1909–1995年"},"name":{"en":"Mou Zongsan","zh":"牟宗三"},"role":{"en":"Leading second-generation New Confucian and translator of Kant","zh":"第二代现代新儒家的代表，康德著作的译者"},"idea":{"en":"Argued, against Kant, that humans do have “intellectual intuition” through the moral mind, and built a Confucian “moral metaphysics” that he traced mainly through the Lu–Wang line.","zh":"针对康德，主张人通过道德本心确有“智的直觉”，并建立了儒家的“道德形上学”，以陆王一系为其主要传承。"},"detail":{"en":"Mou Zongsan, born in 1909 in Qixia, Shandong, studied philosophy at Peking University, working on logic and on Russell and Whitehead, and in 1932 met Xiong Shili, who became his teacher. He left the mainland in 1949, taught in Taiwan and from 1960 in Hong Kong, and in 1958 co-signed with Tang Junyi, Xu Fuguan and Carsun Chang a manifesto asking the West to treat Chinese culture as a living tradition. He translated Kant’s three Critiques and died in Taipei in 1995.\n\nKant held that only an infinite mind, such as God’s, could have “intellectual intuition”, knowing things in themselves rather than as they appear. Mou argued that the Confucian tradition shows otherwise: the original moral mind, Mencius’s heart and Wang Yangming’s innate knowing, is an infinite mind in us, and in moral action it grasps reality directly as value. On this basis he built a “moral metaphysics” and claimed that science and democracy could grow from Confucianism if innate knowing deliberately “lowers itself” into ordinary cognition. Controversially, he judged Zhu Xi a side branch of the tradition. He shaped Confucian philosophy in Taiwan and Hong Kong for decades.","zh":"牟宗三1909年生于山东栖霞，就读北大哲学系，研究逻辑与罗素、怀特海，1932年结识熊十力，从此以之为师。1949年他离开大陆，先在台湾任教，1960年起在香港。1958年他与唐君毅、徐复观、张君劢联名发表宣言，呼吁西方把中国文化当作活着的传统。他译出康德三大批判，1995年在台北去世。\n\n康德认为只有上帝那样的无限心灵才有“智的直觉”，能认识物自身。牟宗三论证儒家传统表明并非如此：孟子的本心、阳明的良知正是我们身上的无限心，在道德实践中直接把握作为价值的实在。据此他建立“道德的形上学”，并主张良知自觉“坎陷”为认知心，便能从儒学开出科学与民主。他还颇具争议地判朱熹为“别子为宗”。他塑造了数十年港台的儒家哲学。"},"schools":["new-confucianism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Mind and Human Nature","zh":"《心体与性体》"},"y":1968},{"t":{"en":"Phenomenon and Thing-in-Itself","zh":"《现象与物自身》"},"y":1975}],"wiki":"Mou Zongsan"},
  {"id":"uddalaka","tradition":"indian","born":-780,"died":-700,"life":{"en":"fl. c. 8th c. BCE (traditional)","zh":"约公元前8世纪（传统说法）"},"name":{"en":"Uddālaka Āruṇi","zh":"乌达罗迦·阿鲁尼"},"role":{"en":"Upanishadic teacher who told his son ‘You are that’","zh":"对儿子说出“你就是那个”的奥义书导师"},"idea":{"en":"In the Chāndogya Upaniṣad he teaches his son Śvetaketu, with images like salt dissolved in water, that the subtle essence of all things is the self: ‘you are that’ (tat tvam asi). He also held that the world arose from ‘being’ (sat), not from non-being.","zh":"在《歌者奥义书》中，他用盐溶于水等比喻教导儿子希婆多凯土：万物精微的本质就是自我——“你就是那个”。他还认为世界源于“有”，而非源于“无”。"},"detail":{"en":"Uddālaka Āruṇi is one of the two most quoted teachers of the early Upanishads, a Brahmin of the Kuru-Pañcāla country of northern India, perhaps of the 8th century BCE. Nothing about him is known outside these texts, where he appears as a learned ritualist, as the teacher of his son Śvetaketu and, in the Vedic lineages, of Yājñavalkya, who later bested him in debate.\n\nHis most famous lesson, in the sixth chapter of the Chāndogya Upaniṣad, is a series of thought experiments. Dissolve salt in water: you cannot see it, yet every sip tastes of salt. Split a banyan seed: you find nothing, yet from that subtle nothing a great tree grows. So, he says, one fine essence pervades everything, and “that is the self—you are that” (tat tvam asi). Against those who said the world began from non-being, he held that it came from being (sat). Śaṅkara later made “you are that” one of Advaita’s great sayings.","zh":"乌达罗迦·阿鲁尼是早期奥义书中被引用最多的两位导师之一，是北印度俱卢—般遮罗地区的婆罗门，大约生活在公元前8世纪。除这些文献外，我们对他一无所知：书中的他是精通祭祀的学者，是儿子希婆多盖杜的老师；在吠陀师承谱系里，他也是耶若婆佉的老师，后者后来在辩论中胜过了他。\n\n他最著名的教导见于《歌者奥义书》第六章，是一组思想实验：把盐溶在水里，看不见盐，却每一口都是咸的；剖开一粒榕树种子，里面什么也看不见，可正是从这微细的“无”中长出参天大树。他说，同样有一种精微的本质遍在万物，“那就是自我——你就是那个”。他还反对世界从“无”中产生的说法，主张万物源于“有”。后来商羯罗把“你就是那个”奉为不二论的“大格言”之一。"},"schools":["upanishads"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Chāndogya Upaniṣad (teachings ascribed to him)","zh":"《歌者奥义书》（归于他的教导）"},"y":-650}],"wiki":"Uddalaka Aruni"},
  {"id":"yajnavalkya","tradition":"indian","born":-750,"died":-680,"life":{"en":"fl. c. 8th–7th c. BCE (traditional)","zh":"约公元前8–前7世纪（传统说法）"},"name":{"en":"Yājñavalkya","zh":"耶若婆佉"},"role":{"en":"Sage who wins the great debate at King Janaka’s court","zh":"在遮那迦王宫廷辩论中胜出的圣者"},"idea":{"en":"Known from his dialogues in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka Upaniṣad, he taught that the self is the unseen seer and inner controller, describable only as ‘not this, not this’ (neti neti).","zh":"他的思想见于《广林奥义书》中的对话：自我是不可见的见者、内在的主宰，只能用“不是这个，不是这个”来描述。"},"detail":{"en":"Yājñavalkya is the dominant voice of the Bṛhadāraṇyaka Upaniṣad (c. 700 BCE), the oldest of the major Upanishads. Tradition makes him a priest at the Videha court in eastern India, a pupil of Uddālaka and the source of the “White” Yajurveda. The Upaniṣad shows him carrying off a thousand cows as prize at King Janaka’s great debate, and later teaching his wife Maitreyī before leaving home as a renouncer. No dates or independent records survive.\n\nIn the debate, questioner after questioner asks what lies behind the world, and he keeps pointing inward. The self (ātman) is “the seer of seeing” and “the inner controller”: it cannot be seen, because it is what does the seeing, so it can only be described by negation—“not this, not this” (neti neti). To Maitreyī he explains that we love husband, wife or wealth for the sake of the self, so it is the self that must be known. These passages became the scriptural core of Vedānta.","zh":"耶若婆佉是《广林奥义书》（约公元前700年）的主角，这是主要奥义书中最古老的一部。按传统，他是东印度毗提诃宫廷的祭司、乌达罗迦的弟子、“白夜柔吠陀”的传承者。奥义书记载他在遮那迦王举办的大辩论中赢走了作为奖赏的一千头牛，后来在向妻子弥勒伊传授教导之后离家出世。他没有留下任何可考的年代或其他史料。\n\n辩论中，提问者一个接一个追问世界背后是什么，他却总是把目光引向内部：自我（阿特曼）是“见之能见者”、是“内在的主宰”；它不能被看见，因为它正是那个在看的东西，所以只能用否定来描述——“非此，非此”。他对弥勒伊说，人爱丈夫、妻子或财富，其实都是为了自我，所以真正应当认识的是自我。这些段落后来成为吠檀多学派的经典核心。"},"schools":["upanishads"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Bṛhadāraṇyaka Upaniṣad (dialogues traditionally credited to him)","zh":"《广林奥义书》（传统上归于他的对话）"},"y":-700}],"wiki":"Yajnavalkya"},
  {"id":"mahavira","tradition":"indian","born":-599,"died":-527,"life":{"en":"traditionally 599–527 BCE (many scholars: c. 499–425 BCE)","zh":"传统说法为公元前599–前527年（许多学者定为约前499–前425年）"},"name":{"en":"Mahāvīra","zh":"大雄"},"role":{"en":"Twenty-fourth Jain tīrthaṅkara, a contemporary of the Buddha","zh":"耆那教第二十四位祖师，与佛陀同时代"},"idea":{"en":"Left princely life, practised severe austerities, and taught that liberation comes from the ‘three jewels’—right faith, right knowledge, right conduct—and five vows led by non-violence. His teachings were preserved orally in the Jain canon.","zh":"他放弃王子生活，修习严苛的苦行，教导解脱来自“三宝”——正信、正知、正行——以及以不杀生为首的五大誓。他的教导经口传保存在耆那教圣典中。"},"detail":{"en":"Vardhamāna, later called Mahāvīra (“great hero”), was born into a warrior clan near Vaiśālī in today’s Bihar. Jain tradition dates him 599–527 BCE; many historians put him about a century later, as an older contemporary of the Buddha. At about thirty he renounced household life and spent some twelve years wandering in severe austerity before attaining omniscience (kevala). Jains revere him as the twenty-fourth and last ford-maker (tīrthaṅkara) of this age, not as the founder of their religion.\n\nHe wrote nothing; his teachings were memorised by disciples and later compiled in the Jain canon. Their core: every living thing, even plants and particles of earth and water, has a soul; karma is a fine matter that clings to the soul when we act with passion; and liberation comes by stopping its inflow and burning off what has bound. Hence the five great vows—non-violence, truth, not stealing, chastity and non-possession—with ahiṃsā first. Buddhist texts criticised his stress on austerity; Gandhi absorbed his non-violence.","zh":"筏驮摩那后来被尊为“大雄”，出身于今比哈尔邦吠舍离附近的刹帝利氏族。耆那教传统把他定在公元前599–前527年，许多史家则认为约晚一百年，因而他是佛陀稍年长的同时代人。他约三十岁时舍弃家庭生活，以极严苛的苦行游方约十二年，终于证得“全知”。耆那教徒尊他为本时代第二十四位、也是最后一位“渡津者”，而不认为他是本教的创立者。\n\n他没有著作，教导由弟子背诵，后来编入耆那教经典。其核心是：一切生命，甚至草木与地水中的微粒，都有灵魂；业是一种微细的物质，人带着贪嗔行动时便附着于灵魂；解脱就是阻止业的流入，并把已经附着的业烧尽。由此有五大誓：不杀生、不妄语、不偷盗、不淫、不执著，其中不害居首。佛经批评他过于强调苦行，甘地则吸收了他的非暴力精神。"},"schools":["jainism"],"works":[],"wiki":"Mahavira"},
  {"id":"ajita","tradition":"indian","born":-560,"died":-480,"life":{"en":"fl. 6th–5th c. BCE","zh":"约公元前6–前5世纪"},"name":{"en":"Ajita Kesakambalī","zh":"阿耆多·翅舍钦婆罗"},"role":{"en":"Materialist teacher of the Buddha’s time, named for his hair blanket","zh":"佛陀时代的唯物论导师，以身披发毯得名"},"idea":{"en":"Wrote nothing that survives. As reported in the Buddhist Sāmaññaphala Sutta, he taught that a person is made of four elements that disperse at death, so there is no fruit of good or bad deeds and no other world.","zh":"没有著作传世。据佛经《沙门果经》转述，他认为人由四大元素组成，死后即各自散去，因此善恶行为没有果报，也不存在来世。"},"detail":{"en":"Ajita Kesakambalī—“Ajita of the hair blanket”, after the garment of human hair he reportedly wore—was one of six famous wandering teachers active in the Ganges valley in the Buddha’s lifetime, in the 5th century BCE. None of his own words survive. We know him only through his rivals: the Pali Sāmaññaphala Sutta, in which King Ajātasattu reports what each teacher told him, and later Jain sources.\n\nAs reported there, Ajita taught a thoroughgoing materialism. A person is made of earth, water, fire and air; at death these return to their elements and the senses scatter into space. There is no other world, no fruit of good or bad deeds and no rebirth: fools and wise alike, the sutta has him say, are cut off and perish when the body breaks up. Gifts and sacrifices are therefore pointless. Buddhists made this the classic case of “annihilationism”, the wrong view that denies moral consequence, and later Indian authors treated him as a forerunner of the Cārvāka or Lokāyata school.","zh":"阿耆多·翅舍钦婆罗，意为“披发毯的阿耆多”，据说他身披人发编成的衣服。他是公元前5世纪佛陀在世时恒河流域著名的“六师”之一，本人没有任何言论传世。我们只能通过他的论敌了解他：主要是巴利文《沙门果经》中阿阇世王转述六师教义的段落，以及后来的耆那教文献。\n\n据这些记载，阿耆多主张彻底的唯物论：人由地、水、火、风四大组成，死后四大各归其类，诸根散入虚空；没有他世，善恶行为没有果报，也没有轮回——经中记他说，愚者与智者，身坏之后同样断灭。因此布施与祭祀都毫无意义。佛教把这当作“断见”的典型，即否认道德后果的邪见；后来的印度作者则把他视为顺世论（遮婆迦派）的先驱。"},"schools":["charvaka"],"works":[],"wiki":"Ajita Kesakambali"},
  {"id":"buddha","tradition":"indian","born":-480,"died":-400,"life":{"en":"c. 480–400 BCE (debated; an older dating is c. 563–483 BCE)","zh":"约公元前480–前400年（有争议；较早的说法为约前563–前483年）"},"name":{"en":"The Buddha (Siddhārtha Gautama)","zh":"释迦牟尼（乔达摩·悉达多）"},"role":{"en":"Founder of Buddhism, who taught a middle way out of suffering","zh":"佛教创立者，教导一条脱离痛苦的中道"},"idea":{"en":"He wrote nothing; his disciples memorised his discourses. He taught that suffering comes from craving and from ignorance of impermanence and non-self, and that it can be ended through the Eightfold Path of ethics, meditation and wisdom.","zh":"他没有留下著作，弟子们背诵记下他的教说。他教导：苦来自渴爱，以及对无常、无我的无知；通过戒、定、慧的八正道，苦可以止息。"},"detail":{"en":"Siddhārtha Gautama was born into the Śākya clan at Lumbinī, in today’s Nepal, and lived in the 5th century BCE (the exact dates are debated). According to the canonical accounts, he left a comfortable household at about twenty-nine, tried severe asceticism and rejected it, and at thirty-five, meditating at Bodh Gayā, reached awakening. He then taught for some forty-five years across the Ganges plain and founded an order of monks and nuns. He wrote nothing: his discourses were memorised and written down centuries later.\n\nHis teaching starts from a diagnosis. Life as we live it is unsatisfactory (duḥkha); its cause is craving, fed by ignorance; it can cease; and the Eightfold Path of ethics, meditation and wisdom leads there. The key insight is that nothing we cling to is permanent or a self: a person is a changing stream of body, feeling, perception, mental formations and consciousness. Seeing this cuts craving at the root. This “middle way” spread across Asia and later shaped Schopenhauer and Ambedkar.","zh":"乔达摩·悉达多出生于今尼泊尔蓝毗尼的释迦族，生活在公元前5世纪（确切年代尚有争议）。按经典记载，他约二十九岁时离开优裕的家庭，修过严酷的苦行又放弃了它，三十五岁在菩提伽耶静坐时觉悟，此后在恒河平原教化约四十五年，建立了比丘与比丘尼的僧团。他没有写下任何著作，他的说法由弟子背诵，几百年后才写定成文。\n\n他的教导从诊断开始：我们所过的人生是“苦”；苦的原因是渴爱，而渴爱由无明滋养；苦可以止息；通向止息的道路是包括戒、定、慧的八正道。关键的洞见是：我们所执著的一切既非恒常，也不是“我”——人只是色、受、想、行、识不断流转的五蕴。看清这一点，渴爱便被斩断了根。这条“中道”传遍亚洲，后来也影响了叔本华与安贝德卡尔。"},"schools":["buddhism"],"works":[],"wiki":"The Buddha","c":"Buddha in Sarnath Museum (Dhammajak Mutra).jpg","portrait":{"w":600,"h":769,"v":"29d5b37b75","author":"พระมหาเทวประภาส วชิรญาณเมธี","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Buddha_in_Sarnath_Museum_(Dhammajak_Mutra).jpg"}},
  {"id":"jaimini","tradition":"indian","born":-250,"died":-180,"life":{"en":"c. 3rd–2nd c. BCE (traditional)","zh":"约公元前3–前2世纪（传统说法）"},"name":{"en":"Jaimini","zh":"阇弥尼"},"role":{"en":"Traditional author of the Mīmāṃsā Sūtra","zh":"传统上《弥曼差经》的作者"},"idea":{"en":"His sūtras open with ‘Now, the inquiry into dharma’, define duty as what Vedic injunctions command, and give systematic rules for interpreting ritual texts.","zh":"他的经文以“今欲探究达磨”开篇，把职责定义为吠陀命令所规定之事，并提出解释祭祀文本的系统规则。"},"detail":{"en":"Jaimini is the traditional author of the Mīmāṃsā Sūtra, the founding text of Pūrva Mīmāṃsā (“prior exegesis”), the school devoted to interpreting the ritual portions of the Veda. Almost nothing is known of him; later legend makes him a pupil of Vyāsa. The sūtras, some 2,700 terse aphorisms in twelve books, were probably compiled around the 3rd–2nd century BCE and survive with the commentary of Śabara, from the early centuries CE.\n\nThe text opens: “Now, therefore, the inquiry into dharma.” Its answer is that dharma—what we ought to do—is known only from Vedic injunctions, because perception tells us what is, not what ought to be done. To apply those injunctions, the sūtras set out rules of reading: how to tell a command from an explanatory passage, how to resolve apparent conflicts, how to carry details from one rite over to another. These rules made Mīmāṃsā the hermeneutics of Hindu law and theology, used even by its Vedāntin opponents.","zh":"阇弥尼是《弥曼差经》的传统作者，此经是前弥曼差派（“前探究派”）的奠基文献，该派专门诠释吠陀中的祭祀部分。关于他本人几乎一无所知，后世传说称他是毗耶娑的弟子。此经由十二卷、约两千七百条极简短的格言组成，大约编成于公元前3至前2世纪，与夏伯拉（约公元初数世纪）的注释一同流传至今。\n\n此经开篇即说：“现在，开始探究法。”它的回答是：法——即人应当做什么——只能从吠陀的命令句中得知，因为感官只能告诉我们事物是什么，而不能告诉我们应当做什么。为了正确执行这些命令，经中订出一整套解读规则：如何区分命令与解释性的段落，如何化解看似矛盾的经文，如何把一种祭祀的细节移用到另一种。这些规则使弥曼差成为印度教律法与神学的诠释学，连与它对立的吠檀多派也加以采用。"},"schools":["mimamsa"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Mīmāṃsā Sūtra","zh":"《弥曼差经》"},"y":-200}],"wiki":"Jaimini"},
  {"id":"kanada","tradition":"indian","born":-200,"died":-130,"life":{"en":"c. 2nd c. BCE? (dates uncertain)","zh":"约公元前2世纪？（年代不确定）"},"name":{"en":"Kaṇāda","zh":"迦那陀"},"role":{"en":"Founder of Vaiśeṣika, India’s classic atomism","zh":"胜论派创始人，印度经典原子论的奠基者"},"idea":{"en":"He classified reality into categories—substance, quality, action, universal, particularity, inherence—and held that the material world is built from eternal, indivisible atoms.","zh":"他把实在分为若干范畴——实、德、业、同、异、和合——并认为物质世界由永恒而不可分的极微构成。"},"detail":{"en":"Kaṇāda is the legendary founder of Vaiśeṣika, the school of “distinctions”. His name, often explained as “grain-eater” or “atom-eater”, already hints at its signature doctrine; nothing else reliable is known about him. Estimates for his life range from the 6th to the 2nd century BCE, and the Vaiśeṣika Sūtra attributed to him probably took its present form between 200 BCE and the start of the common era. Vaiśeṣika later merged with Nyāya into a single school.\n\nThe Sūtra sets out to list everything that exists under six categories: substance, quality, action, universal, particularity and inherence (a seventh, absence, was added later). Physical substances—earth, water, fire, air—are made of atoms. Vaiśeṣika authors argued that division must stop somewhere, or a mountain and a mustard seed would contain equally infinite parts. Atoms are eternal and partless; things arise when they combine and perish when they separate. This realist map of the world became the ontology most Indian logicians argued with.","zh":"迦那陀是胜论派（“殊胜之论”）的传说创立者。他的名字常被解释为“食米粒者”或“食微尘者”，已经暗示了该派的标志性学说；除此之外，关于他没有可靠的生平资料。对他年代的推定从公元前6世纪到前2世纪不等，托名于他的《胜论经》大约在公元前200年至公元纪元开始之间定型。胜论后来与正理派合流为一派。\n\n《胜论经》试图把一切存在者归入六个范畴：实、德、业、同、异、和合（后来又加上第七个范畴“无”）。地、水、火、风等物质实体由原子构成。胜论学者论证说，分割必须有个尽头，否则一座山和一粒芥子都会含有同样无穷多的部分。原子永恒而无部分，它们结合则事物生成，分离则事物消亡。这幅实在论的世界图景，成了此后大多数印度逻辑学家争论的本体论框架。"},"schools":["nyaya"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Vaiśeṣika Sūtra","zh":"《胜论经》"},"y":-150}],"wiki":"Kanada (philosopher)"},
  {"id":"akshapada","tradition":"indian","born":100,"died":170,"life":{"en":"c. 2nd c. BCE – 2nd c. CE (traditional author)","zh":"约公元前2世纪–公元2世纪（传统上的作者）"},"name":{"en":"Akṣapāda Gautama","zh":"足目（乔达摩）"},"role":{"en":"Traditional author of the Nyāya Sūtra","zh":"传统上《正理经》的作者"},"idea":{"en":"He listed sixteen topics for disciplined debate and four means of knowledge, and set out the five-part syllogism—thesis, reason, example, application, conclusion—as the form of sound argument.","zh":"他列出有序辩论的十六句义与四种量，并提出五支论式——宗、因、喻、合、结——作为正确论证的形式。"},"detail":{"en":"Akṣapāda Gautama—“Akṣapāda” means “eye-footed”, a name later legends tried to explain—is the traditional author of the Nyāya Sūtra, the root text of the Nyāya school of logic and epistemology. He is little more than a name: the text grew in layers, is usually dated somewhere between the 2nd century BCE and the 2nd century CE, and was handed down with the commentary of Vātsyāyana (c. 5th century).\n\nThe Sūtra treats philosophy as disciplined debate aimed at liberation, which comes from right knowledge. It recognises four means of knowledge—perception, inference, comparison and reliable testimony—and sets out the five-step argument: “The hill has fire (thesis), because it has smoke (reason); wherever there is smoke there is fire, as in a kitchen (example); this hill has smoke (application); so it has fire (conclusion).” It also catalogues fallacies and tricks of debate. Buddhist logicians from Dignāga on attacked its account of perception, and the argument with them shaped Indian logic for a thousand years.","zh":"足目·乔达摩（“足目”意为“眼在脚上”，后世为此编出过种种传说）是《正理经》的传统作者，此经是正理派逻辑学与认识论的根本典籍。对我们来说，他几乎只是一个名字：这部经是逐层累积而成的，通常被定在公元前2世纪至公元2世纪之间，并与婆蹉衍那（约5世纪）的注释一同流传。\n\n《正理经》把哲学看作以解脱为目的、有规矩的辩论，而解脱来自正确的知识。它承认四种认识手段（量）：现量、比量、譬喻量和圣言量，并给出五支论式：“此山有火（宗），因为有烟（因）；凡有烟处必有火，如厨房（喻）；此山有烟（合）；故此山有火（结）。”它还详细列举了谬误与辩论中的诡计。从陈那开始，佛教逻辑学家不断攻击它的知觉理论，双方的论战塑造了此后千年的印度逻辑学。"},"schools":["nyaya"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Nyāya Sūtra","zh":"《正理经》"},"y":150}],"wiki":"Nyāya Sūtras"},
  {"id":"nagarjuna","tradition":"indian","born":150,"died":250,"life":{"en":"c. 150–250","zh":"约150–250年"},"name":{"en":"Nāgārjuna","zh":"龙树"},"role":{"en":"Founder of Madhyamaka, revered as a ‘second Buddha’","zh":"中观派创始人，被尊为“第二佛陀”"},"idea":{"en":"He argued that nothing has intrinsic nature because everything arises dependently; emptiness is not nothingness but the Middle Way, and emptiness itself is empty.","zh":"他论证：一切皆依缘而生，所以没有任何事物具有自性；空不是虚无，而是中道，而且空本身也是空的。"},"detail":{"en":"Nāgārjuna, active around 150–250 CE in southern India, is the most important Buddhist philosopher after the Buddha himself; Tibetan and East Asian traditions call him a “second Buddha”. Legend says he brought the Perfection of Wisdom scriptures back from the realm of the serpent-spirits (nāgas). Historically, little is certain beyond his writings, which include the Fundamental Verses on the Middle Way and letters of advice traditionally addressed to a Sātavāhana king.\n\nHis method is to take any concept that claims to name something with its own nature (svabhāva)—cause, motion, time, the self, even the Buddha—and show that it collapses into contradiction. If a cause had its own nature, it would need nothing else to produce its effect; if an effect already had one, it would not need producing. Since everything arises dependently, nothing has intrinsic nature: this is emptiness (śūnyatā). Emptiness is not nothingness but the middle way between eternalism and nihilism, and it is itself empty. Madhyamaka, Tibetan scholasticism and Chan and Zen all build on him.","zh":"龙树约活跃于公元150–250年的南印度，是佛陀之后最重要的佛教哲学家，藏传与东亚佛教尊他为“第二佛”。传说他从龙宫取回了般若经典；就史实而言，除了他的著作，几乎没有什么是确定的。这些著作包括《中论》，以及传统上认为是写给一位娑多婆诃那王朝国王的劝诫书信。\n\n他的方法是：拿起任何一个自称指涉“具有自性之物”的概念——因果、运动、时间、自我，甚至佛陀——证明它会陷入自相矛盾。如果原因有自性，它就不需要任何别的条件就能产生结果；如果结果本来就有自性，它也就无须被产生。既然一切都依缘而起，就没有任何东西具有自性：这就是“空”。空不是虚无，而是介于常见与断见之间的中道，而且空本身也是空的。中观派、藏传佛教的经院哲学以及禅宗，都建立在他的思想之上。"},"schools":["madhyamaka"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Mūlamadhyamakakārikā (Fundamental Verses on the Middle Way)","zh":"《中论》"},"y":200},{"t":{"en":"Vigrahavyāvartanī (The Dispeller of Disputes)","zh":"《回诤论》"},"y":200}],"wiki":"Nagarjuna","c":"Nagarjuna with 84 mahasiddha cropped.jpg","portrait":{"w":600,"h":750,"v":"e9d7465f46","author":"Unknown | Recovered from Himalayan Art Resources","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":null,"page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nagarjuna_with_84_mahasiddha_cropped.jpg"}},
  {"id":"asanga","tradition":"indian","born":310,"died":390,"life":{"en":"c. 4th c. CE","zh":"约公元4世纪"},"name":{"en":"Asaṅga","zh":"无著"},"role":{"en":"Co-founder of Yogācāra, elder half-brother of Vasubandhu","zh":"瑜伽行派共同创始人，世亲的兄长"},"idea":{"en":"He systematised Yogācāra—the storehouse consciousness, the three natures and the bodhisattva path—drawing, tradition says, on teachings revealed to him by the future Buddha Maitreya.","zh":"他系统阐述了瑜伽行派的阿赖耶识、三性与菩萨道；据传统说法，他的学说得自未来佛弥勒的启示。"},"detail":{"en":"Asaṅga, a 4th-century monk from Puruṣapura (modern Peshawar), was the elder half-brother of Vasubandhu. Tradition says he meditated for twelve years without success before the future Buddha Maitreya appeared to him and taught him a set of texts; scholars still debate how much of what is ascribed to “Maitreya” Asaṅga wrote himself. Tradition also credits him with winning his brother over from Abhidharma realism to the Mahāyāna.\n\nAsaṅga organised the Yogācāra (“practice of yoga”) school around the analysis of experience in meditation. Beneath the six ordinary kinds of consciousness lies a “storehouse consciousness” (ālaya-vijñāna) holding the seeds of past actions, which explains continuity and karma without a permanent self. Everything can be described in three ways: as imagined (falsely split into a grasping subject and grasped objects), as dependent (the flow of experience itself), and as perfected (that flow free of the split). Liberation is a “turning around” of the storehouse. His Compendium of the Mahāyāna set all this out as a system.","zh":"无著是4世纪来自布路沙布逻（今白沙瓦）的僧人，世亲的同父异母兄长。传说他苦修冥想十二年毫无所得，后来未来佛弥勒向他显现，传授了一组论典；哪些归于“弥勒”的论著其实出自无著之手，学界至今仍有争论。按传统，他还说服弟弟从阿毗达磨的实在论转向大乘。\n\n无著以禅修中对经验的分析为核心，组织起瑜伽行派。在六种日常的识之下，还有一个“阿赖耶识”（藏识），储存着过去行为的种子，这样便能在没有恒常自我的情况下解释生命的延续与业报。一切事物都可以从三方面来看：遍计所执性（经验被虚妄地分裂为能取的主体与所取的对象），依他起性（经验之流本身），圆成实性（脱离这种分裂的经验之流）。解脱就是阿赖耶识的“转依”。他的《摄大乘论》把这一切组织成完整的体系。"},"schools":["yogacara"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Mahāyānasaṃgraha (Compendium of the Mahāyāna)","zh":"《摄大乘论》"},"y":370}],"wiki":"Asanga"},
  {"id":"vasubandhu","tradition":"indian","born":330,"died":410,"life":{"en":"c. 4th–5th c. CE","zh":"约公元4–5世纪"},"name":{"en":"Vasubandhu","zh":"世亲"},"role":{"en":"Abhidharma master who became Yogācāra’s great systematiser","zh":"由阿毗达磨大师转为唯识学的集大成者"},"idea":{"en":"He first summed up Abhidharma in his Treasury; then, converted by Asaṅga, he argued in short verse treatises that experience is ‘mere representation’, using dream examples and a critique of atoms.","zh":"他先在《俱舍论》中总结了阿毗达磨；后来在无著影响下转向大乘，在简短的颂文中以梦为喻、并批判极微说，论证经验“唯是表识”。"},"detail":{"en":"Vasubandhu, from Puruṣapura (Peshawar), is usually dated to the 4th–5th century, though some scholars think tradition merged two men of that name. He first made his name in the Abhidharma schools of Kashmir and Gandhāra, summing up the Sarvāstivāda system—and criticising it from a Sautrāntika standpoint—in his Treasury of Abhidharma. Later, persuaded by his half-brother Asaṅga, he turned to Yogācāra.\n\nHis short Twenty Verses argue that the objects we experience are “mere representation” (vijñapti-mātra). Objectors ask: if there is no outer world, why do things appear at fixed places and times, and to many people at once? He answers with dreams, where objects also appear at definite places and times and can have real effects, and with hungry ghosts, who all see the same river as a river of pus. He then argues that external atoms are incoherent: if they had parts they would not be atoms, and if they had none they could not combine. Dignāga, Xuanzang and the Chinese Weishi school built on him.","zh":"世亲来自布路沙布逻（白沙瓦），通常被定在4至5世纪；也有学者认为，传统把两位同名的人合成了一人。他最初在克什米尔与犍陀罗的阿毗达磨学派中成名，在《阿毗达磨俱舍论》中总结了说一切有部的体系，同时又从经量部的立场加以批评。后来，他在同父异母兄长无著的劝说下转向瑜伽行派。\n\n他的短篇《唯识二十论》论证：我们经验到的对象只是“唯识”所现的表象。反对者问：如果没有外部世界，为什么事物会出现在固定的时间与地点，而且同时被许多人看到？他以梦作答——梦中的事物同样出现在确定的时空，甚至能产生真实的效果；又以饿鬼为例：众饿鬼一同把同一条河看成脓河。接着他论证外在的原子并不融贯：原子若有部分就不是原子，若无部分就无法结合。陈那、玄奘以及中国的唯识宗都继承了他的思想。"},"schools":["yogacara"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Abhidharmakośa (Treasury of Abhidharma)","zh":"《阿毗达磨俱舍论》"},"y":380},{"t":{"en":"Viṃśatikā (Twenty Verses)","zh":"《唯识二十论》"},"y":400}],"wiki":"Vasubandhu"},
  {"id":"umasvati","tradition":"indian","born":350,"died":420,"life":{"en":"c. 2nd–5th c. CE (dates uncertain)","zh":"约公元2–5世纪（年代不确定）"},"name":{"en":"Umāsvāti","zh":"乌玛斯瓦蒂"},"role":{"en":"Author of the Jain text accepted by both main sects","zh":"写出耆那教两大派共同承认之经典的作者"},"idea":{"en":"His Tattvārtha Sūtra compressed Jain teaching into concise aphorisms, setting out seven realities from soul and non-soul to liberation, and the path of right faith, knowledge and conduct.","zh":"他的《谛义证得经》把耆那教教义浓缩为简洁的经句，阐述了从命（灵魂）与非命到解脱的七谛，以及正信、正知、正行之道。"},"detail":{"en":"Umāsvāti (also called Umāsvāmī) composed the Tattvārtha Sūtra, the first major summary of Jain doctrine written in Sanskrit rather than the Prakrit of the canon. His dates are uncertain—scholars place him anywhere from the 2nd to the 5th century CE—and both the Śvetāmbara and Digambara sects claim him, which is why his text is the one work all Jain traditions study.\n\nIn ten chapters of compressed aphorisms, the Sūtra opens: “Right faith, right knowledge and right conduct together are the path to liberation.” It then sets out seven realities: soul (jīva), non-soul, the inflow of karma, its binding to the soul, the stopping of inflow, the shedding of bound karma, and liberation. Along the way it classifies kinds of knowledge, living beings, cosmology and ethics. Its definition of what exists—whatever arises, perishes and yet endures—expresses the Jain view that a thing is permanent in substance but changing in its modes. Hundreds of commentaries followed.","zh":"乌玛斯瓦蒂（又称乌玛斯瓦米）著有《谛义证得经》，这是第一部用梵文（而非耆那教经典所用的俗语）写成的重要耆那教教义纲要。他的年代并不确定，学者的推定从公元2世纪到5世纪不等；白衣派与天衣派都认他为本派人物，因此他的这部书成为所有耆那教传统共同研习的典籍。\n\n此经以十章精炼的格言写成，开篇即说：“正信、正知、正行合起来，就是解脱之道。”接着列出七种真实：命（灵魂）、非命、业的流入、业对灵魂的束缚、流入的止息、已缚之业的脱落，以及解脱。书中还分类论述了知识的种类、众生、宇宙论与伦理。它对“存在”的定义——凡存在者都兼具生起、消逝与持存——表达了耆那教的观点：事物在实体上恒常，在样态上变化。此后为它作注的著作多达数百部。"},"schools":["jainism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Tattvārtha Sūtra","zh":"《谛义证得经》"},"y":400}],"wiki":"Umaswati"},
  {"id":"ishvarakrishna","tradition":"indian","born":350,"died":420,"life":{"en":"c. 4th–5th c. CE","zh":"约公元4–5世纪"},"name":{"en":"Īśvarakṛṣṇa","zh":"自在黑"},"role":{"en":"Author of the classical summary of Sāṃkhya","zh":"古典数论纲要的作者"},"idea":{"en":"His seventy-odd verses argue that effects pre-exist in their cause, that matter evolves into intellect, ego and world through three qualities, and that liberation comes from discriminating consciousness from matter.","zh":"他约七十首颂文论证：果先存于因中；物质经由三德演化出觉、我慢与世界；解脱来自把意识与物质分辨清楚。"},"detail":{"en":"Īśvarakṛṣṇa is known only as the author of the Sāṃkhyakārikā, some seventy verses that form the oldest surviving systematic text of Sāṃkhya, India’s classic dualist philosophy. He is usually placed around the 4th–5th century CE; the text was certainly circulating by the 6th, when Paramārtha translated it into Chinese with a commentary. Earlier Sāṃkhya treatises are lost, so his summary became the school’s canon.\n\nThe verses begin with suffering and the search for a sure way out of it. Reality is of two kinds: pure consciousness (puruṣa), many inactive witnesses; and a single primal matter (prakṛti), woven of three strands—lucidity, energy and inertia. When their balance is disturbed, matter unfolds in a fixed order into intellect, ego-sense, mind, senses, elements and the world. Because effects already exist in their cause, nothing comes from nothing. Bondage is confusing consciousness with matter’s activity; liberation is the knowledge “I am not this”, after which matter, like a dancer whose performance is over, withdraws.","zh":"自在黑只以《数论颂》的作者而为人所知。这部七十余颂的作品是现存最古老的数论派系统性文献，而数论是印度经典的二元论哲学。他通常被定在约公元4至5世纪；6世纪时此书已在流传，真谛把它连同注释译成了汉文，即《金七十论》。更早的数论论著都已失传，因此他的这部纲要成了该派的经典。\n\n全书从“苦”以及寻找确定的解脱之道说起。实在分为两类：一是纯粹意识“神我”，数目众多，只是不动的旁观者；二是唯一的原初物质“自性”，由喜、忧、暗三德交织而成。三德的平衡一旦被打破，物质便按固定的次序展开为觉、我慢、意、诸根、诸元素以及世界。由于结果本已存在于原因之中，没有什么是从无中生出的。束缚就是把意识与物质的活动混为一谈；解脱是认识到“我不是这个”，此后物质便像演完节目的舞女一样退场。"},"schools":["samkhya-yoga"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Sāṃkhyakārikā","zh":"《数论颂》（汉译注本为《金七十论》）"},"y":400}],"wiki":"Samkhyakarika"},
  {"id":"patanjali","tradition":"indian","born":350,"died":420,"life":{"en":"c. 4th–5th c. CE? (dating disputed)","zh":"约公元4–5世纪？（年代有争议）"},"name":{"en":"Patañjali","zh":"帕坦伽利"},"role":{"en":"Compiler of the Yoga Sūtra","zh":"《瑜伽经》的编纂者"},"idea":{"en":"He defined yoga as ‘the stilling of the fluctuations of the mind’, set out eight limbs from ethical restraint to absorption (samādhi), and, within a Sāṃkhya-like framework, allowed a special self, Īśvara, as an object of devotion.","zh":"他把瑜伽定义为“止息心的波动”，提出从道德持戒到三摩地的八支，并在类似数论的框架中，承认一个特殊的自我——自在天——作为虔敬的对象。"},"detail":{"en":"Patañjali is the name attached to the Yoga Sūtra, about 195 short aphorisms that are the classic text of the Yoga school. Most scholars now date the work, together with its earliest commentary (the Bhāṣya, which may be by the same hand), to around 400 CE; the old identification with the 2nd-century-BCE grammarian Patañjali is generally rejected. Of the man himself nothing is known.\n\nThe Sūtra defines yoga as “the stilling of the fluctuations of the mind”; when they are stilled, “the seer rests in its own nature”. The text maps the obstacles—ignorance, egoism, attachment, aversion and clinging to life—and the means: eight limbs, from ethical restraints and observances through posture, breath control and withdrawal of the senses to concentration, meditation and absorption (samādhi). Its metaphysics is largely Sāṃkhya’s, but it adds Īśvara, a special self untouched by karma, as an object and aid of concentration. Modern postural yoga claims the text as its charter, though it says little about postures.","zh":"帕坦伽利是《瑜伽经》所署的名字，此经由约195条简短格言组成，是瑜伽派的经典。如今多数学者把它连同最早的注释（《瑜伽经注》，可能出自同一人之手）定在约公元400年；旧说认为他就是公元前2世纪的文法家帕坦伽利，此说已普遍不被接受。关于他本人，我们一无所知。\n\n《瑜伽经》把瑜伽定义为“止息心的波动”；波动止息之时，“见者便安住于自身的本性”。全书列出修行的障碍——无明、我执、贪、嗔、对生命的执著——也给出方法：八支，从禁制、劝制，经过坐法、调息、制感，到专注、禅定与三摩地。它的形而上学大体沿用数论，但加上了“自在天”——一个不受业力沾染的特殊自我，可作为专注的对象与助缘。现代以体式为主的瑜伽把它奉为宪章，尽管书中对体式着墨极少。"},"schools":["samkhya-yoga"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Yoga Sūtra","zh":"《瑜伽经》"},"y":400}],"wiki":"Patanjali"},
  {"id":"buddhaghosa","tradition":"indian","born":380,"died":450,"life":{"en":"fl. 5th c. CE","zh":"活跃于公元5世纪"},"name":{"en":"Buddhaghosa","zh":"觉音"},"role":{"en":"Theravāda commentator who systematised doctrine in Sri Lanka","zh":"在斯里兰卡系统整理教义的上座部注释家"},"idea":{"en":"Working at the Mahāvihāra in Anurādhapura, he wrote the Path of Purification, a map of Theravāda ethics, meditation and Abhidhamma analysis that is still its standard handbook.","zh":"他在阿努拉德普勒的大寺撰写了《清净道论》，系统描绘了上座部的戒律、禅修与阿毗达磨分析，至今仍是该传统的标准手册。"},"detail":{"en":"Buddhaghosa was a 5th-century Indian monk who came to Sri Lanka and worked at the Mahāvihāra monastery in Anurādhapura, the seat of Theravāda orthodoxy. According to the later chronicle Cūḷavaṃsa, he was a Brahmin convert from northern India. He rendered the old Sinhala commentaries on the Pali scriptures into Pali, condensing them, so that the Theravāda canon came to be read through his explanations; scholars now think some commentaries traditionally ascribed to him are by others.\n\nHis great independent work, the Path of Purification (Visuddhimagga), organises the whole path under three headings: virtue, concentration and wisdom. It gives practical instructions for forty meditation subjects, from coloured discs to the contemplation of death, and then analyses the person, in Abhidhamma fashion, into momentary mental and physical events, so that the meditator can see impermanence, suffering and non-self directly. It remains the standard handbook of Theravāda Buddhism in Sri Lanka and Southeast Asia, and modern insight-meditation movements still draw on it.","zh":"觉音是5世纪的印度僧人，来到斯里兰卡，在上座部正统的中心——阿努拉德普勒的大寺——从事著述。据后来的史书《小史》记载，他原是北印度的婆罗门，后来皈依佛教。他把古老的僧伽罗文巴利经注译为巴利文并加以浓缩，从此上座部经典便通过他的解释被阅读；如今学者认为，一些传统上归于他名下的注释其实出自他人之手。\n\n他最重要的独立著作《清净道论》，把整个修行之道归于戒、定、慧三学。书中为四十种禅修业处——从色遍到念死——给出了具体指导，接着用阿毗达磨的方法，把人分析为刹那生灭的身心事件，使修行者能直接看见无常、苦与无我。直到今天，它仍是斯里兰卡与东南亚上座部佛教的标准手册，现代的内观禅修运动也仍以它为依据。"},"schools":["buddhism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Visuddhimagga (The Path of Purification)","zh":"《清净道论》"},"y":430}],"wiki":"Buddhaghosa"},
  {"id":"dignaga","tradition":"indian","born":480,"died":540,"life":{"en":"c. 480–540","zh":"约480–540年"},"name":{"en":"Dignāga","zh":"陈那"},"role":{"en":"Founder of Buddhist logic and epistemology","zh":"佛教逻辑与认识论的奠基者"},"idea":{"en":"He cut the means of knowledge to perception and inference, defined perception as free of concepts, explained word meaning as exclusion (apoha), and mapped valid and invalid reasons in his ‘wheel of reasons’.","zh":"他把认识手段精简为现量与比量，把现量定义为离分别（不带概念），用“遮遣”解释语词的意义，并以“九句因”系统区分有效与无效的理由。"},"detail":{"en":"Dignāga (c. 480–540), born near Kāñcī in southern India, founded the Buddhist school of logic and epistemology (pramāṇa). Tradition, perhaps reliably, makes him a pupil of Vasubandhu. He spent his life debating at the monastic centres of his day and summed up his thought in the Compendium of Valid Cognition, which survives complete only in Tibetan translation.\n\nDignāga asked what the reliable means of knowledge are, and answered: only two, because there are only two kinds of object. Perception grasps unique, momentary particulars and is free of concepts; inference grasps general features through concepts. Everything else counts as one of these. Words, he argued, do not refer to real universals such as “cowness”; a word like “cow” works by excluding what is not-cow (apoha). In his “wheel of reasons” he tabulated which patterns of a reason’s presence and absence make an inference valid. Nyāya and Mīmāṃsā authors spent centuries answering him; Dharmakīrti refined his system.","zh":"陈那（约480–540年）生于南印度建志附近，是佛教逻辑学与认识论（量论）的创立者。传统说他是世亲的弟子，此说或许可信。他一生在当时的寺院中心辩论，并在《集量论》中总结了自己的思想，此书完整地保存于藏译本中。\n\n陈那问：可靠的认识手段有哪些？他的回答是：只有两种，因为对象只有两类。现量把握独特、刹那的个别事物，不带概念；比量则借助概念把握事物的共同特征。其他所谓的认识手段都可以归入这两种。他还主张，语词并不指称“牛性”这样真实存在的共相，“牛”这个词的作用在于排除一切非牛者，即“遮诠”（离他）。他又以“九句因”列表说明，理由的出现与不出现以哪些方式组合，推理才有效。正理派与弥曼差派用了几个世纪来回应他，法称则进一步完善了他的体系。"},"schools":["pramana"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Pramāṇasamuccaya (Compendium of Valid Cognition)","zh":"《集量论》"},"y":520},{"t":{"en":"Nyāyamukha (Introduction to Logic)","zh":"《因明正理门论》"},"y":500}],"wiki":"Dignāga"},
  {"id":"gaudapada","tradition":"indian","born":500,"died":570,"life":{"en":"c. 6th c. CE","zh":"约公元6世纪"},"name":{"en":"Gauḍapāda","zh":"乔荼波陀"},"role":{"en":"Earliest known Advaita teacher, by tradition Śaṅkara’s teacher’s teacher","zh":"已知最早的不二论导师，传统上是商羯罗的师祖"},"idea":{"en":"In verses on the Māṇḍūkya Upaniṣad he argued for non-origination (ajātivāda): nothing is ever truly born; the world of change is like a dream, and only non-dual consciousness is real.","zh":"他在解释《蛙氏奥义书》的颂文中提出“无生论”：没有任何东西真正生起；变化的世界如同梦境，唯有不二的意识是真实的。"},"detail":{"en":"Gauḍapāda, probably of the 6th century, is the earliest Advaita Vedānta teacher whose work survives. Tradition makes him the teacher of Govinda, who taught Śaṅkara. His Māṇḍūkya Kārikā (also called the Āgama Śāstra) is a verse commentary in four chapters on the very short Māṇḍūkya Upaniṣad; scholars still debate whether all four chapters are his.\n\nStarting from the Upaniṣad’s analysis of waking, dreaming, deep sleep and a “fourth” state of pure awareness, Gauḍapāda argues that waking objects are no more real than dream objects: both appear, both are seen, both vanish. He then argues for non-origination (ajātivāda): nothing can be born—not from itself, not from something else, not from nothing—so the world of change never truly began. Only non-dual consciousness is real. His arguments and even his vocabulary closely resemble those of Madhyamaka and Yogācāra Buddhism, which suggests a shared debate. Śaṅkara commented on his verses and built on them.","zh":"乔荼波陀大约生活在6世纪，是著作传世的最早一位不二论吠檀多导师。按传统，他是乔频陀的老师，而乔频陀又是商羯罗的老师。他的《蛙氏奥义颂》（又称《圣教论》）是为极短的《蛙氏奥义书》所作的四章韵文注释；四章是否都出自他手，学界仍有争论。\n\n他从该奥义书对醒、梦、熟睡以及第四种纯粹觉知状态的分析出发，论证醒时的对象并不比梦中的对象更真实：两者都会显现、都被看见、也都会消失。接着他提出“无生论”：没有任何东西能够产生——既不能从自身产生，也不能从他者或从无中产生——所以变化的世界从来没有真正开始过；唯有不二的意识是真实的。他的论证乃至用语都与佛教的中观派、瑜伽行派十分相近，说明双方身处同一场论辩之中。商羯罗为他的颂文作注，并在此基础上展开自己的学说。"},"schools":["advaita"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Māṇḍūkya Kārikā","zh":"《蛙氏奥义颂》"},"y":550}],"wiki":"Gaudapada"},
  {"id":"chandrakirti","tradition":"indian","born":600,"died":650,"life":{"en":"c. 600–650","zh":"约600–650年"},"name":{"en":"Candrakīrti","zh":"月称"},"role":{"en":"Madhyamaka commentator whose reading became orthodox in Tibet","zh":"其诠释在西藏成为正统的中观注释家"},"idea":{"en":"He defended Buddhapālita against Bhāviveka: a Mādhyamika should only draw out the absurd consequences of opponents’ views rather than offer independent proofs. Tibetan scholars later named this approach Prāsaṅgika.","zh":"他为佛护辩护、反驳清辨：中观论者只应揭示论敌观点的荒谬后果，而不应另立自己的独立论证。后来的藏族学者把这一路线称为“应成派”。"},"detail":{"en":"Candrakīrti (c. 600–650) was a Madhyamaka scholar associated with the great monastery of Nālandā; little is known of his life. His importance lies in his writings on Nāgārjuna: Clear Words, the only complete Sanskrit commentary on the Fundamental Verses to survive, and his own Entering the Middle Way, which became the main textbook of Madhyamaka in Tibet.\n\nCandrakīrti took sides in a dispute about method. Bhāviveka had argued that a Mādhyamika should prove emptiness with independent syllogisms of his own. Candrakīrti, defending Buddhapālita, replied that this smuggles back what Madhyamaka denies—shared, intrinsically real subjects and reasons. A Mādhyamika should instead show the absurd consequences (prasaṅga) of opponents’ views on their own terms. He also defended ordinary, conventional truth against Yogācāra: we may speak of external objects as the world does, so long as we see that they lack intrinsic nature. Tibetan scholars, above all Tsongkhapa, called this approach Prāsaṅgika and ranked it highest.","zh":"月称（约600–650年）是与那烂陀寺有关的中观学者，生平所知甚少。他的重要性在于对龙树的阐释：《明句论》是现存唯一完整的《中论》梵文注释；他自己的《入中论》则成了藏地研习中观的主要教本。\n\n月称卷入了一场关于方法的争论。清辨主张，中观学者应当用自己的独立论式来证明空性。月称为佛护辩护，回答说：这样做会把中观所否定的东西——双方共许的、有自性的论题与理由——又偷偷带回来。中观学者应当只就对方的立场，揭示其荒谬的后果（应成）。他还针对瑜伽行派为世俗谛辩护：我们可以像世人一样谈论外境，只要明白它们没有自性即可。藏地学者，尤其是宗喀巴，把这一路线称为“应成派”，并奉为最高见解。"},"schools":["madhyamaka"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Prasannapadā (Clear Words)","zh":"《明句论》"},"y":630},{"t":{"en":"Madhyamakāvatāra (Entering the Middle Way)","zh":"《入中论》"},"y":630}],"wiki":"Chandrakirti"},
  {"id":"dharmakirti","tradition":"indian","born":600,"died":660,"life":{"en":"c. 600–660 (some scholars: mid-6th century)","zh":"约600–660年（亦有学者定于6世纪中叶）"},"name":{"en":"Dharmakīrti","zh":"法称"},"role":{"en":"Buddhist logician whose theory of knowledge dominated Indian debate","zh":"其认识论主导了印度哲学论辩的佛教逻辑学家"},"idea":{"en":"He grounded valid inference in two real relations, identity and causation, held that only what has causal power is ultimately real, and defended rebirth and the Buddha’s authority by argument rather than scripture.","zh":"他把有效推理建立在同一性与因果这两种真实关系之上，主张只有具有因果效力的东西才是胜义上真实的，并以论证而非经典来为轮回与佛陀的权威辩护。"},"detail":{"en":"Dharmakīrti was born in southern India and taught at Nālandā. He is usually dated c. 600–660, though some scholars place his activity in the mid-6th century. Tibetan sources say he first trained in Brahmanical learning, perhaps Mīmāṃsā, before becoming a Buddhist. The Chinese pilgrim Yijing, in India in the 670s and 680s, names him as a famous logician. His Commentary on Valid Cognition, nominally on Dignāga, is in fact a new system.\n\nDharmakīrti asked what makes an inference reliable. His answer: a reason guarantees its conclusion only through a real relation—either identity (“this is a tree, because it is an oak”) or causation (“there is fire, because there is smoke”). What is ultimately real is what has causal power, which only momentary particulars have; universals are useful constructions. He brought the same rigour to religious questions, arguing that rebirth follows from the continuity of mind and that the Buddha is a reliable authority because his teaching works. His system set the terms of Indian and Tibetan debate for centuries.","zh":"法称生于南印度，曾在那烂陀寺讲学。他通常被定在约600–660年，但也有学者把他的活动提前到6世纪中叶。藏文史料说他起初学习婆罗门教的学问，可能是弥曼差派，后来才皈依佛教。7世纪七八十年代在印度游学的义净，曾把他列为著名的因明学者。他的《释量论》名义上是为陈那作注，实际上建立了一个新的体系。\n\n法称追问：推理凭什么可靠？他的回答是：理由只有借助真实的关系才能保证结论——要么是同一关系（“这是树，因为它是橡树”），要么是因果关系（“那里有火，因为有烟”）。终极真实的东西就是具有因果效力的东西，而只有刹那的个别事物才有这种效力；共相只是有用的建构。他也把同样的严格用于宗教问题，论证轮回来自心识的相续，佛陀之所以可信，是因为他的教法确实有效。此后数百年间，他的体系为印度与西藏的论辩设定了基本框架。"},"schools":["pramana"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Pramāṇavārttika (Commentary on Valid Cognition)","zh":"《释量论》"},"y":630},{"t":{"en":"Nyāyabindu (A Drop of Logic)","zh":"《正理一滴论》"},"y":640}],"wiki":"Dharmakirti"},
  {"id":"kumarila","tradition":"indian","born":620,"died":680,"life":{"en":"fl. c. 660","zh":"约活跃于660年前后"},"name":{"en":"Kumārila Bhaṭṭa","zh":"鸠摩利罗"},"role":{"en":"Mīmāṃsā champion of Vedic authority against the Buddhists","zh":"捍卫吠陀权威、对抗佛教的弥曼差派大师"},"idea":{"en":"He argued that every cognition is valid by itself until defeated, so the authorless Veda deserves trust, and he attacked Buddhist momentariness and the claim that anyone, the Buddha included, could be omniscient.","zh":"他主张每个认识在被推翻之前都自身有效，因此无作者的吠陀值得信任；他还批驳了佛教的刹那灭论，以及任何人（包括佛陀）能够全知的说法。"},"detail":{"en":"Kumārila Bhaṭṭa, who flourished around 660, was the most influential defender of Mīmāṃsā, the school of Vedic exegesis, at a time when Buddhist logicians were challenging Vedic authority. The later legends about him—that he studied with Buddhists in disguise, or burned himself to death in penance—are hagiography. His Verse Commentary (Ślokavārttika) on the opening of Śabara’s commentary, and his Tantravārttika on hermeneutics, founded the Bhāṭṭa branch of the school.\n\nKumārila’s central doctrine is that every cognition is valid by itself (svataḥ prāmāṇya) until something defeats it: we trust what we see unless we find a fault. Applied to the Veda, this is decisive. Since the Veda has no author, there is no author whose flaws could undermine it, so its commands about duty stand. He attacked the Buddhist claims that everything is momentary, that words mean by exclusion, and that anyone, the Buddha included, could be omniscient. Śaṅkara’s Advaita borrowed much of his method while rejecting his ritualism.","zh":"鸠摩利罗·跋吒约活跃于660年前后，是弥曼差派（吠陀诠释学派）最有影响的捍卫者，当时佛教逻辑学家正在挑战吠陀的权威。后世关于他的传说——如乔装跟随佛教徒学习，或为赎罪自焚而死——都属于圣徒传。他为夏伯拉注疏开篇所作的《颂释》，以及另一部论述诠释学的释论，奠定了该派中的跋吒派。\n\n鸠摩利罗的核心学说是“自明有效”：每一个认识本身就有效，直到被别的东西推翻为止——我们相信自己所见，除非发现了错误。把这一点用到吠陀上，结论便很关键：吠陀没有作者，也就没有哪个作者的缺陷能够动摇它，所以它关于责任的命令成立。他攻击佛教的几项主张：一切刹那生灭、语词靠排除来表意、任何人（包括佛陀）都可能全知。商羯罗的不二论借用了他的大量方法，却拒绝了他的祭祀主义。"},"schools":["mimamsa"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Ślokavārttika (Verse Commentary)","zh":"《颂释》"},"y":660}],"wiki":"Kumārila Bhaṭṭa"},
  {"id":"shankara","tradition":"indian","born":700,"died":750,"life":{"en":"c. 700–750 (older scholarship: 788–820)","zh":"约700–750年（早期学界定为788–820年）"},"name":{"en":"Śaṅkara","zh":"商羯罗"},"role":{"en":"Advaita master whose commentaries defined non-dual Vedānta","zh":"以注疏奠定不二论吠檀多的宗师"},"idea":{"en":"He taught that Brahman, pure consciousness, alone is real, that the individual self is identical with it, and that the world is superimposed on it through ignorance; liberation comes from knowledge, not ritual.","zh":"他教导：唯有纯粹意识之梵是真实的，个体自我与梵同一，世界则因无明而被增益于梵之上；解脱来自知识，而非祭祀。"},"detail":{"en":"Śaṅkara, usually dated to the early 8th century, is the most celebrated teacher of Advaita (“non-dual”) Vedānta. Nothing reliable is known of his life beyond his works: traditional biographies written centuries later tell of a Kerala-born renouncer who studied with Govinda, debated across India, founded monasteries and died young. His certain works are commentaries on the Brahma Sūtra, the major Upanishads and the Bhagavad Gītā, and the Thousand Teachings.\n\nŚaṅkara reads the Upanishads as teaching one thing: Brahman, pure consciousness, alone is real, and your innermost self is not a part of it but identical with it. Why then do we experience many separate things and selves? Because ignorance (avidyā) superimposes the world on Brahman, as in the dark we see a snake where there is a rope. The world is not nothing—it is real for practical purposes—but it is not ultimately real. Ritual and good works purify the mind, but only knowledge removes ignorance and liberates. Rāmānuja and Madhva built their systems against him.","zh":"商羯罗通常被定在8世纪初，是不二论吠檀多最负盛名的导师。除了著作，他的生平没有可靠的史料：几百年后写成的传统传记说，他生于喀拉拉，早年出家，师从乔频陀，游历全印度辩论，建立僧院，英年早逝。确定出自他手的著作有对《梵经》、主要奥义书与《薄伽梵歌》的注释，以及《示教千则》。\n\n商羯罗认为奥义书只教一件事：唯有梵——纯粹意识——是真实的，你最内在的自我不是梵的一部分，而就是梵本身。那么我们为什么会经验到许多彼此分离的事物与自我？因为无明把世界“附加”在梵上，就像在黑暗中把绳子看成蛇。世界并非虚无——它在实用层面是真实的——但不是终极真实。祭祀与善行可以净化心灵，但只有知识才能破除无明、带来解脱。罗摩奴阇与摩陀婆都是在反对他的过程中建立起各自的体系。"},"schools":["advaita"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Brahmasūtra-bhāṣya (Commentary on the Brahma Sūtra)","zh":"《梵经注》"},"y":720},{"t":{"en":"Upadeśasāhasrī (A Thousand Teachings)","zh":"《示教千则》"},"y":720}],"wiki":"Adi Shankara","c":"Raja Ravi Varma - Sankaracharya.jpg","portrait":{"w":600,"h":847,"v":"5625310164","author":"Raja Ravi Varma","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":null,"page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Raja_Ravi_Varma_-_Sankaracharya.jpg"},"focus":"30% 45%"},
  {"id":"jayarashi","tradition":"indian","born":770,"died":830,"life":{"en":"fl. c. 800 (estimates range from 725 to 840)","zh":"约活跃于800年前后（推定在725–840年之间）"},"name":{"en":"Jayarāśi Bhaṭṭa","zh":"阇耶罗希·跋吒"},"role":{"en":"Radical sceptic who attacked every theory of valid knowledge","zh":"抨击一切有效认识理论的激进怀疑论者"},"idea":{"en":"He took each school’s definition of valid knowledge, perception included, and showed it to be incoherent, concluding that no means of knowledge can be established. He is linked to the Lokāyata tradition, though his scepticism goes beyond standard Cārvāka materialism.","zh":"他逐一检讨各派对有效认识的定义（包括感知），指出它们都自相矛盾，结论是没有任何认识手段能够成立。他通常被归入顺世论传统，但他的怀疑论比一般的顺世论唯物主义走得更远。"},"detail":{"en":"Jayarāśi Bhaṭṭa, who flourished around 800, probably in southern India, is known from a single work, the Tattvopaplavasiṃha, “The Lion that Devours All Categories”. It is one of the very few texts connected with the Cārvāka (Lokāyata) tradition to survive in full, and was published only in 1940. His relation to that tradition is debated, because the book is less a defence of materialism than an attack on everyone’s theory of knowledge.\n\nJayarāśi takes each school’s definition of a valid means of knowledge—Nyāya’s perception and inference, the Buddhist definitions of Dignāga and Dharmakīrti, Mīmāṃsā’s self-validity—and shows that it cannot be applied without circularity or regress. To know that a perception is “non-erroneous”, for example, you would need a further valid cognition, and so on without end. Since no means of knowledge can be established, no metaphysics—of soul, God, karma, or even the material elements—can be proved. The result is a radical scepticism, unusual in Indian philosophy, which treated sound knowledge as the starting point of every system.","zh":"阇耶罗希·跋吒约活跃于800年前后，可能生活在南印度，仅以一部著作为人所知：《吞噬一切范畴之狮》。这是极少数完整保存下来、与顺世论（遮婆迦派）传统有关的文献之一，直到1940年才得以出版。他与该传统的关系有争议，因为这本书与其说是在为唯物论辩护，不如说是在攻击所有学派的认识论。\n\n阇耶罗希的方法是：拿来各派对“有效认识手段”的定义——正理派的现量与比量，陈那与法称的佛教定义，弥曼差派的“自明有效”——逐一证明它们若要应用，就会陷入循环或无穷后退。比如，要知道一个知觉“无误”，就需要另一个有效的认识来确认，如此以至无穷。既然没有任何认识手段能够成立，任何形而上学——关于灵魂、神、业，乃至物质元素——都无法被证明。其结果是一种彻底的怀疑论，这在把可靠认识视为一切体系起点的印度哲学中相当罕见。"},"schools":["charvaka"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Tattvopaplavasiṃha (The Lion that Devours All Categories)","zh":"《吞噬一切范畴之狮》"},"y":800}],"wiki":"Jayarāśi Bhaṭṭa"},
  {"id":"ramanuja","tradition":"indian","born":1077,"died":1157,"life":{"en":"c. 1077–1157 (traditionally 1017–1137)","zh":"约1077–1157年（传统说法为1017–1137年）"},"name":{"en":"Rāmānuja","zh":"罗摩奴阇"},"role":{"en":"Vaiṣṇava theologian and founder of Viśiṣṭādvaita","zh":"毗湿奴派神学家，限定不二论的创立者"},"idea":{"en":"His ‘qualified non-dualism’ holds that Brahman is one but includes real souls and matter as its body; liberation is reaching God through devotion and surrender, not dissolving into featureless consciousness.","zh":"他的“限定不二论”认为：梵是一，但包含真实的灵魂与物质作为自己的身体；解脱是通过虔爱与皈依抵达神，而不是消融于无差别的意识。"},"detail":{"en":"Rāmānuja was born in Tamil Nadu; tradition gives him 120 years (1017–1137), while scholars place him c. 1077–1157. Trained first in Advaita under Yādavaprakāśa, he broke with his teacher over the meaning of the Upanishads and became leader of the Śrīvaiṣṇava community at the temple of Srirangam, where the devotional poems of the Tamil Āḻvār saints were central to worship. His Śrībhāṣya on the Brahma Sūtra is the classic statement of Viśiṣṭādvaita, “qualified non-dualism”.\n\nRāmānuja agreed with Śaṅkara that Brahman is the one reality, but not that it is featureless or that the world is illusion. Brahman is a personal God, Viṣṇu–Nārāyaṇa, with infinite good qualities. Souls and matter are real, and they relate to God as a body relates to its soul: distinct from him, dependent on him and wholly animated by him. Liberation is not dissolving into undifferentiated consciousness but eternal, loving service of God, reached through devotion (bhakti) and, above all, surrender (prapatti) to his grace.","zh":"罗摩奴阇生于泰米尔纳德；传统说他享年120岁（1017–1137年），学者则把他定在约1077–1157年。他最初师从雅达婆普拉卡沙学习不二论，后来因对奥义书的理解不同而与老师决裂，成为斯里兰加姆神庙室利毗湿奴派信众的领袖——在那里，泰米尔阿尔瓦尔圣者的虔信诗歌是崇拜的核心。他为《梵经》所作的《吉祥注》，是“限定不二论”的经典阐述。\n\n罗摩奴阇同意商羯罗所说的“梵是唯一的实在”，却不同意梵没有属性、世界是幻相。梵是一位人格神，即具有无限美德的毗湿奴—那罗延那。灵魂与物质都是真实的，它们与神的关系就像身体与灵魂：与神有别，依赖于神，并完全由神赋予生命。解脱不是融入无差别的意识，而是永远以爱侍奉神；通往解脱的道路是虔信，尤其是向神的恩典彻底皈依。"},"schools":["theistic-vedanta"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Śrībhāṣya (commentary on the Brahma Sūtra)","zh":"《吉祥注》（梵经注）"},"y":1100},{"t":{"en":"Vedārthasaṃgraha","zh":"《吠陀义摄》"},"y":1100}],"wiki":"Ramanuja"},
  {"id":"madhva","tradition":"indian","born":1238,"died":1317,"life":{"en":"1238–1317 (some sources: 1199–1278)","zh":"1238–1317年（亦有说法为1199–1278年）"},"name":{"en":"Madhva","zh":"摩陀婆"},"role":{"en":"Founder of Dvaita, the dualist school of Vedānta","zh":"吠檀多二元论派的创立者"},"idea":{"en":"He taught five eternal differences—between God and souls, God and matter, soul and soul, soul and matter, and one material thing and another—so the soul never becomes God, though in liberation it enjoys God’s presence.","zh":"他提出五种永恒差别——神与灵魂、神与物质、灵魂与灵魂、灵魂与物质、物质与物质之间——因此灵魂永远不会变成神，但在解脱中能享有与神同在的喜乐。"},"detail":{"en":"Madhva (1238–1317 by the usual dating; some sources give 1199–1278) was born near Udupi on the Karnataka coast. He became a renouncer young, studied Advaita and rejected it, travelled and debated across India, and installed the image of Krishna at Udupi that remains the centre of his tradition. He wrote thirty-seven works, among them commentaries on the Brahma Sūtra, the Upanishads and the Gītā. Most of what we know of his life comes from a biography by the son of one of his disciples.\n\nMadhva’s Dvaita (“dualism”) is the most uncompromising answer to Śaṅkara: difference is real and permanent. He lists five eternal differences—between God and souls, God and matter, one soul and another, souls and matter, and one material thing and another. God (Viṣṇu) alone is independent; everything else depends on him. Souls never become God, even in liberation, where each enjoys his presence in its own degree. He also held, unusually in Indian thought, that some souls are eternally bound and some are damned.","zh":"摩陀婆（通常定为1238–1317年，也有资料作1199–1278年）生于卡纳塔克海岸的乌杜皮附近。他年少出家，学习不二论后又否定了它；他游历全印度辩论，并在乌杜皮安奉了黑天神像，那里至今仍是该派的中心。他著有三十七部作品，包括对《梵经》、奥义书与《薄伽梵歌》的注释。关于他生平的了解，大多来自他一位弟子之子所写的传记。\n\n摩陀婆的二元论是对商羯罗最不妥协的回答：差别是真实而永恒的。他列出五种永恒的差别：神与灵魂、神与物质、灵魂与灵魂、灵魂与物质、物质与物质。唯有神（毗湿奴）是独立的，其他一切都依赖于他。灵魂永远不会变成神，即使在解脱之中，也只是按各自的品级享有神的临在。他还主张有些灵魂永受束缚，有些则注定沉沦，这在印度思想中是罕见的观点。"},"schools":["theistic-vedanta"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Brahmasūtra-bhāṣya (commentary on the Brahma Sūtra)","zh":"《梵经注》"},"y":1280}],"wiki":"Madhvacharya"},
  {"id":"gangesha","tradition":"indian","born":1300,"died":1360,"life":{"en":"fl. 14th c., Mithilā","zh":"活跃于14世纪，弥提罗"},"name":{"en":"Gaṅgeśa Upādhyāya","zh":"甘吉沙"},"role":{"en":"Founder of Navya-Nyāya, the ‘new logic’","zh":"新正理派（“新逻辑”）的创始人"},"idea":{"en":"He reorganised Nyāya around the four means of knowledge and developed a precise technical language of relations and absences, used to define invariable concomitance (vyāpti) with new rigour.","zh":"他围绕四种量重组了正理学说，并发展出一套关于关系与“无”的精密技术语言，以前所未有的严格性来界定遍充。"},"detail":{"en":"Gaṅgeśa Upādhyāya lived in the first half of the 14th century in Mithilā, in today’s Bihar, which for the next centuries was the great centre of Nyāya learning, later rivalled by Navadvīpa in Bengal. Almost nothing is known of his life. His one great book, the Jewel of Reflection on Reality (Tattvacintāmaṇi), launched Navya-Nyāya, the “new logic”, and became the most commented-on text of later Indian philosophy.\n\nInstead of following the Nyāya Sūtra topic by topic, Gaṅgeśa reorganised the whole subject around the four means of knowledge and treated each with new precision. His key tool was a technical language for describing cognitions in terms of relations, qualifiers and absences—something like a formal logic built out of Sanskrit. With it he tested and rejected one proposed definition after another of invariable concomitance (vyāpti), the relation that makes inference work, before giving his own. This style of exact analysis spread from Nyāya into law, grammar and poetics, and dominated Sanskrit scholarship until the colonial era.","zh":"甘吉沙·乌帕迪耶耶生活在14世纪上半叶，居于今比哈尔邦的弥提罗；此后数百年间，那里一直是正理学的重镇，后来孟加拉的纳瓦德维帕与之并驾齐驱。关于他的生平几乎一无所知。他唯一的巨著《真理如意珠》开创了新正理派，即“新逻辑”，并成为后期印度哲学中被注释最多的文本。\n\n甘吉沙不再按《正理经》的章目逐条讨论，而是围绕四种认识手段重组全部论题，并以新的精确度逐一处理。他的关键工具是一套技术语言，用关系、限定者与“无”来描述认识，有点像用梵文搭建的形式逻辑。凭借它，他检验并否定了一个又一个关于“遍充”（使推理得以成立的不变伴随关系）的定义，最后才给出自己的定义。这种精确分析的风格从正理派扩展到法学、文法学与诗学，直到殖民时代一直主导着梵文学术。"},"schools":["nyaya"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Tattvacintāmaṇi (Jewel of Reflection on Reality)","zh":"《真理如意珠》"},"y":1325}],"wiki":"Gangesha"},
  {"id":"vivekananda","tradition":"indian","born":1863,"died":1902,"life":{"en":"1863–1902","zh":"1863–1902年"},"name":{"en":"Swami Vivekananda","zh":"辨喜"},"role":{"en":"Monk who introduced Vedānta and yoga to the West","zh":"把吠檀多与瑜伽介绍到西方的僧人"},"idea":{"en":"A disciple of Ramakrishna, he presented Vedānta as a universal religion at the 1893 Parliament of the World’s Religions in Chicago, and taught that serving the poor is worshipping God in human form.","zh":"他是罗摩克里希纳的弟子，在1893年芝加哥世界宗教议会上把吠檀多介绍为一种普世宗教，并教导：服务穷人，就是敬奉化身为人的神。"},"detail":{"en":"Narendranath Datta (1863–1902) was a Calcutta-educated young man steeped in Western philosophy and the reformist Brahmo Samaj when, around 1881, he met the Bengali mystic Ramakrishna. After his teacher’s death he became a wandering monk as Swami Vivekananda. On 11 September 1893 he addressed the Parliament of the World’s Religions in Chicago, opening with “Sisters and brothers of America”, and became famous almost overnight. He lectured in America and Britain, founded the Ramakrishna Mission in 1897, and died at thirty-nine.\n\nVivekananda recast Śaṅkara’s Advaita as a modern, universal religion. Every soul is potentially divine, and all religions are paths to the same truth, though he presented Vedānta as their fullest form. He turned non-duality into an ethic: if the same divinity lives in everyone, serving the poor is worshipping God, and India’s regeneration needs both spiritual confidence and practical work. His Raja Yoga (1896) reinterpreted Patañjali for Western readers and helped start the global spread of yoga.","zh":"纳伦德拉纳特·达塔（1863–1902）是在加尔各答接受教育的青年，熟读西方哲学，并参加过改革派的梵社。约1881年，他结识了孟加拉神秘主义者罗摩克里希那。老师去世后，他以“辨喜”之名成为游方僧。1893年9月11日，他在芝加哥世界宗教议会上以“美国的姐妹们、兄弟们”开场演讲，几乎一夜成名。此后他在美国与英国讲学，1897年创立罗摩克里希那传道会，三十九岁去世。\n\n辨喜把商羯罗的不二论改造成一种现代的、普世的宗教：每个灵魂都潜在地具有神性，一切宗教都是通向同一真理的道路，不过他把吠檀多呈现为其中最完整的形式。他把“不二”变成了一种伦理：既然同一神性住在每个人身上，服务穷人就是敬拜神；印度的复兴既需要精神上的自信，也需要切实的工作。他的《胜王瑜伽》（1896年）为西方读者重新诠释了帕坦伽利，推动了瑜伽在全球的传播。"},"schools":["neo-vedanta"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Raja Yoga","zh":"《胜王瑜伽》"},"y":1896},{"t":{"en":"Karma Yoga","zh":"《业瑜伽》"},"y":1896}],"wiki":"Swami Vivekananda","c":"Swami Vivekananda-1893-09-signed.jpg","portrait":{"w":600,"h":824,"v":"823fcfdb0f","author":"The original uploader was Dziewa at English Wikipedia .","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":null,"page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Swami_Vivekananda-1893-09-signed.jpg"}},
  {"id":"gandhi","tradition":"indian","born":1869,"died":1948,"life":{"en":"1869–1948","zh":"1869–1948年"},"name":{"en":"Mohandas K. Gandhi","zh":"甘地"},"role":{"en":"Leader of Indian independence and theorist of non-violent resistance","zh":"印度独立运动领袖，非暴力抵抗的理论家"},"idea":{"en":"He developed satyagraha, ‘holding firmly to truth’: resisting injustice through non-violent non-cooperation and willingly accepted suffering, on the conviction that means shape ends and that truth is God.","zh":"他提出“坚持真理”（萨蒂亚格拉哈）：以非暴力的不合作和甘愿承受的苦难来抵抗不义，其信念是手段塑造目的，而真理即是神。"},"detail":{"en":"Mohandas Karamchand Gandhi (1869–1948) grew up in Gujarat in a devout Vaiṣṇava family in a region with a strong Jain presence, trained as a barrister in London, and spent twenty-one years in South Africa, where racial discrimination turned a shy lawyer into an organiser of mass resistance. Back in India from 1915, he led the national movement through campaigns such as the Salt March of 1930. He was assassinated by a Hindu nationalist in January 1948, months after independence.\n\nGandhi’s philosophy was worked out in action. Satyagraha, “holding fast to truth”, is resistance that refuses both submission and violence: the resister breaks an unjust law openly, accepts the penalty, and seeks to convert the opponent rather than defeat him. Behind it lie the convictions that means and ends cannot be separated, that truth is God, and that self-rule (swaraj) begins with ruling oneself. In Hind Swaraj (1909) he turned this into a critique of modern industrial civilisation. Ambedkar fought him over caste; Martin Luther King Jr. adopted his method.","zh":"莫罕达斯·卡拉姆昌德·甘地（1869–1948）在古吉拉特一个虔诚的毗湿奴派家庭中长大，那一带耆那教影响很深；他在伦敦学成律师，又在南非度过二十一年，种族歧视使这位腼腆的律师变成了群众抵抗运动的组织者。1915年回到印度后，他通过1930年的“食盐进军”等运动领导民族运动。独立数月后，1948年1月，他被一名印度教民族主义者刺杀。\n\n甘地的哲学是在行动中形成的。“坚持真理”既拒绝屈服，也拒绝暴力：抵抗者公开违反不义的法律，接受惩罚，力求转化对手而非打败对手。其背后的信念是：手段与目的不可分割，真理就是神，而自治始于治己。在《印度自治》（1909年）中，他把这一思想扩展为对现代工业文明的批判。安贝德卡尔在种姓问题上与他激烈交锋，马丁·路德·金则采用了他的方法。"},"schools":[],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Hind Swaraj (Indian Home Rule)","zh":"《印度自治》"},"y":1909},{"t":{"en":"An Autobiography: The Story of My Experiments with Truth","zh":"《甘地自传：我对真理的实验》"},"y":1927}],"wiki":"Mahatma Gandhi","c":"Mahatma-Gandhi, studio, 1931.jpg","portrait":{"w":600,"h":749,"v":"86d45f98e5","author":"Elliott & Fry","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":null,"page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahatma-Gandhi,_studio,_1931.jpg"}},
  {"id":"aurobindo","tradition":"indian","born":1872,"died":1950,"life":{"en":"1872–1950","zh":"1872–1950年"},"name":{"en":"Sri Aurobindo","zh":"室利·阿罗频多"},"role":{"en":"Nationalist revolutionary turned philosopher of spiritual evolution","zh":"由民族主义革命者转为精神进化哲学家"},"idea":{"en":"After leading radical nationalism in Bengal he withdrew to Pondicherry and developed Integral Yoga: consciousness involved in matter is evolving toward a ‘supermind’ that will transform life on earth.","zh":"他曾领导孟加拉的激进民族主义运动，后隐居本地治里，发展出“整体瑜伽”：潜藏于物质中的意识正向“超心”进化，并将改变地上的生命。"},"detail":{"en":"Aurobindo Ghose (1872–1950) was educated in England from the age of seven, at St Paul’s School and King’s College, Cambridge. Back in India he became a leader of radical nationalism in Bengal, calling for complete independence. Arrested in the Alipore bomb case in 1908, he spent a year in jail, where, he later wrote, spiritual experiences changed his course. Acquitted, he withdrew in 1910 to French-ruled Pondicherry, where he lived until his death and where an ashram grew up around him and his collaborator Mirra Alfassa, “the Mother”.\n\nHis Integral Yoga turns Vedānta toward evolution. Spirit has “involved” itself in matter; evolution is the reverse movement, from matter through life and mind toward higher planes of consciousness. The next step is the “supermind”, a truth-consciousness that would not escape the world, as older yogas sought to, but transform life and the body on earth. The Life Divine, first serialised in 1914–19 and revised as a book in 1939–40, sets out this vision.","zh":"奥罗宾多·高士（1872–1950）七岁起在英国接受教育，就读于圣保罗公学与剑桥大学国王学院。回到印度后，他成为孟加拉激进民族主义的领袖，主张完全独立。1908年他因阿里浦尔炸弹案被捕，在狱中度过一年；据他后来所写，狱中的灵性体验改变了他的方向。被宣判无罪后，他于1910年退隐到法属本地治里，在那里度过余生，一座修道院逐渐围绕他和他的合作者米拉·阿尔法萨（“圣母”）形成。\n\n他的“整体瑜伽”把吠檀多引向进化论：精神已“内卷”于物质之中，进化则是反方向的运动，从物质经由生命与心智走向更高层次的意识。下一步是“超心智”，一种真理意识；它不像旧式瑜伽那样寻求逃离世界，而是要在地上改造生命与身体。最初于1914–1919年连载、1939–1940年修订成书的《神圣人生论》阐述了这一图景。"},"schools":["neo-vedanta"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"The Life Divine","zh":"《神圣人生论》"},"y":1940}],"wiki":"Sri Aurobindo"},
  {"id":"ambedkar","tradition":"indian","born":1891,"died":1956,"life":{"en":"1891–1956","zh":"1891–1956年"},"name":{"en":"B. R. Ambedkar","zh":"安贝德卡尔"},"role":{"en":"Dalit leader, jurist and chief architect of India’s constitution","zh":"达利特（贱民）领袖、法学家、印度宪法的主要起草者"},"idea":{"en":"He argued that caste is a system of graded inequality sanctioned by Hindu scripture and must be annihilated, not reformed. In 1956 he converted to Buddhism with hundreds of thousands of followers, recasting it as a rational, social ethic (Navayana).","zh":"他认为种姓是一种由印度教经典认可的等级不平等制度，必须废除，而不是改良。1956年，他与数十万追随者一同皈依佛教，并把佛教重新诠释为一种理性的社会伦理（新乘佛教）。"},"detail":{"en":"Bhimrao Ramji Ambedkar (1891–1956) was born into the Mahar caste, treated as untouchable, and endured segregation at school. With scholarships he studied at Columbia University, where John Dewey was among his teachers, and at the London School of Economics, earning doctorates in economics and qualifying as a barrister. He led Dalit movements, clashed with Gandhi over separate electorates in 1932, chaired the drafting committee of India’s constitution, and served as independent India’s first law minister.\n\nAmbedkar argued that caste is not a division of labour but a division of labourers—a hierarchy of graded inequality held in place by religious sanction—so it cannot be reformed from within Hinduism: the scriptures that sanction it must lose their authority. That is the case of Annihilation of Caste (1936), written as a speech its organisers cancelled. In October 1956, weeks before his death, he converted to Buddhism at Nagpur with hundreds of thousands of followers, presenting it in The Buddha and His Dhamma as a rational, egalitarian social ethic.","zh":"比姆拉奥·拉姆吉·安贝德卡尔（1891–1956）出身于被视为“不可接触者”的马哈尔种姓，求学时饱受隔离之苦。他凭奖学金先后就读于哥伦比亚大学（约翰·杜威是他的老师之一）和伦敦政治经济学院，获得经济学博士学位，并取得律师资格。他领导达利特运动，1932年在单独选区问题上与甘地激烈交锋，后来主持印度宪法起草委员会，并出任独立印度的首任法律部长。\n\n安贝德卡尔认为，种姓不是劳动的分工，而是劳动者的分割——一种由宗教认可维持的分级不平等——因此不可能在印度教内部改良：认可它的经典必须失去权威。这就是《种姓的消灭》（1936年）的论点，此文原是一篇演讲稿，主办方却取消了演讲。1956年10月，在去世前几周，他在那格浦尔率数十万追随者皈依佛教，并在《佛陀及其正法》中把佛教阐述为一种理性、平等的社会伦理。"},"schools":[],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Annihilation of Caste","zh":"《种姓的消灭》"},"y":1936},{"t":{"en":"The Buddha and His Dhamma","zh":"《佛陀及其正法》"},"y":1957}],"wiki":"B. R. Ambedkar"},
  {"id":"thales","tradition":"greek","born":-624,"died":-546,"life":{"en":"c. 624–546 BCE","zh":"约公元前624–前546年"},"name":{"en":"Thales","zh":"泰勒斯"},"role":{"en":"Sage of Miletus, traditionally the first Greek philosopher","zh":"米利都的贤者，传统上被视为第一位希腊哲学家"},"idea":{"en":"Taught that water is the origin of all things and that the world is “full of gods”, meaning nature is alive with its own powers. He wrote nothing; ancient reports credit him with geometry and with predicting an eclipse, usually dated to 585 BCE.","zh":"主张水是万物的本原，并说世界“充满了神”，意即自然自身就充满活力。他没有留下著作；古代记载称他精通几何，并预言了一次日食（通常定为公元前585年）。"},"detail":{"en":"Thales lived in Miletus, a rich Greek trading city on the coast of Asia Minor, in the early 6th century BCE. Later Greeks counted him among the Seven Sages and told stories of his practical skill: measuring the pyramids by their shadows, diverting a river for an army, and foretelling an eclipse that Herodotus ties to a battle usually dated to 585 BCE. He left no writings, so what we know comes mainly from Aristotle, two centuries later.\n\nAristotle says Thales made water the origin (archē) of all things, perhaps because moisture nourishes life and seeds are wet, and that he thought the magnet has a soul because it moves iron. The answer matters less than the move: instead of telling how gods made the world, he named one natural principle and gave reasons that others could test and dispute. His successors in Miletus did exactly that.","zh":"泰勒斯生活在公元前6世纪初的米利都，那是小亚细亚沿岸一座富庶的希腊商港。后世希腊人把他列为“七贤”之一，流传着许多关于他实际才能的故事：借日影测量金字塔、为军队改道河流，还预言了一次日食——希罗多德把它系于一场通常定在公元前585年的战役。他没有留下著作，我们的了解主要来自两个世纪后的亚里士多德。\n\n亚里士多德说，泰勒斯以水为万物的本原，或许因为湿气滋养生命、种子都是湿的；他还认为磁石有灵魂，因为它能吸动铁。答案本身不如这一步重要：他不再讲述众神如何造出世界，而是指出一个自然的本原，并给出别人可以检验、可以反驳的理由。米利都的后继者正是这样做的。"},"schools":["milesian"],"works":[],"wiki":"Thales of Miletus"},
  {"id":"anaximander","tradition":"greek","born":-610,"died":-546,"life":{"en":"c. 610–546 BCE","zh":"约公元前610–前546年"},"name":{"en":"Anaximander","zh":"阿那克西曼德"},"role":{"en":"Thales’ successor, credited with the first Greek world map","zh":"泰勒斯的后继者，据说绘制了第一幅希腊世界地图"},"idea":{"en":"Argued that the origin cannot be any one element, which would overwhelm the others, but the apeiron, the boundless and indefinite. Opposites separate out of it and “pay penalty to one another for their injustice according to the order of time”.","zh":"认为本原不能是任何一种元素，否则它会压倒其他元素；本原应是“无定”（apeiron），即无界限、无规定之物。对立面从中分离出来，并“按照时间的秩序，为彼此的不义相互补偿”。"},"detail":{"en":"Anaximander, a younger fellow citizen of Thales in Miletus, is said to have written the first Greek prose book on nature and drawn the first map of the inhabited world; one sentence of the book survives. Ancient reports also credit him with a model of the heavens in which the Earth hangs unsupported at the centre, staying put because it is equally distant from everything.\n\nHis central argument is about balance. If the origin were water, as Thales said, water would long ago have overwhelmed its opposite, fire. So the source must have no fixed character: the apeiron, the boundless. Hot and cold, wet and dry separate out of it and, in his surviving fragment, “pay penalty and retribution to one another for their injustice according to the ordering of time”. The cosmos becomes a lawful order of opposites, an idea that runs through later Greek medicine and physics.","zh":"阿那克西曼德是泰勒斯在米利都的年轻同乡，据说写出了第一部论自然的希腊散文著作，并绘制了第一幅人居世界的地图；他的书只有一句话流传下来。古代记载还说他构想了一个宇宙模型：大地悬在中心、无所依托，之所以不坠落，是因为它与四方的距离都相等。\n\n他的核心论证关乎平衡。如果本原是泰勒斯所说的水，水早就压倒了与之相对的火。因此本原必须没有确定的性质，那就是“无定”（apeiron）。冷与热、湿与干从中分离出来，用他传世残篇的话说，它们“按照时间的秩序，为彼此的不义相互偿还、受到惩罚”。宇宙由此成为对立面之间合乎法则的秩序，这一观念贯穿了后来的希腊医学与物理学。"},"schools":["milesian"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"On Nature (one fragment survives)","zh":"《论自然》（仅存一则残篇）"},"y":-550}],"wiki":"Anaximander"},
  {"id":"anaximenes","tradition":"greek","born":-586,"died":-526,"life":{"en":"c. 586–526 BCE","zh":"约公元前586–前526年"},"name":{"en":"Anaximenes","zh":"阿那克西美尼"},"role":{"en":"Third Milesian, who made air the first principle","zh":"第三位米利都哲学家，以气为本原"},"idea":{"en":"Held that air is the basic stuff: rarefied it becomes fire, condensed it becomes wind, water, earth and stone. This gave a single mechanism, change of density, for how one stuff becomes many.","zh":"主张气是基本质料：气稀散则成火，凝聚则依次成为风、水、土和石头。这为“一种质料如何变成万物”提供了单一的机制——密度的变化。"},"detail":{"en":"Anaximenes, the third philosopher of Miletus, is reported to have been Anaximander’s pupil or associate. He worked in the mid-6th century BCE, shortly before the Persians took control of Ionia, and wrote a book in plain prose of which only paraphrases survive.\n\nHe chose air as the basic stuff, and his real contribution is how he explained change. Air thinned out becomes fire; thickened, it becomes wind, cloud, water, earth and finally stone. Differences of quality are reduced to differences of quantity, of density. He offered an everyday test: breath blown through pursed lips feels cold, through an open mouth warm. Air also suited the role of a world-soul, since our own soul, he reportedly said, is air that holds us together. Later Ionian thinkers kept his mechanism even when they dropped his choice of stuff.","zh":"阿那克西美尼是米利都的第三位哲学家，据记载是阿那克西曼德的学生或同伴。他活跃于公元前6世纪中叶，不久之后波斯人便控制了伊奥尼亚。他用朴素的散文写过一本书，如今只剩下转述。\n\n他选择气作为基本的质料，而他真正的贡献在于如何解释变化：气变稀就成为火，变浓则依次成为风、云、水、土，最后成为石头。性质的差别由此被归结为数量的差别，即疏密不同。他还举了一个日常的例子：撮起嘴唇吹出的气是凉的，张口哈出的气是暖的。气也适合充当世界的灵魂——据说他讲过，我们的灵魂就是把我们维系在一起的气。后来的伊奥尼亚思想家即使不再以气为本原，也保留了他的这套机制。"},"schools":["milesian"],"works":[],"wiki":"Anaximenes of Miletus"},
  {"id":"pythagoras","tradition":"greek","born":-570,"died":-495,"life":{"en":"c. 570–495 BCE","zh":"约公元前570–前495年"},"name":{"en":"Pythagoras","zh":"毕达哥拉斯"},"role":{"en":"Founder of a philosophical and religious brotherhood at Croton","zh":"在克罗顿创立哲学兼宗教兄弟会的人"},"idea":{"en":"Taught the immortality and transmigration of the soul and a disciplined way of life; the idea that all things are number is attested for his followers. He wrote nothing, and layers of later legend make it hard to know what was his own.","zh":"教导灵魂不朽与轮回，以及一种严格自律的生活方式；“万物皆数”的观念见于他的追随者。他没有著作，后世传说层层叠加，很难确定哪些思想真正属于他本人。"},"detail":{"en":"Pythagoras was born on Samos around 570 BCE and, around 530, left for Croton in southern Italy, where he founded a community with its own rules of diet, silence and loyalty. The group gained political power in the region; around 510 violence against it drove him to Metapontum, where he died. He wrote nothing, and biographers writing centuries later turned him into a miracle-worker, so the historical man is hard to recover.\n\nThe best-attested teaching is about the soul: it is immortal and passes into other bodies, human and animal, which is why a pure way of life matters. The mathematical side is harder to pin down. The “Pythagorean” theorem was known earlier in Babylon, and the claim that all things are number is attested for his followers. Still, the circle he founded joined numerical order, music and the care of the soul, a combination Plato inherited.","zh":"毕达哥拉斯约公元前570年生于萨摩斯岛，约前530年迁往南意大利的克罗顿，在那里建立了一个对饮食、沉默与忠诚都有规矩的团体。这个团体在当地掌握了政治势力；约前510年，针对它的暴力迫使他出走梅塔庞通，并卒于该地。他没有留下著作，几个世纪后的传记作者把他写成了能行奇迹的人，历史上的毕达哥拉斯因此难以还原。\n\n证据最充分的是他关于灵魂的教导：灵魂不朽，会转入人或动物的其他身体，所以纯洁的生活方式至关重要。数学方面则更难确定：所谓“毕达哥拉斯定理”早在巴比伦就已为人所知，“万物皆数”的说法也见于他的追随者。不过，他创立的团体把数的秩序、音乐与灵魂的修养联系在一起，这一组合后来为柏拉图所继承。"},"schools":["pythagorean"],"works":[],"wiki":"Pythagoras"},
  {"id":"heraclitus","tradition":"greek","born":-535,"died":-475,"life":{"en":"c. 535–475 BCE","zh":"约公元前535–前475年"},"name":{"en":"Heraclitus","zh":"赫拉克利特"},"role":{"en":"Riddling sage of Ephesus who saw the world as ordered flux","zh":"以谜语般言辞著称的以弗所哲人，视世界为有序的流变"},"idea":{"en":"Taught that all things flow and that opposites are one, held together by the logos, a hidden rational order; “you cannot step into the same river twice” is a later paraphrase. Fire, ever-changing yet kept in measure, is his image of the cosmos.","zh":"教导万物流变、对立统一，由“逻各斯”——隐藏的理性秩序——维系在一起；“人不能两次踏进同一条河流”是后人的转述。永远变化却又守着分寸的火，是他心中宇宙的意象。"},"detail":{"en":"Heraclitus came from an old aristocratic family of Ephesus and was active around 500 BCE. He is said to have given up a hereditary office to his brother and to have deposited his single book, probably a collection of sharp, riddling sayings, in the temple of Artemis. He scorned the crowd and famous predecessors alike: Pythagoras’ “much learning”, he said, does not teach understanding.\n\nHis central claim is that the world is a unity of opposites held in tension by the logos, an order that is always there but that most people fail to notice. The road up and the road down are one and the same; sea water is drinkable for fish and deadly for people. Change is not the enemy of order but its form: fire, “kindled in measures and extinguished in measures”, is his image of the cosmos. The line about never stepping into the same river twice is Plato’s paraphrase. The Stoics later made his logos and fire the core of their physics.","zh":"赫拉克利特出身以弗所的古老贵族家庭，活跃于公元前500年前后。据说他把世袭的职位让给了弟弟，并把唯一的一部书——大概是一部犀利而隐晦的格言集——存放在阿耳忒弥斯神庙。他既轻视大众，也看不起有名的前辈，说毕达哥拉斯的“博学并不教人理解”。\n\n他的核心主张是：世界是对立面的统一，由逻各斯维系在张力之中；这一秩序始终存在，多数人却视而不见。上坡路和下坡路是同一条路；海水对鱼可饮，对人致命。变化不是秩序的敌人，而正是秩序的形式：“按尺度燃烧、按尺度熄灭”的火，是他心目中宇宙的形象。“人不能两次踏进同一条河流”是柏拉图的转述。斯多葛派后来把他的逻各斯与火作为自己物理学的核心。"},"schools":[],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Book of sayings (fragments)","zh":"格言集（残篇）"},"y":-500}],"wiki":"Heraclitus"},
  {"id":"parmenides","tradition":"greek","born":-515,"died":-450,"life":{"en":"c. 515 – after 450 BCE","zh":"约公元前515–前450年后"},"name":{"en":"Parmenides","zh":"巴门尼德"},"role":{"en":"Founder of the Eleatic school and of deductive argument","zh":"埃利亚学派的创始人，演绎论证的开创者"},"idea":{"en":"In his poem a goddess reveals that only “what is” can be thought, so being is one, eternal, unchanging and whole. The “way of opinion”, full of change, is presented as deceptive.","zh":"在他的诗中，一位女神启示：只有“存在者”可以被思考，因此存在是一、永恒、不变、完整的。充满变化的“意见之路”则被视为虚幻。"},"detail":{"en":"Parmenides lived in Elea, a Greek colony on the west coast of southern Italy. Plato has him visit Athens at about sixty-five and talk with a young Socrates, which would put his birth around 515 BCE, and ancient reports say he gave his city laws. He wrote a poem in epic verse in which an unnamed goddess shows a young man two paths: the way of truth and the way of mortal opinion.\n\nThe way of truth is an argument. You cannot think or say what is not, since there would be nothing to think. So there is no coming-to-be from what is not and no perishing into it, no gaps of emptiness and no change: what is must be one, whole, unmoving and complete. If our senses report a changing, many-coloured world, so much the worse for the senses. It was the first sustained deductive argument in Western thought, and every later Greek physics, from the pluralists and atomists to Plato, had to answer it.","zh":"巴门尼德生活在南意大利西岸的希腊殖民城邦爱利亚。柏拉图写他在约六十五岁时到访雅典，与年轻的苏格拉底交谈，据此推算他约生于公元前515年；古代记载还说他曾为家乡制定法律。他用史诗格律写了一首诗：一位无名女神向一个青年指出两条道路，一条是真理之路，一条是凡人的意见之路。\n\n真理之路是一个论证：你不能思考或说出“不存在的东西”，因为那里没有可思考的对象。因此既没有从“不存在”中生成，也没有消亡到“不存在”中，没有空隙，也没有变化：存在者必定是一、完整、不动而圆满的。如果感官报告的是一个变动、多彩的世界，那只能说明感官不可靠。这是西方思想中第一个连贯的演绎论证，此后从多元论者、原子论者到柏拉图的每一种希腊物理学，都必须回应它。"},"schools":["eleatic"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"On Nature (poem)","zh":"《论自然》（诗）"},"y":-475}],"wiki":"Parmenides"},
  {"id":"anaxagoras","tradition":"greek","born":-500,"died":-428,"life":{"en":"c. 500–428 BCE","zh":"约公元前500–前428年"},"name":{"en":"Anaxagoras","zh":"阿那克萨戈拉"},"role":{"en":"First philosopher to settle in Athens; tried for impiety","zh":"第一位定居雅典的哲学家，曾因不敬神受审"},"idea":{"en":"Taught that everything contains a portion of everything, and that Mind (nous), unmixed and infinite, set the cosmos rotating. His claim that the sun is a fiery stone led to his prosecution.","zh":"主张万物之中都有万物的一份，而纯粹、无限的“心灵”（努斯）推动宇宙旋转。他声称太阳是一块燃烧的石头，因此遭到起诉。"},"detail":{"en":"Anaxagoras came from Clazomenae in Ionia and settled in Athens, perhaps around 460 BCE (the date is disputed), the first philosopher known to have lived there, and became a friend of Pericles. He taught that the sun is a red-hot stone and that the moon’s light comes from the sun. Ancient reports say he was prosecuted for impiety, perhaps partly as an attack on Pericles, and he withdrew to Lampsacus, where he died around 428.\n\nLike Empedocles he accepted Parmenides’ ban on coming-to-be from nothing, but his solution was more radical: in everything there is a portion of everything. Bread can nourish hair, flesh and bone because those stuffs are already in it, too small to see; things are named after what predominates. What set this mixture moving is Nous, Mind, which alone is unmixed and knows all things. In Plato’s Phaedo, Socrates says he was thrilled by this idea, then disappointed that Anaxagoras never used Mind to explain why things are as it is best for them to be.","zh":"阿那克萨戈拉来自伊奥尼亚的克拉佐美奈，大约在公元前460年前后定居雅典（年代有争议），是已知第一位在雅典生活的哲学家，并与伯里克利交好。他认为太阳是一块炽热的石头，月亮的光来自太阳。古代记载说他以不敬神的罪名受到起诉，这或许也是政敌对伯里克利的打击；他退居兰普萨库斯，约公元前428年卒于该地。\n\n他和恩培多克勒一样接受巴门尼德“不能凭空生成”的禁令，但解决办法更为彻底：万物之中都含有万物的成分。面包之所以能长出头发、血肉和骨头，是因为这些成分早已在面包里，只是小到看不见；事物以其中占优势的成分得名。推动这一混合体运动的是“努斯”（心灵），唯有它不与他物混合，并且知晓一切。在柏拉图的《斐多篇》中，苏格拉底说他起初为这个想法激动，后来却失望：阿那克萨戈拉从未用心灵来解释万物为何以对它们最好的方式存在。"},"schools":["pluralists"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"On Nature (fragments)","zh":"《论自然》（残篇）"},"y":-460}],"wiki":"Anaxagoras"},
  {"id":"empedocles","tradition":"greek","born":-494,"died":-434,"life":{"en":"c. 494–434 BCE","zh":"约公元前494–前434年"},"name":{"en":"Empedocles","zh":"恩培多克勒"},"role":{"en":"Sicilian poet, healer and statesman of the four roots","zh":"西西里的诗人、医者与政治家，提出“四根”说"},"idea":{"en":"Held that earth, water, air and fire are eternal “roots” that Love unites and Strife separates in an endless cosmic cycle. He also taught the transmigration of souls and abstention from killing animals.","zh":"认为土、水、气、火是永恒的“根”，在无尽的宇宙循环中由“爱”结合、由“争”分离。他还教导灵魂轮回，主张不杀动物。"},"detail":{"en":"Empedocles came from Acragas (Agrigento) in Sicily and lived in the 5th century BCE. Ancient stories make him a democratic politician who refused a crown, a healer and wonder-worker, and, in legend, a man who leapt into the crater of Etna to prove himself a god. He wrote in verse; the Strasbourg papyrus, published in 1999, added new lines to those quoted by later authors.\n\nTo satisfy Parmenides without denying change, he proposed four eternal “roots”, earth, water, air and fire, and two forces: Love, which unites them, and Strife, which separates them. Nothing comes from nothing; what we call birth and death is mixing and unmixing, and the cosmos swings in a cycle between total union and total separation. His religious poem adds that souls, too, wander through many bodies, so killing animals is a kind of murder. The four elements then dominated European and Islamic science for two thousand years.","zh":"恩培多克勒来自西西里的阿克拉加斯（今阿格里真托），生活在公元前5世纪。古代故事把他描绘成一位拒绝王冠的民主派政治家、一位医者与奇人；传说他为了证明自己是神而跳进了埃特纳火山口。他用诗体写作；除了后世作者引用的诗句，1999年刊布的斯特拉斯堡纸草又补充了一批残篇。\n\n为了既满足巴门尼德又不否认变化，他提出土、水、气、火四种永恒的“根”，以及两种力量：使它们结合的“爱”和使它们分离的“争”。没有什么出自虚无；我们所说的生与死，只是混合与分离，宇宙在完全结合与完全分离之间循环往复。他的宗教诗还说，灵魂也在许多身体之间流转，因此杀害动物无异于谋杀。此后，四元素说主导欧洲与伊斯兰世界的科学长达两千年。"},"schools":["pluralists"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"On Nature and Purifications (fragments)","zh":"《论自然》与《净化》（残篇）"},"y":-450}],"wiki":"Empedocles"},
  {"id":"zeno-elea","tradition":"greek","born":-490,"died":-430,"life":{"en":"c. 490–430 BCE","zh":"约公元前490–前430年"},"name":{"en":"Zeno of Elea","zh":"埃利亚的芝诺"},"role":{"en":"Parmenides’ pupil, famous for paradoxes of motion and plurality","zh":"巴门尼德的学生，以关于运动与多的悖论闻名"},"idea":{"en":"Defended Parmenides by showing that common-sense belief in motion and plurality leads to absurdities, as in Achilles and the Tortoise and the flying Arrow. His method, reductio ad absurdum, became a basic tool of philosophy.","zh":"为替巴门尼德辩护，他证明常识中对运动与多的信念会导出荒谬，例如“阿基里斯追乌龟”和“飞矢不动”。他所用的归谬法成为哲学的基本工具。"},"detail":{"en":"Zeno of Elea was Parmenides’ younger associate; Plato describes him at about forty, visiting Athens with his master. He wrote a book of arguments, now lost, and ancient reports say he died bravely resisting a tyrant. Our knowledge of his paradoxes comes mostly from Aristotle’s Physics and its commentators.\n\nHis method was to defend Parmenides indirectly: assume what common sense says, that there are many things and that they move, and show it leads to absurdity. To cross a stadium you must first reach the halfway point, then half of the rest, and so on without end, so you can never finish. Swift Achilles never catches the tortoise, since each time he reaches where it was, it has moved on. A flying arrow occupies a space equal to itself at every instant, so it is always at rest. These puzzles about infinity, space and time were not well answered until the mathematics of limits in the 19th century, and some philosophers think they still are not.","zh":"爱利亚的芝诺是巴门尼德年轻的同伴；柏拉图写他约四十岁时随老师访问雅典。他写过一部论证集，今已失传；古代记载说他在反抗一位僭主时英勇赴死。我们对他悖论的了解，主要来自亚里士多德的《物理学》及其注释者。\n\n他的方法是间接地为巴门尼德辩护：先假定常识所说的，事物有许多并且在运动，再证明这会导致荒谬。要跑完一段跑道，你得先到达一半处，再到达剩下的一半，如此无穷无尽，于是永远跑不完。快跑的阿喀琉斯永远追不上乌龟，因为每当他到达乌龟原来的位置，乌龟又往前挪了一点。飞矢在每一瞬间都占据与自身相等的空间，所以它始终是静止的。这些关于无限、空间与时间的难题，直到19世纪的极限理论才得到较好的回答，有些哲学家认为至今仍未解决。"},"schools":["eleatic"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Paradoxes (book lost; reported by Aristotle and Simplicius)","zh":"《悖论》（原书已佚，见于亚里士多德与辛普里丘的转述）"},"y":null}],"wiki":"Zeno of Elea"},
  {"id":"protagoras","tradition":"greek","born":-490,"died":-420,"life":{"en":"c. 490–420 BCE","zh":"约公元前490–前420年"},"name":{"en":"Protagoras","zh":"普罗泰戈拉"},"role":{"en":"The most famous sophist, who taught virtue for a fee","zh":"最著名的智者，收费传授德性"},"idea":{"en":"Declared that “man is the measure of all things”, so that things are for each person as they appear to that person. He was agnostic about the gods, saying the question is obscure and human life is short.","zh":"宣称“人是万物的尺度”，即事物对每个人来说就是它向那个人显现的样子。他对神持不可知论态度，说这个问题晦暗不明，而人生短暂。"},"detail":{"en":"Protagoras came from Abdera in northern Greece and spent some forty years travelling as a teacher, often in Athens, where Pericles reportedly asked him to draft laws for the new colony of Thurii in 444 BCE. He was the first to charge fees for teaching “virtue”, meaning the skills of managing a household and taking part in public life. Later stories that his books were burned and he was expelled for impiety are doubtful.\n\nHis most famous sentence opened his book Truth: “Man is the measure of all things, of things that are, that they are, and of things that are not, that they are not.” If the wind feels cold to me and warm to you, there is no further fact about the wind itself; each perception is true for the perceiver. About the gods he said he could not know whether they exist, since the question is obscure and life is short. Plato devoted the Theaetetus to refuting him: if every belief is true for its holder, so is the belief that Protagoras is wrong.","zh":"普罗泰戈拉来自希腊北部的阿布德拉，作为教师四处游历了约四十年，常住雅典；据说伯里克利曾请他为公元前444年新建的殖民地图里伊起草法律。他是第一个为传授“德性”收费的人，所谓德性，指的是管理家业、参与公共生活的本领。晚出的说法称他因不敬神而被逐、著作遭焚，并不可靠。\n\n他最著名的一句话写在《真理》开篇：“人是万物的尺度，是存在者存在的尺度，也是不存在者不存在的尺度。”如果风让我觉得冷、让你觉得暖，就不存在关于风本身的进一步事实；每一种感知对感知者而言都是真的。关于神，他说自己无从知道神是否存在，因为问题晦涩而人生短暂。柏拉图专门用《泰阿泰德篇》来反驳他：如果每个信念对持有者都为真，那么“普罗泰戈拉错了”这个信念也为真。"},"schools":["sophists"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Truth (lost)","zh":"《论真理》（已佚）"},"y":-440},{"t":{"en":"On the Gods (lost)","zh":"《论神》（已佚）"},"y":-440}],"wiki":"Protagoras"},
  {"id":"gorgias","tradition":"greek","born":-483,"died":-375,"life":{"en":"c. 483–375 BCE","zh":"约公元前483–前375年"},"name":{"en":"Gorgias","zh":"高尔吉亚"},"role":{"en":"Sicilian master of rhetoric and envoy to Athens","zh":"西西里的修辞大师，曾出使雅典"},"idea":{"en":"Argued, perhaps as a parody of Eleatic reasoning, that nothing exists, that if it did it could not be known, and that if known it could not be told. He compared speech to a powerful drug that works on the soul.","zh":"论证说（或许是在戏仿埃利亚学派的推理）：无物存在；即使存在也不可知；即使可知也无法言说。他把言辞比作一种作用于灵魂的强效药物。"},"detail":{"en":"Gorgias of Leontini in Sicily lived, by ancient accounts, to well over a hundred. In 427 BCE he came to Athens as an envoy seeking help against Syracuse, and his dazzling speeches, full of rhythm and balanced antitheses, made him the most famous teacher of rhetoric of his day. A late report makes him a pupil of Empedocles.\n\nHis Encomium of Helen survives whole, and On Non-Being is known from two later summaries. On Non-Being argues in three steps that nothing exists; that if anything existed it could not be known; and that if it could be known it could not be communicated. Whether he meant this seriously or as a parody of Eleatic logic is disputed. The Encomium defends Helen of Troy: if speech persuaded her, she is blameless, because speech is “a powerful lord” that works on the soul as drugs work on the body. Plato’s Gorgias answers him: rhetoric that aims at persuasion rather than truth is flattery.","zh":"西西里莱昂蒂尼的高尔吉亚，据古代记载活到了一百多岁。公元前427年，他作为使节来到雅典，为家乡请求援助以对抗叙拉古；他的演说富于节奏、对仗工整，令听众倾倒，使他成为当时最负盛名的修辞教师。一则晚出的记载说他是恩培多克勒的学生。\n\n他的《海伦颂》完整传世，《论不存在》则通过后世的两种概述为人所知。《论不存在》分三步论证：什么都不存在；即使有东西存在，也无法被认识；即使能被认识，也无法传达给别人。这究竟是认真的主张，还是对爱利亚派逻辑的戏仿，学界仍有争论。《海伦颂》为特洛伊的海伦辩护：如果是言辞说服了她，她就没有过错，因为言辞是“强大的主宰”，作用于灵魂，正如药物作用于身体。柏拉图的《高尔吉亚篇》回应了他：以说服而非真理为目的的修辞，只是一种谄媚。"},"schools":["sophists"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"On Non-Being","zh":"《论非存在》"},"y":-440},{"t":{"en":"Encomium of Helen","zh":"《海伦颂》"},"y":-415}],"wiki":"Gorgias"},
  {"id":"leucippus","tradition":"greek","born":-480,"died":-420,"life":{"en":"5th c. BCE","zh":"公元前5世纪"},"name":{"en":"Leucippus","zh":"留基伯"},"role":{"en":"Founder of atomism, about whom almost nothing is known","zh":"原子论的创始人，生平几乎无从知晓"},"idea":{"en":"Proposed that the world consists of indivisible atoms moving in the void, and that “nothing happens at random, but everything for a reason and by necessity”. Epicurus is reported to have doubted that he existed at all.","zh":"提出世界由在虚空中运动的不可分原子构成，并说“没有什么是偶然发生的，一切都有其理由并出于必然”。据说伊壁鸠鲁甚至怀疑他是否真实存在过。"},"detail":{"en":"Leucippus is the most shadowy figure in early Greek philosophy. Ancient writers say he came from Miletus, Elea or Abdera, studied with Zeno of Elea and taught Democritus; Epicurus apparently doubted that he existed at all. But Aristotle and Theophrastus treat him as the real founder of atomism, active in the 5th century BCE, and one sentence of his, from a work On Mind, survives.\n\nHis idea answered Parmenides directly. Accept that what is cannot come from nothing or vanish into nothing, but deny that there is no void. Then reality can be countless tiny, solid, indivisible bodies, atoms, moving through empty space, and every apparent birth, death and change is just atoms joining and separating. Even whole worlds form this way, in whirls of atoms. His surviving sentence states the programme: nothing happens at random, but everything for a reason and by necessity. It is the first thoroughly mechanical picture of nature.","zh":"留基伯是早期希腊哲学中最模糊的人物。古代作者说他来自米利都、爱利亚或阿布德拉，曾师从爱利亚的芝诺，又是德谟克利特的老师；伊壁鸠鲁似乎怀疑他是否真有其人。但亚里士多德和泰奥弗拉斯托斯都把他视为原子论的真正创始人，活跃于公元前5世纪；他还有一句话出自《论心灵》，流传至今。\n\n他的思想直接回应了巴门尼德：承认存在者不能从虚无中产生、也不能消失于虚无，但否认“没有虚空”。这样一来，实在就可以是无数微小、坚实、不可分的物体，即原子，在空无中运动；一切看似的生灭与变化，只是原子的结合与分离。就连整个世界也是这样在原子的旋涡中形成的。他传世的那句话道出了整个纲领：没有什么是偶然发生的，一切都出于理由并出于必然。这是第一幅彻底机械论的自然图景。"},"schools":["atomism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"The Great World-System (attributed, lost)","zh":"《大宇宙系统》（传为其作，已佚）"},"y":-440}],"wiki":"Leucippus"},
  {"id":"philolaus","tradition":"greek","born":-470,"died":-385,"life":{"en":"c. 470–385 BCE","zh":"约公元前470–前385年"},"name":{"en":"Philolaus","zh":"菲洛劳斯"},"role":{"en":"Pythagorean whose book first set out the school’s doctrines","zh":"毕达哥拉斯派哲学家，其著作首次系统阐述该派学说"},"idea":{"en":"Argued that the cosmos is made of “limiters” and “unlimiteds” fitted together by harmony, and that nothing can be known without number. He also moved the Earth from the centre of the cosmos, having it circle a central fire.","zh":"认为宇宙由“限定者”与“无限定者”构成，并由“和谐”结合在一起；没有数，就无法认识任何事物。他还把地球从宇宙中心移开，让它绕着一团“中心火”运行。"},"detail":{"en":"Philolaus came from Croton or Tarentum in southern Italy and was a contemporary of Socrates. In Plato’s Phaedo, two of Socrates’ young friends are said to have heard him teach at Thebes, where some Pythagoreans had gone after attacks on their communities in Italy. He wrote what was probably the first book by a Pythagorean, On Nature, of which about eleven fragments are judged genuine.\n\nHe argued that the cosmos and everything in it is fitted together from two kinds of thing: “unlimiteds”, such as stuffs, space and time, and “limiters”, the shapes and structures that bound them, joined by a harmony. Since all things that are known have number, number is what makes the world intelligible. His astronomy was bold: the Earth is not the centre but one of the bodies circling a central fire, with a “counter-earth” on the far side. Aristotle’s report that Pythagoreans thought “things are numbers” is largely his own reading of Philolaus.","zh":"菲洛劳斯来自南意大利的克罗顿或塔兰托，与苏格拉底同时代。柏拉图的《斐多篇》提到，苏格拉底的两位年轻朋友曾在忒拜听他讲学——毕达哥拉斯派在意大利遭到攻击后，有人去了那里。他写了《论自然》，大概是第一部出自毕达哥拉斯派之手的书，其中约十一则残篇被认为可靠。\n\n他主张宇宙及其中的一切，都是由两类东西拼合而成的：一类是“无限者”，如质料、空间与时间；一类是“限定者”，即给它们划定界限的形状与结构，两者由和谐结合起来。既然凡被认识的事物都有数，数就是使世界可以理解的东西。他的天文学十分大胆：地球不在中心，而是围绕“中央之火”运行的天体之一，在另一侧还有一个“反地球”。亚里士多德说毕达哥拉斯派认为“事物就是数”，这在很大程度上是他自己对菲洛劳斯的解读。"},"schools":["pythagorean"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"On Nature (fragments)","zh":"《论自然》（残篇）"},"y":-420}],"wiki":"Philolaus"},
  {"id":"socrates","tradition":"greek","born":-470,"died":-399,"life":{"en":"c. 470–399 BCE","zh":"约公元前470–前399年"},"name":{"en":"Socrates","zh":"苏格拉底"},"role":{"en":"Athenian questioner, executed for impiety in 399 BCE","zh":"雅典的诘问者，公元前399年以不敬神罪被处死"},"idea":{"en":"Claimed only to know that he knew nothing, and spent his life questioning Athenians about justice, courage and the good. He wrote nothing; his thought reaches us mainly through Plato’s dialogues, so the historical Socrates is hard to separate from Plato’s character.","zh":"自称唯一知道的是自己一无所知，一生都在向雅典人追问正义、勇敢与善。他没有留下任何著作，其思想主要通过柏拉图的对话录传下来，因此历史上的苏格拉底很难与柏拉图笔下的人物区分开。"},"detail":{"en":"Socrates spent almost his whole life in Athens, the son of a stonemason and a midwife. He fought as a hoplite in the Peloponnesian War, held no regular job and spent his days questioning people in the marketplace. He wrote nothing: we know him through Plato’s dialogues, Xenophon’s memoirs and Aristophanes’ comic portrait, which disagree, so the historical Socrates is partly a reconstruction. In 399 BCE, after Athens’ defeat and a period of oligarchic terror involving some of his former associates, he was tried for impiety and corrupting the young and executed with hemlock.\n\nHis method was the elenchus: ask someone what courage or justice is, then show that their answer conflicts with other things they believe. The point was not to win but to expose false confidence. Behind it lay convictions: that virtue is a kind of knowledge, that no one does wrong willingly, and, as Plato’s Apology has him say, that the unexamined life is not worth living. Nearly every later Greek school claimed him as its ancestor.","zh":"苏格拉底一生几乎都在雅典度过，父亲是石匠，母亲是接生婆。他在伯罗奔尼撒战争中当过重装步兵，没有固定职业，整日在广场上向人发问。他没有留下著作：我们通过柏拉图的对话、色诺芬的回忆录和阿里斯托芬的喜剧来认识他，而三者说法不一，历史上的苏格拉底因此部分出于重构。公元前399年，雅典战败、又经历了寡头恐怖统治（他的一些旧识卷入其中）之后，他以不敬神和败坏青年的罪名受审，被判饮毒芹汁而死。\n\n他的方法是“诘问法”：问对方什么是勇敢、什么是正义，再指出对方的回答与他其他的信念相互矛盾。目的不是辩赢，而是揭穿虚妄的自信。方法背后有几条信念：德性是一种知识；没有人自愿作恶；以及柏拉图《申辩篇》中他所说的，“未经省察的人生不值得过”。此后几乎每一个希腊学派都奉他为先祖。"},"schools":["socratic"],"works":[],"wiki":"Socrates","c":"Socrates Louvre.jpg","portrait":{"w":600,"h":800,"v":"1c96ce774e","author":"Copy of Lysippos (?)","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":null,"page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Socrates_Louvre.jpg"}},
  {"id":"democritus","tradition":"greek","born":-460,"died":-370,"life":{"en":"c. 460–370 BCE","zh":"约公元前460–前370年"},"name":{"en":"Democritus","zh":"德谟克利特"},"role":{"en":"The “laughing philosopher” of Abdera, who developed atomism","zh":"阿布德拉的“欢笑哲学家”，发展了原子论"},"idea":{"en":"Developed atomism into a full account of nature, perception and the soul: sense qualities exist “by convention”, while only atoms and void exist “in reality”. In ethics he prized cheerfulness (euthymia), a calm and well-ordered soul.","zh":"把原子论发展为对自然、感知与灵魂的完整解释：感官性质只是“约定”，“实际上”只有原子和虚空。在伦理上，他推崇“愉悦”（euthymia）——一种平静而有序的灵魂状态。"},"detail":{"en":"Democritus of Abdera, in northern Greece, was a younger contemporary of Socrates and reportedly lived to a great age. Ancient lists credit him with some seventy works on physics, mathematics, music, ethics and more, making him one of antiquity’s most prolific writers; only fragments and reports survive, many of them ethical maxims. Later legend called him the “laughing philosopher”, amused by human folly.\n\nHe took over Leucippus’ atoms and void and built them into a complete account of the world, including perception and the soul. Atoms differ only in shape, arrangement and position; colours, tastes and warmth are the ways atoms affect us: “by convention sweet, by convention bitter… in reality atoms and void.” This made him an early sceptic about the senses, though he still trusted reason. In ethics he prized euthymia, cheerfulness, a calm soul that avoids excess. Epicurus revived his physics, and early modern scientists such as Gassendi and Boyle revived it again.","zh":"阿布德拉的德谟克利特是苏格拉底年轻一辈的同时代人，据说十分长寿。古代书目把约七十部著作归于他的名下，涉及物理、数学、音乐、伦理等领域，使他成为古代最多产的作者之一；如今只剩残篇与转述，其中许多是伦理格言。后世传说称他为“笑的哲学家”，笑看人间的愚蠢。\n\n他接过留基伯的原子与虚空，把它们发展成对世界的完整解释，包括感知与灵魂。原子之间只有形状、排列与位置的差别；颜色、味道与冷热，只是原子作用于我们的方式：“依约定为甜，依约定为苦……实际上只有原子与虚空。”这使他较早怀疑感官，却依然信任理性。在伦理上，他推崇“愉悦”（euthymia），即避免过度的平和心灵。伊壁鸠鲁复兴了他的物理学，近代早期的伽桑狄与波义耳又再次复兴了它。"},"schools":["atomism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Fragments on ethics and nature","zh":"伦理与自然残篇"},"y":null}],"wiki":"Democritus"},
  {"id":"antisthenes","tradition":"greek","born":-446,"died":-366,"life":{"en":"c. 446–366 BCE","zh":"约公元前446–前366年"},"name":{"en":"Antisthenes","zh":"安提斯泰尼"},"role":{"en":"Devoted follower of Socrates, later seen as the Cynics’ founder","zh":"苏格拉底的忠实追随者，后被视为犬儒派的开创者"},"idea":{"en":"Taught that virtue can be taught and is sufficient for happiness, needing nothing but “Socratic strength”. He prized toil and self-sufficiency over pleasure. His many writings are lost.","zh":"主张德性可以传授，且足以带来幸福，所需的只是“苏格拉底式的力量”。他重视辛劳与自足，而非快乐。他的大量著作均已失传。"},"detail":{"en":"Antisthenes was an Athenian, son of a citizen father and a Thracian mother, who first studied rhetoric with Gorgias and then became one of Socrates’ most devoted companions; Plato names him among those present at Socrates’ death. He went on teaching in Athens, reportedly at the Cynosarges gymnasium, and wrote many works on ethics, language and Homer, all now lost.\n\nWhat struck him in Socrates was less the questioning than the character: poverty, endurance and indifference to opinion. Antisthenes taught that virtue can be taught, that it is enough for happiness, and that it needs nothing beyond “Socratic strength”. Pleasure was suspect; toil (ponos) was good, because it trains the soul. Later Greeks made him the first Cynic and the teacher of Diogenes, but that lineage looks like a later construction; Plato and Xenophon never call him a “dog”. Through it, though, his ideas passed to the Stoics.","zh":"安提斯泰尼是雅典人，父亲是公民，母亲是色雷斯人。他起初随高尔吉亚学习修辞，后来成为苏格拉底最忠诚的同伴之一；柏拉图在记述苏格拉底之死时提到他在场。此后他在雅典讲学，据说地点在库诺萨格斯体育场，写过许多关于伦理、语言与荷马的著作，都已失传。\n\n苏格拉底打动他的，与其说是诘问，不如说是品格：清贫、坚韧，对他人的看法毫不在意。安提斯泰尼教导说，德性可以传授，德性足以带来幸福，除了“苏格拉底式的力量”之外别无所求。快乐值得警惕；劳苦（ponos）则是好的，因为它锤炼灵魂。后来的希腊人把他奉为第一位犬儒、第欧根尼的老师，但这条师承看来是后人构建的，柏拉图与色诺芬都从未称他为“狗”。不过，他的思想正是经由这条线索传到了斯多葛派。"},"schools":["cynicism"],"works":[],"wiki":"Antisthenes"},
  {"id":"plato","tradition":"greek","born":-428,"died":-348,"life":{"en":"c. 428–348 BCE","zh":"约公元前428–前348年"},"name":{"en":"Plato","zh":"柏拉图"},"role":{"en":"Socrates’ student, founder of the Academy, master of the dialogue","zh":"苏格拉底的学生，学园的创办者，对话体的大师"},"idea":{"en":"Argued that true knowledge is of eternal Forms, above all the Form of the Good, and that a just soul and a just city both need reason to rule. He wrote dialogues, so his own views must be read through his characters.","zh":"认为真正的知识是关于永恒理念的知识，首先是“善”的理念；正义的灵魂与正义的城邦都要求理性居于统治地位。他以对话体写作，因此他本人的观点需要透过人物去解读。"},"detail":{"en":"Plato was born into an aristocratic Athenian family around 428 BCE and grew up during the Peloponnesian War. He expected a political career, but the oligarchy of 404, which included his relatives, and then the execution of Socrates in 399 turned him away from Athenian politics. After travels that took him to Sicily, he founded the Academy around 387, where Aristotle studied for twenty years. All his known works survive, nearly all as dialogues in which he himself never speaks.\n\nHis central idea is that the things we see are unstable copies, and that knowledge is of something stable: Forms, such as Justice itself or Equality itself, grasped by reason rather than the senses. The Republic’s cave image shows people mistaking shadows for reality, and the philosopher as the one who turns around. The soul, akin to the Forms, is immortal, and a just person is one whose reason rules spirit and appetite; a just city mirrors that order. Nearly every later philosophy has defined itself for or against Plato.","zh":"柏拉图约公元前428年生于雅典的贵族家庭，在伯罗奔尼撒战争中长大。他本打算从政，但公元前404年的寡头政权（其中有他的亲戚）以及前399年苏格拉底被处死，使他远离了雅典政治。他游历四方，曾到过西西里，约前387年创立学园，亚里士多德在那里学习了二十年。他已知的著作全部传世，几乎都是对话，而他本人从不在对话中发言。\n\n他的核心思想是：我们看到的事物只是不稳定的摹本，知识的对象则是稳定的东西，即“理念”，例如正义本身、相等本身，它们由理性而非感官把握。《理想国》的洞穴之喻描写人们把影子当作实在，而哲学家就是那个转过身来的人。灵魂与理念同类，因而不朽；正义的人是理性统领激情与欲望的人，正义的城邦则映照出同样的秩序。此后几乎每一种哲学，都在赞成或反对柏拉图之中确立自己。"},"schools":["platonism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Republic","zh":"《理想国》"},"y":-375},{"t":{"en":"Symposium","zh":"《会饮篇》"},"y":-385},{"t":{"en":"Phaedo","zh":"《斐多篇》"},"y":-380}],"wiki":"Plato","c":"Plato Silanion Musei Capitolini MC1377.png","portrait":{"w":600,"h":900,"v":"2d6bb0e08f","author":"© Marie-Lan Nguyen / Wikimedia Commons","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":null,"page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Plato_Silanion_Musei_Capitolini_MC1377.png"}},
  {"id":"diogenes","tradition":"greek","born":-412,"died":-323,"life":{"en":"c. 412–323 BCE","zh":"约公元前412–前323年"},"name":{"en":"Diogenes of Sinope","zh":"锡诺普的第欧根尼"},"role":{"en":"Exile from Sinope who lived as a “dog” in Athens","zh":"来自锡诺普的流亡者，在雅典像“狗”一样生活"},"idea":{"en":"Lived out Cynic self-sufficiency in public, sleeping in a jar and, according to anecdote, telling Alexander the Great to stand out of his sunlight. Asked where he came from, he reportedly answered, “I am a citizen of the world.”","zh":"在大庭广众之下践行犬儒式的自足：睡在陶瓮里；据轶事记载，他曾让亚历山大大帝“别挡住我的阳光”。有人问他来自哪里，据说他回答：“我是世界公民。”"},"detail":{"en":"Diogenes was born in Sinope on the Black Sea and left it in disgrace, probably after a scandal over the city’s coinage in which his father, a money-changer, was implicated. He spent most of his life in Athens and Corinth, living in public as a beggar, sleeping in a large storage jar (pithos) and owning almost nothing. Writings were later attributed to him, but none survives; he is known through anecdotes collected centuries later, above all by Diogenes Laertius.\n\nThe anecdotes are a philosophy acted out. Nature, not convention, sets what we need, and very little is needed: seeing a child drink from its hands, he threw away his cup. Shame and status are human inventions, so he ate, argued and slept in the street. Asked where he came from, he said he was a citizen of the world (kosmopolitēs). He called his mission “defacing the currency”, an echo of the Sinope scandal and a programme: strip away false values. The Stoics took up his self-sufficiency and cosmopolitanism, in milder form.","zh":"第欧根尼生于黑海之滨的锡诺普，后来声名扫地地离开故乡，起因大概是一桩涉及城邦钱币的丑闻，他那位当银钱兑换商的父亲也牵涉其中。他一生大部分时间在雅典和科林斯度过，以乞丐的身份公开生活，睡在一只大陶瓮（pithos）里，几乎一无所有。后世有著作被归于他的名下，但都没有传世；我们是通过几个世纪后收集的轶事认识他的，主要见于第欧根尼·拉尔修。\n\n这些轶事是一种演出来的哲学。决定我们需要什么的，是自然而不是习俗，而需要的东西少之又少：看到一个孩子用手捧水喝，他就扔掉了自己的杯子。羞耻与身份是人为的发明，所以他在街头吃饭、辩论、睡觉。有人问他从哪里来，他说自己是“世界公民”（kosmopolitēs）。他把自己的使命称为“改铸货币”，这既呼应锡诺普的丑闻，也是一个纲领：剥去虚假的价值。斯多葛派以较温和的方式继承了他的自足与世界主义。"},"schools":["cynicism"],"works":[],"wiki":"Diogenes"},
  {"id":"aristotle","tradition":"greek","born":-384,"died":-322,"life":{"en":"384–322 BCE","zh":"公元前384–前322年"},"name":{"en":"Aristotle","zh":"亚里士多德"},"role":{"en":"Plato’s student, Alexander’s tutor, founder of the Lyceum","zh":"柏拉图的学生，亚历山大的老师，吕克昂学园的创办者"},"idea":{"en":"Brought Plato’s Forms down into things and founded formal logic, biology and the systematic study of ethics and politics. He held that humans are rational, political animals whose good is a life of virtuous activity.","zh":"把柏拉图的理念拉回事物之中，创立了形式逻辑、生物学，以及对伦理与政治的系统研究。他认为人是理性的、政治的动物，人的善在于一生合乎德性的活动。"},"detail":{"en":"Aristotle was born in 384 BCE in Stagira, in northern Greece, the son of a court doctor to the Macedonian king. At seventeen he joined Plato’s Academy and stayed twenty years. After Plato’s death he did research on Lesbos, especially in marine biology, and tutored the young Alexander the Great. In 335 he founded the Lyceum in Athens; after Alexander’s death, anti-Macedonian feeling drove him to Chalcis, where he died in 322. His surviving works are mostly lecture treatises; his polished dialogues are lost.\n\nHe kept Plato’s demand that knowledge be of what is universal but placed forms in things: a horse’s form is its structure and way of life, not a separate Form elsewhere. To understand anything, ask four questions: what is it made of, what is its form, what produced it, and what is it for. He invented formal logic with the syllogism, founded zoology, and argued that human happiness is a life of activity in accordance with virtue within a political community. For medieval Islamic, Jewish and Christian thinkers he was simply “the Philosopher”.","zh":"亚里士多德公元前384年生于希腊北部的斯塔吉拉，父亲是马其顿国王的宫廷医生。他十七岁进入柏拉图的学园，一待就是二十年。柏拉图死后，他在莱斯博斯岛从事研究，尤其是海洋生物学，又做过少年亚历山大大帝的老师。公元前335年，他在雅典创办吕克昂学园；亚历山大死后，雅典的反马其顿情绪迫使他避居卡尔基斯，前322年卒于该地。他传世的著作大多是讲课稿，那些文笔优美的对话已经失传。\n\n他保留了柏拉图“知识必须关乎普遍者”的要求，却把形式放回事物之中：一匹马的形式就是它的结构和生活方式，而不是别处另有的一个理念。要理解任何事物，就问四个问题：它由什么构成？它的形式是什么？是什么产生了它？它为了什么？他以三段论开创了形式逻辑，奠定了动物学，并论证人的幸福在于在城邦共同体中合乎德性的活动。对中世纪的伊斯兰、犹太与基督教思想家来说，他就是“那位哲学家”。"},"schools":["aristotelianism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Nicomachean Ethics","zh":"《尼各马可伦理学》"},"y":-340},{"t":{"en":"Metaphysics","zh":"《形而上学》"},"y":-330},{"t":{"en":"Politics","zh":"《政治学》"},"y":-330}],"wiki":"Aristotle","c":"Aristotle Altemps Inv8575.jpg","portrait":{"w":600,"h":804,"v":"9d0c967f4b","author":"After Lysippos","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":null,"page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aristotle_Altemps_Inv8575.jpg"}},
  {"id":"theophrastus","tradition":"greek","born":-371,"died":-287,"life":{"en":"c. 371–287 BCE","zh":"约公元前371–前287年"},"name":{"en":"Theophrastus","zh":"泰奥弗拉斯托斯"},"role":{"en":"Aristotle’s successor at the Lyceum and founder of botany","zh":"亚里士多德在吕克昂的继任者，植物学的奠基人"},"idea":{"en":"Extended Aristotle’s research programme to plants, stones and human types, while probing weak points in his teacher’s account of purpose in nature. His Characters sketches thirty kinds of flawed person.","zh":"把亚里士多德的研究纲领推广到植物、矿石与人的类型，同时探查老师关于自然目的之说的薄弱之处。他的《人物志》刻画了三十种有缺陷的人。"},"detail":{"en":"Theophrastus came from Eresus on Lesbos and probably met Aristotle there; he followed him to Athens and, when Aristotle left in 323/322 BCE, took over the Lyceum, which he led for some thirty-five years. Ancient sources say he had about two thousand students and wrote more than two hundred works. His botanical books survive almost complete, along with the short Characters, an essay on metaphysics and works on stones and the senses.\n\nHe carried Aristotle’s research programme into new fields, classifying some five hundred plants by their parts, growth and uses, which earned him the title “father of botany”. He was also a loyal critic: in his Metaphysics he asked whether purpose really runs through all of nature, noting that much seems to happen by chance. The Characters, thirty sketches of flawed types such as the Flatterer and the Penny-pincher, became a model for European character writing from La Bruyère on.","zh":"泰奥弗拉斯托斯来自莱斯博斯岛的埃雷索斯，大概就是在那里结识了亚里士多德；他随亚里士多德来到雅典，并在亚里士多德于公元前323/322年离开后接掌吕克昂学园，主持约三十五年。古代资料说他有约两千名学生，著作两百余种。他的植物学著作几乎完整传世，此外还有短小的《品格论》、一篇论形而上学的文章，以及论石头和论感觉的著作。\n\n他把亚里士多德的研究纲领推向新的领域，按照植物的部位、生长方式与用途，对约五百种植物作了分类，因而被称为“植物学之父”。他也是一位忠诚的批评者：在《形而上学》中，他追问目的是否真的贯穿整个自然，并指出许多事情看来出于偶然。《品格论》用三十篇速写勾勒出谄媚者、吝啬鬼等有缺陷的人物类型，从拉布吕耶尔开始，成为欧洲性格小品的范本。"},"schools":["aristotelianism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Enquiry into Plants","zh":"《植物志》"},"y":-300},{"t":{"en":"Characters","zh":"《人物志》"},"y":-319}],"wiki":"Theophrastus"},
  {"id":"pyrrho","tradition":"greek","born":-360,"died":-270,"life":{"en":"c. 360–270 BCE","zh":"约公元前360–前270年"},"name":{"en":"Pyrrho","zh":"皮浪"},"role":{"en":"Founder of Pyrrhonian skepticism, who went to India with Alexander","zh":"皮浪怀疑主义的创始人，曾随亚历山大远征印度"},"idea":{"en":"Taught, according to his pupil Timon, that things are indifferent, unmeasurable and undecidable, so we should be without opinions and unshaken. Ancient sources say he met Indian “naked sages”; whether this shaped his views is debated.","zh":"据其学生蒂蒙记载，他教导说事物无差别、不可衡量、不可判定，因此我们应当不持意见、不为所动。古代资料说他见过印度的“裸体智者”，这是否影响了他的思想，学界仍有争议。"},"detail":{"en":"Pyrrho of Elis started out as a painter, then studied with Anaxarchus, a follower of Democritus, and travelled with him in Alexander’s army as far as India, where the ancient sources say they met “naked sages”. Back in Elis he lived quietly and was so respected that the city made him high priest. He wrote nothing; what we know comes mainly from his pupil Timon, preserved in a later summary.\n\nAccording to Timon, Pyrrho taught that things are “indifferent, unmeasurable and undecidable”, so neither our perceptions nor our opinions are true or false. The right response is to be without opinions, uninclined and unshaken, saying of each thing that it no more is than is not. What follows is first speechlessness, then freedom from disturbance (ataraxia). Whether this describes the world itself or only our inability to know it is debated, as is any Indian influence. Centuries later his name was given to the sceptical movement that Sextus Empiricus recorded.","zh":"埃利斯的皮浪起初是画家，后来随德谟克利特的追随者阿那克萨库斯学习，并与他一同随亚历山大的军队远至印度；古代资料说他们在那里见到了“裸体智者”。回到埃利斯后他过着平静的生活，备受敬重，城邦甚至推举他为大祭司。他没有著作；我们的了解主要来自他的学生蒂蒙，经由后世的一段概述保存下来。\n\n据蒂蒙记载，皮浪教导说事物“无差别、不可衡量、不可判定”，因此我们的感知与意见都无所谓真假。正确的态度是不持意见、不偏不倚、不为所动，对每一件事物都说它“存在并不多于不存在”。由此先是无言，继而得到心灵的宁静（ataraxia）。这究竟是在描述世界本身，还是只在说我们无力认识世界，学界仍有争论，是否受过印度影响亦然。几个世纪后，塞克斯都·恩披里柯所记录的怀疑论运动，便以他的名字命名。"},"schools":["skepticism"],"works":[],"wiki":"Pyrrho"},
  {"id":"epicurus","tradition":"greek","born":-341,"died":-270,"life":{"en":"341–270 BCE","zh":"公元前341–前270年"},"name":{"en":"Epicurus","zh":"伊壁鸠鲁"},"role":{"en":"Founder of the Garden, a philosophical community near Athens","zh":"“花园”学派的创始人，在雅典郊外建立哲学团体"},"idea":{"en":"Taught that the good life is tranquil pleasure, reached by understanding that the world is atoms and void, that the gods do not punish, and that death is nothing to us. He valued friendship and simple food over fame and luxury.","zh":"教导美好的生活在于宁静的快乐：要明白世界由原子与虚空构成，神不会惩罚人，死亡与我们无关。他看重友谊与粗茶淡饭，而非名声与奢华。"},"detail":{"en":"Epicurus was born in 341 BCE to Athenian settlers on Samos. As a young man he studied with Nausiphanes, a Democritean, though he later insisted he was self-taught. After teaching in Mytilene and Lampsacus he moved to Athens around 306 and bought a house and garden that gave his school its name. The Garden admitted women and slaves, lived simply and honoured him almost as a saviour. Of his many books, three letters and the Principal Doctrines survive through Diogenes Laertius, with more sayings in a Vatican manuscript.\n\nHis aim was peace of mind. We fear the gods and death, he argued, because we misunderstand the world. In fact everything is atoms and void; the gods exist but are untroubled and do not punish; the soul is made of atoms and disperses at death, so “death is nothing to us”: where death is, we are not. Pleasure is the good, but the best pleasure is quiet, the absence of pain in the body and trouble in the soul, which simple food, friendship and reflection secure.","zh":"伊壁鸠鲁公元前341年生于萨摩斯岛的雅典移民家庭。年轻时他随德谟克利特派的瑙西芬尼学习，后来却坚称自己是自学成才。他先后在米蒂利尼和兰普萨库斯讲学，约前306年迁居雅典，买下一座带花园的房子，学派由此得名“花园”。花园接纳妇女与奴隶，生活简朴，门人几乎把他当作救主来敬奉。他著述甚多，如今只有三封书信和《主要学说》经由第欧根尼·拉尔修传世，另有一组格言保存在梵蒂冈的一部抄本中。\n\n他的目标是心灵的安宁。他论证说，我们畏惧神明与死亡，是因为误解了世界。事实上万物都是原子与虚空；神确实存在，却无忧无虑，不会降罚；灵魂由原子构成，死时便消散，所以“死亡与我们无关”：死亡在时，我们已不在。快乐就是善，但最好的快乐是宁静的，即身体无痛苦、灵魂无纷扰，这靠简单的饮食、友谊与思考就能获得。"},"schools":["epicureanism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Letter to Menoeceus","zh":"《致美诺寇的信》"},"y":-300},{"t":{"en":"Principal Doctrines","zh":"《基本要道》"},"y":-300}],"wiki":"Epicurus","c":"Epikouros BM 1843.jpg","portrait":{"w":600,"h":900,"v":"ec40e88ed2","author":"Unknown artist","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":null,"page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Epikouros_BM_1843.jpg"}},
  {"id":"zeno-citium","tradition":"greek","born":-334,"died":-262,"life":{"en":"c. 334–262 BCE","zh":"约公元前334–前262年"},"name":{"en":"Zeno of Citium","zh":"季蒂昂的芝诺"},"role":{"en":"Merchant from Cyprus who founded Stoicism in Athens","zh":"来自塞浦路斯的商人，在雅典创立斯多葛学派"},"idea":{"en":"Taught that happiness is a smoothly flowing life in agreement with nature, which is the same as living virtuously. His lost Republic imagined a community of the wise without temples, law courts or money.","zh":"教导幸福是一种与自然相一致、顺畅流动的生活，这与合乎德性的生活是同一回事。他已失传的《国家篇》设想了一个没有神庙、法庭与货币的智者共同体。"},"detail":{"en":"Zeno came from Citium on Cyprus, a city with a large Phoenician population, and is said to have been a merchant. By ancient anecdote he arrived in Athens after a shipwreck, read Xenophon’s memoir of Socrates in a bookshop and asked where such men could be found; the bookseller pointed to the Cynic Crates. He also studied with Stilpo and the Platonist Polemo, and around 300 BCE began teaching in the Painted Porch (Stoa Poikilē), which gave the Stoics their name. None of his books survives.\n\nZeno joined Cynic ethics to a full theory of the world. Virtue is the only good and vice the only evil; health, wealth and reputation are “indifferent”, though some are naturally preferred. The goal is to live in agreement with nature, which is rational and divine throughout, so living rationally and living virtuously come to the same thing. He divided philosophy into logic, physics and ethics, a scheme later Stoics kept. His lost Republic imagined a community of the wise with no temples, courts or coinage.","zh":"芝诺来自塞浦路斯的季蒂昂，那是一座腓尼基人众多的城市，据说他原是商人。按照古代轶事，他因海难来到雅典，在书店里读到色诺芬记述苏格拉底的回忆录，便问哪里能找到这样的人，书商指了指正好路过的犬儒克拉特斯。他还师从斯提尔波和柏拉图派的波勒蒙，约公元前300年开始在“画廊”（Stoa Poikilē）讲学，斯多葛派由此得名。他的著作无一传世。\n\n芝诺把犬儒的伦理学与一套完整的世界理论结合起来。德性是唯一的善，恶是唯一的恶；健康、财富与名声都“无关善恶”，只不过其中一些天然地更值得优先选择。人生的目标是与自然一致地生活，而自然处处是理性的、神圣的，所以理性地生活与有德地生活是一回事。他把哲学分为逻辑学、物理学与伦理学，这一划分为后来的斯多葛派所沿用。他已佚的《理想国》设想了一个由智者组成的共同体，没有神庙、法庭与货币。"},"schools":["stoicism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Republic (lost)","zh":"《国家篇》（已佚）"},"y":-300}],"wiki":"Zeno of Citium"},
  {"id":"chrysippus","tradition":"greek","born":-279,"died":-206,"life":{"en":"c. 279–206 BCE","zh":"约公元前279–前206年"},"name":{"en":"Chrysippus","zh":"克吕西波"},"role":{"en":"Third head of the Stoa, its great systematiser and logician","zh":"斯多葛学派第三任领袖，该派伟大的体系化者与逻辑学家"},"idea":{"en":"Built Stoicism into a rigorous system, including an early propositional logic and a theory of fate compatible with moral responsibility. Ancients said, “Without Chrysippus there would be no Stoa.” Of his reported 700-plus books, only fragments survive.","zh":"把斯多葛主义构建成严密的体系，包括早期的命题逻辑，以及一种与道德责任相容的命运论。古人说：“没有克吕西波，就没有斯多葛。”据说他写了七百多卷书，如今只剩残篇。"},"detail":{"en":"Chrysippus came from Soli in Cilicia, on the south coast of Asia Minor, and moved to Athens, where he studied with Cleanthes, Zeno’s successor, and probably also with the sceptics of the Academy. Around 230 BCE he became the third head of the Stoa and rebuilt its doctrines against the Academics’ attacks. Ancient sources credit him with more than 700 books; none survives whole, and we know him through quotations, often by opponents.\n\nHis greatest achievement was logic. Where Aristotle’s logic dealt with terms such as “all men”, Chrysippus worked out a logic of whole propositions joined by “if”, “and” and “or”, with rules such as: if the first, then the second; the first; therefore the second. It was largely forgotten until modern logicians recognised it. He also defended fate: everything happens through a chain of causes, yet our assent depends on our own character, as a cylinder rolls because it is pushed but rolls because of its shape. “Without Chrysippus there would be no Stoa,” the ancients said.","zh":"克吕西波来自小亚细亚南岸奇里乞亚的索利，后来移居雅典，师从芝诺的继承人克莱安塞，大概也向学园中的怀疑派学习过。约公元前230年，他成为斯多葛学派的第三任领袖，针对学园派的攻击重建了学派的学说。古代资料说他写了七百多卷书，没有一部完整传世，我们是通过引文认识他的，而引用者常常是他的论敌。\n\n他最大的成就是逻辑。亚里士多德的逻辑处理的是“所有人”这样的词项，克吕西波则发展出一种以完整命题为单位、用“如果”“并且”“或者”连接的逻辑，其中有这样的规则：如果第一，那么第二；第一；所以第二。这套逻辑长期被遗忘，直到现代逻辑学家才重新认识到它的价值。他还为命运辩护：万事都出于一条因果链，但我们是否认可，仍取决于自身的品格，就像圆筒滚动是因为被推了一把，而它之所以“滚”，却是因为自己的形状。古人说：“没有克吕西波，就没有斯多葛。”"},"schools":["stoicism"],"works":[],"wiki":"Chrysippus"},
  {"id":"cicero","tradition":"greek","born":-106,"died":-43,"life":{"en":"106–43 BCE","zh":"公元前106–前43年"},"name":{"en":"Cicero","zh":"西塞罗"},"role":{"en":"Roman orator and statesman who brought Greek philosophy into Latin","zh":"罗马演说家与政治家，把希腊哲学带入拉丁语世界"},"idea":{"en":"A follower of the sceptical Academy, he weighed Stoic, Epicurean and Academic views against each other and accepted what seemed most probable. He coined much of the Latin philosophical vocabulary Europe would use for centuries.","zh":"作为怀疑论学园派的追随者，他把斯多葛派、伊壁鸠鲁派与学园派的观点相互权衡，接受看起来最可信的部分。他创造了大量拉丁哲学词汇，欧洲沿用了许多个世纪。"},"detail":{"en":"Marcus Tullius Cicero, a provincial from Arpinum, rose by his oratory to the consulship in 63 BCE, when he suppressed the Catilinarian conspiracy. As a young man he had studied philosophy in Athens and Rhodes with Academics, Stoics and Epicureans. Sidelined under Caesar’s dictatorship and grieving his daughter Tullia, he wrote most of his philosophical works in a burst in 45–44 BCE. After Caesar’s murder he attacked Mark Antony and was killed on Antony’s orders in 43.\n\nCicero belonged to the sceptical Academy: rather than claiming certainty, he set out each school’s arguments and accepted what seemed most probable, leaving the reader free to judge. His dialogues on the gods, the highest good, duties, old age and friendship gave Latin a vocabulary for philosophy, coining words such as qualitas. For over a thousand years they were how Western Europe learned Greek philosophy; Augustine said that reading Cicero’s lost Hortensius first turned him toward wisdom.","zh":"马库斯·图利乌斯·西塞罗是来自阿尔皮努姆的外省人，凭借口才一路升至公元前63年的执政官，并在任内镇压了喀提林阴谋。年轻时他曾在雅典与罗德岛随学园派、斯多葛派和伊壁鸠鲁派学习哲学。凯撒独裁时期他被排挤出政坛，又痛失爱女图利娅，于是在公元前45至前44年间集中写下了他大部分的哲学著作。凯撒遇刺后，他抨击马克·安东尼，前43年被安东尼派人杀害。\n\n西塞罗属于怀疑派的学园：他不自称确知，而是把各派的论证摆出来，接受看来最可能的那一种，把判断留给读者。他关于神性、至善、义务、老年与友谊的对话，为拉丁语建立了一套哲学词汇，像qualitas（性质）这样的词就是他造的。一千多年间，西欧正是通过这些著作认识希腊哲学的；奥古斯丁说，读了西塞罗已佚的《荷尔顿西乌斯》，他才第一次转向对智慧的追求。"},"schools":["skepticism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Academica","zh":"《学园派》"},"y":-45},{"t":{"en":"On the Nature of the Gods","zh":"《论神性》"},"y":-45},{"t":{"en":"On Duties","zh":"《论义务》"},"y":-44}],"wiki":"Cicero"},
  {"id":"lucretius","tradition":"greek","born":-99,"died":-55,"life":{"en":"c. 99–55 BCE","zh":"约公元前99–前55年"},"name":{"en":"Lucretius","zh":"卢克莱修"},"role":{"en":"Roman poet who set Epicurus’ philosophy in verse","zh":"以诗歌阐述伊壁鸠鲁哲学的罗马诗人"},"idea":{"en":"His Latin poem explains atoms, the “swerve” that allows free will, the mortality of the soul and the natural origin of civilisation, to free readers from fear of the gods and of death. Its rediscovery in 1417 helped spark early modern science.","zh":"他的拉丁长诗阐述了原子、使自由意志成为可能的“偏斜”、灵魂的可朽以及文明的自然起源，以帮助读者摆脱对神和死亡的恐惧。这部诗于1417年被重新发现，助燃了早期现代科学。"},"detail":{"en":"Almost nothing reliable is known about Titus Lucretius Carus, except that he wrote one long poem in the last years of the Roman Republic, dedicated to the aristocrat Memmius and read by Cicero by 54 BCE. Jerome’s late story that a love potion drove him mad and he took his own life is generally distrusted. The poem, On the Nature of Things, survived in only a few manuscripts; Poggio Bracciolini found one in a German monastery in 1417.\n\nIt sets out Epicurus’ philosophy in six books of Latin hexameters, to free readers from fear of the gods and of death. It explains atoms and void; the “swerve” that lets atoms, and so minds, break the chain of fate; the soul’s death with the body; and the natural rise of language, technology and society without divine help. Its vivid images, such as dust motes dancing in a sunbeam as a sign of unseen atoms in motion, made it a source for Renaissance and early modern scientists and freethinkers.","zh":"关于提图斯·卢克莱修·卡鲁斯，几乎没有可靠的生平记载，只知道他在罗马共和国末年写了一部长诗，献给贵族梅米乌斯，西塞罗在公元前54年已经读过。哲罗姆晚出的说法称他因服下春药而发疯、最后自尽，一般不被采信。这部《物性论》只在少数抄本中流传下来；1417年，波焦·布拉乔利尼在德意志的一座修道院里找到了其中一部。\n\n全诗用六卷拉丁六步格诗阐述伊壁鸠鲁的哲学，目的是把读者从对神与死亡的恐惧中解放出来。它讲解原子与虚空；讲解“偏斜”如何让原子、进而让心灵挣脱命运的锁链；讲解灵魂与身体一同死亡；讲解语言、技术与社会如何不借神助而自然兴起。诗中的意象十分生动，例如阳光中飞舞的尘埃，被用来证明看不见的原子在运动。这使它成为文艺复兴和近代早期科学家与自由思想者的思想源泉。"},"schools":["epicureanism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"On the Nature of Things","zh":"《物性论》"},"y":-55}],"wiki":"Lucretius"},
  {"id":"seneca","tradition":"greek","born":-4,"died":65,"life":{"en":"c. 4 BCE – 65 CE","zh":"约公元前4年–公元65年"},"name":{"en":"Seneca","zh":"塞涅卡"},"role":{"en":"Roman statesman, playwright and tutor to Nero","zh":"罗马政治家、剧作家，尼禄的老师"},"idea":{"en":"Wrote practical Stoic ethics on anger, grief and the use of time, insisting that philosophy is training for life, not cleverness. Ordered by Nero to kill himself, he died, by ancient accounts, with Stoic composure.","zh":"撰写关于愤怒、悲伤与时间运用的实践性斯多葛伦理，坚持哲学是为生活而进行的训练，而非卖弄聪明。他被尼禄下令自尽；据古代记载，他死时保持着斯多葛式的从容。"},"detail":{"en":"Lucius Annaeus Seneca was born in Córdoba in Spain around 4 BCE and raised in Rome, where he became a famous orator and writer. Exiled to Corsica under Claudius, he was recalled in 49 CE to tutor the young Nero and, once Nero was emperor, was for some years among the most powerful and richest men in Rome, which critics then and since have held against him. He withdrew from court in 62, and in 65 was ordered to kill himself after a conspiracy against Nero.\n\nSeneca wrote Stoicism for daily use: essays on anger, grief, mercy and the shortness of life, and above all the Letters to Lucilius. Their message is that philosophy is training of the mind, not a display of cleverness. We suffer more in imagination than in reality; time is our only true possession, so do not waste it; and since fortune can take anything external, rehearse its blows in advance. He quoted Epicurus freely where he found him right. His tragedies also shaped Renaissance drama.","zh":"卢基乌斯·安奈乌斯·塞涅卡约公元前4年生于西班牙的科尔多瓦，在罗马长大，成为著名的演说家与作家。克劳狄乌斯在位时他被流放到科西嘉岛，公元49年被召回担任少年尼禄的老师；尼禄即位后，他有几年是罗马最有权势、也最富有的人之一，这一点从当时到后世一直为批评者所诟病。他在62年退出宫廷，65年因一桩反尼禄的阴谋被勒令自尽。\n\n塞涅卡写的是供日常使用的斯多葛哲学：论愤怒、论悲伤、论仁慈、论生命之短暂的文章，尤其是《致卢基里乌斯书信集》。它们传达的是：哲学是对心灵的训练，而不是卖弄聪明。我们在想象中受的苦多于在现实中；时间是我们唯一真正拥有的东西，不要虚掷；既然命运能夺走一切外物，就预先演练它的打击。他觉得伊壁鸠鲁说得对的地方，也会大方引用。他的悲剧同样影响了文艺复兴时期的戏剧。"},"schools":["stoicism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Letters to Lucilius","zh":"《致鲁基里乌斯的道德书简》"},"y":65},{"t":{"en":"On the Shortness of Life","zh":"《论生命之短暂》"},"y":49}],"wiki":"Seneca the Younger"},
  {"id":"epictetus","tradition":"greek","born":50,"died":135,"life":{"en":"c. 50–135","zh":"约50–135年"},"name":{"en":"Epictetus","zh":"爱比克泰德"},"role":{"en":"Freed slave who taught Stoicism at Nicopolis","zh":"获释的奴隶，在尼科波利斯讲授斯多葛哲学"},"idea":{"en":"Taught that some things are up to us, namely our judgements, desires and choices, and everything else is not; we are disturbed not by events but by our opinions about them. He wrote nothing himself; his student Arrian recorded his lectures.","zh":"教导有些事取决于我们——我们的判断、欲望与选择——其余一切都不取决于我们；困扰人的不是事情本身，而是我们对事情的看法。他本人没有写作，其讲课由学生阿里安记录下来。"},"detail":{"en":"Epictetus was born a slave in Hierapolis in Phrygia around 50 CE and served in Rome under Epaphroditus, a freedman of Nero’s household, who let him study with the Stoic Musonius Rufus. Once freed, he taught in Rome until Domitian expelled the philosophers around 93, then opened a school at Nicopolis in western Greece, where he taught until his death. He was lame, perhaps from mistreatment. He wrote nothing; his student Arrian took notes that became the Discourses and the short handbook, the Enchiridion.\n\nHis teaching starts from one distinction: some things are up to us, namely our judgements, desires and choices, and everything else is not, including body, property, reputation and office. Unhappiness comes from treating what is not up to us as if it were. “It is not things that disturb us, but our judgements about things”: if you are insulted, the harm lies in your own judgement. For a former slave, freedom was therefore inner. The Enchiridion was adapted for Christian monks, admired in the Renaissance and drawn on by modern cognitive therapy.","zh":"爱比克泰德约公元50年生于弗里吉亚的希拉波利斯，生来就是奴隶。他在罗马为尼禄宫中的被释奴埃帕弗罗狄图斯为奴，主人允许他随斯多葛派的穆索尼乌斯·鲁弗斯学习。获得自由后，他在罗马讲学，约公元93年图密善驱逐哲学家，他便到希腊西部的尼科波利斯开办学校，一直教到去世。他跛足，可能是受虐所致。他没有留下著作；学生阿里安记下了他的讲话，编成《论说集》和简短的《道德手册》。\n\n他的教导始于一个区分：有些事取决于我们，即我们的判断、欲望与选择；其余的一切都不取决于我们，包括身体、财产、名声与职位。不幸来自把不取决于我们的事当作取决于我们的事。“使人不安的不是事物本身，而是人们对事物的看法”：如果你受到侮辱，伤害就在于你自己的判断。因此，对这位曾经的奴隶来说，自由是内在的。《道德手册》曾被改编给基督教修士使用，在文艺复兴时期备受推崇，今天的认知疗法也从中汲取思想。"},"schools":["stoicism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Discourses (recorded by Arrian)","zh":"《论说集》（阿里安记录）"},"y":108},{"t":{"en":"Enchiridion","zh":"《道德手册》"},"y":125}],"wiki":"Epictetus"},
  {"id":"marcus-aurelius","tradition":"greek","born":121,"died":180,"life":{"en":"121–180","zh":"121–180年"},"name":{"en":"Marcus Aurelius","zh":"马可·奥勒留"},"role":{"en":"Roman emperor who wrote Stoic notes to himself","zh":"罗马皇帝，写下给自己的斯多葛式札记"},"idea":{"en":"In private notes, partly written on military campaigns, he reminded himself to accept fate, do his duty to the human community and remember that everything is fleeting. They show Stoicism as daily self-examination rather than theory.","zh":"他在部分写于军旅途中的私人札记里提醒自己：接受命运，履行对人类共同体的责任，记住一切都转瞬即逝。这些札记展示的斯多葛主义是一种日常的自我省察，而非一套理论。"},"detail":{"en":"Marcus Aurelius was adopted into the imperial succession as a youth and ruled Rome from 161 to 180 CE, spending much of his reign fighting Germanic peoples on the Danube frontier while the Antonine Plague ravaged the empire. His teacher Junius Rusticus introduced him to Epictetus’ lectures, and he treated Stoicism as a guide for his own conduct rather than an academic subject.\n\nThe Meditations, twelve books of notes written in Greek “to himself”, partly in army camps, were never meant for publication. Again and again he reminds himself of a few Stoic thoughts: everything is fleeting and soon forgotten; whatever happens is part of a rational whole and can be accepted; others’ wrongs cannot harm his character unless he lets them; and his task is to act justly for the common good, since all rational beings share in one reason. The book shows Stoicism as daily self-examination, and it has found readers ever since it was first printed in the 16th century.","zh":"马可·奥勒留少年时被收养进入帝位继承序列，公元161年至180年统治罗马，在位期间大部分时间都在多瑙河边境与日耳曼诸族作战，而安东尼瘟疫正肆虐整个帝国。他的老师尤尼乌斯·鲁斯提库斯引他读到爱比克泰德的讲录；对他来说，斯多葛主义是规范自身行为的指南，而不是学院里的科目。\n\n《沉思录》是他用希腊文写给“自己”的十二卷札记，部分写于军营之中，本不打算公之于世。他一再提醒自己几条斯多葛的道理：一切转瞬即逝、很快就被遗忘；发生的事都是一个理性整体的一部分，可以坦然接受；别人的过错伤害不了他的品格，除非他自己允许；他的职责是为共同的利益而正直行事，因为一切理性存在者分有同一个理性。这本书展现的斯多葛主义是日常的自我省察，自16世纪首次付印以来一直拥有读者。"},"schools":["stoicism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Meditations","zh":"《沉思录》"},"y":175}],"wiki":"Marcus Aurelius"},
  {"id":"sextus-empiricus","tradition":"greek","born":160,"died":210,"life":{"en":"fl. 2nd–3rd c. CE","zh":"活跃于公元2–3世纪"},"name":{"en":"Sextus Empiricus","zh":"塞克斯都·恩披里柯"},"role":{"en":"Physician whose books are our main source for ancient skepticism","zh":"医生，其著作是我们了解古代怀疑主义的主要来源"},"idea":{"en":"Collected the “modes” that lead to suspension of judgement, such as conflicting appearances and the problem that every proof needs a further proof. He presented skepticism not as a doctrine but as an ability and a way of life.","zh":"汇集了导向悬置判断的各种“论式”，例如现象相互冲突、每个证明都需要进一步证明等。他把怀疑主义呈现为一种能力和生活方式，而非一种学说。"},"detail":{"en":"Almost nothing is known of Sextus Empiricus’ life, not even his city or exact century: he lived some time between about 100 and the early 3rd century CE. His surname shows he was a doctor of the Empiric school, which relied on observation rather than theories about hidden causes. His surviving books, the Outlines of Pyrrhonism and the longer Against the Mathematicians (that is, the learned), are our fullest source for ancient scepticism, and much of what we know of Stoic and other Hellenistic theories comes from his attacks on them.\n\nSextus presents scepticism not as a doctrine but as an ability: to set appearances and arguments against each other so evenly that judgement is suspended, after which tranquillity follows “as a shadow follows a body”. His toolkit includes the Ten Modes (things appear differently to different animals, people, senses and circumstances) and the Five Modes of Agrippa, among them the regress: every proof needs a further proof. Printed in Latin in the 1560s, his books set off the early modern sceptical crisis that Montaigne explored and Descartes tried to answer.","zh":"塞克斯都·恩披里柯的生平几乎无从知晓，连他的城市和确切的世纪都不清楚，只知道他生活在约公元100年至3世纪初之间。他的姓氏表明他是医学“经验派”的医生，这一派依靠观察，而不依靠关于隐秘原因的理论。他传世的《皮浪学说概要》和篇幅更长的《反数理学家》（意为“反对学者”），是古代怀疑论最完整的资料；我们对斯多葛派及其他希腊化理论的许多了解，也来自他对它们的攻击。\n\n塞克斯都说，怀疑论不是一种学说，而是一种能力：把种种现象与论证彼此对立起来，对立得如此均衡，以至判断被悬置，随后宁静便“如影随形”而来。他的工具包括“十式”（同一事物对不同的动物、人、感官与情境显现得不同），以及阿格里帕的“五式”，其中之一便是无穷后退：每个证明都需要另一个证明。16世纪60年代，他的书以拉丁文出版，引发了近代早期的怀疑论危机，蒙田探索过它，笛卡尔则试图回应它。"},"schools":["skepticism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Outlines of Pyrrhonism","zh":"《皮浪学说概要》"},"y":200},{"t":{"en":"Against the Mathematicians","zh":"《反数学家》"},"y":200}],"wiki":"Sextus Empiricus"},
  {"id":"plotinus","tradition":"greek","born":204,"died":270,"life":{"en":"c. 204–270","zh":"约204–270年"},"name":{"en":"Plotinus","zh":"普罗提诺"},"role":{"en":"Founder of Neoplatonism, teaching in Rome","zh":"新柏拉图主义的创始人，在罗马讲学"},"idea":{"en":"Described reality as emanating from the One through Intellect and Soul, and the philosophical life as the soul’s inward ascent to union with its source. Porphyry reports that he experienced such union several times.","zh":"把实在描述为从太一经由理智与灵魂流溢而出，把哲学生活视为灵魂向内上升、与其源头合一的过程。据波菲利记载，他曾数次体验到这种合一。"},"detail":{"en":"Plotinus was born in Egypt around 204 CE and at twenty-seven found his teacher, Ammonius Saccas, in Alexandria, studying with him for eleven years. He joined Emperor Gordian’s campaign against Persia hoping to learn Persian and Indian wisdom, but the expedition failed. From 244 he taught in Rome, attracting senators and the emperor Gallienus. He began writing only around fifty; his pupil Porphyry arranged the fifty-four treatises into six groups of nine, the Enneads.\n\nPlotinus saw reality as overflowing from a single source, the One, beyond being and thought, as light pours from the sun without the sun losing anything. From it comes Intellect, the realm of Plato’s Forms thinking itself, and from Intellect comes Soul, which animates the visible world; matter, at the far edge, is almost nothing. The human soul belongs to every level, and philosophy is the journey inward and upward to the One, ending in a union beyond words; Porphyry says Plotinus reached it four times while he knew him. Augustine and medieval mystics drew on this picture.","zh":"普罗提诺约公元204年生于埃及，二十七岁时在亚历山大里亚找到了自己的老师阿摩尼乌斯·萨卡斯，随他学习了十一年。他曾加入戈尔迪安皇帝征讨波斯的远征，希望借此学习波斯与印度的智慧，但远征失败了。从244年起，他在罗马讲学，吸引了元老们乃至加里恩努斯皇帝。他年近五十才开始写作；学生波菲利把他的五十四篇论文编为六组、每组九篇，即《九章集》。\n\n普罗提诺认为，实在从一个唯一的源头流溢而出，那就是“太一”，它超越存在与思想，正如光从太阳倾泻而出，太阳却毫无损耗。从太一产生“理智”，即柏拉图诸理念思考自身的领域；从理智又产生“灵魂”，赋予可见世界以生命；处在最边缘的质料，几乎是虚无。人的灵魂贯通所有层次，哲学就是向内、向上回归太一的旅程，终点是一种无法言说的合一；波菲利说，他与普罗提诺相处期间，普罗提诺曾四次达到这种境界。奥古斯丁和中世纪的神秘主义者都借鉴了这一图景。"},"schools":["neoplatonism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Enneads (edited by Porphyry)","zh":"《九章集》（波菲利编订）"},"y":270}],"wiki":"Plotinus"},
  {"id":"porphyry","tradition":"greek","born":234,"died":305,"life":{"en":"c. 234–305","zh":"约234–305年"},"name":{"en":"Porphyry","zh":"波菲利"},"role":{"en":"Plotinus’ student, editor and biographer","zh":"普罗提诺的学生、编者与传记作者"},"idea":{"en":"Edited Plotinus’ Enneads and wrote the Isagoge, an introduction to Aristotle’s logic that posed the medieval problem of universals: do genera and species really exist? He also wrote a polemic against the Christians that was later burned.","zh":"编订了普罗提诺的《九章集》，并写了《导论》——一部亚里士多德逻辑学入门，它提出了中世纪的“共相问题”：属和种真的存在吗？他还写过一部反对基督徒的论战著作，后被焚毁。"},"detail":{"en":"Porphyry was born in Tyre, in Phoenicia, around 234 CE, with the Semitic name Malchus (“king”). He studied in Athens with Longinus, then in 263 joined Plotinus’ circle in Rome. After a bout of depression he left for Sicily on Plotinus’ advice, and after his master’s death he edited the treatises as the Enneads, with a Life of Plotinus. He wrote prolifically on philosophy, religion, Homer and vegetarianism.\n\nHis most lasting work is the Isagoge, a short introduction to Aristotle’s logic. In it he asks, and deliberately leaves open, whether genera and species, such as “animal” and “human”, exist in reality or only in thought, and, if real, whether they are bodily and separate from things. Translated into Latin by Boethius, this aside launched the medieval problem of universals. He also wrote Against the Christians, a learned critique of scripture that Christian emperors ordered burned; it survives only in fragments. Through him Aristotle’s logic became a standard part of a Platonist education.","zh":"波菲利约公元234年生于腓尼基的推罗，闪族名字叫马尔库斯（意为“王”）。他先在雅典随朗吉努斯学习，263年加入罗马的普罗提诺学圈。一度陷入抑郁后，他听从普罗提诺的建议前往西西里；老师去世后，他把老师的论文编成《九章集》，并写了《普罗提诺传》。他著述极丰，涉及哲学、宗教、荷马与素食。\n\n他影响最深远的著作是《导论》（Isagoge），一部简短的亚里士多德逻辑入门。书中他提出、却有意不作回答这样的问题：“动物”“人”这样的属与种，是真实存在，还是只存在于思想中？如果真实存在，它们是有形的吗？是否与事物相分离？经波爱修斯译成拉丁文后，这段题外话开启了中世纪的共相问题之争。他还写过《驳基督徒》，对基督教经典作了博学的批评，后来被基督教皇帝下令焚毁，只有残篇传世。经由他，亚里士多德的逻辑成为柏拉图派教育的标准内容。"},"schools":["neoplatonism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Isagoge","zh":"《导论》"},"y":270},{"t":{"en":"Life of Plotinus","zh":"《普罗提诺传》"},"y":301}],"wiki":"Porphyry (philosopher)"},
  {"id":"hypatia","tradition":"greek","born":360,"died":415,"life":{"en":"born c. 350–370; died 415","zh":"约生于350–370年，卒于415年"},"name":{"en":"Hypatia","zh":"希帕提娅"},"role":{"en":"Mathematician and Neoplatonist teacher in Alexandria","zh":"亚历山大里亚的数学家与新柏拉图主义教师"},"idea":{"en":"Taught Platonic philosophy, astronomy and mathematics to pagan and Christian students alike and was a respected public figure. She was murdered by a Christian mob in 415 amid a political feud; none of her writings survives with certainty.","zh":"向异教徒与基督徒学生讲授柏拉图哲学、天文学与数学，是受人尊敬的公众人物。415年，在一场政治纷争中，她被一群基督徒暴民杀害；她的著作没有一部能确定地流传下来。"},"detail":{"en":"Hypatia was the daughter of Theon, a mathematician and astronomer in Alexandria; her birth year is unknown, perhaps between 350 and 370. She worked with her father on editions and commentaries on Ptolemy and Euclid, and led a Neoplatonic circle that taught philosophy, astronomy and mathematics to pagans and Christians alike. Her student Synesius, later a bishop, wrote her admiring letters that survive; none of her own writings does with certainty.\n\nShe taught a Platonism in the line of Plotinus, in which mathematics trains the mind to rise from the senses toward the intelligible, and she was a public figure consulted by the city’s officials. That role cost her her life. In March 415, amid a bitter feud between the prefect Orestes, her friend, and the bishop Cyril, a Christian mob led by a man named Peter seized and killed her. Her death has since been made a symbol of many things: the end of pagan learning, religious violence, and women in science.","zh":"希帕提娅是亚历山大里亚数学家、天文学家席翁的女儿，出生年份不详，大约在350至370年之间。她与父亲一同校订、注释托勒密与欧几里得的著作，并领导一个新柏拉图主义学圈，向异教徒和基督徒一视同仁地讲授哲学、天文与数学。她的学生、后来成为主教的叙涅修斯给她写过许多满怀敬意的书信，这些书信传世至今；而她本人的著作，没有一部能确定流传了下来。\n\n她讲授的是普罗提诺一脉的柏拉图主义，在其中，数学训练心灵从感官上升到可知的领域；她也是城中官员会去请教的公众人物。这个角色让她付出了生命。415年3月，在她的朋友、总督俄瑞斯忒斯与主教西里尔激烈相争之际，一群由名叫彼得的人带领的基督徒暴民抓住并杀害了她。她的死此后被赋予多种象征意义：异教学术的终结、宗教暴力，以及科学中的女性。"},"schools":["neoplatonism"],"works":[],"wiki":"Hypatia"},
  {"id":"proclus","tradition":"greek","born":412,"died":485,"life":{"en":"412–485","zh":"412–485年"},"name":{"en":"Proclus","zh":"普罗克洛"},"role":{"en":"Head of the Platonic school in Athens and Neoplatonism’s great systematiser","zh":"雅典柏拉图学派的领袖，新柏拉图主义伟大的体系化者"},"idea":{"en":"Organised Neoplatonism into a deductive system of 211 propositions, in which every effect remains in, proceeds from and returns to its cause. Through Pseudo-Dionysius and the Arabic Book of Causes he shaped medieval theology.","zh":"把新柏拉图主义整理成由211个命题构成的演绎体系，其中每个结果都“停留于”、“出离于”并“回归于”它的原因。经由伪狄奥尼修斯和阿拉伯文的《原因之书》，他塑造了中世纪神学。"},"detail":{"en":"Proclus was born in Constantinople in 412, grew up in Lycia and studied in Alexandria before moving to Athens, where he studied under Plutarch of Athens and Syrianus at the refounded Platonic school. He became its head around 437 and led it for nearly fifty years, until his death in 485. His biographer Marinus describes a life of ascetic discipline, prayer to many gods and immense productivity: commentaries on Plato and on Euclid, hymns and systematic treatises.\n\nProclus organised Neoplatonism into a precise, almost geometrical system. His Elements of Theology sets out 211 propositions with proofs, built around one pattern: every effect remains in its cause, proceeds from it and returns to it. Between the One and the world he placed many intermediate levels, such as henads, intellects and souls, so that everything is linked by graded steps. Through the Christian writer Pseudo-Dionysius and the Arabic Book of Causes, long attributed to Aristotle, his system shaped medieval theology in East and West.","zh":"普罗克洛412年生于君士坦丁堡，在吕基亚长大，曾在亚历山大里亚求学，后来到雅典，在重建的柏拉图派学校中师从雅典的普鲁塔克和叙利亚努斯。约437年他成为这所学校的领袖，主持近五十年，直到485年去世。他的传记作者马里努斯描写了他的一生：苦修自律，向众神祈祷，著述极为丰富，包括对柏拉图和欧几里得的注释、颂诗以及系统性的论著。\n\n普罗克洛把新柏拉图主义整理成一个精确的、近乎几何式的体系。他的《神学原本》列出211条命题并一一证明，全都围绕一个模式展开：每一个结果都停留于它的原因之中，从原因中发出，又回归原因。在太一与世界之间，他安排了许多中间层级，例如“一元”、诸理智与诸灵魂，使万物都由逐级的阶梯联系起来。通过基督教作家伪狄奥尼修斯和长期被归于亚里士多德的阿拉伯文《原因之书》，他的体系塑造了东西方中世纪的神学。"},"schools":["neoplatonism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Elements of Theology","zh":"《神学要素》"},"y":450},{"t":{"en":"Platonic Theology","zh":"《柏拉图神学》"},"y":460}],"wiki":"Proclus"},
  {"id":"al-kindi","tradition":"islamic","born":801,"died":873,"life":{"en":"c. 801–873","zh":"约801–873年"},"name":{"en":"Al-Kindī","zh":"铿迪"},"role":{"en":"Baghdad polymath called “the philosopher of the Arabs”","zh":"巴格达的博学家，被称为“阿拉伯人的哲学家”"},"idea":{"en":"Truth should be welcomed whatever its source, even foreign nations; philosophy and prophecy agree, and the world was created in time by the One, the true cause.","zh":"无论真理来自何处，哪怕来自异族，都应当接受；哲学与先知启示相一致，世界由真正的原因“太一”在时间中创造。"},"detail":{"en":"Abū Yūsuf al-Kindī was born around 800, probably in Kufa, into the noble Arab tribe of Kinda, and worked in Baghdad at the height of the translation movement that brought Greek science into Arabic. He tutored a son of the caliph al-Muʿtaṣim, to whom he dedicated his best-known treatise, and oversaw a circle of translators that produced Arabic versions of Aristotle’s Metaphysics and the paraphrase of Plotinus known as the Theology of Aristotle. He fell from favour under a later caliph and died after 866, probably in the early 870s.\n\nHe was the first to write philosophy in Arabic in his own name, and he had to argue that it was legitimate: truth, he wrote, should be welcomed even when it comes from distant peoples. On First Philosophy argues that an actual infinite is impossible, so the world cannot be eternal and must have been created; its cause is the True One, beyond every multiplicity. Unlike later falāsifa, he saw no conflict with the Qur’an.","zh":"阿布·优素福·金迪约生于800年，大概出生在库法，属于阿拉伯贵族部落金达。他在巴格达工作，那时正值把希腊科学译成阿拉伯文的翻译运动的高峰。他做过哈里发穆阿台绥姆一个儿子的老师，并把自己最著名的论著献给这位哈里发；他还主持一个翻译圈子，产生了亚里士多德《形而上学》的阿拉伯文译本，以及被称为《亚里士多德神学》的普罗提诺改写本。后来的哈里发时期他失宠，卒于866年之后，大约在870年代初。\n\n他是第一位以自己名义用阿拉伯文写哲学的人，因而必须先论证哲学的正当性：他写道，真理即使来自遥远的民族，也应当欢迎。《论第一哲学》论证实无限是不可能的，所以世界不可能永恒，必定是被创造的；它的原因是超越一切多样性的“真正的一”。与后来的哲学家不同，他认为这与《古兰经》并无冲突。"},"schools":["falsafa"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"On First Philosophy","zh":"《论第一哲学》"},"y":840}],"wiki":"Al-Kindi"},
  {"id":"al-farabi","tradition":"islamic","born":870,"died":950,"life":{"en":"c. 870–950","zh":"约870–950年"},"name":{"en":"Al-Fārābī","zh":"法拉比"},"role":{"en":"Logician called the “Second Teacher” after Aristotle","zh":"继亚里士多德之后被称为“第二导师”的逻辑学家"},"idea":{"en":"The ideal city is ruled by a philosopher-prophet who grasps the truth by reason and conveys it to the people in images; religion is philosophy expressed in symbols.","zh":"理想城邦由哲学家兼先知统治，他以理性把握真理，再以形象传达给民众；宗教是以象征表达的哲学。"},"detail":{"en":"Abū Naṣr al-Fārābī was born around 870, probably in Fārāb in Central Asia, though whether his family was Turkic or Persian is disputed. He studied logic in Baghdad with Christian scholars of the Aristotelian school, lived there for decades, and spent his last years in Aleppo and Damascus, where he died in 950 or 951. Later tradition called him the “Second Teacher”, after Aristotle.\n\nHe set out to rebuild the whole of philosophy in Arabic: logic as the grammar of all languages, a classification of the sciences, and a cosmology in which intellects emanate from the First Cause down to the active intellect that lights up human minds. His Virtuous City joins this to Plato’s Republic. A city is virtuous when it aims at true happiness, and its ruler must be a philosopher who also has the prophet’s gift of turning truths into images and laws that ordinary people can follow. Religion, on this view, is philosophy in symbolic form. Avicenna said he understood Aristotle’s Metaphysics only through al-Fārābī.","zh":"阿布·纳斯尔·法拉比约生于870年，大概出生在中亚的法拉布，其家族是突厥人还是波斯人尚有争议。他在巴格达跟随亚里士多德学派的基督徒学者学习逻辑，在那里生活了几十年，晚年住在阿勒颇和大马士革，950或951年卒于大马士革。后世称他为仅次于亚里士多德的“第二导师”。\n\n他要用阿拉伯文重建整个哲学：把逻辑看作一切语言共通的语法，为各门学问分类，并构建一套宇宙论——诸理智从第一因层层流出，直到照亮人类心灵的“主动理智”。《德性城邦》把这套体系与柏拉图的《理想国》结合起来：一个城邦若以真正的幸福为目标，便是有德性的；它的统治者必须是哲学家，同时具有先知的天赋，能把真理化为普通人可以遵行的形象与律法。照这样看，宗教是象征形式的哲学。伊本·西那说，他是读了法拉比才读懂亚里士多德的《形而上学》。"},"schools":["falsafa"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"The Enumeration of the Sciences","zh":"《科学纲要》"},"y":930},{"t":{"en":"The Opinions of the People of the Virtuous City","zh":"《美德城邦居民意见书》"},"y":942}],"wiki":"Al-Farabi","c":"Alpharabius in Liber Chronicarum 1493 AD.png","portrait":{"w":600,"h":618,"v":"738b2b0184","author":"Mr.Nostalgic","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":null,"page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alpharabius_in_Liber_Chronicarum_1493_AD.png"}},
  {"id":"al-ashari","tradition":"islamic","born":874,"died":936,"life":{"en":"c. 874–936","zh":"约874–936年"},"name":{"en":"Abū al-Ḥasan al-Ashʿarī","zh":"艾布·哈桑·艾什尔里"},"role":{"en":"Muʿtazili theologian who broke with his school around 912","zh":"约912年脱离本派的穆尔太齐赖派神学家"},"idea":{"en":"God’s power and will are unlimited: God creates every act, humans only “acquire” them, and good and evil are what God commands and forbids. He used rational argument to defend traditional beliefs.","zh":"真主的权能与意志没有限制：真主创造一切行为，人只是“获取”它们；善恶就是真主所命令与禁止的。他用理性论证来捍卫传统信仰。"},"detail":{"en":"Abū al-Ḥasan al-Ashʿarī was born in Basra around 874 and studied under al-Jubbāʾī, the leading Muʿtazili theologian of the day. By the traditional account, around his fortieth year, after dreams of the Prophet, he publicly renounced Muʿtazili doctrine. He spent the rest of his life defending the creed of the traditionalists with the rational methods he had learned from his opponents, and died in Baghdad around 936.\n\nThe Muʿtazila held that God must act justly by standards reason can grasp, and that humans create their own acts, or reward and punishment would be unfair. Al-Ashʿarī replied that this limits God. God creates everything, including each human act; the human being “acquires” the act, which is enough for responsibility. Good and evil are what God commands and forbids, not standards above God. The Qur’an is God’s uncreated speech, and its descriptions of God are to be accepted “without asking how”. His school, Ashʿarism, became one of the two main theologies of Sunni Islam; al-Ghazālī was its most famous later member.","zh":"阿布·哈桑·艾什尔里约874年生于巴士拉，师从当时最重要的穆尔太齐赖派神学家朱巴伊。按传统说法，他约在四十岁时多次梦见先知，随后公开放弃穆尔太齐赖派教义。此后一生，他用从论敌那里学来的理性方法为传统派信条辩护，约936年卒于巴格达。\n\n穆尔太齐赖派认为，神必须按理性能理解的标准公正行事，人自己创造自己的行为，否则赏罚就不公平。艾什尔里回应说，这限制了神。神创造一切，包括人的每一个行为；人只是“获取”这个行为，这足以使人负责。善恶就是神所命令和禁止的，而不是高于神的标准。《古兰经》是神非受造的言语，经中对神的描述应当“不问如何”地接受。他的学派艾什尔里派成为逊尼派伊斯兰的两大神学之一，后来最著名的成员就是安萨里。"},"schools":["kalam"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Doctrines of the Muslims (Maqālāt al-Islāmiyyīn)","zh":"《伊斯兰教派言论集》"},"y":null},{"t":{"en":"Elucidation of the Principles of Religion (al-Ibāna)","zh":"《宗教原理阐释》"},"y":null}],"wiki":"Abu al-Hasan al-Ash'ari"},
  {"id":"ibn-sina","tradition":"islamic","born":980,"died":1037,"life":{"en":"980–1037","zh":"980–1037年"},"name":{"en":"Ibn Sīnā (Avicenna)","zh":"伊本·西那（阿维森纳）"},"role":{"en":"Persian physician-philosopher, the most influential Islamic philosopher","zh":"波斯医生兼哲学家，影响最大的伊斯兰哲学家"},"idea":{"en":"Essence and existence are distinct: knowing what a thing is does not tell you that it is, so all contingent things need a Necessary Existent. His “floating man”, suspended without any sensation, still knows that he exists.","zh":"本质与存在相区分：知道一物是什么，并不意味着知道它存在，因此一切偶然之物都需要一个必然存在者。他设想的“飘浮人”在没有任何感觉的状态下，依然知道自己存在。"},"detail":{"en":"Ibn Sīnā, known in Latin as Avicenna, was born in 980 near Bukhara, in Central Asia. A prodigy, he had mastered medicine by his late teens and was rewarded with the run of the Samanid royal library. When that dynasty fell he moved from court to court across Iran, serving as physician and sometimes vizier, writing at night and on campaigns, and once in prison. He died in Hamadan in 1037.\n\nHis Canon of Medicine was taught in Europe for five centuries, and his Book of Healing was an encyclopedia of philosophy. Its central idea is the distinction between essence and existence: you can understand what a triangle or a horse is without knowing whether any exists, so existence is not part of a thing’s essence. Everything whose existence is merely possible must get it from something else, and the chain must end in a Necessary Existent, whose essence is to exist: God. His “floating man”, created in mid-air with no sensations, would still know that he exists, so the soul knows itself directly.","zh":"伊本·西那，拉丁名阿维森纳，980年生于中亚布哈拉附近。他是神童，十几岁就精通医学，因而获准出入萨曼王朝的皇家图书馆。王朝覆灭后，他辗转于伊朗各地宫廷，担任医生，有时出任维齐尔，在夜里、在行军途中、甚至在狱中写作，1037年卒于哈马丹。\n\n他的《医典》在欧洲被讲授了五百年，《治疗论》则是一部哲学百科全书。其核心观念是本质与存在的区分：你可以明白三角形或马是什么，却不知道是否有任何一个存在，所以存在不属于事物的本质。凡是其存在仅仅可能的东西，都必须从别的东西获得存在，这条链条必须终止于一个“必然存在者”，其本质就是存在，那就是神。他设想一个“漂浮的人”：凭空被造、悬在空中、毫无感觉，他仍然知道自己存在，所以灵魂是直接认识自身的。"},"schools":["falsafa"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"The Canon of Medicine","zh":"《医典》"},"y":1025},{"t":{"en":"The Book of Healing (al-Shifāʾ)","zh":"《治疗论》"},"y":1027},{"t":{"en":"Pointers and Reminders","zh":"《指要与诠释》"},"y":1030}],"wiki":"Avicenna","c":"Avicenna Bust, left profile (cropped).jpg","portrait":{"w":600,"h":774,"v":"887c4adcdc","author":"National Library of Medicine","license":"No restrictions","licenseUrl":"https://www.flickr.com/commons/usage/","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Avicenna_Bust,_left_profile_(cropped).jpg"}},
  {"id":"al-ghazali","tradition":"islamic","born":1058,"died":1111,"life":{"en":"c. 1058–1111","zh":"约1058–1111年"},"name":{"en":"Al-Ghazālī","zh":"安萨里"},"role":{"en":"Ashʿarite theologian who left his Baghdad chair for Sufism","zh":"辞去巴格达教席、转向苏非之道的艾什尔里派神学家"},"idea":{"en":"The philosophers fail to prove their own claims, such as the eternity of the world; fire does not burn cotton by necessity, God creates the burning. Certainty finally came to him not from argument but from Sufi experience.","zh":"哲学家们未能证明自己的主张，例如世界永恒；火并非必然地烧着棉花，是真主创造了燃烧。最终给他确定性的不是论证，而是苏非体验。"},"detail":{"en":"Abū Ḥāmid al-Ghazālī was born around 1058 in Tus, in north-eastern Iran, and rose to the most prestigious teaching post in the Islamic world, at the Niẓāmiyya college in Baghdad, in 1091. In 1095, in a crisis of faith and health that left him unable to speak, he gave up his chair, distributed his wealth and spent years as a wandering Sufi in Damascus, Jerusalem and Mecca. He returned to teach briefly and died in Tus in 1111.\n\nIn The Incoherence of the Philosophers he argued, on the philosophers’ own logical terms, that they had not proved what they claimed: that the world is eternal, that God knows only universals, that causes produce effects by necessity. When fire touches cotton, we only observe the burning following the contact; God creates the burning, and could withhold it. His autobiography, Deliverance from Error, tells how certainty finally came to him not from argument but from Sufi practice. He made mysticism respectable within Sunni orthodoxy; Ibn Rushd wrote a whole book against him.","zh":"阿布·哈米德·安萨里约1058年生于伊朗东北部的图斯，1091年登上伊斯兰世界最负盛名的教席——巴格达尼扎米亚学院。1095年，他陷入信仰与身体的双重危机，一度说不出话来，于是辞去教席、散尽财产，作为游方苏非在大马士革、耶路撒冷和麦加度过多年。他后来短暂重返讲坛，1111年卒于图斯。\n\n在《哲学家的矛盾》中，他用哲学家自己的逻辑论证：他们并没有证明自己所声称的东西——世界永恒、神只知道普遍者、原因必然产生结果。火碰到棉花时，我们只看到燃烧紧随接触而来；是神创造了燃烧，神也可以不让它燃烧。他的自传《迷途指津》讲述了确定性最终不是来自论证，而是来自苏非修行。他使神秘主义在逊尼正统中获得了地位；伊本·鲁世德专门写了一整本书反驳他。"},"schools":["kalam","sufism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"The Incoherence of the Philosophers","zh":"《哲学家的矛盾》"},"y":1095},{"t":{"en":"The Revival of the Religious Sciences","zh":"《宗教学科的复兴》"},"y":1100},{"t":{"en":"Deliverance from Error","zh":"《摆脱迷误》"},"y":1108}],"wiki":"Al-Ghazali"},
  {"id":"ibn-tufayl","tradition":"islamic","born":1105,"died":1185,"life":{"en":"c. 1105–1185","zh":"约1105–1185年"},"name":{"en":"Ibn Ṭufayl","zh":"伊本·图斐利"},"role":{"en":"Andalusian court physician and philosophical novelist","zh":"安达卢斯宫廷医生，哲理小说作者"},"idea":{"en":"In his novel, a child raised alone on an island reaches by reason and contemplation the same truths that religion teaches in symbols.","zh":"在他的小说中，一个在荒岛上独自长大的孩子，仅凭理性与沉思，就领悟了宗教以象征教导的同样真理。"},"detail":{"en":"Ibn Ṭufayl was born around 1105 near Guadix, in al-Andalus, and served the Almohad rulers as secretary and then as court physician to the caliph Abū Yaʿqūb Yūsuf in Marrakesh. He was the patron who introduced the young Ibn Rushd to the caliph, who asked for clear explanations of Aristotle. He died in Marrakesh in 1185.\n\nHis one surviving work, Ḥayy ibn Yaqẓān (“Alive, son of Awake”), is a philosophical novel. A child grows up alone on an island, raised by a gazelle. When she dies he dissects her to find what has left her body, and step by step, by observation and reasoning, he discovers the order of nature, the existence of a Creator, and finally mystical union with God. When he meets people from a nearby island, he finds that their religion teaches the same truths in images, and that most of them cannot bear the truth unveiled. The book was translated into Latin in 1671 and read by Locke’s generation.","zh":"伊本·图斐利约1105年生于安达卢斯的瓜迪克斯附近，先后为阿尔摩哈德王朝担任秘书，后在马拉喀什任哈里发阿布·雅古卜·优素福的宫廷医生。正是他把年轻的伊本·鲁世德引荐给哈里发，哈里发请后者为亚里士多德写出清晰的解说。他1185年卒于马拉喀什。\n\n他唯一传世的作品《哈义·本·叶格赞》（意为“觉醒者之子，生者”）是一部哲学小说。一个孩子独自在岛上由一只羚羊养大。羚羊死后，他解剖它，想找出离开它身体的是什么；此后他凭观察和推理一步步发现了自然的秩序、造物主的存在，最终达到与神的神秘合一。后来他遇到邻岛的人，发现他们的宗教用形象教导着同样的真理，而他们大多承受不住毫无遮掩的真理。此书1671年被译成拉丁文，在洛克那一代人中广为流传。"},"schools":["falsafa"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Ḥayy ibn Yaqẓān","zh":"《哈伊·本·叶格赞》"},"y":1175}],"wiki":"Ibn Tufail"},
  {"id":"ibn-rushd","tradition":"islamic","born":1126,"died":1198,"life":{"en":"1126–1198","zh":"1126–1198年"},"name":{"en":"Ibn Rushd (Averroes)","zh":"伊本·鲁世德（阿威罗伊）"},"role":{"en":"Córdoban judge, known in Europe simply as “the Commentator”","zh":"科尔多瓦法官，在欧洲被直接称为“评注家”"},"idea":{"en":"The Qur’an itself commands philosophical study, and where demonstration conflicts with the literal text, the text must be interpreted. He defended Aristotle against al-Ghazālī and against Avicenna’s Neoplatonic additions.","zh":"《古兰经》本身就要求人研究哲学；当证明与经文字面意义冲突时，应当对经文加以诠释。他为亚里士多德辩护，既反驳安萨里，也反对阿维森纳添加的新柏拉图主义成分。"},"detail":{"en":"Ibn Rushd, Averroes in Latin, was born in Córdoba in 1126 into a family of leading jurists. He became a judge in Seville and chief judge of Córdoba, and court physician in Marrakesh, after Ibn Ṭufayl introduced him to the Almohad caliph, who asked him to explain Aristotle clearly. Late in life he fell out of favour, was briefly exiled and his books were burned; he was soon pardoned and died in Marrakesh in 1198.\n\nHe wrote commentaries on almost all of Aristotle, short, middle and long, which made him simply “the Commentator” in Latin Europe. In The Decisive Treatise he argued, as a jurist, that the Qur’an itself commands rational reflection on creation, so philosophy is obligatory for those capable of it; where a demonstration conflicts with the literal text, the text must be interpreted. The Incoherence of the Incoherence answers al-Ghazālī point by point. His view that the material intellect is one for all humans became the centre of the “Averroist” controversy in Paris.","zh":"伊本·鲁世德，拉丁名阿威罗伊，1126年生于科尔多瓦一个显赫的法学世家。经伊本·图斐利引荐，阿尔摩哈德哈里发请他把亚里士多德讲清楚，此后他出任塞维利亚法官、科尔多瓦首席法官，以及马拉喀什的宫廷医生。晚年他失宠，被短暂流放，著作遭焚，不久获赦，1198年卒于马拉喀什。\n\n他几乎为亚里士多德的全部著作写了短、中、长三种注释，因而在拉丁欧洲被径称为“注释家”。在《决断论》中，他以法学家的身份论证：《古兰经》本身就命令人们理性地思考造化，所以有能力的人研究哲学是义务；当证明与经文字面冲突时，应当解释经文。《矛盾的矛盾》逐条反驳安萨里。他主张质料理智是全人类共有的唯一理智，这成了巴黎“阿威罗伊主义”争论的焦点。"},"schools":["falsafa"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"The Decisive Treatise","zh":"《决定性论述》"},"y":1179},{"t":{"en":"The Incoherence of the Incoherence","zh":"《矛盾的矛盾》"},"y":1180},{"t":{"en":"Long Commentary on Aristotle’s On the Soul","zh":"《亚里士多德〈论灵魂〉详注》"},"y":1190}],"wiki":"Averroes","c":"Andrea di bonaiuto, apoteosi di san tommaso d'aquino, 11 averroè.jpg","portrait":{"w":600,"h":647,"v":"d3c9c6fa92","author":"Sailko","license":"CC BY 3.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Andrea_di_bonaiuto,_apoteosi_di_san_tommaso_d%27aquino,_11_averro%C3%A8.jpg"}},
  {"id":"suhrawardi","tradition":"islamic","born":1154,"died":1191,"life":{"en":"1154–1191","zh":"1154–1191年"},"name":{"en":"Shihāb al-Dīn Suhrawardī","zh":"希哈布丁·苏赫拉瓦迪"},"role":{"en":"Persian founder of Illuminationism, executed in Aleppo","zh":"照明学派的波斯创始人，在阿勒颇被处死"},"idea":{"en":"Reality is light in degrees of intensity, from the Light of Lights down to darkness; the self knows itself by direct presence, not through definitions, which the Peripatetics wrongly relied on.","zh":"实在是强度不同的光，从“众光之光”一直到黑暗；自我凭借直接临在认识自身，而不是靠定义，逍遥学派对定义的依赖是错误的。"},"detail":{"en":"Shihāb al-Dīn Suhrawardī was born in 1154 in Suhraward, in north-western Iran, studied philosophy in Maragha and Isfahan, and travelled as a Sufi through Anatolia and Syria. In Aleppo he won the friendship of the governor, al-Malik al-Ẓāhir, a son of Saladin, but the city’s jurists accused him of heresy, and on Saladin’s orders he was put to death in 1191, in his thirties. He is often called al-Maqtūl, “the one who was killed”.\n\nTrained as an Avicennan, he came to think that the Peripatetics’ method could never reach a thing’s essence: a definition only lists features we already know. Real knowledge is “knowledge by presence”, the way the self is aware of itself without any concept in between. In The Philosophy of Illumination reality is light, graded in intensity from the Light of Lights down to the dark barriers of bodies, with lights governing each species in place of Plato’s Forms. He drew on Plato, Persian wisdom and Sufism, and his school continued in Iran to Mullā Ṣadrā.","zh":"希哈布丁·苏哈拉瓦迪1154年生于伊朗西北部的苏哈拉瓦德，在马拉盖和伊斯法罕学习哲学，后来作为苏非游历安纳托利亚和叙利亚。在阿勒颇，他赢得了萨拉丁之子、总督扎希尔的友谊，但当地法学家指控他为异端，他奉萨拉丁之命于1191年被处死，年仅三十多岁，因此常被称为“被杀者”。\n\n他出身伊本·西那学派，后来却认为逍遥学派的方法永远无法触及事物的本质：定义只是罗列我们本已知道的特征。真正的知识是“临在之知”，就像自我无需任何概念居间就能意识到自身。在《照明哲学》中，实在就是光，从“众光之光”到物体的幽暗屏障，强度层层递减，而统摄各物种的光取代了柏拉图的理念。他汲取了柏拉图、古波斯智慧和苏非主义，其学派在伊朗一直延续到穆拉·萨德拉。"},"schools":["illuminationism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"The Philosophy of Illumination (Ḥikmat al-Ishrāq)","zh":"《照明哲学》"},"y":1186}],"wiki":"Shahab al-Din Yahya ibn Habash Suhrawardi"},
  {"id":"ibn-arabi","tradition":"islamic","born":1165,"died":1240,"life":{"en":"1165–1240","zh":"1165–1240年"},"name":{"en":"Ibn ʿArabī","zh":"伊本·阿拉比"},"role":{"en":"Andalusian mystic, called “the Greatest Master” by followers","zh":"安达卢斯神秘主义者，被追随者尊为“最伟大的导师”"},"idea":{"en":"God alone truly exists; the world is God’s continual self-disclosure, and each thing shows a divine name. The “Perfect Human” is the mirror in which God’s names are fully reflected.","zh":"唯有真主真正存在；世界是真主持续不断的自我显现，每一物都显示一个神圣之名。“完人”是完整映现真主诸名的镜子。"},"detail":{"en":"Muḥyī al-Dīn Ibn ʿArabī was born in 1165 in Murcia, in al-Andalus, and grew up in Seville, where he had visionary experiences in his youth; he is said to have met the aged Ibn Rushd as a boy. Around 1200 he left the West for good, made the pilgrimage to Mecca, where he began his vast Meccan Revelations, travelled through Anatolia and Iraq, and settled in Damascus, where he died in 1240. His followers call him al-Shaykh al-Akbar, “the Greatest Master”.\n\nHis central teaching, later summed up as “the oneness of being”, is that only God truly exists. The world is God’s continual self-disclosure: each thing is a place where a divine name, the Merciful, the Knowing, the Creator, becomes visible, and creation is renewed at every instant. Human beings can mirror all the names together; the “Perfect Human”, above all the prophets, reflects God completely. His ideas shaped Sufism, Persian poetry and philosophy, Mullā Ṣadrā among them, and also provoked fierce critics such as Ibn Taymiyya.","zh":"穆希丁·伊本·阿拉比1165年生于安达卢斯的穆尔西亚，在塞维利亚长大，少年时就有异象经验，据说还曾以少年身份会见过年迈的伊本·鲁世德。约1200年他永远离开西方，到麦加朝觐，在那里开始撰写卷帙浩繁的《麦加启示》，又游历安纳托利亚和伊拉克，最后定居大马士革，1240年在那里去世。追随者称他为“最伟大的导师”。\n\n他的核心学说后来被概括为“存在单一论”：唯有神真正存在。世界是神不断的自我显现：每一物都是某个神名——至慈者、全知者、创造者——显现之处，而创造在每一瞬间都在更新。人能够同时映照所有神名；“完人”，首先是诸先知，完整地反映神。他的思想塑造了苏非主义、波斯诗歌和哲学（包括穆拉·萨德拉），也招致伊本·泰米叶等人的激烈批评。"},"schools":["sufism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"The Bezels of Wisdom (Fuṣūṣ al-Ḥikam)","zh":"《智慧的珍宝》"},"y":1229},{"t":{"en":"The Meccan Revelations","zh":"《麦加启示》"},"y":1231}],"wiki":"Ibn Arabi"},
  {"id":"rumi","tradition":"islamic","born":1207,"died":1273,"life":{"en":"1207–1273","zh":"1207–1273年"},"name":{"en":"Jalāl al-Dīn Rūmī","zh":"贾拉鲁丁·鲁米"},"role":{"en":"Persian poet and Sufi teacher in Konya","zh":"在科尼亚讲学的波斯诗人与苏非导师"},"idea":{"en":"The soul is a reed cut from its reed-bed and longs to return; love, not reason alone, is the way back to God.","zh":"灵魂像一支从芦苇丛中被割下的芦笛，渴望回到原处；通往真主的道路是爱，而不仅仅是理性。"},"detail":{"en":"Jalāl al-Dīn Rūmī was born in 1207 in Central Asia, in Balkh or nearby Vakhsh, and as a child left with his family, perhaps ahead of the Mongol invasions, for Anatolia, where his father became a preacher in Konya. Rumi succeeded him as a respected teacher of religious law. In 1244 he met Shams of Tabriz, a wandering dervish, and the friendship overturned his life: he turned to poetry, music and ecstatic dance. After Shams disappeared for good around 1248, Rumi poured out thousands of lyric poems in his name. He died in Konya in 1273, and his followers founded the Mevlevi order, known for its whirling ceremony.\n\nHis Dīwān-e Shams gathers those lyrics. His Masnavi, six books of rhymed couplets, teaches Sufi ideas through stories, jokes and parables. It opens with a reed flute complaining that it was cut from its reed-bed: the soul, separated from its source, longs to return. The way back is love, which consumes the self; reason alone, like people feeling an elephant in a dark room, grasps only parts.","zh":"贾拉鲁丁·鲁米1207年生于中亚的巴尔赫或其附近的瓦赫什，幼年随家人西迁安纳托利亚（或许是为躲避蒙古入侵），父亲在科尼亚做了布道者，鲁米后来接替父亲，成为受人尊敬的宗教法教师。1244年，他结识了游方苦行僧大不里士的沙姆斯，这段友谊颠覆了他的生活：他转向诗歌、音乐和忘我的舞蹈。约1248年沙姆斯永久消失后，鲁米以沙姆斯之名倾泻出数千首抒情诗。他1273年卒于科尼亚，弟子们创立了以旋转仪式闻名的梅夫列维教团。\n\n《沙姆斯诗集》汇集了这些抒情诗。《玛斯纳维》是六卷押韵对句，通过故事、笑话和寓言讲授苏非思想。它开篇是一支芦笛的哀诉：它被从芦苇丛中割下——灵魂与本源分离，渴望回归。回归之路是爱，爱会焚尽自我；单凭理性，就像在黑屋里摸象的人，只能摸到局部。"},"schools":["sufism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Dīwān-e Shams-e Tabrīzī","zh":"《沙姆斯诗集》"},"y":1250},{"t":{"en":"Masnavi","zh":"《玛斯纳维》"},"y":1273}],"wiki":"Rumi"},
  {"id":"ibn-khaldun","tradition":"islamic","born":1332,"died":1406,"life":{"en":"1332–1406","zh":"1332–1406年"},"name":{"en":"Ibn Khaldūn","zh":"伊本·赫勒敦"},"role":{"en":"North African historian and judge, founder of a science of society","zh":"北非历史学家与法官，社会科学的开创者"},"idea":{"en":"Dynasties rise and fall in cycles driven by ʿaṣabiyya, group solidarity, which is strong among hardy desert peoples and decays in luxurious cities. History needs a science of human society to judge which reports are even possible.","zh":"王朝以周期兴衰，推动力是“群体凝聚力”：它在坚韧的游牧民中强大，在奢华的城市中衰败。历史需要一门关于人类社会的科学，来判断哪些记载根本可能发生。"},"detail":{"en":"Ibn Khaldūn was born in Tunis in 1332 into a family of Andalusian origin. He spent his youth and middle years in the dangerous politics of North African and Granadan courts, as secretary, envoy and minister, several times imprisoned or forced to flee. In 1375 he withdrew to a castle in present-day Algeria and wrote the Muqaddimah, the “Introduction” to his universal history. He spent his last decades in Cairo as a judge and teacher, met the conqueror Timur outside Damascus in 1401, and died in 1406.\n\nHe began from a historian’s problem: chronicles repeat impossible reports because their authors do not know how societies work. So he proposed a science of human society. Its key is ʿaṣabiyya, group solidarity. Hardy nomadic peoples have it strongly; it lets them conquer and found dynasties. But in the cities, wealth and luxury dissolve it, and after about three generations the dynasty falls to a group with fresher solidarity. Modern sociologists and economists have read him as a forerunner.","zh":"伊本·赫勒敦1332年生于突尼斯，家族源自安达卢斯。他的青年和中年在北非和格拉纳达宫廷的险恶政治中度过，做过秘书、使节和大臣，数次入狱或被迫出逃。1375年他隐居到今阿尔及利亚境内的一座城堡，写下《历史绪论》，即他的世界史的“导言”。晚年数十年他在开罗任法官和教师，1401年在大马士革城外会见征服者帖木儿，1406年去世。\n\n他从史学家的问题出发：编年史反复转述不可能的事，是因为作者不懂社会如何运作。于是他提出一门关于人类社会的学问，其关键是“阿萨比亚”，即群体凝聚力。强悍的游牧民族凝聚力很强，靠它征服并建立王朝；但在城市里，财富与奢靡使凝聚力瓦解，大约三代之后，王朝就会被另一个凝聚力更新鲜的群体取代。现代社会学家和经济学家常把他读作先驱。"},"schools":[],"works":[{"t":{"en":"The Muqaddimah","zh":"《历史绪论》"},"y":1377}],"wiki":"Ibn Khaldun"},
  {"id":"mulla-sadra","tradition":"islamic","born":1571,"died":1636,"life":{"en":"c. 1571–1636","zh":"约1571–1636年"},"name":{"en":"Mullā Ṣadrā","zh":"穆拉·萨德拉"},"role":{"en":"Safavid philosopher of Shiraz, founder of Transcendent Theosophy","zh":"萨法维王朝设拉子的哲学家，超验神智学的创立者"},"idea":{"en":"Existence, not essence, is what is real; it is one reality in many degrees. Things change in their very substance, so the whole world is in constant motion toward perfection.","zh":"真实的是存在而非本质；存在是一个实在，却有许多等级。事物在实体本身中变化，因此整个世界都在不断朝向完满运动。"},"detail":{"en":"Ṣadr al-Dīn Shīrāzī, known as Mullā Ṣadrā, was born around 1571 in Shiraz, into a wealthy family, and studied in Isfahan, the Safavid capital, with Mīr Dāmād and Shaykh Bahāʾī. Attacked by jurists for his views, he withdrew for years to the village of Kahak near Qom, where he began his great Four Journeys in 1606. Later he taught at a new college in Shiraz, finishing the book there in 1628. He died in Basra in 1635–36 while travelling on pilgrimage, though a later tradition gives 1640.\n\nEarlier philosophers argued whether essence or existence is primary. Ṣadrā held that existence is what is real and essences are only the outlines our minds draw around it. Existence is one reality in degrees of intensity, like light in Suhrawardī, from God down to matter. From this he drew a startling thesis: things change not only in their qualities but in their very substance, so the whole world is in constant motion toward perfection. His synthesis still shapes philosophy teaching in Iran.","zh":"萨德尔丁·设拉子依，人称穆拉·萨德拉，约1571年生于设拉子一个富裕家庭，曾在萨法维王朝首都伊斯法罕师从米尔·达马德和谢赫·巴哈伊。他的观点遭到法学家攻击，于是隐居库姆附近的卡哈克村多年，1606年在那里开始撰写巨著《心灵四旅》。后来他在设拉子一所新学院任教，1628年在那里完成此书。他于1635–1636年在朝觐途中卒于巴士拉，后来的传统说法则作1640年。\n\n此前的哲学家争论本质与存在何者为先。萨德拉认为，存在才是实在的，本质只是我们的心灵围绕它勾画出的轮廓。存在是同一个实在，但有强度上的等级，就像苏哈拉瓦迪所说的光，从神一直到物质。由此他得出一个惊人的论点：事物不仅在性质上变化，在实体本身上也在变化，因此整个世界都在不断朝向完善运动。他的综合至今仍主导着伊朗的哲学教学。"},"schools":["illuminationism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"The Four Journeys (al-Asfār al-Arbaʿa)","zh":"《心灵四旅》"},"y":1628}],"wiki":"Mulla Sadra"},
  {"id":"augustine","tradition":"medieval","born":354,"died":430,"life":{"en":"354–430","zh":"354–430年"},"name":{"en":"Augustine of Hippo","zh":"希波的奥古斯丁"},"role":{"en":"North African bishop who fused Christianity with Platonism","zh":"把基督教与柏拉图主义融为一体的北非主教"},"idea":{"en":"Evil is a privation of good, freely chosen by a will turned away from God; we find truth by turning inward, where God illuminates the mind.","zh":"恶是善的缺乏，源于背离上帝的自由意志；我们转向内心才能找到真理，在那里上帝照亮心灵。"},"detail":{"en":"Augustine was born in 354 in Thagaste, in Roman North Africa, to a pagan father and a Christian mother, Monica. He taught rhetoric in Carthage, Rome and Milan, spent nine years among the Manichaeans, and passed through scepticism before reading Neoplatonist books and hearing Bishop Ambrose. After his conversion in 386 he returned to Africa, became bishop of Hippo in about 395 and died in 430 as the Vandals besieged the city.\n\nHis Confessions tell this search from the inside. Two ideas from it shaped the West. Evil is not a substance competing with God, as the Manichaeans held, but a lack of good: a will that turns from the highest good to lesser ones. And truth is found by turning inward: certain truths, like those of number, cannot come from the changing senses, so God must illuminate the mind. The City of God, written after the sack of Rome in 410, read all history as two loves at war.","zh":"奥古斯丁354年生于罗马治下北非的塔加斯特，父亲是异教徒，母亲莫妮卡是基督徒。他在迦太基、罗马和米兰教授修辞学，做了九年摩尼教徒，又一度倾向怀疑论，后来读到新柏拉图主义的书、听了米兰主教安布罗斯的讲道，于386年皈依。此后他回到北非，约395年任希波主教，430年在汪达尔人围城时去世。\n\n《忏悔录》从内部讲述了这段寻求。书中有两个观念深刻影响了西方：恶不是与神对抗的实体（摩尼教如此主张），而是善的缺失，是意志背离至善而转向次等的善；真理要向内寻找——像数学那样确定的真理不可能来自变动的感官，必定是神照亮了心灵。410年罗马被洗劫后写成的《上帝之城》，把整部历史读作两种爱的争战。"},"schools":["patristic"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Confessions","zh":"《忏悔录》"},"y":400},{"t":{"en":"On the Trinity","zh":"《论三位一体》"},"y":417},{"t":{"en":"The City of God","zh":"《上帝之城》"},"y":426}],"wiki":"Augustine of Hippo","c":"Saint Augustine by Philippe de Champaigne.jpg","portrait":{"w":600,"h":766,"v":"693186f2c6","author":"Philippe de Champaigne","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":null,"page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint_Augustine_by_Philippe_de_Champaigne.jpg"}},
  {"id":"boethius","tradition":"medieval","born":477,"died":524,"life":{"en":"c. 477–524 or 526","zh":"约477–524或526年"},"name":{"en":"Boethius","zh":"波爱修斯"},"role":{"en":"Roman statesman who translated Aristotle’s logic into Latin","zh":"把亚里士多德逻辑译成拉丁文的罗马政治家"},"idea":{"en":"God’s foreknowledge does not destroy free will, because God is eternal and sees all times at once, as a present. He also posed the medieval problem of universals.","zh":"上帝的预知并不取消自由意志，因为上帝是永恒的，把一切时间当作当下一并看见。他还提出了贯穿中世纪的共相问题。"},"detail":{"en":"Boethius was a Roman aristocrat born around 477–480, a generation after the last western emperor. Orphaned young, he was raised in the household of the senator Symmachus, became consul in 510 and later chief of the palace offices under the Ostrogothic king Theodoric. Accused of treason, he was imprisoned and executed around 524 or 526.\n\nHe had planned to translate all of Plato and Aristotle into Latin and show that they agreed; he finished Aristotle’s logic, with commentaries, and for six centuries this was nearly all the Aristotle the Latin West had. His commentary on Porphyry posed the question that ran through medieval philosophy: are genera and species real things or only concepts? In prison he wrote the Consolation of Philosophy, in which Philosophy, as a woman, shows him that fortune’s gifts were never his, and that God’s foreknowledge does not destroy freedom, because God sees all times in one eternal present.","zh":"波爱修是罗马贵族，约生于477至480年间，那时西罗马最后一位皇帝刚被废黜一代人。他自幼丧父，在元老西马库斯家中长大，510年任执政官，后来在东哥特国王狄奥多里克手下任宫廷总管。他被控叛国，入狱后约于524或526年被处决。\n\n他曾计划把柏拉图和亚里士多德全部译成拉丁文，并证明两人观点一致；实际完成的是亚里士多德的逻辑著作及注释，六百年间这几乎就是拉丁西方所知的全部亚里士多德。他对波菲利的注释提出了贯穿整个中世纪的问题：属和种是实在之物，还是仅仅是概念？狱中他写下《哲学的慰藉》：化身为女子的“哲学”告诉他，命运的赐予本不属于他；神的预知也不取消自由，因为神在一个永恒的当下同时看见一切时间。"},"schools":["patristic"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Commentaries on Porphyry’s Isagoge","zh":"《波菲利〈导论〉注》"},"y":510},{"t":{"en":"The Consolation of Philosophy","zh":"《哲学的慰藉》"},"y":524}],"wiki":"Boethius"},
  {"id":"saadia","tradition":"medieval","born":882,"died":942,"life":{"en":"882–942","zh":"882–942年"},"name":{"en":"Saadia Gaon","zh":"萨阿迪亚·加昂"},"role":{"en":"Egyptian-born head of the Sura academy in Babylonia","zh":"生于埃及、执掌巴比伦苏拉经学院的学者"},"idea":{"en":"There are four sources of knowledge: the senses, reason, inference and reliable tradition. Revelation teaches what reason could eventually discover, so that everyone can have the truth at once.","zh":"知识有四个来源：感官、理性、推论和可靠的传统。启示所教的，理性终究也能发现；启示的作用是让所有人立刻拥有真理。"},"detail":{"en":"Saadia ben Joseph was born in 882 in the Fayyum, in Egypt, and left as a young man for Palestine and then Babylonia (Iraq), the centre of Jewish learning. He made his name in controversy, against the Karaites, who rejected rabbinic tradition, and in a dispute over the Jewish calendar, and in 928 he became gaon, head of the ancient academy of Sura. Quarrels with the exilarch cost him the post for some years, though he was reinstated before his death in Baghdad in 942.\n\nHe translated the Bible into Arabic, wrote a Hebrew grammar and dictionary, and in The Book of Beliefs and Opinions (933) wrote the first systematic Jewish philosophy, using the methods of Muʿtazili kalām. Knowledge, he argued, comes from four sources: the senses, reason, inference, and reliable tradition. Revelation teaches the same truths that reason can reach, but at once and for everyone, so that no one has to wait a lifetime of study for them. He proved that the world was created and that its Creator is one.","zh":"萨阿迪亚·本·约瑟夫882年生于埃及法尤姆，青年时离开埃及，先到巴勒斯坦，后到当时犹太学术的中心巴比伦尼亚（伊拉克）。他在论战中成名：一方面反驳否认拉比传统的卡拉派，一方面参与关于犹太历法的争论。928年他出任历史悠久的苏拉学院院长（加昂），后来因与流亡首领（exilarch）的冲突一度失去职位，但在942年卒于巴格达之前已经复职。\n\n他把《圣经》译成阿拉伯文，编写了希伯来语法和词典，并在《信仰与见解之书》（933年）中运用穆尔太齐赖派凯拉姆的方法，写出了第一部系统的犹太哲学。他认为知识有四个来源：感官、理性、推理和可靠的传统。启示所教的真理，理性同样可以达到，但启示让所有人立即拥有它们，不必穷尽一生去研习。他论证世界是被创造的，创造者是唯一的。"},"schools":["jewish-medieval"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Arabic translation of the Torah (Tafsīr)","zh":"《托拉》阿拉伯文译注"},"y":null},{"t":{"en":"The Book of Beliefs and Opinions","zh":"《信仰与见解之书》"},"y":933}],"wiki":"Saadia Gaon"},
  {"id":"anselm","tradition":"medieval","born":1033,"died":1109,"life":{"en":"1033–1109","zh":"1033–1109年"},"name":{"en":"Anselm of Canterbury","zh":"坎特伯雷的安瑟伦"},"role":{"en":"Benedictine monk and Archbishop of Canterbury","zh":"本笃会修士，坎特伯雷大主教"},"idea":{"en":"Faith seeks understanding. God is “that than which nothing greater can be conceived”, and such a being must exist in reality, not only in the mind: the ontological argument.","zh":"信仰寻求理解。上帝是“无法设想比之更伟大者”，这样的存在者不能只存在于心灵中，必定也实际存在：这就是本体论证明。"},"detail":{"en":"Anselm was born in 1033 in Aosta, in the Alps, and crossed into Normandy to study at the abbey of Bec under the scholar Lanfranc. He became prior and then abbot there, and in 1093 succeeded Lanfranc as Archbishop of Canterbury, where quarrels with English kings over the Church’s rights sent him twice into exile. He died in 1109.\n\nHis motto was “faith seeking understanding”: he did not reason his way to belief, but tried to understand by argument what he already believed. In the Proslogion he defined God as “that than which nothing greater can be conceived”. Even a fool who denies God understands this phrase, so such a being exists at least in the mind. But a being that existed in reality as well would be greater; so if it existed only in the mind, it would not be the greatest conceivable. Therefore it exists in reality. A monk, Gaunilo, replied at once with a perfect island; Aquinas and Kant rejected the argument; Descartes and Leibniz revived it.","zh":"安瑟伦1033年生于阿尔卑斯山间的奥斯塔，后来翻山来到诺曼底，在贝克修道院师从学者兰弗朗克，先后任副院长和院长。1093年他接替兰弗朗克任坎特伯雷大主教，因教会权利问题与英格兰国王冲突，两度流亡，1109年去世。\n\n他的格言是“信仰寻求理解”：不是靠推理得出信仰，而是用论证去理解已经相信的东西。《宣讲》把神定义为“无法设想比之更伟大者”。就连否认神的愚人也懂这句话，所以这样的存在至少存在于心中；但同时存在于现实中的存在会更伟大，所以它若只存在于心中，就不是可设想的最伟大者。因此它必定在现实中存在。修士高尼罗立即以“最完美的岛屿”反驳；阿奎那和康德都拒绝这一论证，笛卡尔和莱布尼茨则重新加以阐发。"},"schools":["scholasticism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Monologion","zh":"《独白》"},"y":1076},{"t":{"en":"Proslogion","zh":"《宣讲》"},"y":1078},{"t":{"en":"Why God Became Man (Cur Deus Homo)","zh":"《上帝为何成为人》"},"y":1098}],"wiki":"Anselm of Canterbury"},
  {"id":"abelard","tradition":"medieval","born":1079,"died":1142,"life":{"en":"1079–1142","zh":"1079–1142年"},"name":{"en":"Peter Abelard","zh":"彼得·阿伯拉尔"},"role":{"en":"Paris logician famed for his dialectic and his love for Héloïse","zh":"以辩证法和与爱洛伊丝的爱情闻名的巴黎逻辑学家"},"idea":{"en":"Universals are neither things nor mere sounds but words insofar as they signify; in ethics, what makes an act sinful is the intention behind it.","zh":"共相既不是事物，也不只是声音，而是就其有所指称而言的语词；在伦理上，使行为成为罪的是背后的意图。"},"detail":{"en":"Peter Abelard was born in 1079 at Le Pallet in Brittany, the eldest son of a knight, and gave up his inheritance for logic. He studied with Roscelin and with William of Champeaux in Paris, then defeated his teachers in debate and drew crowds of students. His affair with his pupil Héloïse ended with his castration by her uncle’s men; both entered religious life, and their later letters are famous. His theology was condemned at Soissons in 1121 and, under pressure from Bernard of Clairvaux, at Sens in 1141. He died in 1142.\n\nOn universals he took a middle road: “man” is neither a thing shared by all men nor a mere sound, but a word that signifies, grounded in a real likeness between individuals. Sic et Non set contradictory authorities side by side so that students would learn to resolve them by reason, the method of later scholastic teaching. In ethics he argued that sin lies in consenting to what one knows is wrong, in intention, not in the outward deed.","zh":"彼得·阿伯拉尔1079年生于布列塔尼的勒帕莱，是骑士长子，却为了逻辑学放弃了继承权。他先后师从罗瑟林和巴黎的尚波的威廉，又在辩论中击败老师，吸引了大批学生。他与学生爱洛依丝相恋，结果被她舅父派人阉割，两人随后都进入修道生活，晚年的通信成为名篇。他的神学先于1121年在苏瓦松被定罪，1141年又在明谷的伯尔纳推动下于桑斯受谴，1142年去世。\n\n在共相问题上他走了中间道路：“人”既不是所有人共有的一个东西，也不只是一个声音，而是一个有所指的词，其根据是个体之间真实的相似。《是与否》把相互矛盾的权威并列，让学生学会用理性加以调和，这成了后来经院教学的方法。在伦理学上，他认为罪在于同意去做明知错误的事，即在于意图，而不在于外在的行为。"},"schools":["scholasticism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Sic et Non (Yes and No)","zh":"《是与否》"},"y":1121},{"t":{"en":"Ethics, or Know Thyself","zh":"《伦理学：认识你自己》"},"y":1138}],"wiki":"Peter Abelard"},
  {"id":"maimonides","tradition":"medieval","born":1138,"died":1204,"life":{"en":"1138–1204","zh":"1138–1204年"},"name":{"en":"Maimonides (Moses ben Maimon)","zh":"迈蒙尼德"},"role":{"en":"Rabbi, physician and philosopher from Córdoba, later in Cairo","zh":"生于科尔多瓦、后居开罗的拉比、医生与哲学家"},"idea":{"en":"God can only be described negatively; biblical images of God are figures for the unlearned. Reason cannot prove whether the world is eternal or created, so on that question one may follow scripture.","zh":"上帝只能以否定方式描述；《圣经》中关于上帝的形象是为未受教育者设的比喻。理性无法证明世界是永恒的还是被创造的，因此在这个问题上可以依从经典。"},"detail":{"en":"Moses Maimonides was born in Córdoba in 1138. When the Almohads ended toleration of Jews and Christians in al-Andalus, his family wandered through Spain and North Africa, settling in Fez, then briefly in Palestine, and finally in Fustat (Old Cairo) in about 1166. After his brother drowned on a trading voyage, he supported the family as a physician, eventually to the court of Saladin’s vizier, and led Egypt’s Jewish community. He died in 1204.\n\nHis Mishneh Torah arranged the whole of Jewish law in clear Hebrew. His Guide for the Perplexed, written in Arabic for a pupil torn between scripture and Aristotle, argued that the Bible’s bodily images of God are figures: of God we can only say what God is not. Where philosophy truly demonstrates something, scripture must be read to agree; but no demonstration settles whether the world is eternal or created, so one may follow scripture there. Aquinas cited him as “Rabbi Moses”; Spinoza argued against him.","zh":"摩西·迈蒙尼德1138年生于科尔多瓦。阿尔摩哈德王朝在安达卢斯终止对犹太人和基督徒的宽容后，他的家庭辗转于西班牙和北非，先定居非斯，又短暂到过巴勒斯坦，约1166年落脚福斯塔特（旧开罗）。弟弟在经商途中溺亡后，他以行医养家，后来成为萨拉丁的维齐尔的宫廷医生，并领导埃及的犹太社团，1204年去世。\n\n他的《密释纳托拉》用清晰的希伯来文把全部犹太律法系统编排。《迷途指津》用阿拉伯文写给一位在经典与亚里士多德之间无所适从的学生，主张《圣经》中描述神身体的形象都是比喻：关于神，我们只能说神不是什么。凡哲学真正证明了的，就应把经文解释得与之一致；但世界是永恒的还是被创造的，没有任何证明能够定论，所以在这个问题上可以遵从经典。阿奎那称引他为“拉比摩西”，斯宾诺莎则与他论辩。"},"schools":["jewish-medieval"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Mishneh Torah","zh":"《重述托拉》"},"y":1180},{"t":{"en":"The Guide for the Perplexed","zh":"《迷途指津》"},"y":1190}],"wiki":"Maimonides"},
  {"id":"aquinas","tradition":"medieval","born":1225,"died":1274,"life":{"en":"1225–1274","zh":"1225–1274年"},"name":{"en":"Thomas Aquinas","zh":"托马斯·阿奎那"},"role":{"en":"Dominican friar who wove Aristotle into Christian theology","zh":"把亚里士多德编织进基督教神学的多明我会修士"},"idea":{"en":"Reason and faith are two roads to one truth: reason can prove that God exists, through the “five ways”, while revelation adds what reason cannot reach. In every creature, its essence is distinct from its act of existing; only in God are they one.","zh":"理性与信仰是通往同一真理的两条道路：理性可以通过“五路论证”证明上帝存在，启示则补充理性无法企及的内容。在一切受造物中，本质与存在相区别，唯有在上帝中二者合一。"},"detail":{"en":"Thomas Aquinas was born in 1225 at Roccasecca, south of Rome, to a noble family that expected him to become abbot of Monte Cassino. Instead he joined the new Dominican order of begging friars; his brothers held him captive for about a year, but he persisted, studied under Albert the Great in Paris and Cologne, and taught in Paris, Naples and Italian priories. In December 1273 he stopped writing, saying that all he had written seemed like straw, and he died in 1274.\n\nAquinas built a synthesis in which reason and revelation are two routes to one truth. Reason, working from what the senses show, as Aristotle taught, can prove that God exists: things move and are caused, the chain cannot run back forever, so there is a first mover and first cause, the “five ways”. Revelation adds what reason cannot reach. In every creature what it is differs from the fact that it is; only in God are essence and existence the same. Partly condemned in 1277 and canonised in 1323, he later became the Catholic Church’s official philosopher.","zh":"托马斯·阿奎那1225年生于罗马以南的罗卡塞卡，贵族家庭本指望他将来做卡西诺山修道院院长，他却加入了新兴的托钵修会多明我会。兄弟们把他软禁了约一年，他仍不改初衷，后来在巴黎和科隆师从大阿尔伯特，又在巴黎、那不勒斯和意大利各修院任教。1273年12月他停笔，说自己写过的一切都像稻草，次年去世。\n\n阿奎那建立了一个综合体系：理性与启示是通往同一真理的两条路。理性从感官所见出发（如亚里士多德所教），可以证明神存在：事物在运动、被引起，这条链条不能无限回溯，所以有第一推动者和第一因，这就是“五路”。启示补充理性达不到的东西。每个受造物的“是什么”与“存在”彼此不同，只有在神那里本质与存在合一。他的部分论点1277年曾受谴责，1323年被封圣，其体系后来成为天主教会的官方哲学。"},"schools":["scholasticism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"On Being and Essence","zh":"《论存在者与本质》"},"y":1255},{"t":{"en":"Summa contra Gentiles","zh":"《反异教大全》"},"y":1265},{"t":{"en":"Summa Theologiae","zh":"《神学大全》"},"y":1273}],"wiki":"Thomas Aquinas","c":"St-thomas-aquinasFXD.jpg","portrait":{"w":600,"h":900,"v":"34de5a0a92","author":"Carlo Crivelli","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":null,"page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St-thomas-aquinasFXD.jpg"}},
  {"id":"siger","tradition":"medieval","born":1240,"died":1284,"life":{"en":"c. 1240–c. 1284 (born 1235–40, died 1281–84)","zh":"约1240–约1284年（生于1235–1240年间，卒于1281–1284年间）"},"name":{"en":"Siger of Brabant","zh":"布拉班特的西格尔"},"role":{"en":"Radical Aristotelian master at the Paris arts faculty","zh":"巴黎艺学院的激进亚里士多德主义教师"},"idea":{"en":"Following Averroes, he argued that Aristotle’s principles imply one intellect for all humans and an eternal world, while professing that faith teaches otherwise. Dante placed him in Paradise beside Aquinas, his critic.","zh":"他追随阿威罗伊，认为亚里士多德的原理意味着全人类共享一个理智、世界是永恒的，同时声明信仰的教导与此不同。但丁把他安置在天堂，与批评他的阿奎那并列。"},"detail":{"en":"Siger of Brabant was born between 1235 and 1240 in the Low Countries and taught in the arts faculty at Paris from the 1260s. He read Aristotle with the help of Averroes’ commentaries and followed the argument where it led, even against Christian doctrine. His teaching was among the targets of the bishop of Paris’s condemnations of 1270 and 1277; summoned by the inquisitor, he left Paris and died at the papal court in Orvieto in the early 1280s, reportedly killed by his secretary.\n\nSiger argued that, read correctly, Aristotle implies one intellect shared by all humans and a world without beginning, while professing that faith teaches otherwise and is true. Aquinas answered him in 1270 with On the Unity of the Intellect, insisting that it is this man who thinks, and Siger later moved partly toward Aquinas’s view. His opponents accused the “Averroists” of holding a “double truth”, which they denied. Dante nonetheless placed Siger in Paradise, introduced by Aquinas himself.","zh":"布拉班特的西格尔约生于1235至1240年间的低地国家，1260年代起在巴黎大学文学院任教。他借助阿威罗伊的注释读亚里士多德，论证走到哪里就跟到哪里，即使违背基督教教义。1270年和1277年巴黎主教的两次谴责都把他的学说列为对象；他被宗教裁判官传唤后离开巴黎，1280年代初卒于奥尔维耶托的教廷，据说是被自己的秘书杀死的。\n\n西格尔主张，正确理解的亚里士多德意味着全人类共有一个理智，世界也没有开端；同时他声明信仰的教导与此不同，而且信仰是真的。阿奎那1270年写《论理智的统一性》回应他，坚持“是这个人在思考”，西格尔后来部分转向了阿奎那的观点。论敌指责“阿威罗伊派”主张“双重真理”，他们本人则否认这一点。尽管如此，但丁仍把西格尔安排在天堂，由阿奎那亲自介绍。"},"schools":["latin-averroism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Questions on the Third Book of On the Soul","zh":"《〈论灵魂〉第三卷问题集》"},"y":1269},{"t":{"en":"On the Intellective Soul","zh":"《论理智灵魂》"},"y":1272}],"wiki":"Siger of Brabant"},
  {"id":"duns-scotus","tradition":"medieval","born":1266,"died":1308,"life":{"en":"c. 1266–1308","zh":"约1266–1308年"},"name":{"en":"John Duns Scotus","zh":"约翰·邓斯·司各脱"},"role":{"en":"Franciscan theologian, the “Subtle Doctor”","zh":"方济各会神学家，人称“精微博士”"},"idea":{"en":"“Being” means the same thing when said of God and of creatures, so we can reason about God; each individual is made itself by a unique “thisness” (haecceity), and the will is freer and higher than the intellect.","zh":"“存在”用于上帝和受造物时意义相同，因此我们能对上帝进行推理；每个个体凭借独一无二的“此性”成为它自己；意志比理智更自由、更高。"},"detail":{"en":"John Duns Scotus was born around 1266 in Duns, in the Scottish Borders, and joined the Franciscans as a youth. He studied and lectured at Oxford and Paris, was briefly expelled from Paris in 1303 for siding with the pope against King Philip IV, and was sent to Cologne in 1307, where he died in 1308, leaving his main work, the Ordinatio, unfinished. His intricate distinctions earned him the title “Subtle Doctor”.\n\nAquinas had said that words like “being” or “good” apply to God and creatures only by analogy. Scotus answered that if they meant something different in each case, our reasoning about God would equivocate; at least “being” must mean the same, univocally, or natural theology is impossible. He also asked what makes this horse this one, and answered: not its matter, but an individual “thisness” (haecceity). And he gave the will priority over the intellect, even in God. Ockham attacked his realism; Renaissance humanists mocked his followers, whence the word “dunce”.","zh":"约翰·邓斯·司各脱约1266年生于苏格兰边区的邓斯，少年时加入方济各会。他在牛津和巴黎学习与讲学，1303年因在教宗与法王腓力四世之争中站在教宗一边，一度被逐出巴黎；1307年被派往科隆，次年在那里去世，主要著作《牛津评注》未能完成。他的区分细致入微，因而被称为“精微博士”。\n\n阿奎那认为，“存在”“善”这类词用于神和受造物只是类比。司各脱反驳说：如果这些词在两处意思不同，我们关于神的推理就会偷换概念；至少“存在”必须是单义的，否则自然神学就不可能。他还追问：是什么使这匹马成为这一匹？答案不是质料，而是个体的“此性”。他也把意志置于理智之上，连在神那里也是如此。奥卡姆批评他的实在论；文艺复兴的人文主义者嘲笑他的门徒，英语中的“dunce”（笨蛋）一词即由此而来。"},"schools":["scholasticism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Ordinatio","zh":"《〈箴言集〉评注》"},"y":1300},{"t":{"en":"Treatise on the First Principle","zh":"《论第一原理》"},"y":1305}],"wiki":"Duns Scotus"},
  {"id":"ockham","tradition":"medieval","born":1287,"died":1347,"life":{"en":"c. 1287–1347","zh":"约1287–1347年"},"name":{"en":"William of Ockham","zh":"奥卡姆的威廉"},"role":{"en":"English Franciscan logician who defied the pope","zh":"敢于对抗教宗的英格兰方济各会逻辑学家"},"idea":{"en":"Only individuals exist; universals are concepts that signify many individuals. Do not posit more entities than you need, the principle later called “Ockham’s razor”.","zh":"只有个体存在；共相是指称多个个体的概念。不要设定超出需要的实体，这一原则后来被称为“奥卡姆剃刀”。"},"detail":{"en":"William of Ockham was born in late 1287 or early 1288 in Ockham, Surrey, and became a Franciscan. His lectures at Oxford on Peter Lombard’s Sentences were reported to the pope, and in 1324 he was summoned to Avignon to answer charges of heresy. There he took his order’s side in a dispute over apostolic poverty, judged Pope John XXII himself a heretic, and in 1328 fled with the Franciscan minister-general to the protection of Emperor Louis of Bavaria. He spent his last two decades in Munich writing on politics and church power, and died there in 1347.\n\nIn philosophy he was a nominalist: only individual things exist. A universal like “whiteness” is not a thing in many white objects but a concept, a natural sign in the mind that stands for many individuals. Much of the realist apparatus of his predecessors, common natures and formal distinctions, could therefore be cut away, by the principle of economy later called “Ockham’s razor”.","zh":"奥卡姆的威廉生于1287年末或1288年初，出生地是萨里郡的奥卡姆村，后来加入方济各会。他在牛津讲授彼得·伦巴德的《箴言集》，被人告到教宗那里，1324年被传唤到阿维尼翁接受异端指控审查。在那里他卷入关于“使徒贫穷”的争论，站在本会一边，认定教宗约翰二十二世本人才是异端，1328年随方济各会总会长出逃，投奔巴伐利亚的路易皇帝。此后二十年他在慕尼黑撰写政治与教权问题的著作，1347年卒于该地。\n\n在哲学上他是唯名论者：只有个体存在。“白”这样的共相不是存在于许多白色物体中的一个东西，而是一个概念，是心中代表许多个体的自然记号。因此，前人实在论中的大量设施，如“共同本性”和“形式区分”，都可以剪除，这一经济原则后来被称为“奥卡姆剃刀”。"},"schools":["nominalism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Summa Logicae","zh":"《逻辑大全》"},"y":1323},{"t":{"en":"Quodlibetal Questions","zh":"《任意论题问答》"},"y":1324}],"wiki":"William of Ockham"},
  {"id":"buridan","tradition":"medieval","born":1300,"died":1360,"life":{"en":"c. 1300–c. 1360","zh":"约1300–约1360年"},"name":{"en":"Jean Buridan","zh":"让·布里丹"},"role":{"en":"Paris arts master, logician and natural philosopher","zh":"巴黎艺学院教师，逻辑学家与自然哲学家"},"idea":{"en":"A thrown body keeps moving because of an impressed force, “impetus”, rather than the surrounding air, a step toward the idea of inertia. The paradox of the ass starving between two equal haystacks is named after him, though it is not in his writings.","zh":"被抛出的物体之所以继续运动，是因为它被赋予了一种“冲力”，而不是周围空气在推动它，这是迈向惯性概念的一步。“饿死在两堆等量干草之间的驴”这一悖论以他命名，但并不见于他的著作。"},"detail":{"en":"John Buridan was born around 1300 in or near Béthune in Picardy and spent his whole career in the arts faculty at Paris, twice serving as rector of the university. Unusually, he never joined a religious order or took a higher degree in theology: he remained a teacher of logic and natural philosophy for some forty years, and died around 1360.\n\nHe was a nominalist in Ockham’s spirit, and his textbook of logic, the Summulae, was used for generations. In physics he asked why a thrown stone keeps moving after it leaves the hand. Aristotelians said the air pushes it, but that seemed absurd. Buridan proposed that the thrower impresses on the stone a quantity of “impetus”, proportional to its speed and its quantity of matter, which lasts until air resistance and weight wear it away; in the heavens, with no resistance, it could last forever. This was not yet the law of inertia, but it was a step toward it. The famous ass between two haystacks is a later parody, not his.","zh":"让·布里丹约1300年生于皮卡第的贝蒂讷或其附近，一生都在巴黎大学文学院任教，两度出任校长。与众不同的是，他从未加入修会，也没有去攻读更高的神学学位，而是做了约四十年的逻辑和自然哲学教师，约1360年去世。\n\n他是奥卡姆式的唯名论者，所著逻辑教科书《辩证法纲要》被沿用了好几代人。在物理学上，他追问：石头离手之后为什么还会继续飞？亚里士多德派说是空气在推它，这显得很荒谬。布里丹提出，投掷者把一定量的“冲力”传给了石头，它与速度和物质的量成正比，会一直保持，直到被空气阻力和重量耗尽；在没有阻力的天上，它可以永远持续。这还不是惯性定律，却是朝它迈出的一步。著名的“两堆干草之间的驴”是后人的讽刺，并非出自他本人。"},"schools":["nominalism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Summulae de Dialectica","zh":"《辩证法纲要》"},"y":1340},{"t":{"en":"Questions on Aristotle’s Physics","zh":"《亚里士多德〈物理学〉问题集》"},"y":1350}],"wiki":"Jean Buridan"},
  {"id":"pico","tradition":"modern","born":1463,"died":1494,"life":{"en":"1463–1494","zh":"1463–1494年"},"name":{"en":"Giovanni Pico della Mirandola","zh":"皮科·德拉·米兰多拉"},"role":{"en":"Italian humanist who proclaimed humanity’s freedom to shape itself","zh":"宣告人有塑造自身之自由的意大利人文主义者"},"idea":{"en":"In his Oration, God gives Adam no fixed place in creation: humans alone can make themselves into beasts or into angels. He hoped to show that all philosophies and religions share a single hidden truth.","zh":"在他的《论人的尊严》中，上帝没有给亚当在造物中安排固定的位置：唯有人能把自己造就为禽兽或天使。他希望证明，一切哲学与宗教共享同一个隐藏的真理。"},"detail":{"en":"Giovanni Pico della Mirandola, from the family of the lords of Mirandola in northern Italy, studied canon law at Bologna and philosophy at Padua and Paris, and learned Hebrew and Aramaic to read the Kabbalah. In 1486, aged twenty-three, he published 900 theses and invited scholars to debate them in Rome. Pope Innocent VIII suspended the debate and a commission condemned thirteen theses; Pico fled to France, was briefly arrested, and settled in Florence near Marsilio Ficino’s Platonic circle. He died there in 1494, aged thirty-one.\n\nThe speech he wrote to open the debate, later called the Oration on the Dignity of Man, retells creation: every creature received a fixed place, but Adam was given none, so that he could sink to the level of the beasts or rise towards the divine by his own choice. Human dignity lies in this freedom to shape oneself. Pico also hoped to show that Plato, Aristotle, the Kabbalah and Christianity share one hidden truth. Printed after his death, the Oration became a manifesto of Renaissance humanism.","zh":"皮科出身意大利北部米兰多拉的领主家族，先后在博洛尼亚学教会法、在帕多瓦和巴黎学哲学，还学了希伯来文与阿拉姆文以研读卡巴拉。1486年，二十三岁的他发表九百条论题，邀请各地学者到罗马辩论。教宗英诺森八世叫停了辩论，审查委员会谴责其中十三条；皮科逃往法国，一度被捕，后在佛罗伦萨落脚，靠近菲奇诺的柏拉图学园，1494年去世，年仅三十一岁。\n\n他为辩论写的开场演说，后称《论人的尊严》，重述了创世：万物都被赋予固定的位置，唯独亚当没有，于是人可以凭自己的选择堕落为禽兽，也可以上升趋近神圣。人的尊严就在于这种塑造自己的自由。皮科还希望证明柏拉图、亚里士多德、卡巴拉与基督教共享同一个隐秘的真理。这篇身后出版的演说，成了文艺复兴人文主义的宣言。"},"schools":["renaissance"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Oration on the Dignity of Man","zh":"《论人的尊严》"},"y":1486},{"t":{"en":"900 Theses","zh":"《九百论题》"},"y":1486}],"wiki":"Giovanni Pico della Mirandola"},
  {"id":"erasmus","tradition":"modern","born":1466,"died":1536,"life":{"en":"1466 or 1469–1536","zh":"1466或1469–1536年"},"name":{"en":"Desiderius Erasmus","zh":"伊拉斯谟"},"role":{"en":"Dutch humanist scholar, editor of the Greek New Testament","zh":"荷兰人文主义学者，希腊文《新约》的编订者"},"idea":{"en":"He wanted a simple, inward “philosophy of Christ” recovered from the original sources, and mocked clerical greed and the schoolmen’s hair-splitting. He defended free will against Luther but refused to join either side of the Reformation.","zh":"他希望从原典中恢复一种朴素、内在的“基督哲学”，并嘲讽教士的贪婪和经院学者的咬文嚼字。他为自由意志辩护、反对路德，却拒绝加入宗教改革的任何一方。"},"detail":{"en":"Erasmus was born in Rotterdam in 1466 or 1469, the illegitimate son of a priest. Schooled at Deventer, he entered the Augustinian canons at Steyn and was ordained, but left the cloister to study in Paris and spent his life as a travelling scholar in England, Italy, Leuven and Basel, living on patrons and on his work with printers such as Johann Froben. By 1520 he was the best-known writer in Europe.\n\nErasmus wanted to bring Christianity back to its sources. In 1516 he published the first Greek New Testament to reach the market, with a new Latin translation, so readers could see what the text actually said. His “philosophy of Christ” was inner piety and decent conduct rather than ceremonies, relics or scholastic hair-splitting, all of which he mocked in The Praise of Folly. When the Reformation came, he defended human free will against Luther (1524), who replied with The Bondage of the Will (1525); yet he would not condemn reform wholesale, and both camps came to distrust him.","zh":"伊拉斯谟生于鹿特丹，年份是1466或1469年，是一位神父的私生子。他在代芬特尔读书，后入斯泰恩的奥斯定会修院并晋铎，却离开修院去巴黎求学，此后一生在英格兰、意大利、鲁汶和巴塞尔之间游学，依靠赞助人和与弗罗本等印刷商的合作为生。到1520年，他已是全欧洲最负盛名的作家。\n\n伊拉斯谟想让基督教回到源头。1516年他出版了第一部面市的希腊文《新约》，并附新的拉丁文译文，让读者看清经文原本怎么说。他倡导的“基督的哲学”是内心的虔敬与正直的行为，而不是仪式、圣物或经院式的繁琐辩论——这些都在《愚人颂》中遭到嘲讽。宗教改革来临时，他为人的自由意志辩护，反驳路德（1524），路德以《论意志的捆绑》（1525）回应；但他又不肯全盘谴责改革，结果两边都对他心存疑虑。"},"schools":["renaissance"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"The Praise of Folly","zh":"《愚人颂》"},"y":1511},{"t":{"en":"Greek New Testament (Novum Instrumentum)","zh":"希腊文《新约》（《新工具书》）"},"y":1516},{"t":{"en":"On Free Will","zh":"《论自由意志》"},"y":1524}],"wiki":"Erasmus"},
  {"id":"machiavelli","tradition":"modern","born":1469,"died":1527,"life":{"en":"1469–1527","zh":"1469–1527年"},"name":{"en":"Niccolò Machiavelli","zh":"尼科洛·马基雅维利"},"role":{"en":"Florentine diplomat who studied power as it actually works","zh":"研究权力实际如何运作的佛罗伦萨外交官"},"idea":{"en":"A ruler who wants to keep his state must sometimes act against conventional virtue, and is judged by results. In the Discourses he praised republics in which free citizens and well-managed conflict sustain liberty.","zh":"想保住国家的君主有时必须违背通常的美德行事，人们以成败来评判他。在《论李维》中，他赞美那种由自由公民和受到引导的冲突来维持自由的共和国。"},"detail":{"en":"Niccolò Machiavelli served the Florentine republic from 1498 as secretary of its Second Chancery, travelling on missions to the king of France, to Cesare Borgia and to the papal court, and organising a citizen militia. When the Medici returned to power in 1512 he lost his post; early in 1513 he was imprisoned and tortured on suspicion of conspiracy. Released, he retired to his farm near San Casciano, where he wrote The Prince at the end of 1513 and, over the following years, the Discourses on Livy.\n\nThe Prince asks how a new ruler can keep a state. Machiavelli looks at “the effectual truth of the matter” rather than imagined commonwealths: a ruler who tries to be good in every situation is ruined among so many who are not, so he must be ready to be feared, to break his word or to be cruel when the state requires it. Fortune can be resisted only by virtù — energy, foresight and nerve. The Discourses praise republics, where conflict between nobles and people, kept within laws, preserves liberty. Whether read as a manual for tyrants or a realist’s warning, his work founded the modern study of politics.","zh":"马基雅维利自1498年起担任佛罗伦萨共和国第二国务厅秘书，出使法国国王、切萨雷·博尔贾和教廷，还组建了公民民兵。1512年美第奇家族重掌政权，他丢了官职；1513年初又因涉嫌密谋被捕受刑。获释后他隐居圣卡夏诺附近的农庄，于1513年底写成《君主论》，并在此后几年写出《论李维》。\n\n《君主论》追问新君主如何保住国家。马基雅维利要看“事物实际的真相”，而不是想象中的理想国：一个处处都想做好人的统治者，在众多不善之人中间必然覆灭，所以在国家需要时，他必须敢于让人畏惧、背弃诺言，甚至残忍。命运只能靠“能力”（virtù）——魄力、远见与胆识——来抗衡。《论李维》则称颂共和国：贵族与平民的冲突只要纳入法律，反而能维护自由。无论被读作暴君手册还是现实主义的警告，他的著作都开创了现代政治学。"},"schools":["renaissance"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"The Prince","zh":"《君主论》"},"y":1513},{"t":{"en":"Discourses on Livy","zh":"《论李维》"},"y":1517}],"wiki":"Niccolò Machiavelli"},
  {"id":"montaigne","tradition":"modern","born":1533,"died":1592,"life":{"en":"1533–1592","zh":"1533–1592年"},"name":{"en":"Michel de Montaigne","zh":"米歇尔·德·蒙田"},"role":{"en":"French magistrate who invented the essay as self-examination","zh":"把随笔发明为自我审视之文体的法国法官"},"idea":{"en":"“What do I know?” was his motto: studying himself, he found human judgement shifting, custom-bound and fallible. He urged tolerance, since other peoples’ customs seem barbarous only to those who never question their own.","zh":"“我知道什么？”是他的座右铭：在审视自己时，他发现人的判断变动不居、受习俗束缚而且容易出错。他主张宽容，因为别的民族的习俗之所以显得野蛮，只是因为人们从不质疑自己的习俗。"},"detail":{"en":"Michel de Montaigne grew up in a wealthy family near Bordeaux; his father had him spoken to only in Latin as a small child, so Latin was almost his first language. He served as a magistrate in the Bordeaux parlement, where he formed an intense friendship with Étienne de La Boétie, whose death in 1563 marked him for life. In 1571 he withdrew to the tower library of his château and began writing, while France was torn by its wars of religion; he later served two terms as mayor of Bordeaux.\n\nHe called his pieces essais — trials or attempts — and in them tested his own judgement on everything from friendship and cannibals to kidney stones. In the long “Apology for Raymond Sebond” he uses the ancient skeptics to show how weak reason is when it claims to know God or nature; his motto became “What do I know?” From self-knowledge comes humility and tolerance: in “Of Cannibals” he observes that each people calls barbarous whatever is not its own custom. Descartes, Pascal and every later essayist wrote in his shadow.","zh":"蒙田生长于波尔多附近的富裕人家；父亲安排他幼年只听拉丁语，拉丁语几乎成了他的母语。他曾任波尔多高等法院法官，在那里与拉博埃西结下深厚友谊，后者1563年去世，令他终生难忘。1571年，在宗教战争撕裂法国之时，他退居城堡塔楼里的书房开始写作，后来又两度出任波尔多市长。\n\n他把这些文章称为“随笔”（essais），意为尝试，用它们检验自己对一切事物的判断——从友谊、食人族到肾结石。长文《为雷蒙·塞邦辩护》借古代怀疑论说明：理性自称认识上帝或自然时是多么无力；“我知道什么？”成了他的座右铭。从认识自己中生出谦逊与宽容：《论食人族》指出，各民族都把不合自己习俗的东西称为野蛮。笛卡尔、帕斯卡尔以及后来的所有随笔作家，都在他的影子里写作。"},"schools":["renaissance"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Essays","zh":"《随笔集》"},"y":1580}],"wiki":"Michel de Montaigne"},
  {"id":"bacon","tradition":"modern","born":1561,"died":1626,"life":{"en":"1561–1626","zh":"1561–1626年"},"name":{"en":"Francis Bacon","zh":"弗朗西斯·培根"},"role":{"en":"English statesman who called for science built on experiment","zh":"呼吁以实验为基础建立科学的英国政治家"},"idea":{"en":"Human knowledge and human power meet in one: we master nature only by careful observation and gradual induction, not by syllogisms. He catalogued the “idols” — biases of human nature, language and authority — that distort understanding.","zh":"人类的知识与人类的力量合而为一：只有通过细致的观察和逐步的归纳，而不是三段论，我们才能驾驭自然。他列举了扭曲理解的种种“假象”——来自人性、语言和权威的偏见。"},"detail":{"en":"Francis Bacon was the son of Elizabeth I’s Lord Keeper of the Great Seal. He studied at Cambridge, trained as a lawyer and rose under James I to Attorney General and Lord Chancellor. In 1621 he admitted to accepting gifts from people with cases before him, was fined and removed from office, and spent his last years writing. He died in 1626; by a story told by John Aubrey, of a chill caught while stuffing a chicken with snow to see whether cold would preserve it.\n\nBacon’s grand plan was to renew all knowledge. The New Organon (1620) attacks the old logic: syllogisms only rearrange what we already believe. Science should instead collect careful observations and experiments and climb step by step from particulars to general laws, looking especially for “negative instances” that rule hypotheses out. He also named the “idols” that distort the mind: those of the tribe, the cave, the marketplace and the theatre. Knowledge and human power, he held, come to the same thing, because understanding nature lets us improve human life. The Royal Society later took him as its patron.","zh":"弗朗西斯·培根是伊丽莎白一世掌玺大臣之子，在剑桥求学，后习法律，在詹姆斯一世治下升任总检察长和大法官。1621年他承认收受诉讼当事人的礼物，被罚款并免职，此后专心著述。1626年去世；据奥布里记载，他是为了试验寒冷能否保鲜，把雪塞进鸡肚里时着凉而死。\n\n培根的宏大计划是更新全部知识。《新工具》（1620）抨击旧逻辑：三段论只是重新排列我们已经相信的东西。科学应当收集细致的观察与实验，从个别事例一步步上升到普遍规律，并特别留意能排除假说的“反例”。他还列举了扭曲心智的“假象”：种族假象、洞穴假象、市场假象与剧场假象。他认为知识与人的力量是一回事，因为理解自然才能改善人类生活。后来的英国皇家学会把他奉为精神导师。"},"schools":["empiricism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"The Advancement of Learning","zh":"《学术的进展》"},"y":1605},{"t":{"en":"Novum Organum","zh":"《新工具》"},"y":1620}],"wiki":"Francis Bacon"},
  {"id":"hobbes","tradition":"modern","born":1588,"died":1679,"life":{"en":"1588–1679","zh":"1588–1679年"},"name":{"en":"Thomas Hobbes","zh":"托马斯·霍布斯"},"role":{"en":"English materialist who grounded the state in fear and consent","zh":"以恐惧与同意为国家奠基的英国唯物论者"},"idea":{"en":"Without a common power, life would be a war of all against all — “solitary, poor, nasty, brutish, and short”. Rational people would therefore authorise an absolute sovereign to keep the peace.","zh":"没有一个共同的权力，人生就会是一切人反对一切人的战争——“孤独、贫困、卑污、残忍而短寿”。因此，理性的人会授权一个绝对的主权者来维持和平。"},"detail":{"en":"Thomas Hobbes was born in 1588, the year of the Spanish Armada; he later wrote that his mother gave birth to twins, himself and fear. After Oxford he spent most of his life as tutor and secretary to the Cavendish family, travelling in Europe, meeting Galileo and falling in love with geometry. As the English Civil War approached, his royalist sympathies sent him into exile in Paris from 1640 to 1651, where he wrote Leviathan.\n\nHobbes was a thoroughgoing materialist: everything, thought included, is matter in motion. His politics starts from an imagined state of nature without government. Because people are roughly equal and compete for gain, safety and glory, each has reason to fear the others, and life there is “solitary, poor, nasty, brutish, and short”. Reason therefore advises everyone to give up their right to all things and authorise one sovereign strong enough to keep the peace; even a bad sovereign is better than civil war. Locke, Rousseau and Rawls kept his method of imagining a contract while rejecting his absolutism.","zh":"霍布斯生于1588年，正是西班牙无敌舰队来袭之年；他后来写道，母亲生下了一对双胞胎：他自己和恐惧。从牛津毕业后，他大半生担任卡文迪许家族的家庭教师和秘书，随之游历欧洲，见过伽利略，并迷上了几何学。英国内战将起时，他因同情王党而于1640至1651年流亡巴黎，在那里写成《利维坦》。\n\n霍布斯是彻底的唯物主义者：一切，包括思想，都是运动中的物质。他的政治学从想象中没有政府的“自然状态”出发。人大致平等，又为利益、安全和荣誉相互竞争，所以人人都有理由畏惧他人，那里的生活“孤独、贫困、卑污、残忍而短寿”。因此理性劝告每个人放弃对一切事物的权利，授权一个强大到足以维持和平的主权者；即使是坏的主权者，也胜过内战。洛克、卢梭直到罗尔斯都沿用了他设想契约的方法，却抛弃了他的专制主义。"},"schools":["social-contract"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"De Cive (On the Citizen)","zh":"《论公民》"},"y":1642},{"t":{"en":"Leviathan","zh":"《利维坦》"},"y":1651}],"wiki":"Thomas Hobbes"},
  {"id":"descartes","tradition":"modern","born":1596,"died":1650,"life":{"en":"1596–1650","zh":"1596–1650年"},"name":{"en":"René Descartes","zh":"勒内·笛卡尔"},"role":{"en":"French mathematician who rebuilt philosophy on the certainty of thought","zh":"以思维的确定性重建哲学的法国数学家"},"idea":{"en":"Doubting everything that could be doubted, he found one certainty: “I think, therefore I am.” He held that mind and body are two distinct substances, opening the modern mind–body problem.","zh":"在怀疑一切可以怀疑的东西之后，他找到了一个确定之点：“我思故我在。”他主张心灵与身体是两种不同的实体，由此开启了近代的心身问题。"},"detail":{"en":"René Descartes was educated by the Jesuits at La Flèche and took a law degree at Poitiers. As a young gentleman soldier he had, on the night of 10 November 1619, a series of dreams that he took as a call to rebuild knowledge. He spent most of his working life in the Dutch Republic, writing on optics, physics, physiology and geometry, where he joined algebra to geometry through coordinates. Invited by Queen Christina, he moved to Stockholm in 1649 and died there in February 1650.\n\nThe Meditations (1641) begin by doubting everything that can be doubted: the senses, the existence of his body, even mathematics, in case an all-powerful demon is deceiving him. One thing survives: while he doubts, he thinks, and so he exists. From this he argues to a God who is no deceiver, and so to the reliability of what he perceives clearly and distinctly. Mind, whose essence is thinking, turns out to be a substance distinct from body, whose essence is extension. That split gave modern philosophy its mind–body problem, and his method made certainty the goal of knowledge.","zh":"笛卡尔曾在拉弗莱什的耶稣会学校受教育，后在普瓦捷取得法律学位。年轻时他以绅士身份从军，1619年11月10日夜里接连做了几个梦，他把这当作重建知识的召唤。他一生大部分时间在荷兰共和国从事研究，写过光学、物理学、生理学和几何学著作，并用坐标把代数与几何结合起来。1649年他应瑞典女王克里斯蒂娜之邀前往斯德哥尔摩，次年2月卒于当地。\n\n《第一哲学沉思集》（1641）从怀疑一切可疑之物开始：感官、自己身体的存在，甚至数学——万一有个全能的恶魔在欺骗他呢。只有一件事经得起怀疑：他在怀疑时就在思维，因而他存在。由此他论证存在一位不欺骗的上帝，进而保证清楚分明的认识可靠。心灵的本质是思维，物体的本质是广延，二者是不同的实体。这一划分给近代哲学留下了身心问题，他的方法也让“确定性”成了知识的目标。"},"schools":["rationalism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Discourse on the Method","zh":"《谈谈方法》"},"y":1637},{"t":{"en":"Meditations on First Philosophy","zh":"《第一哲学沉思集》"},"y":1641},{"t":{"en":"Principles of Philosophy","zh":"《哲学原理》"},"y":1644}],"wiki":"René Descartes","c":"Frans Hals - Portret van René Descartes.jpg","portrait":{"w":600,"h":735,"v":"97f58701c1","author":"After Frans Hals","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":null,"page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Frans_Hals_-_Portret_van_Ren%C3%A9_Descartes.jpg"}},
  {"id":"spinoza","tradition":"modern","born":1632,"died":1677,"life":{"en":"1632–1677","zh":"1632–1677年"},"name":{"en":"Baruch Spinoza","zh":"巴鲁赫·斯宾诺莎"},"role":{"en":"Dutch lens-grinder, expelled by his synagogue, who equated God with Nature","zh":"被犹太会堂逐出、视上帝即自然的荷兰磨镜片匠人"},"idea":{"en":"There is only one substance, “God or Nature”, and everything follows from it with necessity; mind and body are two aspects of one thing. Freedom is not escape from necessity but understanding it, which releases us from bondage to the passions.","zh":"只有一个实体，即“神或自然”，万物都必然地由它而出；心灵与身体是同一事物的两个方面。自由不是摆脱必然，而是理解必然，这种理解让我们摆脱激情的奴役。"},"detail":{"en":"Baruch (Benedictus) de Spinoza was born in Amsterdam to a family of Portuguese Jewish merchants. In 1656 the city’s synagogue issued a herem, a writ of expulsion, against him for “abominable heresies”. He lived modestly, grinding lenses for microscopes and telescopes, corresponded with scientists, and turned down a professorship at Heidelberg to keep his freedom. His Theological-Political Treatise came out anonymously in 1670 and was widely banned; his Ethics appeared only after his death in 1677.\n\nThe Ethics is set out like geometry, with definitions, axioms and proofs. Its conclusion is that there is only one substance, infinite and the cause of itself — “God or Nature” — and that minds and bodies are its finite modes, one and the same thing seen under thought or under extension. Everything follows necessarily; nature has no purposes and we have no free will in the usual sense. Freedom means understanding the causes of our emotions, which turns passive passions into active joy. Branded an atheist for a century, he was rediscovered by the German idealists, and Novalis called him a “God-intoxicated man”.","zh":"斯宾诺莎生于阿姆斯特丹一个葡萄牙裔犹太商人家庭。1656年，当地犹太会堂以“可憎的异端”为由对他发布“赫雷姆”，即驱逐令。此后他生活简朴，以磨制显微镜和望远镜镜片为业，与科学家通信，并谢绝了海德堡大学的教职以保全思想自由。《神学政治论》于1670年匿名出版，随即在各地遭禁；《伦理学》直到他1677年去世后才面世。\n\n《伦理学》仿照几何学写成，有定义、公理和证明。结论是：只有一个实体，无限且自因，即“神或自然”；心灵与身体都是它的有限样式，是同一事物分别从思维和广延来看。一切都必然发生；自然没有目的，通常意义上的自由意志也不存在。自由在于理解自己情感的原因，从而把被动的激情转化为主动的喜悦。他被当作无神论者咒骂了一个世纪，后来德国观念论者重新发现了他，诺瓦利斯称他为“醉心于神的人”。"},"schools":["rationalism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Theological-Political Treatise","zh":"《神学政治论》"},"y":1670},{"t":{"en":"Ethics","zh":"《伦理学》"},"y":1677}],"wiki":"Baruch Spinoza","c":"Spinoza.jpg","portrait":{"w":600,"h":697,"v":"20518478e5","author":"anonymous","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":null,"page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spinoza.jpg"}},
  {"id":"locke","tradition":"modern","born":1632,"died":1704,"life":{"en":"1632–1704","zh":"1632–1704年"},"name":{"en":"John Locke","zh":"约翰·洛克"},"role":{"en":"English physician and philosopher of experience, consent and toleration","zh":"英国医生，经验、同意与宽容的哲学家"},"idea":{"en":"The mind begins as “white paper” written on by experience. Governments exist to protect life, liberty and property, rest on the consent of the governed, and may be resisted when they betray that trust.","zh":"心灵起初是一张由经验书写的“白纸”。政府的存在是为了保护生命、自由和财产，它建立在被统治者的同意之上，一旦背弃这份信托，人民就可以反抗。"},"detail":{"en":"John Locke studied at Oxford, trained as a physician and became doctor and adviser to the Earl of Shaftesbury, leader of the opposition to the Catholic heir, James. Under suspicion after Shaftesbury’s fall, Locke fled to the Netherlands in 1683 and returned after the Glorious Revolution of 1688. In 1689 he published, in quick succession, the Essay Concerning Human Understanding (its title page dated 1690), the Two Treatises of Government and the Letter Concerning Toleration.\n\nThe Essay asks where our ideas come from. Against innate ideas, Locke argues that the mind begins like white paper and receives all its materials from sensation and reflection; even ideas such as substance or infinity are built from them. Knowledge is therefore limited, and belief should be proportioned to evidence. In politics, people in the state of nature already have rights to life, liberty and property; they set up government by consent to protect those rights, and may resist a government that violates them. Toleration follows from the limits of knowledge and the purpose of the state. These ideas ran through the Enlightenment and into the American Declaration of Independence.","zh":"洛克在牛津求学，学过医，后来成为沙夫茨伯里伯爵的医生和顾问；伯爵是反对天主教王储詹姆斯继位的领袖。伯爵失势后洛克受到怀疑，1683年逃往荷兰，1688年光荣革命后才回国。1689年他接连出版了《人类理解论》（扉页印1690年）、《政府论》和《论宗教宽容》。\n\n《人类理解论》追问我们的观念从何而来。洛克反对天赋观念，认为心灵起初像一张白纸，一切材料都来自感觉与反省；就连实体、无限这样的观念也由它们构成。因此知识有限，信念应当与证据相称。在政治上，人在自然状态中已拥有生命、自由和财产的权利；他们出于同意建立政府来保护这些权利，政府若侵犯它们，人民可以反抗。宽容则源于知识的有限和国家的目的。这些思想贯穿整个启蒙运动，并写进了美国《独立宣言》。"},"schools":["empiricism","social-contract"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"An Essay Concerning Human Understanding","zh":"《人类理解论》"},"y":1689},{"t":{"en":"Two Treatises of Government","zh":"《政府论》"},"y":1689},{"t":{"en":"A Letter Concerning Toleration","zh":"《论宗教宽容》"},"y":1689}],"wiki":"John Locke","c":"Godfrey Kneller - Portrait of John Locke (Hermitage).jpg","portrait":{"w":600,"h":732,"v":"9a3ff7463a","author":"Godfrey Kneller","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":null,"page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Godfrey_Kneller_-_Portrait_of_John_Locke_(Hermitage).jpg"}},
  {"id":"leibniz","tradition":"modern","born":1646,"died":1716,"life":{"en":"1646–1716","zh":"1646–1716年"},"name":{"en":"Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz","zh":"戈特弗里德·威廉·莱布尼茨"},"role":{"en":"German polymath, co-inventor of calculus, theorist of monads","zh":"德国博学家，微积分的发明者之一，单子论的提出者"},"idea":{"en":"The world consists of simple, mind-like substances, monads, whose states God harmonised in advance. Because God chose by the principle of sufficient reason, this is the best of all possible worlds — the claim Voltaire mocked in Candide.","zh":"世界由单纯的、类似心灵的实体——单子——构成，上帝预先协调了它们的状态。由于上帝依据充足理由律进行选择，这个世界是一切可能世界中最好的一个——伏尔泰在《老实人》里嘲讽的正是这一主张。"},"detail":{"en":"Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz was born in Leipzig and took a doctorate in law at twenty. He spent his life in the service of courts, above all as librarian, historian and counsellor to the House of Hanover. During a stay in Paris in 1672–76 he invented the calculus independently of Newton, which led to a bitter dispute over priority; he also built a calculating machine and founded the Berlin Academy of Sciences. Most of his philosophy lies in letters and short essays rather than a single great book.\n\nLeibniz builds on two principles: contradiction, and sufficient reason — nothing happens without a reason why it is so and not otherwise. The true units of the world are monads: simple substances without parts, each perceiving the whole universe from its own point of view. Monads do not act on one another; God set them in a “pre-established harmony”, like clocks that keep the same time. Because God chose among infinitely many possible worlds, he chose the best, so the evil in it is part of the greatest overall good. Voltaire mocked this in Candide; Kant was trained in the school it founded.","zh":"莱布尼茨生于莱比锡，二十岁便获法学博士学位。他一生为宫廷效力，主要担任汉诺威王室的图书馆长、史官和顾问。1672–1676年旅居巴黎期间，他独立于牛顿发明了微积分，由此引发一场激烈的优先权之争；他还造出一台计算机器，并创办了柏林科学院。他的哲学大多散见于书信与短文，而不是一部鸿篇巨制。\n\n莱布尼茨的哲学建立在两条原则上：矛盾律与充足理由律——任何事情的发生，都有它为何如此而非别样的理由。世界真正的单位是“单子”：没有部分的单纯实体，每一个都从自己的视角知觉整个宇宙。单子彼此不发生作用，是上帝预先为它们安排了“前定和谐”，就像几只走时一致的钟。既然上帝是在无数可能世界中作出选择，他选的必是最好的一个，世上的恶也是整体最大善的一部分。伏尔泰在《老实人》中嘲讽了这一观点；康德正是在这一学派中受的训练。"},"schools":["rationalism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Discourse on Metaphysics","zh":"《形而上学论》"},"y":1686},{"t":{"en":"Theodicy","zh":"《神正论》"},"y":1710},{"t":{"en":"Monadology","zh":"《单子论》"},"y":1714}],"wiki":"Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz","c":"Christoph Bernhard Francke - Bildnis des Philosophen Leibniz (ca. 1695).jpg","portrait":{"w":600,"h":741,"v":"b26ff5e3e1","author":"Christoph Bernhard Francke","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":null,"page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Christoph_Bernhard_Francke_-_Bildnis_des_Philosophen_Leibniz_(ca._1695).jpg"}},
  {"id":"berkeley","tradition":"modern","born":1685,"died":1753,"life":{"en":"1685–1753","zh":"1685–1753年"},"name":{"en":"George Berkeley","zh":"乔治·贝克莱"},"role":{"en":"Irish bishop who argued that to be is to be perceived","zh":"主张“存在就是被感知”的爱尔兰主教"},"idea":{"en":"We never perceive matter itself, only ideas, so the notion of unthinking material substance is empty. Objects are collections of ideas, kept in being when no human is looking by the continual perception of God.","zh":"我们从未知觉到物质本身，只知觉到观念，因此“无思维的物质实体”这一概念是空洞的。物体是观念的集合；当无人观看时，它们依靠上帝持续的感知而存在。"},"detail":{"en":"George Berkeley was born in Ireland and studied at Trinity College Dublin, where, still in his twenties, he wrote his main philosophical books. From 1729 to 1731 he lived in Rhode Island, waiting for government funds for a college in Bermuda that never arrived. He became Anglican Bishop of Cloyne in Ireland in 1734, worked on the poverty around him, and in later life promoted tar-water as a medicine.\n\nBerkeley’s immaterialism starts from Locke: what we directly perceive are ideas. But then, he argues, we have no reason — indeed no coherent idea — for unthinking matter lying behind them. For a sensible thing, to be is to be perceived. A cherry is its colour, taste, shape and softness, and all of these are ideas. This does not make the world unreal: things exist in the minds that perceive them, and go on existing when we look away because God always perceives them. Berkeley meant this as a defence of common sense and religion against skeptics and atheists. Hume took over his attack on abstract ideas.","zh":"贝克莱生于爱尔兰，就读于都柏林三一学院，二十多岁时就写出了主要的哲学著作。1729至1731年他住在罗得岛，等待政府拨款在百慕大建一所学院，款项却始终没有到位。1734年他出任爱尔兰克洛因的圣公会主教，致力于救济当地的贫困，晚年还推广焦油水作为药物。\n\n贝克莱的“非物质主义”从洛克出发：我们直接知觉到的是观念。他进而论证，我们没有理由——甚至没有一个融贯的观念——去设想观念背后还有不会思维的物质。对可感事物来说，存在就是被感知。一颗樱桃就是它的颜色、味道、形状与柔软，这些都是观念。这并不使世界变得虚幻：事物存在于感知它们的心灵中，我们移开目光时它们仍然存在，因为上帝始终感知着它们。贝克莱本想以此捍卫常识与宗教，对抗怀疑论者和无神论者。休谟则继承了他对抽象观念的批判。"},"schools":["empiricism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"A Treatise Concerning the Principles of Human Knowledge","zh":"《人类知识原理》"},"y":1710},{"t":{"en":"Three Dialogues between Hylas and Philonous","zh":"《海拉斯和斐洛诺斯的三篇对话》"},"y":1713}],"wiki":"George Berkeley"},
  {"id":"montesquieu","tradition":"modern","born":1689,"died":1755,"life":{"en":"1689–1755","zh":"1689–1755年"},"name":{"en":"Montesquieu","zh":"孟德斯鸠"},"role":{"en":"French magistrate who compared the laws of many nations","zh":"比较各国法律的法国法官"},"idea":{"en":"Laws must suit each people’s climate, customs, commerce and religion. Liberty is safest where legislative, executive and judicial powers are separated, so that power checks power.","zh":"法律必须适合每个民族的气候、风俗、商业与宗教。立法、行政、司法三权分立、以权力制约权力的地方，自由才最有保障。"},"detail":{"en":"Charles-Louis de Secondat, baron de Montesquieu, inherited a title and a senior judicial office in the parlement of Bordeaux. His satirical Persian Letters (1721), in which two Persian visitors describe the oddities of France, made him famous. He sold his office, travelled through Europe — spending almost two years in England — and worked for some twenty years on The Spirit of the Laws, published in Geneva in 1748 and soon placed on the Catholic Index.\n\nMontesquieu asked why laws differ from one people to another. His answer was that laws must fit a nation’s “general spirit”, formed by climate, soil, religion, commerce, customs and form of government. He classified governments by their driving principle: republics by virtue, monarchies by honour, despotisms by fear. Admiring the English constitution, he argued that liberty is safe only where legislative, executive and judicial power are in different hands, so that power checks power. The framers of the United States Constitution cited him constantly when they designed its separation of powers.","zh":"孟德斯鸠男爵继承了爵位和波尔多高等法院的要职。他的讽刺小说《波斯人信札》（1721）借两位波斯旅人之口描述法国的种种怪事，使他一举成名。后来他卖掉官职，游历欧洲，在英国住了将近两年，又花了约二十年写成《论法的精神》，1748年在日内瓦出版，不久即被天主教会列入禁书目录。\n\n孟德斯鸠追问：为什么各民族的法律各不相同？他的回答是，法律必须适合一个民族的“普遍精神”，而这种精神由气候、土壤、宗教、商业、风俗和政体共同塑造。他按推动原则区分政体：共和政体靠美德，君主政体靠荣誉，专制政体靠恐惧。他推崇英国宪制，认为只有立法、行政、司法三权分属不同之手，以权力制约权力，自由才有保障。美国宪法的制定者在设计分权制度时，不断引用他的论述。"},"schools":["enlightenment"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Persian Letters","zh":"《波斯人信札》"},"y":1721},{"t":{"en":"The Spirit of the Laws","zh":"《论法的精神》"},"y":1748}],"wiki":"Montesquieu"},
  {"id":"voltaire","tradition":"modern","born":1694,"died":1778,"life":{"en":"1694–1778","zh":"1694–1778年"},"name":{"en":"Voltaire","zh":"伏尔泰"},"role":{"en":"French writer who campaigned against intolerance and fanaticism","zh":"反对不宽容与狂热的法国作家"},"idea":{"en":"He popularised Locke and Newton in France and mocked Leibnizian optimism in Candide. His campaign for the executed Protestant Jean Calas made religious toleration a public cause.","zh":"他在法国普及了洛克和牛顿的思想，又在《老实人》中嘲讽莱布尼茨式的乐观主义。他为被处死的新教徒让·卡拉斯奔走申冤，使宗教宽容成为公共议题。"},"detail":{"en":"François-Marie Arouet, who wrote as Voltaire, was the son of a Paris notary and was educated by the Jesuits. His sharp tongue twice landed him in the Bastille, and in 1726 he left for some two and a half years in England, where he came to admire Newton, Locke and English religious toleration. He wrote plays, poems, histories and philosophical tales, and from 1759 lived at Ferney near the Swiss border as the most famous writer in Europe.\n\nVoltaire was less a builder of systems than a campaigner. His Letters on the English (1733; in French as the Lettres philosophiques, 1734) used English liberty and science to shame French absolutism and was burned by order of the Paris parlement. Candide (1759), written after the Lisbon earthquake, mocks the optimism that this is the best of all possible worlds and ends with the advice to cultivate our garden. From 1762 he fought to clear the name of Jean Calas, a Protestant executed on a false charge of murdering his son, and his Treatise on Tolerance argued that persecution is irrational and cruel. “Écrasez l’infâme” — crush the infamous thing, meaning fanaticism and the abuses of the Church — was his rallying cry.","zh":"伏尔泰本名弗朗索瓦-马利·阿鲁埃，是巴黎一位公证人的儿子，受教于耶稣会学校。他的尖刻言辞两度让他进了巴士底狱；1726年他前往英国住了约两年半，从此推崇牛顿、洛克和英国的宗教宽容。他写剧本、诗歌、史书和哲理小说，1759年起定居瑞士边境附近的费尔内，是当时全欧洲最著名的作家。\n\n伏尔泰与其说是体系的建造者，不如说是斗士。他的《哲学通信》（英文版1733年，法文版1734年）以英国的自由和科学来羞辱法国的专制，被巴黎高等法院下令焚毁。《老实人》（1759）写于里斯本大地震之后，嘲讽“这是一切可能世界中最好的一个”的乐观论，结尾劝人“耕种自己的园地”。1762年起，他为新教徒让·卡拉斯奔走昭雪——此人被诬杀子而遭处决；他的《论宽容》指出迫害既不理性又残忍。“消灭败类”（指狂热和教会的种种恶行）是他的战斗口号。"},"schools":["enlightenment"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Letters on the English (Philosophical Letters)","zh":"《哲学通信》"},"y":1733},{"t":{"en":"Candide","zh":"《老实人》"},"y":1759},{"t":{"en":"Treatise on Tolerance","zh":"《论宽容》"},"y":1763}],"wiki":"Voltaire"},
  {"id":"hume","tradition":"modern","born":1711,"died":1776,"life":{"en":"1711–1776","zh":"1711–1776年"},"name":{"en":"David Hume","zh":"大卫·休谟"},"role":{"en":"Scottish skeptic who traced knowledge and morals to experience and feeling","zh":"把知识与道德追溯到经验和情感的苏格兰怀疑论者"},"idea":{"en":"We never observe necessary connections, only constant conjunctions, so belief in causes is a habit of the mind. Reason alone cannot move us to act — “reason is, and ought only to be the slave of the passions” — and morality rests on sentiment.","zh":"我们从未观察到必然联系，只观察到恒常的伴随，所以对原因的信念是心灵的一种习惯。单凭理性无法驱使我们行动——“理性是、并且只应当是激情的奴隶”——道德建立在情感之上。"},"detail":{"en":"David Hume grew up near Edinburgh and studied at its university. After a breakdown in his early twenties he went to France, where at La Flèche he wrote A Treatise of Human Nature (1739–40), which, he said, “fell dead-born from the press”. Twice refused a professorship because of his reputation for irreligion, he became famous instead as an essayist and as the author of a bestselling History of England, and served as a diplomat in Paris. His Dialogues Concerning Natural Religion were published only after his death in 1776.\n\nHume applied the experimental method to the mind. All our ideas copy impressions, so what is our idea of a cause? We see one billiard ball strike another and the second move, but we never perceive a necessary connection — only constant conjunction, after which the mind, by habit, expects the effect. Our trust in induction rests on custom, not on reason. Morality too rests on feeling: reason tells us facts, but only passion moves us to act, and no “ought” follows from an “is” alone. His skepticism woke Kant and shaped utilitarianism and modern naturalism.","zh":"休谟在爱丁堡附近长大，就读于爱丁堡大学。二十出头经历一场精神危机后，他去了法国，在拉弗莱什写成《人性论》（1739–1740），他自嘲此书“一出版就死了”。他因不信宗教的名声两度未能获得教职，转而以随笔作家和畅销书《英国史》的作者闻名，还曾在巴黎担任外交官。《自然宗教对话录》直到他1776年去世后才出版。\n\n休谟把实验方法用于研究心灵。我们的一切观念都摹写印象，那么“原因”的观念从何而来？我们看到一个台球撞上另一个，后者随即滚动，却从未知觉到“必然联系”，只看到恒常的相伴出现，此后心灵出于习惯期待结果。我们对归纳的信赖建立在习惯上，而不是理性上。道德同样建立在情感上：理性告诉我们事实，只有情感推动我们行动，单凭“是”推不出“应当”。他的怀疑论惊醒了康德，也塑造了功利主义和现代自然主义。"},"schools":["empiricism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"A Treatise of Human Nature","zh":"《人性论》"},"y":1739},{"t":{"en":"An Enquiry Concerning Human Understanding","zh":"《人类理智研究》"},"y":1748},{"t":{"en":"Dialogues Concerning Natural Religion","zh":"《自然宗教对话录》"},"y":1779}],"wiki":"David Hume","c":"David Hume Ramsay.jpg","portrait":{"w":600,"h":727,"v":"22ce8b8a2b","author":"Allan Ramsay","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":null,"page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:David_Hume_Ramsay.jpg"}},
  {"id":"rousseau","tradition":"modern","born":1712,"died":1778,"life":{"en":"1712–1778","zh":"1712–1778年"},"name":{"en":"Jean-Jacques Rousseau","zh":"让-雅克·卢梭"},"role":{"en":"Geneva-born writer who blamed society, not nature, for human corruption","zh":"认为人的败坏源于社会而非本性的日内瓦作家"},"idea":{"en":"In the state of nature humans were not warlike but free and capable of pity; inequality grew with property and society. Legitimate authority comes from the “general will” of citizens who give the laws to themselves.","zh":"在自然状态中，人并不好战，而是自由并怀有怜悯之心；不平等随着私有财产和社会而产生。正当的权威来自公民的“公意”，即公民为自己立法。"},"detail":{"en":"Jean-Jacques Rousseau was born in Geneva; his mother died days after his birth, and at fifteen he left the city to wander as an apprentice, servant, music copyist and protégé of Madame de Warens. In Paris he befriended Diderot, and in 1750 he won fame with a prize essay arguing that the arts and sciences had corrupted morals. The Social Contract and Emile (both 1762) were condemned in Paris and Geneva, and he spent years in flight, quarrelling with former friends.\n\nRousseau turned the contract tradition around. In the Discourse on Inequality, natural human beings are solitary and peaceable, moved by self-love and pity; vanity, dependence and inequality arrive with society and property — the first man who fenced off a piece of land and said “this is mine” began the trouble. The Social Contract asks how people can live under law and still be free: by each giving themselves to a community ruled by the general will, so that in obeying the law they obey themselves. His ideas fed the French Revolution, Kant’s ethics of autonomy and modern theories of education.","zh":"卢梭生于日内瓦，母亲在他出生几天后去世；十五岁时他离开故乡四处漂泊，当过学徒、仆人、乐谱抄写员，并受到华伦夫人的庇护。在巴黎他与狄德罗结为好友，1750年以一篇论证艺术与科学败坏了风俗的征文一举成名。《社会契约论》和《爱弥儿》（均为1762年）在巴黎和日内瓦遭到查禁，此后他多年流亡，并与昔日朋友相继反目。\n\n卢梭把契约论传统翻转过来。在《论人类不平等的起源》中，自然状态下的人孤独而平和，受自爱与怜悯驱动；虚荣、依附与不平等是随着社会和财产出现的——第一个圈起一块地说“这是我的”的人，开启了一切麻烦。《社会契约论》追问：人怎样才能生活在法律之下而依然自由？答案是每个人把自己交给由“公意”统治的共同体，这样服从法律就是服从自己。他的思想滋养了法国大革命、康德的自律伦理学和现代教育理论。"},"schools":["social-contract"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Discourse on the Origin of Inequality","zh":"《论人类不平等的起源和基础》"},"y":1755},{"t":{"en":"The Social Contract","zh":"《社会契约论》"},"y":1762},{"t":{"en":"Emile, or On Education","zh":"《爱弥儿》"},"y":1762}],"wiki":"Jean-Jacques Rousseau","c":"Maurice Quentin de La Tour - Portrait of Jean-Jacques Rousseau - adjusted.jpg","portrait":{"w":600,"h":761,"v":"13afc98319","author":"Maurice Quentin de La Tour (Life time: 1788)","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":null,"page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maurice_Quentin_de_La_Tour_-_Portrait_of_Jean-Jacques_Rousseau_-_adjusted.jpg"}},
  {"id":"diderot","tradition":"modern","born":1713,"died":1784,"life":{"en":"1713–1784","zh":"1713–1784年"},"name":{"en":"Denis Diderot","zh":"德尼·狄德罗"},"role":{"en":"Chief editor of the Encyclopédie, materialist and critic","zh":"《百科全书》主编，唯物论者与评论家"},"idea":{"en":"He edited the Encyclopédie to gather all human knowledge and “change the common way of thinking”. In dialogues unpublished in his lifetime he imagined a materialist account of life in which matter itself comes to feel and think.","zh":"他主编《百科全书》，要汇集人类的全部知识，“改变人们普遍的思维方式”。在生前未发表的对话中，他构想了一种唯物论的生命观：物质本身可以产生感觉与思想。"},"detail":{"en":"Denis Diderot, a master cutler’s son from Langres, studied in Paris and lived for years as a translator and hack writer. In 1749 his Letter on the Blind, which hinted at atheism, earned him some three months’ imprisonment at Vincennes. From 1747 he was chief editor of the Encyclopédie, which grew to seventeen volumes of text and eleven of plates (1751–72); he steered it for a quarter of a century through bans, censorship and the withdrawal of his co-editor d’Alembert. Late in life he visited Catherine the Great in St Petersburg.\n\nThe Encyclopédie aimed, in Diderot’s words, to change the common way of thinking. It arranged knowledge on a tree adapted from Bacon, gave crafts and machines the same dignity as theology, and slipped criticism of Church and state into cross-references. In private works such as D’Alembert’s Dream (written 1769, published 1830) he developed a materialism in which sensitivity is a property of matter itself, so that life and thought emerge from organised matter without any soul — an idea that anticipates evolutionary biology. His Salons also founded modern art criticism.","zh":"狄德罗是朗格勒一位刀匠师傅的儿子，在巴黎求学，多年以翻译和卖文为生。1749年，他因暗含无神论的《论盲人书简》在万塞讷被关押约三个月。从1747年起他担任《百科全书》主编，这部书最终有十七卷正文和十一卷图版（1751–1772）；他在禁令、审查和合编者达朗贝尔退出的重重困难中主持了四分之一个世纪。晚年他曾赴圣彼得堡拜访叶卡捷琳娜二世。\n\n用狄德罗的话说，《百科全书》要“改变人们普遍的思维方式”。它依照改编自培根的知识之树编排条目，让手艺和机械与神学享有同等的尊严，还把对教会与国家的批评藏进条目之间的互见注释里。在《达朗贝尔之梦》（1769年写成，1830年出版）等私下写作中，他发展出一种唯物主义：感受性是物质本身的属性，生命与思想从有组织的物质中产生，无需灵魂——这预示了进化生物学的观念。他的《沙龙》还开创了现代艺术批评。"},"schools":["enlightenment"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Encyclopédie (chief editor)","zh":"《百科全书》（主编）"},"y":1751},{"t":{"en":"D’Alembert’s Dream","zh":"《达朗贝尔的梦》"},"y":1769}],"wiki":"Denis Diderot"},
  {"id":"adam-smith","tradition":"modern","born":1723,"died":1790,"life":{"en":"1723–1790","zh":"1723–1790年"},"name":{"en":"Adam Smith","zh":"亚当·斯密"},"role":{"en":"Scottish moral philosopher and founder of political economy","zh":"苏格兰道德哲学家，政治经济学的奠基人"},"idea":{"en":"Morality grows from sympathy — imagining ourselves in another’s place — and from an inner “impartial spectator”. In commerce, the division of labour and free exchange can serve the common good without anyone intending it.","zh":"道德源于同情——设身处地地想象他人的处境——以及内心的“公正的旁观者”。在商业中，分工与自由交换可以在无人有意为之的情况下促进公共利益。"},"detail":{"en":"Adam Smith was born in Kirkcaldy in Scotland, studied at Glasgow under Francis Hutcheson and then at Oxford, and in 1752 became professor of moral philosophy at Glasgow. In 1764 he left to tutor the young Duke of Buccleuch on a tour of France, where he met Quesnay and the physiocrats, then spent a decade writing The Wealth of Nations (1776). He ended his life as a commissioner of customs in Edinburgh. He was a close friend of Hume.\n\nThe Theory of Moral Sentiments (1759) explains morality through sympathy: we imagine ourselves into others’ situations, and judge our own conduct as an imagined impartial spectator would. The Wealth of Nations explains how nations grow rich: the division of labour multiplies productivity — his example is a pin factory where ten workers make thousands of pins a day — and exchange in free markets coordinates the work of strangers, as if by an “invisible hand”, though no one intends the common good. Smith also warned that merchants conspire against the public and that repetitive work dulls the mind. He founded classical economics.","zh":"亚当·斯密生于苏格兰的柯卡尔迪，先在格拉斯哥大学师从哈奇森，又到牛津求学，1752年出任格拉斯哥大学道德哲学教授。1764年他辞去教职，陪同年轻的巴克卢公爵游历法国，结识了魁奈和重农学派，随后用十年时间写成《国富论》（1776）。晚年他在爱丁堡任海关专员。他与休谟是挚友。\n\n《道德情操论》（1759）用“同情”解释道德：我们设身处地想象他人的处境，并像一位想象中的“公正旁观者”那样评判自己的行为。《国富论》解释国家如何致富：分工成倍提高了生产率——他举的例子是一家制针工场，十个工人一天能造出几千枚针；而自由市场中的交换协调着陌生人的劳动，仿佛有一只“看不见的手”，尽管谁也没有存心增进公共利益。斯密也警告商人会合谋损害公众，单调重复的劳动会使人头脑迟钝。他是古典经济学的奠基人。"},"schools":["enlightenment"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"The Theory of Moral Sentiments","zh":"《道德情操论》"},"y":1759},{"t":{"en":"The Wealth of Nations","zh":"《国富论》"},"y":1776}],"wiki":"Adam Smith"},
  {"id":"kant","tradition":"modern","born":1724,"died":1804,"life":{"en":"1724–1804","zh":"1724–1804年"},"name":{"en":"Immanuel Kant","zh":"伊曼努尔·康德"},"role":{"en":"Königsberg professor whose critical philosophy redrew modern thought","zh":"以批判哲学重塑近代思想的柯尼斯堡教授"},"idea":{"en":"The mind actively structures experience, so we can know appearances with certainty but never things in themselves. Morality is a law reason gives itself: act only on a maxim you could will to be a universal law.","zh":"心灵主动地构造经验，因此我们能确定地认识现象，却永远无法认识物自体。道德是理性为自己颁布的法则：只按照你能同时意愿它成为普遍法则的准则去行动。"},"detail":{"en":"Immanuel Kant spent his whole life in and around Königsberg in East Prussia, the son of a Pietist harness-maker. For decades he lectured at its university on everything from physics and logic to geography and anthropology. After a long “silent decade” he published the Critique of Pure Reason in 1781, at fifty-seven, and over the next ten years the works that reshaped ethics, aesthetics and political thought.\n\nKant’s “Copernican revolution” reverses the usual picture: instead of the mind conforming to objects, objects as we experience them must conform to the mind’s forms — space, time and categories such as cause and effect. That explains how we can know some things a priori, such as that every event has a cause, but it limits knowledge to appearances: God, freedom and the immortal soul lie beyond proof, though not beyond rational faith. In ethics, the moral law comes neither from God nor from the pursuit of happiness but from reason itself: act only on a maxim you could will as a universal law, and treat humanity never merely as a means. Almost every later school defines itself by how it answers him.","zh":"康德一生都生活在东普鲁士的柯尼斯堡及其周边，父亲是虔敬派的马具匠。他在柯尼斯堡大学讲课几十年，内容从物理学、逻辑学一直到地理学和人类学。经过漫长的“沉默的十年”，他在1781年、五十七岁时出版了《纯粹理性批判》，此后十年间接连发表了重塑伦理学、美学和政治思想的著作。\n\n康德的“哥白尼式革命”颠倒了通常的图景：不是心灵符合对象，而是我们所经验的对象必须符合心灵的形式——空间、时间以及因果等范畴。这解释了我们为何能先天地认识某些事情，比如每个事件都有原因；但它也把知识限制在现象之内：上帝、自由和不朽的灵魂超出了证明的范围，却未超出理性信仰的范围。在伦理学中，道德法则既不来自上帝，也不来自对幸福的追求，而来自理性自身：只按你愿意它成为普遍法则的准则行动，永远不要仅仅把人当作手段。几乎所有后来的学派，都以如何回应他来界定自己。"},"schools":["kantianism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Critique of Pure Reason","zh":"《纯粹理性批判》"},"y":1781},{"t":{"en":"Groundwork of the Metaphysics of Morals","zh":"《道德形而上学奠基》"},"y":1785},{"t":{"en":"Critique of Practical Reason","zh":"《实践理性批判》"},"y":1788}],"wiki":"Immanuel Kant","c":"Immanuel Kant - Gemaelde 1.jpg","portrait":{"w":600,"h":784,"v":"688e05ed3d","author":"Johann Gottlieb Becker (1720-1782)","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":null,"page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Immanuel_Kant_-_Gemaelde_1.jpg"}},
  {"id":"bentham","tradition":"modern","born":1748,"died":1832,"life":{"en":"1748–1832","zh":"1748–1832年"},"name":{"en":"Jeremy Bentham","zh":"杰里米·边沁"},"role":{"en":"English legal reformer who founded utilitarianism","zh":"创立功利主义的英国法律改革家"},"idea":{"en":"Nature has placed humankind under two sovereign masters, pain and pleasure, so laws should aim at the greatest happiness of the greatest number. He extended moral concern to animals: “The question is not, Can they reason? nor, Can they talk? but, Can they suffer?”","zh":"自然把人类置于痛苦与快乐这两位主宰之下，因此法律应当以最大多数人的最大幸福为目标。他把道德关怀扩展到动物：“问题不在于它们能否推理，也不在于它们能否说话，而在于它们能否感受痛苦。”"},"detail":{"en":"Jeremy Bentham, the son of a London attorney, entered Oxford at twelve and was trained for the bar, but was so repelled by the confusion of English law, and by the praise heaped on it by William Blackstone, that he devoted his life to reforming it. He wrote endlessly on law, prisons (including his “Panopticon” design), poor relief, elections and economics, and gathered disciples such as James Mill. At his own wish his preserved body, the “auto-icon”, is kept at University College London.\n\nBentham’s principle of utility says that the rightness of any action or law depends on its tendency to increase or reduce happiness, understood as pleasure and the absence of pain. Everyone’s happiness counts equally, and pleasures can be compared by their intensity, duration, certainty and so on. Laws should be codified, punishment should deter rather than avenge, and talk of natural rights was, he said, “nonsense upon stilts”. He argued privately for decriminalising homosexuality and held that animals count because they can suffer. His followers, the “philosophical radicals”, drove many nineteenth-century reforms.","zh":"边沁是伦敦一位律师的儿子，十二岁进入牛津，后受训成为律师；但英国法律的混乱，以及布莱克斯通对它的大肆赞美，令他极为反感，于是他把一生献给了法律改革。他就法律、监狱（包括著名的“全景监狱”设计）、济贫、选举和经济问题写下大量著作，詹姆斯·密尔等人追随他。按照他的遗愿，他的遗体被制成“自体圣像”，保存在伦敦大学学院。\n\n边沁的效用原则认为：任何行为或法律的对错，取决于它增加还是减少幸福，而幸福就是快乐和痛苦的免除。每个人的幸福同等计算，快乐可以按强度、持续时间、确定性等加以比较。法律应当编纂成典，刑罚应当用于威慑而非报复；至于“自然权利”之说，他称之为“踩着高跷的胡话”。他还私下主张同性恋不应入罪，并认为动物因为能感受痛苦而应被纳入道德考量。他的追随者“哲学激进派”推动了十九世纪的许多改革。"},"schools":["utilitarianism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"A Fragment on Government","zh":"《政府片论》"},"y":1776},{"t":{"en":"An Introduction to the Principles of Morals and Legislation","zh":"《道德与立法原理导论》"},"y":1789}],"wiki":"Jeremy Bentham"},
  {"id":"wollstonecraft","tradition":"modern","born":1759,"died":1797,"life":{"en":"1759–1797","zh":"1759–1797年"},"name":{"en":"Mary Wollstonecraft","zh":"玛丽·沃斯通克拉夫特"},"role":{"en":"English writer who argued that women are rational beings owed education","zh":"主张女性是理性存在、理应受教育的英国作家"},"idea":{"en":"Women seem weak and frivolous not by nature but because they are denied education and treated as ornaments. If rights rest on reason, women share them, and society needs them as educated, independent citizens.","zh":"女性显得软弱轻浮，不是出于天性，而是因为她们被剥夺了教育、被当作装饰品。如果权利以理性为基础，女性就同样享有权利，社会也需要她们成为受过教育、独立自主的公民。"},"detail":{"en":"Mary Wollstonecraft grew up in a family impoverished by her father’s failures and violence. She ran a school at Newington Green, worked as a governess in Ireland, and then in London as a writer and reviewer for the radical publisher Joseph Johnson. Her reply to Edmund Burke’s attack on the French Revolution made her name in 1790, and two years later she published A Vindication of the Rights of Woman. She lived in revolutionary Paris, had a daughter with Gilbert Imlay, and later married the philosopher William Godwin; she died in 1797, days after giving birth to Mary, the future author of Frankenstein.\n\nHer argument starts from the Enlightenment claim that rights rest on reason. If women are rational beings, they share those rights; if they seem weak, vain and frivolous, it is because they are educated only to please men, as Rousseau recommended in Emile. Give girls the same education as boys, she argued, and they would become virtuous companions, wise mothers and useful citizens able to support themselves. Long overshadowed by scandal about her private life, her book is now read as a founding text of feminism.","zh":"玛丽·沃斯通克拉夫特的家庭因父亲经营失败和酗酒暴力而陷入贫困。她在纽因顿格林办过学校，在爱尔兰当过家庭教师，后来在伦敦为激进派出版商约瑟夫·约翰逊撰稿和写书评。1790年，她回应埃德蒙·伯克对法国大革命的攻击，由此成名；两年后出版了《女权辩护》。她曾在大革命时期的巴黎生活，与吉尔伯特·伊姆利生下一个女儿，后来嫁给哲学家威廉·葛德文；1797年，她在生下女儿玛丽（日后《弗兰肯斯坦》的作者）几天后去世。\n\n她的论证从启蒙运动的主张出发：权利建立在理性之上。如果女性是理性的存在，她们就同样享有这些权利；如果她们显得软弱、虚荣、轻浮，那是因为她们受的教育只教她们取悦男人，正如卢梭在《爱弥儿》中所主张的那样。她认为，让女孩接受和男孩一样的教育，她们就会成为有德行的伴侣、明智的母亲和能够自立的有用公民。这本书长期被她私生活的丑闻所遮蔽，如今被视为女性主义的奠基之作。"},"schools":["feminism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"A Vindication of the Rights of Men","zh":"《为男权辩护》"},"y":1790},{"t":{"en":"A Vindication of the Rights of Woman","zh":"《女权辩护》"},"y":1792}],"wiki":"Mary Wollstonecraft","c":"Mary Wollstonecraft Portrait.jpg","portrait":{"w":600,"h":724,"v":"913f30723d","author":"John Opie","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":null,"page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mary_Wollstonecraft_Portrait.jpg"}},
  {"id":"fichte","tradition":"modern","born":1762,"died":1814,"life":{"en":"1762–1814","zh":"1762–1814年"},"name":{"en":"Johann Gottlieb Fichte","zh":"约翰·戈特利布·费希特"},"role":{"en":"German idealist who grounded all knowledge in the self’s activity","zh":"把一切知识奠基于自我活动的德国观念论者"},"idea":{"en":"Kant’s thing in itself must go: philosophy should start from the I that “posits” itself and, in striving, posits a world that resists it. His Addresses to the German Nation (1808) made him a voice of German nationalism.","zh":"康德的物自体必须取消：哲学应当从“设定”自身的自我出发，自我在奋进中设定一个与之对立的世界。他的《对德意志民族的演讲》（1808）使他成为德意志民族主义的代言人。"},"detail":{"en":"Johann Gottlieb Fichte was the son of a poor ribbon-weaver in Saxony; a local nobleman, impressed by the boy, paid for his schooling. His first book, Attempt at a Critique of All Revelation (1792), appeared anonymously and was at first taken for Kant’s, which made him famous overnight. He taught at Jena until the “atheism dispute” forced him out in 1799, then moved to Berlin, where he gave his Addresses to the German Nation (1807–08) under French occupation. He died in 1814 of typhus caught from his wife, who had been nursing soldiers.\n\nFichte thought Kant’s “thing in itself” — an unknowable cause of our sensations — made no sense. Philosophy should begin instead from the activity of the self: the I posits itself, and in order to act it must also posit something that resists it, the not-I. The world exists as the arena of our moral striving; freedom comes first. He rewrote his Science of Knowledge many times. Politically he moved from defending the French Revolution to German nationalism. His self-positing subject opened the way for Schelling and Hegel.","zh":"费希特是萨克森一个贫穷织带工的儿子；当地一位贵族赏识这个孩子，资助他上学。他的第一本书《试评一切天启》（1792）匿名出版，起初被当成康德的作品，他因此一夜成名。他在耶拿任教，直到1799年因“无神论之争”被迫离职，后迁居柏林，在法军占领下发表了《对德意志民族的演讲》（1807–1808）。1814年，他的妻子在照料伤兵时染上斑疹伤寒，又传给了他，他因此去世。\n\n费希特认为，康德的“物自体”——我们感觉的一个不可知的原因——是说不通的。哲学应当从自我的活动出发：自我设定自身，而为了行动，它还必须设定一个与之对抗的东西，即“非我”。世界作为我们道德奋斗的舞台而存在；自由是首要的。他的《知识学》改写了许多遍。政治上，他从为法国大革命辩护转向德意志民族主义。他的“自我设定”的主体，为谢林和黑格尔开辟了道路。"},"schools":["german-idealism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Foundations of the Entire Science of Knowledge","zh":"《全部知识学的基础》"},"y":1794},{"t":{"en":"Addresses to the German Nation","zh":"《对德意志民族的演讲》"},"y":1808}],"wiki":"Johann Gottlieb Fichte"},
  {"id":"hegel","tradition":"modern","born":1770,"died":1831,"life":{"en":"1770–1831","zh":"1770–1831年"},"name":{"en":"Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel","zh":"格奥尔格·威廉·弗里德里希·黑格尔"},"role":{"en":"German philosopher of spirit, history and dialectic","zh":"研究精神、历史与辩证法的德国哲学家"},"idea":{"en":"Reality is spirit coming to know itself through a dialectical process in which every limited position produces its own contradiction and is overcome. World history is the progress of the consciousness of freedom.","zh":"实在是精神通过辩证过程认识自身：每一个有限的立场都会产生自身的矛盾并被扬弃。世界历史就是自由意识的进展。"},"detail":{"en":"Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel studied at the Tübingen seminary with Schelling and Hölderlin and worked for years as a private tutor. He finished the Phenomenology of Spirit at Jena in 1806 as Napoleon’s army fought outside the town. He then edited a newspaper in Bamberg and ran a school in Nuremberg, before professorships at Heidelberg and, from 1818, Berlin, where he became the most influential philosopher in Germany. He died in 1831, probably of cholera.\n\nHegel’s guiding claim is that “the true is the whole”. Any limited idea or way of life, followed to its consequences, runs into contradictions that force it to be taken up into a richer one; he called this movement dialectic. The Phenomenology follows consciousness through such stages, including the struggle between master and slave, until spirit comes to understand itself. History too is the story of freedom becoming conscious: in the ancient East one was free, in Greece and Rome some, in the modern world all. His followers split into conservatives and radicals, and Marx, Kierkegaard and the pragmatists all began by arguing with him.","zh":"黑格尔在图宾根神学院与谢林、荷尔德林同学，此后多年当家庭教师。1806年拿破仑的军队在耶拿城外交战时，他正在完成《精神现象学》。随后他在班贝格编过报纸，在纽伦堡主持过中学，之后先后在海德堡和（1818年起）柏林任教授，成为德国最有影响的哲学家。1831年他去世，很可能死于霍乱。\n\n黑格尔的核心主张是“真理是整体”。任何有限的观念或生活方式，一旦推到底，都会陷入矛盾，迫使它被扬弃到更丰富的形态中；他把这一运动称为辩证法。《精神现象学》追随意识走过这样的一个个阶段，包括主人与奴隶的斗争，直到精神理解了自身。历史同样是自由逐步被意识到的过程：古代东方只有一人自由，希腊罗马有些人自由，现代世界则人人自由。他的追随者分裂为保守派与激进派；马克思、克尔凯郭尔和实用主义者，都是从与他争论开始的。"},"schools":["german-idealism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Phenomenology of Spirit","zh":"《精神现象学》"},"y":1807},{"t":{"en":"Science of Logic","zh":"《逻辑学》"},"y":1812},{"t":{"en":"Elements of the Philosophy of Right","zh":"《法哲学原理》"},"y":1820}],"wiki":"Georg Wilhelm Friedrich Hegel","c":"Jakob Schlesinger - Hegel 1831.jpg","portrait":{"w":600,"h":757,"v":"c362aab162","author":"Jakob Schlesinger","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":null,"page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jakob_Schlesinger_-_Hegel_1831.jpg"}},
  {"id":"schelling","tradition":"modern","born":1775,"died":1854,"life":{"en":"1775–1854","zh":"1775–1854年"},"name":{"en":"Friedrich Wilhelm Joseph Schelling","zh":"弗里德里希·谢林"},"role":{"en":"German idealist philosopher of nature, art and freedom","zh":"研究自然、艺术与自由的德国观念论哲学家"},"idea":{"en":"Nature and mind are two expressions of one absolute: nature is visible spirit, spirit invisible nature. Early on he saw art as the place where this unity shows itself; later he turned to freedom, evil and the limits of reason.","zh":"自然与心灵是同一个绝对者的两种表现：自然是可见的精神，精神是不可见的自然。早年他认为艺术是这种统一显现之处；后来他转向自由、恶以及理性的界限等问题。"},"detail":{"en":"Friedrich Wilhelm Joseph Schelling studied theology at the Tübingen seminary, where he shared rooms with Hegel and the poet Hölderlin. A prodigy, he became a professor at Jena at twenty-three, close to the Romantic circle there. His early systems made him famous; later, as Hegel eclipsed him, he published little for decades, until in 1841 the Prussian king called him to Berlin to counter Hegel’s legacy. Kierkegaard, Engels and Bakunin were among his listeners.\n\nAgainst Fichte, Schelling held that nature is not just raw material for the self’s striving: it is itself alive and productive, “visible spirit”, while mind is nature become aware of itself. Both spring from an absolute in which subject and object are one. In the System of Transcendental Idealism (1800), art is where this unity shows itself, since a work of genius is at once free and necessary. His later essay on human freedom (1809) asked how evil is possible in a world grounded in God, and his late philosophy insisted that existence cannot be derived from concepts — a theme the existentialists took up.","zh":"谢林在图宾根神学院学习，与黑格尔和诗人荷尔德林同住一室。他少年早慧，二十三岁便成为耶拿大学教授，与当地的浪漫派圈子过从甚密。早期的体系使他声名鹊起；后来黑格尔的光芒盖过了他，他几十年间很少发表著作，直到1841年普鲁士国王召他到柏林，去抵消黑格尔的影响。克尔凯郭尔、恩格斯和巴枯宁都曾是他的听众。\n\n与费希特不同，谢林认为自然不只是自我奋斗的原料：自然本身是活的、能创造的，是“可见的精神”，而心灵则是意识到自身的自然。二者都出自一个主体与客体合一的绝对。在《先验唯心论体系》（1800）中，艺术是这种统一显现之处，因为天才的作品既自由又必然。他后来的《论人类自由的本质》（1809）追问：在以上帝为根据的世界里，恶何以可能？晚期哲学则坚持存在不能从概念中推导出来——这一主题后来被存在主义者接了过去。"},"schools":["german-idealism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"System of Transcendental Idealism","zh":"《先验唯心论体系》"},"y":1800},{"t":{"en":"Philosophical Investigations into the Essence of Human Freedom","zh":"《论人类自由的本质》"},"y":1809}],"wiki":"Friedrich Wilhelm Joseph Schelling"},
  {"id":"schopenhauer","tradition":"modern","born":1788,"died":1860,"life":{"en":"1788–1860","zh":"1788–1860年"},"name":{"en":"Arthur Schopenhauer","zh":"亚瑟·叔本华"},"role":{"en":"German pessimist who saw a blind will behind all things","zh":"认为万物背后是盲目意志的德国悲观主义者"},"idea":{"en":"Behind the world as representation lies one blind, insatiable striving — the will — that makes life swing between pain and boredom. Relief comes through art, compassion and the denial of the will, ideas he found echoed in Indian thought.","zh":"作为表象的世界背后，是一个盲目而永不满足的冲动——意志——它让人生在痛苦与无聊之间摇摆。解脱之道在于艺术、同情与意志的否定，他认为这些思想与印度思想遥相呼应。"},"detail":{"en":"Arthur Schopenhauer was the son of a wealthy Danzig merchant and of the novelist Johanna Schopenhauer. After his father’s death he abandoned a business career, studied at Göttingen and Berlin, where Fichte was among his teachers, and published The World as Will and Representation at the end of 1818, aged thirty. His attempt to teach in Berlin failed when he scheduled his lectures at the same hour as Hegel’s. Living on his inheritance in Frankfurt, he was largely ignored until a collection of essays, Parerga and Paralipomena (1851), made him famous in his last decade.\n\nSchopenhauer accepted Kant’s division between appearance and thing in itself, but claimed we know the thing in itself from within: in our own bodies we experience it as will — blind, endless striving. The whole world is this one will, appearing as countless things that struggle against each other. Because desire is never satisfied for long, life swings between pain and boredom. Relief comes through art, above all music, through compassion, which recognises the same suffering in others, and through ascetic denial of the will — ideas he found echoed in Buddhism and the Upanishads. Nietzsche, Wagner and Freud read him closely.","zh":"叔本华的父亲是但泽的富商，母亲是小说家约翰娜·叔本华。父亲去世后他放弃经商，先后在哥廷根和柏林求学，费希特也是他的老师之一；1818年底，三十岁的他出版了《作为意志和表象的世界》。他想在柏林大学授课，却把课排在与黑格尔相同的时段，结果失败。此后他靠遗产住在法兰克福，长期无人问津，直到随笔集《附录与补遗》（1851）让他在晚年最后十年声名大噪。\n\n叔本华接受康德对现象与物自体的区分，但认为我们可以从内部认识物自体：在自己的身体里，我们把它体验为意志——盲目的、无尽的冲动。整个世界就是这唯一的意志，显现为无数相互争斗的事物。欲望从来得不到长久的满足，人生便在痛苦与无聊之间摇摆。解脱之路在于艺术（尤其是音乐）、在他人身上认出同样痛苦的同情，以及禁欲式地否定意志——他认为佛教和《奥义书》里有同样的思想。尼采、瓦格纳和弗洛伊德都细读过他。"},"schools":[],"works":[{"t":{"en":"The World as Will and Representation","zh":"《作为意志和表象的世界》"},"y":1818},{"t":{"en":"On the Basis of Morality","zh":"《论道德的基础》"},"y":1840}],"wiki":"Arthur Schopenhauer"},
  {"id":"mill","tradition":"modern","born":1806,"died":1873,"life":{"en":"1806–1873","zh":"1806–1873年"},"name":{"en":"John Stuart Mill","zh":"约翰·斯图尔特·密尔"},"role":{"en":"English philosopher of liberty, utility and women’s equality","zh":"论述自由、功利与女性平等的英国哲学家"},"idea":{"en":"The only reason to interfere with anyone’s liberty against their will is to prevent harm to others. He refined utilitarianism by distinguishing higher from lower pleasures, and argued for the full legal equality of women.","zh":"违背一个人的意志而干涉其自由，唯一正当的理由是防止他伤害他人。他区分了高级快乐与低级快乐，以此修正功利主义，并主张女性应享有完全的法律平等。"},"detail":{"en":"John Stuart Mill was educated by his father, James Mill, in an experiment of intensive schooling: Greek at three, Latin at eight, political economy at thirteen. At twenty he fell into a depression that convinced him that analysis alone starves the feelings, and he found relief in Wordsworth’s poetry. He worked for the East India Company for thirty-five years, wrote major books on logic, economics, liberty and the position of women, much of it in partnership with Harriet Taylor, whom he married in 1851, and sat in Parliament from 1865 to 1868.\n\nMill revised Bentham’s utilitarianism: some pleasures, those of intellect and feeling, are higher than others — “better to be Socrates dissatisfied than a fool satisfied”. On Liberty defends the harm principle: society may coerce someone only to prevent harm to others, never for their own good, and free discussion is needed because even true opinions turn into dead dogma when no one challenges them. The Subjection of Women argued that women’s legal subordination was a relic of the rule of force, and in 1867 he proposed in Parliament to give women the vote. He remains the classic voice of liberalism.","zh":"约翰·斯图尔特·密尔由父亲詹姆斯·密尔亲自施教，这是一场强化教育的实验：三岁学希腊文，八岁学拉丁文，十三岁学政治经济学。二十岁时他陷入抑郁，由此认定单靠分析会使情感枯竭，最终在华兹华斯的诗中得到慰藉。他在东印度公司工作了三十五年，写下逻辑学、经济学、自由与女性地位方面的重要著作，其中许多是与1851年结婚的哈丽雅特·泰勒合作完成的；1865至1868年他还担任过国会议员。\n\n密尔修正了边沁的功利主义：有些快乐——智识与情感的快乐——比其他快乐更高级，“做一个不满足的苏格拉底，胜过做一个满足的傻瓜”。《论自由》捍卫“伤害原则”：社会只有为防止伤害他人才可以强制一个人，绝不能以为他好为理由；自由讨论必不可少，因为即使是真理，无人挑战也会变成僵死的教条。《妇女的屈从地位》指出，女性在法律上的从属地位是强权统治的遗迹；1867年他在国会提议给予女性选举权。他至今仍是自由主义的经典声音。"},"schools":["utilitarianism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"On Liberty","zh":"《论自由》"},"y":1859},{"t":{"en":"Utilitarianism","zh":"《功利主义》"},"y":1861},{"t":{"en":"The Subjection of Women","zh":"《妇女的屈从地位》"},"y":1869}],"wiki":"John Stuart Mill"},
  {"id":"schleiermacher","tradition":"continental","born":1768,"died":1834,"life":{"en":"1768–1834","zh":"1768–1834年"},"name":{"en":"Friedrich Schleiermacher","zh":"弗里德里希·施莱尔马赫"},"role":{"en":"Berlin theologian and translator of Plato, father of modern hermeneutics","zh":"柏林神学家、柏拉图的译者，现代诠释学之父"},"idea":{"en":"Schleiermacher turned interpretation into a general art of understanding: to understand a text we must reconstruct both its language and its author’s individual way of thinking, since misunderstanding is the normal case. He also grounded religion in a feeling of absolute dependence.","zh":"施莱尔马赫把诠释变成一门普遍的理解艺术：要理解文本，既要重构其语言，也要重构作者独特的思维方式，因为误解才是常态。他还把宗教建立在“绝对依赖感”之上。"},"detail":{"en":"Friedrich Schleiermacher (1768–1834) grew up among the Moravian Pietists, studied at Halle and joined Friedrich Schlegel’s Romantic circle in Berlin. His On Religion (1799) defended faith to its “cultured despisers”; he translated almost all of Plato into German and helped plan the new University of Berlin, where he taught theology from 1810.\n\nHis lectures on hermeneutics, given from 1805 to 1833, turned interpretation from a set of rules for reading Scripture or law into a general discipline. Understanding, he argued, has two sides: the grammatical (what the words could mean in their language) and the psychological (how this author, with this way of thinking, used them). Because misunderstanding is the normal case, understanding must be worked for at every point. Dilthey revived this programme; Gadamer later criticised its aim of reconstructing the author’s mind.","zh":"施莱尔马赫（1768–1834）在摩拉维亚虔敬派的环境中长大，就读于哈雷大学，后来在柏林加入了弗里德里希·施莱格尔的浪漫派圈子。他的《论宗教》（1799）向“有教养的蔑视者”为信仰辩护；他把柏拉图的著作几乎全部译成德文，并参与筹建柏林大学，自1810年起在那里讲授神学。\n\n他从1805年到1833年反复讲授诠释学，使理解不再只是解读《圣经》或法律的规则，而成为一门普遍的学问。他认为理解有两面：语法的一面，看这些词在其语言中可能意味着什么；心理的一面，看这位作者以其独特的思路如何使用它们。既然误解才是常态，理解就必须在每一处刻意追求。狄尔泰复兴了这一纲领，伽达默尔则批评它试图重建作者心灵的目标。"},"schools":["hermeneutics"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"On Religion: Speeches to Its Cultured Despisers","zh":"《论宗教：对蔑视宗教的有教养者的讲话》"},"y":1799},{"t":{"en":"Hermeneutics and Criticism","zh":"《诠释学与批判》"},"y":1838}],"wiki":"Friedrich Schleiermacher"},
  {"id":"kierkegaard","tradition":"continental","born":1813,"died":1855,"life":{"en":"1813–1855","zh":"1813–1855年"},"name":{"en":"Søren Kierkegaard","zh":"索伦·克尔凯郭尔"},"role":{"en":"Danish Christian thinker of the anxious, choosing individual","zh":"丹麦基督教思想家，关注焦虑而必须抉择的个人"},"idea":{"en":"Against Hegel’s system, Kierkegaard insisted that truth is subjectivity: what matters is how a single person lives, and faith is a passionate leap that no reasoning can secure.","zh":"克尔凯郭尔反对黑格尔的体系，主张“真理即主体性”：重要的是一个具体的人如何生活；信仰是理性无法担保的激情一跃。"},"detail":{"en":"Søren Kierkegaard (1813–1855) spent almost his whole life in Copenhagen, living on an inheritance from his father, a wealthy merchant. After breaking off his engagement to Regine Olsen in 1841 he poured out books, many under pseudonyms such as “Johannes de silentio”, so that readers would meet different views of life without being told which was his. In his last years he attacked the Danish state church for turning Christianity into a comfortable convention.\n\nAgainst Hegel’s system, which absorbed every individual into world history, Kierkegaard insisted that existence cannot be thought from outside. In Fear and Trembling he asks how Abraham could be willing to sacrifice Isaac: no universal ethics can justify it, so faith is a leap made in fear and trembling. He described the aesthetic, ethical and religious stages of life, and read anxiety and despair as signs of our freedom. Heidegger, Sartre and other existentialists took up these themes.","zh":"克尔凯郭尔（1813–1855）几乎一生都住在哥本哈根，靠父亲——一位富商——留下的遗产生活。1841年他解除了与雷吉娜·奥尔森的婚约，此后著述如泉涌，许多书用“沉默的约翰尼斯”等假名发表，让读者直接面对各种人生观，而不告诉他们哪一种是作者自己的。晚年，他猛烈抨击丹麦国教会把基督教变成了舒适的习俗。\n\n黑格尔的体系把每个个人都吸纳进世界历史，克尔凯郭尔则坚持：生存无法从外部去思考。在《恐惧与战栗》中，他追问亚伯拉罕怎么会愿意献祭以撒：任何普遍的伦理都无法为此辩护，所以信仰是一次在恐惧与战栗中的跳跃。他把人生分为审美、伦理、宗教三个阶段，并把焦虑与绝望解读为人之自由的标志。海德格尔、萨特等存在主义者继承了这些主题。"},"schools":["existentialism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Either/Or","zh":"《非此即彼》"},"y":1843},{"t":{"en":"Fear and Trembling","zh":"《恐惧与战栗》"},"y":1843},{"t":{"en":"The Sickness unto Death","zh":"《致死的疾病》"},"y":1849}],"wiki":"Søren Kierkegaard","c":"Søren Kierkegaard (1813-1855) - (cropped).jpg","portrait":{"w":564,"h":796,"v":"aa0281b9f1","author":"La Biblioteca Real de Dinamarca","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":null,"page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:S%C3%B8ren_Kierkegaard_(1813-1855)_-_(cropped).jpg"}},
  {"id":"marx","tradition":"continental","born":1818,"died":1883,"life":{"en":"1818–1883","zh":"1818–1883年"},"name":{"en":"Karl Marx","zh":"卡尔·马克思"},"role":{"en":"German philosopher, economist and revolutionary in London exile","zh":"流亡伦敦的德国哲学家、经济学家与革命家"},"idea":{"en":"Marx turned Hegel’s dialectic into historical materialism: the way a society produces its means of life shapes its politics and ideas, and capitalism’s class conflict points beyond it.","zh":"马克思把黑格尔的辩证法改造成历史唯物主义：一个社会生产生活资料的方式决定其政治与观念，而资本主义的阶级冲突预示着对它的超越。"},"detail":{"en":"Karl Marx (1818–1883) was born in Trier, studied law and philosophy in Bonn and Berlin, where he joined the Young Hegelians, and became a radical journalist. Expelled from Paris, Brussels and Cologne in turn, he settled in London in 1849 and spent decades in the British Museum reading room, supported largely by Friedrich Engels, while living through poverty and the deaths of several of his children.\n\nMarx kept Hegel’s idea that history moves through conflict but grounded it in material life: how people produce food, clothing and shelter, and who owns the means of production, shapes law, politics and ideas. In Capital he argued that profit comes from surplus value: workers sell their labour-power for a wage but produce more value than the wage, and the owner keeps the difference. Competition and crises, he predicted, would push capitalism towards its own overthrow. His work inspired socialist movements, revolutions and whole schools of social theory.","zh":"马克思（1818–1883）生于特里尔，在波恩和柏林学习法律与哲学，加入了青年黑格尔派，后来成为激进的报人。他先后被逐出巴黎、布鲁塞尔和科隆，1849年定居伦敦，此后几十年在大英博物馆阅览室里读书写作，主要靠恩格斯接济，其间饱受贫困之苦，还失去了几个孩子。\n\n马克思保留了黑格尔“历史在冲突中前进”的思想，却把它建立在物质生活之上：人们怎样生产衣食住所、谁占有生产资料，决定了法律、政治与观念。在《资本论》中，他论证利润来自剩余价值：工人出卖劳动力得到工资，生产出的价值却多于工资，差额归资本家所有。他预言，竞争与危机将推动资本主义走向自身的颠覆。他的著作激发了社会主义运动、革命以及整整几派社会理论。"},"schools":["marxism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Economic and Philosophic Manuscripts of 1844","zh":"《1844年经济学哲学手稿》"},"y":1844},{"t":{"en":"The Communist Manifesto (with Engels)","zh":"《共产党宣言》（与恩格斯合著）"},"y":1848},{"t":{"en":"Capital, Volume I","zh":"《资本论》第一卷"},"y":1867}],"wiki":"Karl Marx","c":"Karl Marx by John Jabez Edwin Mayall 1875 - Restored.png","portrait":{"w":600,"h":889,"v":"d97752ac80","author":"John Jabez Edwin Mayall","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":null,"page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karl_Marx_by_John_Jabez_Edwin_Mayall_1875_-_Restored.png"}},
  {"id":"engels","tradition":"continental","born":1820,"died":1895,"life":{"en":"1820–1895","zh":"1820–1895年"},"name":{"en":"Friedrich Engels","zh":"弗里德里希·恩格斯"},"role":{"en":"Manufacturer’s son who co-founded Marxism with Marx","zh":"工厂主之子，与马克思共同创立马克思主义"},"idea":{"en":"Engels documented industrial poverty first-hand, financed and edited Marx’s work, and popularised Marxism as a scientific worldview, extending the dialectic to nature and to the origins of the family.","zh":"恩格斯亲身记录了工业时代的贫困，资助并编辑马克思的著作，把马克思主义普及为一种“科学的世界观”，还把辩证法推广到自然界以及家庭的起源。"},"detail":{"en":"Friedrich Engels (1820–1895) was the eldest son of a cotton-mill owner in Barmen, Prussia. Sent in 1842 to the family firm’s mill near Manchester, he saw the industrial slums at first hand and wrote The Condition of the Working Class in England (1845). He began his lifelong partnership with Marx in Paris in 1844, co-wrote The Communist Manifesto, and from 1850 worked in the Manchester business for two decades, partly to support Marx.\n\nEngels was more than Marx’s patron. After Marx died he edited the second and third volumes of Capital from the manuscripts, and in Anti-Dühring and other works he presented Marxism as a scientific worldview, extending the dialectic from society to nature. The Origin of the Family, Private Property and the State argued that women’s subordination arose with private property and the patriarchal family, a thesis feminists later debated. Much of what the twentieth century called “Marxism” reached it through his popularisations.","zh":"恩格斯（1820–1895）是普鲁士巴门一位棉纺厂主的长子。1842年，他被派往家族企业在曼彻斯特附近的工厂，亲眼看到工业贫民窟的景象，写成《英国工人阶级状况》（1845）。1844年他在巴黎与马克思开始了终身的合作，两人合写了《共产党宣言》；从1850年起，他在曼彻斯特的家族生意中工作了二十年，部分是为了接济马克思。\n\n恩格斯不只是马克思的资助者。马克思去世后，他根据手稿编成《资本论》第二、三卷；在《反杜林论》等著作中，他把马克思主义阐述为一种科学的世界观，把辩证法从社会推广到自然。《家庭、私有制和国家的起源》主张，女性的从属地位是随着私有制和父权家庭出现的，这一论点后来引起女性主义者的讨论。二十世纪所说的“马克思主义”，很大程度上是经由他的普及才为人所知的。"},"schools":["marxism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"The Condition of the Working Class in England","zh":"《英国工人阶级状况》"},"y":1845},{"t":{"en":"Anti-Dühring","zh":"《反杜林论》"},"y":1878},{"t":{"en":"The Origin of the Family, Private Property and the State","zh":"《家庭、私有制和国家的起源》"},"y":1884}],"wiki":"Friedrich Engels"},
  {"id":"dilthey","tradition":"continental","born":1833,"died":1911,"life":{"en":"1833–1911","zh":"1833–1911年"},"name":{"en":"Wilhelm Dilthey","zh":"威廉·狄尔泰"},"role":{"en":"German historian of ideas who defended the human sciences","zh":"德国思想史家，为人文科学的独立地位辩护"},"idea":{"en":"Dilthey argued that nature is explained but human life is understood: the human sciences grasp lived experience and its expressions from the inside, through historical understanding rather than causal laws.","zh":"狄尔泰认为，自然要被说明，人的生命则要被理解：人文科学要从内部把握亲历的生命及其表达，依靠的是历史性的理解，而非因果规律。"},"detail":{"en":"Wilhelm Dilthey (1833–1911), the son of a Reformed pastor in Nassau, studied theology before turning to history and philosophy, and in 1882 took the Berlin chair once held by Hegel. He wrote a large biography of Schleiermacher and spent his career on a project he never finished: a “critique of historical reason” that would do for the human sciences what Kant had done for natural science.\n\nHis central claim is that the human sciences, such as history, law, literature and psychology, have their own method. Nature we explain from outside, by causes and laws; human life we understand from inside, because we share the experiences that actions, texts and institutions express. A historian grasps a letter or a law by re-living its meaning within a wider context, moving back and forth between part and whole. This made hermeneutics the method of the humanities and shaped Heidegger, Gadamer and German sociology.","zh":"狄尔泰（1833–1911）是拿骚一位归正宗牧师之子，先学神学，后转向历史与哲学，1882年接任了黑格尔曾经执掌的柏林大学教席。他写过一部施莱尔马赫的大部头传记，毕生致力于一项未竟的计划：一部“历史理性批判”，要为人文科学做康德为自然科学所做的事。\n\n他的核心主张是：历史、法律、文学、心理学等人文科学有自己的方法。对于自然，我们从外部用原因与规律去说明；对于人的生活，我们从内部去理解，因为行动、文本与制度所表达的经验，我们自己也有。历史学家理解一封信或一条法律，是在更大的语境中重新体验它的意义，在部分与整体之间往返。这使诠释学成为人文学科的方法，并深刻影响了海德格尔、伽达默尔和德国社会学。"},"schools":["hermeneutics"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Introduction to the Human Sciences","zh":"《精神科学引论》"},"y":1883},{"t":{"en":"The Formation of the Historical World in the Human Sciences","zh":"《精神科学中历史世界的建构》"},"y":1910}],"wiki":"Wilhelm Dilthey"},
  {"id":"nietzsche","tradition":"continental","born":1844,"died":1900,"life":{"en":"1844–1900","zh":"1844–1900年"},"name":{"en":"Friedrich Nietzsche","zh":"弗里德里希·尼采"},"role":{"en":"German philologist who diagnosed the death of God","zh":"德国古典语文学家，诊断了“上帝之死”"},"idea":{"en":"Nietzsche argued that once belief in God collapses, the values built on it lose their ground; he traced morality’s origins to resentment and the will to power, and called for a revaluation of values that affirms life.","zh":"尼采认为，一旦对上帝的信仰崩塌，建立在其上的价值便失去根基；他把道德的起源追溯到怨恨与权力意志，并呼吁“重估一切价值”，以肯定生命。"},"detail":{"en":"Friedrich Nietzsche (1844–1900), a Lutheran pastor’s son, became professor of classical philology at Basel at only 24. Ill health forced him to resign in 1879, and for a decade he moved between Swiss and Italian resorts, writing book after book that sold few copies. He collapsed mentally in Turin in January 1889 and never recovered; his sister later edited his notes and steered his reputation towards the German nationalism and antisemitism he had despised.\n\nNietzsche asked what follows once “God is dead”, that is, once belief in God can no longer ground European values. In On the Genealogy of Morality he argued that Christian morality grew from the resentment of the weak, who renamed the strong “evil” and their own weakness “good”. He saw life as will to power, imagined the Übermensch as one who creates values, and offered eternal recurrence as a test: could you will your life again, exactly as it was? Existentialists, Heidegger, Foucault and Deleuze all read him closely.","zh":"尼采（1844–1900）是路德宗牧师之子，年仅24岁就成为巴塞尔大学古典语文学教授。1879年他因病辞职，此后十年辗转于瑞士和意大利的疗养地，一本接一本地写书，却销量寥寥。1889年1月他在都灵精神崩溃，再未康复；他的妹妹后来编辑他的遗稿，把他的名声引向他本人所鄙视的德意志民族主义和反犹主义。\n\n尼采追问：“上帝死了”——也就是说，对上帝的信仰再也无法为欧洲的价值奠基——之后会怎样？在《论道德的谱系》中，他论证基督教道德源于弱者的怨恨：弱者把强者重新命名为“恶”，把自己的软弱说成“善”。他把生命理解为权力意志，设想能创造价值的“超人”，并提出“永恒轮回”作为一种考验：你是否愿意让自己的一生原样再来一次？存在主义者、海德格尔、福柯和德勒兹都细读过他。"},"schools":["existentialism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Thus Spoke Zarathustra","zh":"《查拉图斯特拉如是说》"},"y":1883},{"t":{"en":"Beyond Good and Evil","zh":"《善恶的彼岸》"},"y":1886},{"t":{"en":"On the Genealogy of Morality","zh":"《论道德的谱系》"},"y":1887}],"wiki":"Friedrich Nietzsche","c":"Nietzsche187a.jpg","portrait":{"w":600,"h":814,"v":"6fca40e237","author":"Friedrich Hermann Hartmann","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":null,"page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nietzsche187a.jpg"}},
  {"id":"saussure","tradition":"continental","born":1857,"died":1913,"life":{"en":"1857–1913","zh":"1857–1913年"},"name":{"en":"Ferdinand de Saussure","zh":"费迪南·德·索绪尔"},"role":{"en":"Geneva linguist whose lectures founded structural linguistics","zh":"日内瓦语言学家，其讲课奠定了结构语言学"},"idea":{"en":"Saussure treated language as a system of signs whose meanings come from their differences, with an arbitrary link between sound and concept, and proposed a general science of signs, semiology. His Course was compiled by students after his death.","zh":"索绪尔把语言视为一个符号系统，其意义来自彼此的差异，而声音与概念之间的联系是任意的；他还设想了一门研究一切符号的普通科学——符号学。《普通语言学教程》是他去世后由学生整理出版的。"},"detail":{"en":"Ferdinand de Saussure (1857–1913) was born in Geneva and made his name at 21 with a brilliant study of the vowel system of Indo-European. After teaching in Paris he returned to Geneva, where between 1907 and 1911 he gave three courses on general linguistics. He published almost nothing from them: the Course in General Linguistics (1916) was reconstructed after his death by his colleagues Charles Bally and Albert Sechehaye from students’ notes.\n\nSaussure separated language as a system (langue) from individual acts of speaking (parole), and studied the system as it stands at one moment rather than its history. A sign joins a sound-image (the signifier) to a concept (the signified), and the link is arbitrary: nothing in the sound “tree” resembles a tree. So a sign means what it does only through its differences from the other signs in the system. This idea, that meaning lies in relations rather than in things, became the founding principle of structuralism.","zh":"索绪尔（1857–1913）生于日内瓦，21岁时就以一部论印欧语元音系统的出色研究成名。他曾在巴黎任教，后回到日内瓦，在1907至1911年间三次讲授普通语言学。这些课程他本人几乎没有发表；《普通语言学教程》（1916）是他去世后，由同事巴利和薛施蔼根据学生笔记整理而成的。\n\n索绪尔把作为系统的语言（langue）与个人的言语行为（parole）区分开，并研究某一时刻存在的系统，而不是它的历史演变。一个符号把音响形象（能指）与概念（所指）联结起来，这种联结是任意的：“树”这个声音丝毫不像一棵树。因此，一个符号之所以有意义，只是因为它与系统中其他符号的差别。“意义在于关系而不在于事物”，这一思想成了结构主义的奠基原则。"},"schools":["structuralism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Course in General Linguistics","zh":"《普通语言学教程》"},"y":1916}],"wiki":"Ferdinand de Saussure"},
  {"id":"husserl","tradition":"continental","born":1859,"died":1938,"life":{"en":"1859–1938","zh":"1859–1938年"},"name":{"en":"Edmund Husserl","zh":"埃德蒙德·胡塞尔"},"role":{"en":"Moravian-born mathematician turned philosopher, founder of phenomenology","zh":"生于摩拉维亚、由数学转向哲学的现象学创始人"},"idea":{"en":"Husserl held that philosophy must become a rigorous science of consciousness: by bracketing our natural belief in the world, we can describe the intentional structures through which anything appears.","zh":"胡塞尔主张，哲学必须成为一门关于意识的严格科学：通过悬置对世界存在的自然信念，我们可以描述任何事物得以显现的意向性结构。"},"detail":{"en":"Edmund Husserl (1859–1938) was born into a Jewish family in Prossnitz, Moravia, took a doctorate in mathematics and turned to philosophy after hearing Franz Brentano in Vienna. He taught at Halle, Göttingen and, from 1916, Freiburg, where Heidegger became his assistant. After 1933 the Nazi regime treated him as a Jew and cut him off from academic life; after his death some 40,000 pages of his manuscripts were smuggled to Leuven, where they are still kept.\n\nHusserl wanted philosophy to become a rigorous science by returning “to the things themselves”, as they show up in experience. Consciousness, he held, is always consciousness of something (intentionality). To study it we perform the epoché: we bracket the everyday belief that the world exists independently, not to doubt it but to describe how a tree, a number or another person appears to us, and through which acts of perceiving, remembering or imagining. His method launched phenomenology and shaped Heidegger, Sartre, Merleau-Ponty and Levinas.","zh":"胡塞尔（1859–1938）出身于摩拉维亚普罗斯尼茨的一个犹太家庭，先获得数学博士学位，后在维也纳听布伦塔诺讲课而转向哲学。他先后执教于哈雷、哥廷根，1916年起在弗莱堡任教，海德格尔曾任他的助手。1933年后，纳粹政权视他为犹太人，把他排斥在学术生活之外；他去世后，约四万页手稿被秘密运往比利时鲁汶，至今保存在那里。\n\n胡塞尔希望哲学成为一门严格的科学，办法是“回到事物本身”，回到它们在经验中显现的样子。他认为意识总是关于某物的意识（意向性）。为了研究意识，我们要进行“悬搁”：把“世界独立存在”这一日常信念放进括号——不是怀疑它，而是去描述一棵树、一个数或另一个人如何向我们显现，又经由哪些知觉、回忆或想象的行为显现。他的方法开创了现象学，塑造了海德格尔、萨特、梅洛-庞蒂和列维纳斯。"},"schools":["phenomenology"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Logical Investigations","zh":"《逻辑研究》"},"y":1900},{"t":{"en":"Ideas I","zh":"《纯粹现象学通论》（《观念I》）"},"y":1913},{"t":{"en":"The Crisis of European Sciences and Transcendental Phenomenology","zh":"《欧洲科学的危机与超越论的现象学》"},"y":1936}],"wiki":"Edmund Husserl"},
  {"id":"heidegger","tradition":"continental","born":1889,"died":1976,"life":{"en":"1889–1976","zh":"1889–1976年"},"name":{"en":"Martin Heidegger","zh":"马丁·海德格尔"},"role":{"en":"German philosopher of Being, compromised by his Nazi party membership","zh":"追问“存在”的德国哲学家，因加入纳粹党而饱受争议"},"idea":{"en":"Heidegger argued that Western philosophy forgot the question of the meaning of Being; he analysed human existence (Dasein) as being-in-the-world, care and being-towards-death, and later criticised technology for revealing everything as a resource.","zh":"海德格尔认为，西方哲学遗忘了“存在的意义”这一问题；他把人的生存（此在）分析为“在世界之中存在”、操心与“向死而在”，后期又批判技术把一切都揭示为可用资源。"},"detail":{"en":"Martin Heidegger (1889–1976) grew up in Messkirch, a small Catholic town in Baden, and first trained for the priesthood. He became Husserl’s assistant in Freiburg, made his name with Being and Time (1927) and succeeded Husserl in his chair in 1928. In 1933 he joined the Nazi party and served for a year as rector of Freiburg University; his private notebooks, published from 2014, contain antisemitic passages, and how far his politics shapes his philosophy is still debated.\n\nBeing and Time asks what it means for anything to be. Heidegger starts from Dasein, the being that we ourselves are, and argues that we are not detached minds facing objects but always already in a world of tasks and tools: a hammer shows up first as something to hammer with, not as a thing with properties. Our being is care, stretched between past and future, and facing our own death can call us back from the anonymous “they” to an authentic life. Later he argued that modern technology reveals everything, people included, as a stockpile of resources.","zh":"海德格尔（1889–1976）在巴登的天主教小镇梅斯基希长大，最初接受的是神职训练。他在弗莱堡任胡塞尔的助手，以《存在与时间》（1927）成名，1928年接任胡塞尔的教席。1933年他加入纳粹党，担任了一年弗莱堡大学校长；自2014年起出版的私人笔记中有反犹段落，他的政治立场在多大程度上影响了他的哲学，至今仍有争论。\n\n《存在与时间》追问：任何东西“存在”究竟意味着什么。海德格尔从“此在”——也就是我们自身这种存在者——入手，主张我们并不是面对客体的旁观心灵，而总已身处一个由事务和用具构成的世界：锤子首先显现为拿来锤打的东西，而不是一件具有各种属性的物体。我们的存在是“操心”，伸展在过去与将来之间；直面自己的死亡，能把我们从无名的“常人”中唤回到本真的生活。后期他认为，现代技术把一切——包括人在内——都揭示为可供调用的资源储备。"},"schools":["phenomenology"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Being and Time","zh":"《存在与时间》"},"y":1927},{"t":{"en":"The Question Concerning Technology","zh":"《技术的追问》"},"y":1954}],"wiki":"Martin Heidegger"},
  {"id":"gramsci","tradition":"continental","born":1891,"died":1937,"life":{"en":"1891–1937","zh":"1891–1937年"},"name":{"en":"Antonio Gramsci","zh":"安东尼奥·葛兰西"},"role":{"en":"Italian communist leader who wrote his major work in Mussolini’s prisons","zh":"意大利共产党领袖，在墨索里尼的监狱中写下主要著作"},"idea":{"en":"Gramsci argued that ruling classes govern less by force than by hegemony, a cultural common sense that makes their rule seem natural, so social change requires a long struggle over ideas and institutions.","zh":"葛兰西认为，统治阶级主要不是靠暴力，而是靠“文化霸权”来统治——一种让其统治显得理所当然的文化常识；因此社会变革需要在观念与制度上进行长期斗争。"},"detail":{"en":"Antonio Gramsci (1891–1937) grew up poor in Sardinia, won a scholarship to the University of Turin and was drawn into the city’s factory-council movement after the First World War. He co-founded the Communist Party of Italy in 1921 and became its leader. Arrested by Mussolini’s regime in 1926, he was sentenced to more than twenty years; at the trial the prosecutor reportedly said his brain had to be stopped from working. In prison, in failing health, he filled some thirty notebooks, and he died in 1937 just after his release.\n\nGramsci asked why revolution had succeeded in Russia but failed in Western Europe. His answer was hegemony: in developed societies the ruling class governs less by force than by consent, through schools, churches, newspapers and the “common sense” they spread, which makes the existing order look natural. Changing society therefore needs a long “war of position” in culture and civil society, led by “organic intellectuals” who come from the working class. Hegemony became a key idea in cultural studies, postcolonial thought and political science.","zh":"葛兰西（1891–1937）在撒丁岛的贫困中长大，获奖学金进入都灵大学，一战后投身该市的工厂委员会运动。1921年他参与创建意大利共产党，后来成为党的领导人。1926年他被墨索里尼政权逮捕，判处二十多年徒刑；据说检察官在庭上称，必须让这个头脑停止运转。他在狱中身体日渐衰弱，却写满了约三十本笔记，1937年刚获释便去世。\n\n葛兰西追问：为什么革命在俄国成功，却在西欧失败？他的回答是“霸权”：在发达社会，统治阶级主要不是靠暴力，而是靠同意来统治——通过学校、教会、报纸以及它们传播的“常识”，使现存秩序显得理所当然。因此，改造社会需要在文化与市民社会中进行长期的“阵地战”，由从工人阶级中成长起来的“有机知识分子”来领导。霸权概念后来成为文化研究、后殖民思想和政治学中的关键概念。"},"schools":["marxism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Prison Notebooks","zh":"《狱中札记》"},"y":1935}],"wiki":"Antonio Gramsci"},
  {"id":"benjamin","tradition":"continental","born":1892,"died":1940,"life":{"en":"1892–1940","zh":"1892–1940年"},"name":{"en":"Walter Benjamin","zh":"瓦尔特·本雅明"},"role":{"en":"Berlin critic who joined Marxism, messianism and media theory","zh":"柏林批评家，融合了马克思主义、弥赛亚思想与媒介理论"},"idea":{"en":"Benjamin argued that technical reproduction strips art of its “aura” and could make it political, and that history should be read against the grain, from the standpoint of the defeated. He died fleeing the Nazis in 1940.","zh":"本雅明认为，技术复制使艺术失去“灵晕”，也可能使艺术政治化；历史应当“逆着纹理”去读，站在失败者的立场上。1940年，他在逃离纳粹途中去世。"},"detail":{"en":"Walter Benjamin (1892–1940) came from a prosperous, assimilated Jewish family in Berlin. When his habilitation thesis on the German Trauerspiel (mourning play) failed at Frankfurt University in 1925, an academic career closed to him, and he lived as a critic, translator of Baudelaire and Proust, and radio writer. Friendships with Gershom Scholem and Bertolt Brecht drew him towards Jewish mysticism and Marxism at once. He fled to Paris in 1933; in September 1940, turned back at the Spanish border at Portbou, he took his own life.\n\nIn “The Work of Art in the Age of Mechanical Reproduction” he argued that photography and film strip artworks of their “aura”, the unique presence of an original in one time and place. The loss could be liberating, letting art serve politics rather than ritual, though fascism could also aestheticise politics. In his last text, the theses “On the Concept of History”, he rejected faith in steady progress and asked historians to brush history against the grain, recovering the hopes of the defeated.","zh":"本雅明（1892–1940）出身于柏林一个富裕的、已同化的犹太家庭。1925年，他论德国悲苦剧的教授资格论文在法兰克福大学未获通过，学术之路由此断绝，此后他以批评家、波德莱尔与普鲁斯特的译者以及电台撰稿人为生。他与肖勒姆和布莱希特的友谊，同时把他引向犹太神秘主义与马克思主义。1933年他流亡巴黎；1940年9月，他在西班牙边境的波尔特沃被拒入境，随即自尽。\n\n在《机械复制时代的艺术作品》中，他论证摄影和电影使艺术品失去了“灵韵”，即原作在特定时空中独一无二的在场。这种失去可能带来解放，让艺术服务于政治而不是仪式，尽管法西斯主义也会把政治审美化。在最后的文本《论历史的概念》中，他拒绝对“稳步进步”的信仰，要求历史学家逆着纹理梳理历史，打捞失败者的希望。"},"schools":["critical-theory"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"The Work of Art in the Age of Mechanical Reproduction","zh":"《机械复制时代的艺术作品》"},"y":1935},{"t":{"en":"Theses on the Philosophy of History","zh":"《历史哲学论纲》"},"y":1940},{"t":{"en":"The Arcades Project (unfinished)","zh":"《拱廊街计划》（未完成）"},"y":1940}],"wiki":"Walter Benjamin"},
  {"id":"horkheimer","tradition":"continental","born":1895,"died":1973,"life":{"en":"1895–1973","zh":"1895–1973年"},"name":{"en":"Max Horkheimer","zh":"马克斯·霍克海默"},"role":{"en":"Director of the Frankfurt Institute who defined critical theory","zh":"法兰克福社会研究所所长，界定了“批判理论”"},"idea":{"en":"Horkheimer distinguished critical theory, which aims to free people from domination, from traditional theory, which accepts society as it is; with Adorno he argued that instrumental reason had turned enlightenment against itself.","zh":"霍克海默区分了以人从支配中解放为目标的批判理论，与接受社会现状的传统理论；他和阿多诺一起论证，工具理性已使启蒙走向了自身的反面。"},"detail":{"en":"Max Horkheimer (1895–1973) was the son of a Jewish textile manufacturer in Stuttgart and was expected to take over the family business before he turned to philosophy. In 1930 he became director of the Institute for Social Research in Frankfurt and editor of its journal, and after 1933 he led it into exile, first to Geneva and then to New York. After the war he brought the Institute back to Frankfurt and served as rector of the university.\n\nIn “Traditional and Critical Theory” (1937) Horkheimer argued that ordinary social science describes society as it is and so ends up serving it, while critical theory examines how its own concepts are shaped by society and aims at freeing people from domination. With Adorno, in Dialectic of Enlightenment, he went further: the reason that once freed people from myth had become instrumental, a tool for calculating and controlling nature and people, and so could breed new myths and new barbarism, as fascism and the culture industry showed.","zh":"霍克海默（1895–1973）是斯图加特一位犹太纺织厂主之子，原本要接管家族生意，后来才转向哲学。1930年，他出任法兰克福社会研究所所长并主编其刊物；1933年后，他带领研究所流亡，先到日内瓦，再到纽约。战后他把研究所迁回法兰克福，并担任法兰克福大学校长。\n\n在《传统理论与批判理论》（1937）中，霍克海默指出：通常的社会科学只是描述社会现状，结果为现状服务；批判理论则审视自身的概念如何被社会塑造，并以把人从支配中解放出来为目标。在与阿多诺合著的《启蒙辩证法》中，他更进一步：曾把人从神话中解放出来的理性，变成了工具理性，即计算与控制自然和人的手段，因而可能孕育新的神话和新的野蛮，法西斯主义和文化工业就是明证。"},"schools":["critical-theory"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Traditional and Critical Theory","zh":"《传统理论与批判理论》"},"y":1937},{"t":{"en":"Dialectic of Enlightenment (with Adorno)","zh":"《启蒙辩证法》（与阿多诺合著）"},"y":1947},{"t":{"en":"Eclipse of Reason","zh":"《理性之蚀》"},"y":1947}],"wiki":"Max Horkheimer"},
  {"id":"marcuse","tradition":"continental","born":1898,"died":1979,"life":{"en":"1898–1979","zh":"1898–1979年"},"name":{"en":"Herbert Marcuse","zh":"赫伯特·马尔库塞"},"role":{"en":"Frankfurt School exile who became the New Left’s philosopher","zh":"流亡美国的法兰克福学派成员，后来成为新左派的哲学代言人"},"idea":{"en":"Marcuse argued that affluent industrial society absorbs opposition by satisfying “false needs”, producing one-dimensional people; drawing on Freud, he imagined a non-repressive civilisation.","zh":"马尔库塞认为，富裕的工业社会通过满足“虚假需求”来吸收反对力量，造就了“单向度的人”；他借助弗洛伊德，设想了一种非压抑性的文明。"},"detail":{"en":"Herbert Marcuse (1898–1979) grew up in a Jewish family in Berlin, studied with Heidegger in Freiburg, then broke with him and joined the Institute for Social Research in 1933. In American exile he worked during the war for the US Office of Strategic Services, analysing Nazi Germany, and later taught at Brandeis and at the University of California, San Diego, where students in the 1960s made him the philosopher of the New Left.\n\nMarcuse combined Hegel, Marx and Freud. In Eros and Civilization he argued that modern society imposes more repression than civilisation needs (“surplus repression”) and that a freer, more playful order was possible. One-Dimensional Man claimed that advanced industrial societies neutralise opposition not by terror but by comfort: they create “false needs” for consumer goods that people experience as their own, so critical thought loses its footing. He looked for change to outsiders, students and the excluded, a view that made him both famous and controversial.","zh":"马尔库塞（1898–1979）在柏林一个犹太家庭中长大，曾在弗莱堡追随海德格尔学习，后与他决裂，1933年加入社会研究所。流亡美国期间，他在战时为美国战略情报局工作，分析纳粹德国；战后先后任教于布兰迪斯大学和加州大学圣迭戈分校，20世纪60年代的学生把他奉为新左派的哲学家。\n\n马尔库塞把黑格尔、马克思和弗洛伊德结合起来。在《爱欲与文明》中，他论证现代社会施加了超出文明所需的压抑（“额外压抑”），一个更自由、更具游戏性的秩序是可能的。《单向度的人》则认为，发达工业社会不是用恐怖，而是用舒适来消解反对：它制造出人们对消费品的“虚假需求”，人们却把这些需求当成自己的，于是批判性思维失去了立足点。他把变革的希望寄托在局外人、学生和被排斥者身上，这一看法使他声名大噪，也饱受争议。"},"schools":["critical-theory"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Reason and Revolution","zh":"《理性与革命》"},"y":1941},{"t":{"en":"Eros and Civilization","zh":"《爱欲与文明》"},"y":1955},{"t":{"en":"One-Dimensional Man","zh":"《单向度的人》"},"y":1964}],"wiki":"Herbert Marcuse"},
  {"id":"gadamer","tradition":"continental","born":1900,"died":2002,"life":{"en":"1900–2002","zh":"1900–2002年"},"name":{"en":"Hans-Georg Gadamer","zh":"汉斯-格奥尔格·伽达默尔"},"role":{"en":"Heidegger’s student, who made hermeneutics a philosophy of understanding","zh":"海德格尔的学生，把诠释学发展为一种关于理解的哲学"},"idea":{"en":"Gadamer argued that understanding is not a method but an event: we always interpret from within tradition and its prejudices, and truth emerges in a “fusion of horizons” between our present and the text.","zh":"伽达默尔认为，理解不是一种方法，而是一种发生：我们总是从传统及其成见之内进行诠释，真理在我们的当下与文本之间的“视域融合”中显现。"},"detail":{"en":"Hans-Georg Gadamer (1900–2002) grew up in Breslau and Marburg, where he studied with Heidegger and in 1929 completed his habilitation under him. He spent the Nazi years teaching in Leipzig, briefly served as its rector under Soviet occupation, and settled in Heidelberg in 1949. Truth and Method appeared only in 1960, when he was sixty; he lived to 102, still lecturing and debating.\n\nGadamer argued that understanding is not a technique for removing our biases but something that happens to us within a tradition. We always approach a text with “prejudices”, pre-judgements inherited from history and language, and these are not only obstacles but the very conditions that let the text speak to us. Understanding is a “fusion of horizons”: our questions and the text’s world meet and both are changed, as in a good conversation. Habermas objected that tradition can also hide domination, and their debate defined post-war hermeneutics.","zh":"伽达默尔（1900–2002）在布雷斯劳和马堡长大，在马堡追随海德格尔学习，1929年在他的指导下取得教授资格。纳粹时期他在莱比锡任教，苏联占领期间曾短暂担任莱比锡大学校长，1949年定居海德堡。《真理与方法》直到1960年才出版，那时他已年满六十；他活到102岁，晚年仍在讲学和论辩。\n\n伽达默尔认为，理解不是一种消除偏见的技术，而是我们在传统之中遭遇的事件。我们总是带着“前见”——从历史与语言中继承来的预先判断——去接近文本；这些前见不只是障碍，更是让文本能够对我们说话的条件。理解是一种“视域融合”：我们的问题与文本的世界相遇，双方都因此改变，就像一场好的谈话。哈贝马斯反驳说，传统也可能掩盖支配关系；两人的论争定义了战后的诠释学。"},"schools":["hermeneutics"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Truth and Method","zh":"《真理与方法》"},"y":1960}],"wiki":"Hans-Georg Gadamer"},
  {"id":"adorno","tradition":"continental","born":1903,"died":1969,"life":{"en":"1903–1969","zh":"1903–1969年"},"name":{"en":"Theodor W. Adorno","zh":"西奥多·阿多诺"},"role":{"en":"Frankfurt philosopher, music theorist and critic of the culture industry","zh":"法兰克福学派哲学家、音乐理论家，文化工业的批判者"},"idea":{"en":"Adorno argued that modern society integrates everything into exchange and administration, so thought must resist by attending to what concepts leave out: a “negative dialectics” with no final reconciliation.","zh":"阿多诺认为，现代社会把一切都纳入交换与管理之中，因此思想必须通过关注概念所遗漏的东西来进行抵抗：这是一种没有最终和解的“否定的辩证法”。"},"detail":{"en":"Theodor W. Adorno (1903–1969) was born in Frankfurt to a Jewish wine merchant and a Catholic singer, and trained in both philosophy and music, studying composition with Alban Berg in Vienna. Driven out by the Nazis, he spent years in Oxford, New York and Los Angeles, where he wrote Dialectic of Enlightenment with Horkheimer. After returning to Frankfurt in 1949 he became the Institute’s leading voice and a public intellectual of post-war Germany.\n\nAdorno argued that modern society reduces everything to what can be exchanged, measured and administered, and that the “culture industry” of film, radio and popular music turns out standardised pleasures that keep people content with the system. Philosophy, he thought, cannot offer a final synthesis as Hegel did: “the whole is the false”. Negative dialectics instead attends to what concepts leave out, the particular and the suffering, without promising reconciliation. His remark that writing poetry after Auschwitz is barbaric posed a question he later qualified himself.","zh":"阿多诺（1903–1969）生于法兰克福，父亲是犹太葡萄酒商，母亲是天主教徒歌唱家；他兼修哲学与音乐，曾在维也纳随贝尔格学习作曲。纳粹上台后他被迫离开德国，先后在牛津、纽约和洛杉矶生活多年，在洛杉矶与霍克海默合写了《启蒙辩证法》。1949年回到法兰克福后，他成为社会研究所的主要声音，也是战后德国的公共知识分子。\n\n阿多诺认为，现代社会把一切都化约为可交换、可计量、可管理之物；电影、广播和流行音乐构成的“文化工业”批量生产标准化的快乐，让人们对体制心满意足。他认为哲学无法像黑格尔那样给出最终的综合：“整体是虚假的”。否定的辩证法转而关注概念所遗漏的东西——个别之物与苦难——而不许诺和解。他说“奥斯维辛之后写诗是野蛮的”，提出了一个他自己后来也加以修正的问题。"},"schools":["critical-theory"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Dialectic of Enlightenment (with Horkheimer)","zh":"《启蒙辩证法》（与霍克海默合著）"},"y":1947},{"t":{"en":"Minima Moralia","zh":"《最低限度的道德》"},"y":1951},{"t":{"en":"Negative Dialectics","zh":"《否定的辩证法》"},"y":1966}],"wiki":"Theodor W. Adorno"},
  {"id":"sartre","tradition":"continental","born":1905,"died":1980,"life":{"en":"1905–1980","zh":"1905–1980年"},"name":{"en":"Jean-Paul Sartre","zh":"让-保罗·萨特"},"role":{"en":"Parisian novelist and philosopher, the public face of existentialism","zh":"巴黎的小说家与哲学家，存在主义最广为人知的代表"},"idea":{"en":"Sartre argued that consciousness is a nothingness that can always negate what is given, so humans are radically free and responsible; later he tried to reconcile this freedom with Marxism.","zh":"萨特认为，意识是一种能够否定一切既定之物的“虚无”，因此人是彻底自由且必须负责的；后期他试图把这种自由与马克思主义调和起来。"},"detail":{"en":"Jean-Paul Sartre (1905–1980) studied at the École normale supérieure in Paris, where he met Simone de Beauvoir, his lifelong partner. He discovered phenomenology in Berlin in 1933–34, made his name with the novel Nausea (1938) and was a prisoner of war in 1940–41. After the Liberation he became perhaps the most famous intellectual in the world and co-founded the journal Les Temps modernes; in 1964 he declined the Nobel Prize in Literature.\n\nBeing and Nothingness starts from consciousness, which is not a thing but a power to negate: we can always imagine what is not, so we are never simply what we are. Hence “existence precedes essence”: there is no fixed human nature or God-given purpose, and we define ourselves by our choices. This freedom is a burden, and we flee it in “bad faith”, like a waiter playing at being a waiter as if he had no choice. In the later Critique of Dialectical Reason he tried to fit this freedom into Marxist history.","zh":"萨特（1905–1980）就读于巴黎高等师范学校，在那里结识了终身伴侣西蒙娜·德·波伏瓦。1933–1934年他在柏林接触到现象学，以小说《恶心》（1938）成名，1940–1941年曾沦为战俘。战后他或许是世界上最著名的知识分子，参与创办了《现代》杂志；1964年他拒绝了诺贝尔文学奖。\n\n《存在与虚无》从意识出发：意识不是一件东西，而是一种否定的能力——我们总能设想不存在的东西，因此我们从来不只是我们所是。由此，“存在先于本质”：没有固定的人性，也没有神赋予的目的，我们通过选择来定义自己。这种自由是一种重负，我们常常以“自欺”来逃避它，就像一个侍者把侍者演得好像自己别无选择。在后期的《辩证理性批判》中，他试图把这种自由纳入马克思主义的历史观。"},"schools":["existentialism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Nausea","zh":"《恶心》"},"y":1938},{"t":{"en":"Being and Nothingness","zh":"《存在与虚无》"},"y":1943},{"t":{"en":"Existentialism Is a Humanism","zh":"《存在主义是一种人道主义》"},"y":1946}],"wiki":"Jean-Paul Sartre"},
  {"id":"levinas","tradition":"continental","born":1906,"died":1995,"life":{"en":"1906–1995","zh":"1906–1995年"},"name":{"en":"Emmanuel Levinas","zh":"伊曼努尔·列维纳斯"},"role":{"en":"Lithuanian-born French philosopher of ethics as first philosophy","zh":"生于立陶宛的法国哲学家，主张伦理学是第一哲学"},"idea":{"en":"Levinas argued that the face of another person makes an ethical demand on me before any knowledge or theory, so responsibility for the other, not ontology, is philosophy’s starting point.","zh":"列维纳斯认为，他人的面容在任何认识或理论之前就向我提出了伦理要求；因此哲学的起点不是存在论，而是对他人的责任。"},"detail":{"en":"Emmanuel Levinas (1906–1995) was born into a Jewish family in Kaunas, Lithuania, studied in Strasbourg and then in Freiburg with Husserl and Heidegger, and helped introduce phenomenology to French readers. A naturalised French citizen, he served in the army and spent the war in a German prisoner-of-war camp, while most of his family in Lithuania was murdered in the Holocaust. Afterwards he directed a Jewish teachers’ school in Paris and gave Talmudic readings, becoming a professor only later, at Poitiers, Nanterre and the Sorbonne.\n\nLevinas argued that Western philosophy, Heidegger’s included, tries to fit everything into a totality of being and knowledge, and so reduces the other person to something understood. But when I meet another’s face, its exposure and vulnerability say “do not kill me” before I have formed any theory; the other is infinite, beyond my grasp. Ethics, the responsibility this face lays on me, is therefore “first philosophy”, prior to ontology. His work reshaped ethics, theology and political thought.","zh":"列维纳斯（1906–1995）出生于立陶宛考纳斯的一个犹太家庭，先在斯特拉斯堡求学，后到弗莱堡追随胡塞尔和海德格尔，并帮助把现象学介绍给法国读者。他入籍法国，二战中从军，在德国战俘营中度过了战争岁月，而他在立陶宛的大多数亲人死于大屠杀。战后他在巴黎主持一所犹太师范学校，讲解《塔木德》，后来才成为教授，先后任教于普瓦捷、南泰尔和索邦。\n\n列维纳斯认为，包括海德格尔在内的西方哲学，一直试图把一切都纳入存在与知识的“总体”，这就把他人化约成了被理解的对象。但当我面对他人的“面容”时，它的暴露与脆弱在我形成任何理论之前就在说：“不要杀我”；他人是无限的，超出我的把握。因此，伦理——这张面容加诸我的责任——才是“第一哲学”，先于存在论。他的著作重塑了伦理学、神学与政治思想。"},"schools":["phenomenology"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Totality and Infinity","zh":"《总体与无限》"},"y":1961},{"t":{"en":"Otherwise than Being","zh":"《别于存在或超越本质》"},"y":1974}],"wiki":"Emmanuel Levinas"},
  {"id":"arendt","tradition":"continental","born":1906,"died":1975,"life":{"en":"1906–1975","zh":"1906–1975年"},"name":{"en":"Hannah Arendt","zh":"汉娜·阿伦特"},"role":{"en":"German-Jewish political theorist who fled Nazism for America","zh":"逃离纳粹、流亡美国的德国犹太裔政治理论家"},"idea":{"en":"Arendt analysed totalitarianism as a new form of rule built on terror and ideology, coined the phrase “the banality of evil”, and defended political action among equals as the heart of human freedom.","zh":"阿伦特把极权主义分析为一种以恐怖和意识形态为基础的新型统治，提出“平庸之恶”的说法，并主张平等者之间的政治行动是人类自由的核心。"},"detail":{"en":"Hannah Arendt (1906–1975) grew up in a secular Jewish family in Königsberg and studied with Heidegger, Husserl and Karl Jaspers, who supervised her doctorate on Augustine. Arrested by the Gestapo in 1933, she fled to Paris, was interned in France in 1940 and escaped to New York in 1941. Stateless for years, she became a US citizen in 1951, the year The Origins of Totalitarianism made her name, and later taught at Chicago and the New School.\n\nArendt argued that Nazism and Stalinism were not old tyrannies in new dress but a new form of rule that used ideology and terror to make human beings superfluous. In The Human Condition she distinguished labour (keeping alive), work (making lasting things) and action (speaking and acting together in public), and held that modern society had pushed action aside. Reporting on the trial of Adolf Eichmann, she described the “banality of evil”: great crimes committed by a thoughtless functionary rather than a demon, a phrase that provoked fierce controversy.","zh":"汉娜·阿伦特（1906–1975）在柯尼斯堡一个世俗化的犹太家庭中长大，曾师从海德格尔、胡塞尔和雅斯贝尔斯，博士论文研究奥古斯丁，由雅斯贝尔斯指导。1933年她被盖世太保逮捕，随后逃往巴黎，1940年在法国被拘押，1941年辗转到达纽约。她多年处于无国籍状态，1951年取得美国国籍，同年出版的《极权主义的起源》使她成名，后来她任教于芝加哥大学和新学院。\n\n阿伦特认为，纳粹主义和斯大林主义并非旧式暴政换了新装，而是一种全新的统治形式：用意识形态与恐怖使人变得多余。在《人的境况》中，她区分了劳动（维持生存）、制作（造出持久之物）与行动（在公共领域中共同言说和行动），认为现代社会把行动挤到了边缘。在报道艾希曼审判时，她提出“平庸之恶”：滔天罪行可能出自一个不思考的官僚，而不是恶魔——这一说法引起了激烈争论。"},"schools":[],"works":[{"t":{"en":"The Origins of Totalitarianism","zh":"《极权主义的起源》"},"y":1951},{"t":{"en":"The Human Condition","zh":"《人的境况》"},"y":1958},{"t":{"en":"Eichmann in Jerusalem","zh":"《艾希曼在耶路撒冷》"},"y":1963}],"wiki":"Hannah Arendt"},
  {"id":"beauvoir","tradition":"continental","born":1908,"died":1986,"life":{"en":"1908–1986","zh":"1908–1986年"},"name":{"en":"Simone de Beauvoir","zh":"西蒙娜·德·波伏瓦"},"role":{"en":"French existentialist whose The Second Sex reshaped feminism","zh":"法国存在主义者，《第二性》重塑了女性主义"},"idea":{"en":"Beauvoir argued that women have been made into man’s “Other”, defined by men rather than by themselves; since there is no feminine essence, freedom means refusing that assigned situation.","zh":"波伏瓦指出，女人一直被塑造成男人的“他者”，由男人而不是由她们自己来界定；既然并不存在女性的本质，自由就意味着拒绝这种被指派的处境。"},"detail":{"en":"Simone de Beauvoir (1908–1986) grew up in a Catholic bourgeois family in Paris whose fortunes declined, and decided early to earn her own living. In 1929 she passed the agrégation in philosophy, placed second only to Sartre, with whom she formed a lifelong, unmarried partnership. She taught in lycées until 1943, then wrote novels, philosophy and memoirs; The Mandarins won the Prix Goncourt in 1954.\n\nIn The Ethics of Ambiguity she argued that existentialist freedom is not licence: I can only will my own freedom by also willing the freedom of others. The Second Sex applied this to women. Men have made themselves the Subject and women the Other, defined by and for men; biology, myths, upbringing and marriage turn a female human being into a “woman”. Hence “one is not born, but rather becomes, a woman”. Women’s freedom means economic independence and refusing that assigned role. The book became a founding text of second-wave feminism.","zh":"西蒙娜·德·波伏瓦（1908–1986）在巴黎一个家道中落的天主教资产阶级家庭中长大，很早就决心自食其力。1929年她通过哲学教师资格考试，名列第二，仅次于萨特，并与他结成终身而不婚的伴侣关系。她在中学任教至1943年，此后写小说、哲学与回忆录；《名士风流》获1954年龚古尔文学奖。\n\n在《模糊性的道德》中，她论证存在主义的自由并非为所欲为：我只有在意愿他人自由的同时，才能意愿自己的自由。《第二性》把这一点用于女性。男人把自己当作“主体”，把女人当作“他者”，由男人并为男人来界定；生理、神话、教养与婚姻把一个女性的人塑造成“女人”。所以，“女人不是天生的，而是后天形成的”。女性的自由意味着经济独立，并拒绝那个被指派的角色。这本书成为第二波女性主义的奠基之作。"},"schools":["existentialism","feminism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"The Ethics of Ambiguity","zh":"《模糊性的道德》"},"y":1947},{"t":{"en":"The Second Sex","zh":"《第二性》"},"y":1949}],"wiki":"Simone de Beauvoir"},
  {"id":"merleau-ponty","tradition":"continental","born":1908,"died":1961,"life":{"en":"1908–1961","zh":"1908–1961年"},"name":{"en":"Maurice Merleau-Ponty","zh":"莫里斯·梅洛-庞蒂"},"role":{"en":"French phenomenologist of perception and the lived body","zh":"研究知觉与身体的法国现象学家"},"idea":{"en":"Merleau-Ponty argued that we meet the world first through the body, not as minds observing objects: perception is a bodily, pre-reflective engagement that comes before the split between subject and object.","zh":"梅洛-庞蒂认为，我们首先是通过身体与世界相遇，而不是作为心灵去观察对象：知觉是一种身体性的、前反思的投入，先于主体与客体的分裂。"},"detail":{"en":"Maurice Merleau-Ponty (1908–1961) studied at the École normale supérieure alongside Sartre and Beauvoir, and with them founded and edited Les Temps modernes until he broke with Sartre over communism in the early 1950s. After the war he taught psychology at Lyon and then child psychology at the Sorbonne, and in 1952 he was elected to the chair of philosophy at the Collège de France. He died suddenly of a heart attack at 53.\n\nMerleau-Ponty argued that philosophy had wrongly started from a mind looking at objects. We meet the world first as bodies: reaching for a cup, we do not calculate positions; the hand already “knows” the way. Perception is this pre-reflective, bodily grip on things, which comes before the split into subject and object. Drawing on psychology and on patients with brain injuries, he showed how habits, gestures and the felt sense of one’s own body shape experience. His ideas now run through cognitive science and philosophy of mind.","zh":"梅洛-庞蒂（1908–1961）与萨特、波伏瓦同在巴黎高等师范学校求学，并与他们一起创办和主编《现代》杂志，直到20世纪50年代初因共产主义问题与萨特决裂。战后他先在里昂大学讲授心理学，后在索邦讲授儿童心理学，1952年当选法兰西公学院哲学教授。他53岁时因心脏病突然去世。\n\n梅洛-庞蒂认为，哲学错误地从一个注视客体的心灵出发。我们首先是作为身体与世界相遇的：伸手去拿杯子时，我们并不计算位置，手已经“知道”该怎么走。知觉就是这种前反思的、身体性的对事物的把握，它先于主体与客体的分裂。他借助心理学和脑损伤病人的病例，说明习惯、姿势以及对自身身体的感受如何塑造经验。如今，他的思想贯穿于认知科学和心灵哲学之中。"},"schools":["phenomenology"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Phenomenology of Perception","zh":"《知觉现象学》"},"y":1945},{"t":{"en":"The Visible and the Invisible","zh":"《可见的与不可见的》"},"y":1964}],"wiki":"Maurice Merleau-Ponty"},
  {"id":"levi-strauss","tradition":"continental","born":1908,"died":2009,"life":{"en":"1908–2009","zh":"1908–2009年"},"name":{"en":"Claude Lévi-Strauss","zh":"克洛德·列维-斯特劳斯"},"role":{"en":"French anthropologist who founded structural anthropology","zh":"法国人类学家，结构人类学的创始人"},"idea":{"en":"Lévi-Strauss argued that kinship systems, myths and classifications in all societies follow the same underlying logic of binary oppositions, so “primitive” thought is as rigorous as science.","zh":"列维-斯特劳斯认为，所有社会的亲属制度、神话与分类，都遵循同样的深层二元对立逻辑；因此所谓“原始”思维与科学一样严密。"},"detail":{"en":"Claude Lévi-Strauss (1908–2009) was born in Brussels to French parents, studied law and philosophy in Paris, and in 1935 went to teach at the University of São Paulo, from where he made expeditions to Indigenous peoples of the Brazilian interior. Escaping Vichy France in 1941, he reached New York, where the linguist Roman Jakobson introduced him to structural linguistics. He later held the chair of social anthropology at the Collège de France and lived to be 100.\n\nLévi-Strauss applied Saussure’s insight, that meaning lies in differences within a system, to culture. In The Elementary Structures of Kinship he argued that marriage rules are systems of exchange between groups, organised around the incest prohibition. In his studies of myth he showed that stories across the Americas recombine the same oppositions, such as raw and cooked or nature and culture, to work through contradictions. The Savage Mind argued that so-called primitive thought is as logical as science, only applied to concrete things.","zh":"列维-斯特劳斯（1908–2009）生于布鲁塞尔，父母是法国人；他在巴黎学习法律与哲学，1935年赴巴西圣保罗大学任教，并从那里深入巴西内陆考察原住民部族。1941年他逃离维希法国来到纽约，语言学家雅各布森在那里把结构语言学介绍给他。他后来执掌法兰西公学院的社会人类学讲席，活到了一百岁。\n\n列维-斯特劳斯把索绪尔“意义在于系统内的差异”的洞见用于文化。在《亲属关系的基本结构》中，他论证婚姻规则是群体之间的交换系统，围绕乱伦禁忌组织起来。在神话研究中，他表明美洲各地的故事不断重新组合同一组对立，如生与熟、自然与文化，以处理种种矛盾。《野性的思维》认为，所谓原始思维与科学一样合乎逻辑，只是用于具体的事物。"},"schools":["structuralism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"The Elementary Structures of Kinship","zh":"《亲属关系的基本结构》"},"y":1949},{"t":{"en":"Tristes Tropiques","zh":"《忧郁的热带》"},"y":1955},{"t":{"en":"The Savage Mind","zh":"《野性的思维》"},"y":1962}],"wiki":"Claude Lévi-Strauss"},
  {"id":"camus","tradition":"continental","born":1913,"died":1960,"life":{"en":"1913–1960","zh":"1913–1960年"},"name":{"en":"Albert Camus","zh":"阿尔贝·加缪"},"role":{"en":"Algerian-born French writer of the absurd and of revolt","zh":"生于阿尔及利亚的法国作家，书写荒诞与反抗"},"idea":{"en":"Camus held that life is absurd, a clash between our demand for meaning and a silent universe, yet suicide and religious leaps are evasions: we must live in lucid revolt. He himself rejected the label “existentialist”.","zh":"加缪认为，人生是荒诞的——人对意义的渴求与沉默的宇宙相互冲撞；但自杀与宗教式的跳跃都是逃避，人应当清醒地反抗着活下去。他本人拒绝“存在主义者”这一标签。"},"detail":{"en":"Albert Camus (1913–1960) was born in French Algeria, the son of a farm worker killed in the First World War and a nearly deaf mother who cleaned houses. A scholarship took him to the University of Algiers, but tuberculosis ended his hopes of an academic career. He worked as a journalist, edited the Resistance newspaper Combat in Paris, and won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1957. He died in a car crash at 46.\n\nThe Myth of Sisyphus opens with what Camus called the one truly serious philosophical problem: whether life is worth living. The absurd arises from the clash between our demand for meaning and a world that gives none. Suicide and religious faith both escape the clash instead of facing it; Camus urged living lucidly within it, like Sisyphus pushing his rock: “one must imagine Sisyphus happy”. In The Rebel he defended revolt against injustice but rejected revolutions that justify murder in the name of history, which led to his break with Sartre. He refused the label “existentialist”.","zh":"加缪（1913–1960）生于法属阿尔及利亚，父亲是农场工人，在一战中阵亡，母亲几乎失聪，以帮佣为生。他靠奖学金进入阿尔及尔大学，但肺结核断送了他从事学术的希望。他当过记者，在巴黎主编抵抗运动报纸《战斗报》，1957年获诺贝尔文学奖。他46岁时死于车祸。\n\n《西西弗神话》开篇提出加缪所说的唯一真正严肃的哲学问题：人生是否值得活下去。荒诞产生于我们对意义的要求与一个不给出任何意义的世界之间的冲突。自杀与宗教信仰都是逃避这一冲突，而非直面它；加缪主张清醒地活在冲突之中，就像推石上山的西西弗：“应当设想西西弗是幸福的。”在《反抗者》中，他为反抗不义辩护，却拒绝以历史之名为杀戮开脱的革命，这导致他与萨特决裂。他本人拒绝“存在主义者”的标签。"},"schools":["existentialism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"The Stranger","zh":"《局外人》"},"y":1942},{"t":{"en":"The Myth of Sisyphus","zh":"《西西弗神话》"},"y":1942},{"t":{"en":"The Rebel","zh":"《反抗者》"},"y":1951}],"wiki":"Albert Camus"},
  {"id":"ricoeur","tradition":"continental","born":1913,"died":2005,"life":{"en":"1913–2005","zh":"1913–2005年"},"name":{"en":"Paul Ricœur","zh":"保罗·利科"},"role":{"en":"French philosopher who joined phenomenology, hermeneutics and narrative","zh":"把现象学、诠释学与叙事研究结合起来的法国哲学家"},"idea":{"en":"Ricœur held that we understand ourselves only indirectly, through symbols, texts and the stories we tell; he balanced a “hermeneutics of suspicion” (Marx, Nietzsche, Freud) with a hermeneutics of trust.","zh":"利科认为，我们只能间接地理解自己——通过象征、文本以及我们讲述的故事；他在“怀疑的诠释学”（马克思、尼采、弗洛伊德）与信任的诠释学之间寻求平衡。"},"detail":{"en":"Paul Ricœur (1913–2005) was orphaned young and raised by Protestant grandparents in Rennes. Captured in 1940, he spent the war in German prison camps, where he translated Husserl’s Ideas I. He taught at Strasbourg, the Sorbonne and the new university at Nanterre, where he served as dean during the turmoil after 1968, and for many years at the University of Chicago.\n\nRicœur held that we never know ourselves directly, as Descartes hoped, but only by a detour through signs, symbols and texts. Freud, Marx and Nietzsche practised a “hermeneutics of suspicion”, unmasking hidden desires, interests and wills; Ricœur took them seriously but argued that interpretation must also listen to what symbols disclose. In Time and Narrative he argued that stories, both history and fiction, give shape to time, so a person’s identity is a narrative identity, the unity of a life told and retold. Oneself as Another tied this self to ethics: aiming at a good life with and for others, in just institutions.","zh":"利科（1913–2005）幼年父母双亡，由新教徒祖父母在雷恩抚养长大。1940年他被俘，在德国战俘营中度过了战争，并在营中翻译了胡塞尔的《观念I》。他先后任教于斯特拉斯堡、索邦和新建的南泰尔大学——1968年风潮之后他曾在那里担任院长——并在芝加哥大学执教多年。\n\n利科认为，我们从来不能像笛卡尔所希望的那样直接认识自己，只能绕道经过符号、象征与文本。弗洛伊德、马克思和尼采实践的是“怀疑的诠释学”，揭穿隐藏的欲望、利益和意志；利科认真对待他们，但主张诠释也必须倾听象征所揭示的东西。在《时间与叙事》中，他论证故事——无论历史还是虚构——赋予时间以形态，因此一个人的身份是一种叙事身份，即一段被讲述又被重述的生命的统一。《作为他者的自身》把这种自我与伦理相连：在正义的制度中，与他人并为他人追求美好的生活。"},"schools":["hermeneutics"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Freud and Philosophy","zh":"《弗洛伊德与哲学》"},"y":1965},{"t":{"en":"Time and Narrative","zh":"《时间与叙事》"},"y":1983},{"t":{"en":"Oneself as Another","zh":"《作为他者的自身》"},"y":1990}],"wiki":"Paul Ricœur"},
  {"id":"barthes","tradition":"continental","born":1915,"died":1980,"life":{"en":"1915–1980","zh":"1915–1980年"},"name":{"en":"Roland Barthes","zh":"罗兰·巴特"},"role":{"en":"French critic who read everyday culture as signs","zh":"把日常文化当作符号来解读的法国批评家"},"idea":{"en":"Barthes showed how advertising, wrestling and wine carry hidden “myths” that make bourgeois values seem natural, and later declared the “death of the author”: a text’s meaning is made by its reader.","zh":"巴特揭示了广告、摔跤、葡萄酒如何承载隐藏的“神话”，使资产阶级的价值显得天经地义；后来他宣布“作者之死”：文本的意义由读者生成。"},"detail":{"en":"Roland Barthes (1915–1980) lost his father, a naval officer, in the First World War and grew up with his mother in Bayonne and Paris. Recurrent tuberculosis kept him in sanatoriums for years and off the usual academic path. He became known as a critic in the 1950s, taught at the École pratique des hautes études and was elected to the Collège de France in 1976. He was struck by a laundry van in a Paris street in February 1980 and died a month later.\n\nIn Mythologies Barthes read wrestling, steak and chips, soap-powder adverts and a magazine cover as signs. Each carries a second, hidden message, a “myth”, that makes something historical and political look natural: a cover photo of a young Black soldier saluting the French flag says that the empire is good and its subjects loyal. Later, in “The Death of the Author”, he argued that a text’s meaning is not fixed by its author’s intentions but produced in reading, and in S/Z he showed how a short story by Balzac weaves many codes together.","zh":"罗兰·巴特（1915–1980）的父亲是海军军官，在一战中阵亡；他随母亲在巴约讷和巴黎长大。肺结核反复发作，使他多年住在疗养院，也走不上寻常的学术道路。他在20世纪50年代以批评家成名，后任教于高等研究实践学院，1976年当选法兰西公学院教授。1980年2月他在巴黎街头被一辆洗衣店货车撞倒，一个月后去世。\n\n在《神话学》中，巴特把摔跤、牛排薯条、洗衣粉广告和一张杂志封面都当作符号来解读。每一个都携带第二层隐藏的信息——“神话”——把历史的、政治的东西变得看似自然：一张年轻黑人士兵向法国国旗敬礼的封面照片，传达的是“帝国是好的，臣民是忠诚的”。后来，他在《作者之死》中主张，文本的意义不由作者的意图决定，而是在阅读中产生；在《S/Z》中，他展示了巴尔扎克的一篇短篇小说如何交织着多重代码。"},"schools":["structuralism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Mythologies","zh":"《神话学》"},"y":1957},{"t":{"en":"The Death of the Author","zh":"《作者之死》"},"y":1967},{"t":{"en":"S/Z","zh":"《S/Z》"},"y":1970}],"wiki":"Roland Barthes"},
  {"id":"lyotard","tradition":"continental","born":1924,"died":1998,"life":{"en":"1924–1998","zh":"1924–1998年"},"name":{"en":"Jean-François Lyotard","zh":"让-弗朗索瓦·利奥塔"},"role":{"en":"French philosopher who defined the postmodern condition","zh":"界定了“后现代状况”的法国哲学家"},"idea":{"en":"Lyotard defined the postmodern as “incredulity toward metanarratives”: grand stories of progress, emancipation or spirit no longer legitimate knowledge, which splits into many incommensurable language games.","zh":"利奥塔把后现代界定为“对元叙事的怀疑”：关于进步、解放或精神的宏大故事已不再能为知识提供正当性，知识分裂为许多不可通约的语言游戏。"},"detail":{"en":"Jean-François Lyotard (1924–1998) taught philosophy at a lycée in French-ruled Algeria in the early 1950s and spent more than a decade in the radical Marxist group Socialisme ou Barbarie, writing on the Algerian war. After 1968 he broke with Marxism and taught at the experimental University of Paris VIII. The Postmodern Condition, written as a report on knowledge for the government of Quebec, made him famous worldwide in 1979.\n\nLyotard defined the postmodern as “incredulity toward metanarratives”: the great stories that once justified knowledge and politics, of reason’s progress, of humanity’s emancipation or of spirit’s self-realisation, no longer convince. What remains are many local “language games”, in science, law and art, each with its own rules. In The Differend he described cases where a wrong cannot even be stated because the parties share no common idiom, as when a victim must prove harm in the language of the court that judges them. Philosophy, he held, should bear witness to such differends rather than settle them by force.","zh":"利奥塔（1924–1998）在20世纪50年代初曾在法属阿尔及利亚的一所中学教哲学，并在激进马克思主义团体“社会主义或野蛮”中活动了十余年，撰文讨论阿尔及利亚战争。1968年后他与马克思主义决裂，任教于实验性的巴黎第八大学。《后现代状况》原是为魁北克政府撰写的一份关于知识的报告，1979年出版后使他闻名世界。\n\n利奥塔把后现代界定为“对元叙事的怀疑”：那些曾经为知识与政治提供正当性的宏大故事——理性进步的故事、人类解放的故事、精神自我实现的故事——都不再令人信服。剩下的是许多局部的“语言游戏”，科学、法律、艺术，各有各的规则。在《争异》中，他描述了这样的情形：一种不公甚至无法被陈述，因为双方没有共同的语言，比如受害者必须用审判他的法庭的语言来证明自己受到了伤害。他认为，哲学应当为这种“争异”作见证，而不是用强力去裁决它们。"},"schools":["poststructuralism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"The Postmodern Condition","zh":"《后现代状况》"},"y":1979},{"t":{"en":"The Differend","zh":"《异识》"},"y":1983}],"wiki":"Jean-François Lyotard"},
  {"id":"deleuze","tradition":"continental","born":1925,"died":1995,"life":{"en":"1925–1995","zh":"1925–1995年"},"name":{"en":"Gilles Deleuze","zh":"吉尔·德勒兹"},"role":{"en":"French philosopher of difference, desire and becoming","zh":"关注差异、欲望与生成的法国哲学家"},"idea":{"en":"Deleuze sought a philosophy of difference in itself, not difference as a gap between fixed identities; with Félix Guattari he described desire as productive and thought as a rhizome, branching without a centre.","zh":"德勒兹追求一种关于“差异本身”的哲学，而不把差异看作固定同一性之间的空隙；他与加塔利一起，把欲望描述为生产性的，把思想描述为没有中心、四处蔓生的“块茎”。"},"detail":{"en":"Gilles Deleuze (1925–1995) studied at the Sorbonne and spent years writing studies of other philosophers, among them Hume, Nietzsche, Bergson and Spinoza, which he described as portraits through which he did his own thinking. He taught at Lyon and then at the experimental University of Paris VIII from 1969 until 1987. Severely ill with lung disease in his last years, he took his own life in 1995.\n\nWestern philosophy, Deleuze argued, has always treated difference as secondary: a gap between things that are first identical with themselves. In Difference and Repetition he reversed this, making difference and becoming primary and identity a passing effect. With the psychoanalyst Félix Guattari he wrote Anti-Oedipus, attacking psychoanalysis for reducing desire to family dramas; desire, they argued, is productive, always connecting and building. A Thousand Plateaus offered the “rhizome”, a network like grass roots with no centre or hierarchy, as a model for thought, against the tree with its trunk and branches.","zh":"德勒兹（1925–1995）就读于索邦，用多年时间写下关于休谟、尼采、柏格森、斯宾诺莎等哲学家的研究，他称这些书为“肖像”，借以透过他们展开自己的思考。他先后任教于里昂大学和实验性的巴黎第八大学（1969–1987）。晚年他饱受肺病折磨，1995年自杀身亡。\n\n德勒兹认为，西方哲学一向把差异当作次要的东西：只是那些本来自我同一的事物之间的间隙。在《差异与重复》中，他颠倒了这一点，让差异与生成成为首要的，同一性只是转瞬即逝的效果。他与精神分析学家加塔利合写《反俄狄浦斯》，抨击精神分析把欲望化约为家庭剧；他们主张欲望是生产性的，总在连接与建造。《千高原》提出“根茎”——像草根一样没有中心、没有等级的网络——作为思想的模型，以对抗有主干和分枝的“树”。"},"schools":["poststructuralism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Difference and Repetition","zh":"《差异与重复》"},"y":1968},{"t":{"en":"Anti-Oedipus (with Félix Guattari)","zh":"《反俄狄浦斯》（与加塔利合著）"},"y":1972},{"t":{"en":"A Thousand Plateaus (with Félix Guattari)","zh":"《千高原》（与加塔利合著）"},"y":1980}],"wiki":"Gilles Deleuze"},
  {"id":"fanon","tradition":"continental","born":1925,"died":1961,"life":{"en":"1925–1961","zh":"1925–1961年"},"name":{"en":"Frantz Fanon","zh":"弗朗茨·法农"},"role":{"en":"Martinican psychiatrist and theorist of anticolonial liberation","zh":"马提尼克精神科医生，反殖民解放的理论家"},"idea":{"en":"Fanon analysed how colonial racism enters the psyche of the colonised, making them see themselves through white eyes, and argued that decolonisation requires a total break with the colonial order.","zh":"法农分析了殖民种族主义如何侵入被殖民者的心理，使他们透过白人的眼睛看待自己；他主张去殖民化需要与殖民秩序彻底决裂。"},"detail":{"en":"Frantz Fanon (1925–1961) was born in Martinique, then a French colony, where the poet Aimé Césaire was among his teachers. He volunteered for the Free French forces in 1944 and was wounded; after the war he studied medicine and psychiatry in Lyon and trained under François Tosquelles at Saint-Alban. In 1953 he became head of a psychiatric service at the Blida-Joinville hospital in Algeria, joined the Algerian National Liberation Front and was expelled in 1957. He died of leukaemia at 36.\n\nBlack Skin, White Masks describes being seen through a racist gaze: the Black man in France finds himself “overdetermined from without”, fixed by others’ looks into a body of stereotypes. Colonialism, Fanon argued, enters the mind, so the colonised try to become white in language and desire. The Wretched of the Earth, written as he was dying, argued that colonialism is violence ended only by a radical break, warned that a new national middle class might simply replace the colonisers, and called for a new humanism.","zh":"法农（1925–1961）生于当时还是法国殖民地的马提尼克，诗人塞泽尔曾是他的老师之一。1944年他志愿加入自由法国军队并负伤；战后他在里昂学习医学与精神病学，又在圣阿尔班随托斯克耶斯接受训练。1953年，他出任阿尔及利亚卜利达-茹安维尔医院的精神科主任，加入阿尔及利亚民族解放阵线，1957年被驱逐出境。他36岁时死于白血病。\n\n《黑皮肤，白面具》描述了在种族主义目光下被观看的经验：在法国的黑人发现自己“被外部过度决定”，被他人的目光固定成一具由刻板印象构成的身体。法农认为，殖民主义会进入心灵，于是被殖民者在语言和欲望上努力变“白”。他在病中写成的《全世界受苦的人》论证殖民主义就是暴力，只有彻底的决裂才能终结它；他警告新的民族资产阶级可能只是取代殖民者，并呼唤一种新的人道主义。"},"schools":["postcolonial"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Black Skin, White Masks","zh":"《黑皮肤，白面具》"},"y":1952},{"t":{"en":"The Wretched of the Earth","zh":"《全世界受苦的人》"},"y":1961}],"wiki":"Frantz Fanon"},
  {"id":"foucault","tradition":"continental","born":1926,"died":1984,"life":{"en":"1926–1984","zh":"1926–1984年"},"name":{"en":"Michel Foucault","zh":"米歇尔·福柯"},"role":{"en":"French historian of madness, prisons and sexuality","zh":"研究疯癫、监狱与性经验史的法国思想家"},"idea":{"en":"Foucault showed that categories like madness, delinquency and sexuality were produced by historical practices of knowledge and power; modern power works less by punishment than by discipline, surveillance and norms.","zh":"福柯揭示，疯癫、犯罪、性等范畴是由知识与权力的历史实践生产出来的；现代权力主要不是通过惩罚，而是通过规训、监视与规范来运作。"},"detail":{"en":"Michel Foucault (1926–1984), a surgeon’s son from Poitiers, studied philosophy and psychology at the École normale supérieure and worked in a psychiatric hospital before writing the doctoral thesis published as History of Madness (1961). He taught in Sweden, Poland, Germany and Tunisia, and in 1970 was elected to the Collège de France to a chair in the “history of systems of thought”. He was also a political activist, notably for prisoners’ rights, and died of an AIDS-related illness in 1984.\n\nFoucault wrote histories of things that seem timeless, such as madness, illness, crime and sexuality, to show that they were shaped by particular practices of knowledge and power. Discipline and Punish opens with a gruesome public execution in 1757 and asks why, within a century, punishment moved behind prison walls. His answer: modern power works less by spectacular violence than by discipline, through timetables, examinations and constant visibility, as in Bentham’s Panopticon, producing “docile bodies” in schools, barracks and factories as well as prisons.","zh":"福柯（1926–1984）是普瓦捷一位外科医生的儿子，在巴黎高等师范学校学习哲学与心理学，曾在精神病院工作，后来写成博士论文，即《疯癫史》（1961）。他先后在瑞典、波兰、德国和突尼斯任教，1970年当选法兰西公学院教授，讲席名为“思想体系史”。他也是政治活动家，尤其为囚犯权利奔走，1984年死于与艾滋病相关的疾病。\n\n福柯为那些看似永恒的东西——疯癫、疾病、犯罪和性——写历史，以表明它们是被特定的知识与权力实践塑造出来的。《规训与惩罚》以1757年一场骇人的公开处决开篇，追问为什么不到一个世纪，惩罚就退到了监狱的高墙之后。他的回答是：现代权力较少依靠惊人的暴力，而更多依靠规训——时间表、考试和无处不在的可见性，就像边沁的“全景敞视监狱”——在学校、兵营、工厂乃至监狱中生产出“驯顺的身体”。"},"schools":["poststructuralism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"History of Madness","zh":"《古典时代疯狂史》"},"y":1961},{"t":{"en":"The Order of Things","zh":"《词与物》"},"y":1966},{"t":{"en":"Discipline and Punish","zh":"《规训与惩罚》"},"y":1975}],"wiki":"Michel Foucault"},
  {"id":"habermas","tradition":"continental","born":1929,"died":2026,"life":{"en":"1929–2026","zh":"1929–2026年"},"name":{"en":"Jürgen Habermas","zh":"尤尔根·哈贝马斯"},"role":{"en":"Leading second-generation Frankfurt theorist of democracy and communication","zh":"法兰克福学派第二代领军人物，研究民主与交往"},"idea":{"en":"Habermas argued that besides instrumental reason there is communicative reason, aimed at mutual understanding; legitimate norms and laws are those all affected could accept in free and equal public discussion.","zh":"哈贝马斯认为，除工具理性之外，还有以相互理解为目标的交往理性；正当的规范与法律，是所有相关者在自由平等的公共讨论中都能够接受的规范与法律。"},"detail":{"en":"Jürgen Habermas (1929–2026) grew up in Gummersbach under Nazism; he later said that learning the truth about the regime’s crimes after 1945 shaped his whole life’s work. He was Adorno’s assistant at the Institute for Social Research in the 1950s, taught at Heidelberg and Frankfurt, and directed a Max Planck institute in Starnberg. For six decades he intervened in German public debate, from the “historians’ dispute” over the Nazi past to European unity. He died in March 2026, aged 96.\n\nHabermas argued that the first Frankfurt School had been too pessimistic about reason. Besides instrumental reason, which calculates how to reach goals, there is communicative reason, at work whenever people try to reach understanding by giving reasons others can accept or reject. From this he built discourse ethics: a norm is valid only if all those affected could agree to it in free and equal discussion. His study of the “public sphere” and his theory of law and democracy made him the leading defender of modernity as an unfinished project.","zh":"哈贝马斯（1929–2026）在纳粹统治下的古默斯巴赫长大；他后来说，1945年后得知纳粹罪行的真相，塑造了他一生的工作。20世纪50年代他担任阿多诺在社会研究所的助手，后执教于海德堡大学和法兰克福大学，并在施塔恩贝格主持一所马克斯·普朗克研究所。六十年间，他不断介入德国的公共辩论，从关于纳粹历史的“历史学家之争”到欧洲统一问题。他于2026年3月去世，享年96岁。\n\n哈贝马斯认为，第一代法兰克福学派对理性过于悲观。除了计算如何达成目标的工具理性，还有交往理性：每当人们试图通过提出他人可以接受或拒绝的理由来达成理解，它就在起作用。由此他建立了商谈伦理学：一项规范只有在所有受其影响者都能在自由平等的讨论中同意它时，才是有效的。他对“公共领域”的研究以及关于法律与民主的理论，使他成为“现代性是一项未竟事业”这一立场的首要辩护者。"},"schools":["critical-theory"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"The Structural Transformation of the Public Sphere","zh":"《公共领域的结构转型》"},"y":1962},{"t":{"en":"The Theory of Communicative Action","zh":"《交往行为理论》"},"y":1981},{"t":{"en":"Between Facts and Norms","zh":"《在事实与规范之间》"},"y":1992}],"wiki":"Jürgen Habermas"},
  {"id":"derrida","tradition":"continental","born":1930,"died":2004,"life":{"en":"1930–2004","zh":"1930–2004年"},"name":{"en":"Jacques Derrida","zh":"雅克·德里达"},"role":{"en":"Algerian-born French philosopher who founded deconstruction","zh":"生于阿尔及利亚的法国哲学家，解构的开创者"},"idea":{"en":"Derrida argued that Western thought privileges presence (speech over writing, the original over the copy), yet every such hierarchy depends on what it excludes; deconstruction reads texts to show this instability.","zh":"德里达认为，西方思想一直偏重“在场”（言语高于书写，原本高于摹本），但每一种这样的等级秩序都依赖于它所排斥的东西；解构就是通过阅读文本来揭示这种不稳定性。"},"detail":{"en":"Jacques Derrida (1930–2004) was born into a Sephardic Jewish family in El Biar, near Algiers. At twelve he was expelled from his school under the Vichy regime’s antisemitic quotas, an experience of exclusion he returned to often. He studied and later taught at the École normale supérieure in Paris, and in 1967 published three books at once that launched deconstruction. From the 1970s he taught regularly in the United States, where his influence on literary studies was immense.\n\nDerrida argued that Western philosophy rests on pairs in which one side is privileged as original and present: speech over writing, the literal over the metaphorical, the inside over the outside. Plato and Rousseau treated writing as a mere copy of speech, yet speech already works like writing, through marks whose meaning depends on differences and on contexts that can never be closed. He coined “différance”, both differing and deferring, for this play. Deconstruction is not destruction: it reads a text closely to show how its central hierarchy depends on what it excludes.","zh":"德里达（1930–2004）出生于阿尔及尔附近埃尔比亚尔的一个塞法迪犹太家庭。12岁时，他因维希政权的反犹配额被学校开除，这段被排斥的经历是他日后反复回顾的主题。他在巴黎高等师范学校求学并在那里任教，1967年同时出版三本书，由此开创了解构。自20世纪70年代起，他经常在美国讲学，对美国文学研究影响巨大。\n\n德里达认为，西方哲学建立在一系列二元对立之上，其中一方被尊为本源与在场：言语高于文字，字面意义高于隐喻，内高于外。柏拉图和卢梭把文字视为言语的单纯摹本，然而言语本身已经像文字一样运作——依靠种种标记，其意义取决于差异，取决于永远无法封闭的语境。他为这种游戏造了一个词“延异”，兼有“差异”与“延迟”之义。解构不是破坏：它细读文本，揭示其核心的等级秩序如何依赖于被它排除的东西。"},"schools":["poststructuralism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Speech and Phenomena","zh":"《声音与现象》"},"y":1967},{"t":{"en":"Of Grammatology","zh":"《论文字学》"},"y":1967},{"t":{"en":"Writing and Difference","zh":"《书写与差异》"},"y":1967}],"wiki":"Jacques Derrida"},
  {"id":"said","tradition":"continental","born":1935,"died":2003,"life":{"en":"1935–2003","zh":"1935–2003年"},"name":{"en":"Edward Said","zh":"爱德华·萨义德"},"role":{"en":"Palestinian-American literary critic at Columbia University","zh":"任教于哥伦比亚大学的巴勒斯坦裔美国文学批评家"},"idea":{"en":"Said argued that Western scholarship, art and policy created “the Orient” as an exotic, inferior Other, a body of knowledge that served imperial power and still shapes how the Middle East is seen.","zh":"萨义德认为，西方的学术、艺术与政策把“东方”塑造成一个异域而低劣的他者；这套知识服务于帝国权力，至今仍影响着人们看待中东的方式。"},"detail":{"en":"Edward Said (1935–2003) was born in Jerusalem under the British Mandate to a Palestinian Christian family, grew up mostly in Cairo and was sent to boarding school in the United States. He studied at Princeton and Harvard and taught comparative literature at Columbia University for four decades. He was also a leading advocate of Palestinian rights, a member of the Palestinian National Council from 1977 to 1991, and a gifted pianist who co-founded the West–Eastern Divan Orchestra with Daniel Barenboim.\n\nOrientalism argued that Western scholars, novelists and officials did not simply describe “the Orient” but constructed it: as exotic, irrational, unchanging and in need of Western rule. Drawing on Foucault’s idea that knowledge and power are entwined, Said showed how this discourse, from Napoleon’s scholars in Egypt to modern area studies, made empire seem natural. Culture and Imperialism read Jane Austen and Joseph Conrad alongside the colonies their worlds depended on. The book helped found postcolonial studies; critics still debate how far its picture of a single Orientalism holds.","zh":"爱德华·萨义德（1935–2003）生于英国托管时期的耶路撒冷，出身巴勒斯坦基督徒家庭，主要在开罗长大，后被送到美国读寄宿学校。他在普林斯顿和哈佛求学，在哥伦比亚大学讲授比较文学长达四十年。他也是巴勒斯坦权利的著名倡导者，1977至1991年为巴勒斯坦全国委员会成员；他还是出色的钢琴家，与巴伦博伊姆共同创办了西东合集管弦乐团。\n\n《东方学》论证，西方学者、小说家和官员并非单纯描述“东方”，而是建构了它：异域的、非理性的、停滞不变的、需要西方来统治的。萨义德借用福柯“知识与权力交织”的思想，表明这套话语——从拿破仑带到埃及的学者，到现代的区域研究——如何使帝国显得理所当然。《文化与帝国主义》把简·奥斯汀和康拉德的小说与其世界所依赖的殖民地对照阅读。此书帮助奠定了后殖民研究，而它所描绘的单一“东方主义”在多大程度上成立，批评者至今仍有争论。"},"schools":["postcolonial"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Orientalism","zh":"《东方学》"},"y":1978},{"t":{"en":"Culture and Imperialism","zh":"《文化与帝国主义》"},"y":1993}],"wiki":"Edward Said"},
  {"id":"spivak","tradition":"continental","born":1942,"died":null,"life":{"en":"born 1942","zh":"1942年生"},"name":{"en":"Gayatri Chakravorty Spivak","zh":"佳亚特里·查克拉沃蒂·斯皮瓦克"},"role":{"en":"Indian-born theorist and translator of Derrida, at Columbia University","zh":"生于印度的理论家、德里达的译者，任教于哥伦比亚大学"},"idea":{"en":"Spivak asked whether the subaltern, those shut out of power and representation, can speak: well-meaning intellectuals who claim to speak for them often silence them again.","zh":"斯皮瓦克追问：被排斥在权力与表述之外的底层人能否发声？那些自认为在替他们说话的好心知识分子，往往再次使他们沉默。"},"detail":{"en":"Gayatri Chakravorty Spivak (born 1942) grew up in Calcutta, graduated from Presidency College in 1959 and went to the United States to study comparative literature at Cornell, where she wrote her doctorate on W. B. Yeats under Paul de Man. Her translation of Derrida’s Of Grammatology (1976), with its long preface, introduced deconstruction to many English readers. She has taught at Columbia since 1991, became a University Professor there in 2007, and has long run rural schools in West Bengal; she received the Holberg Prize in 2025.\n\n“Can the Subaltern Speak?” asks whether those at the bottom of colonial and postcolonial society, above all poor women, can make themselves heard. Her example is sati, widow-burning: British officials claimed to be saving women from their men, while Indian traditionalists claimed the widows chose to die, and in both stories the women’s own voices vanish. Her answer, that the subaltern cannot speak, means not that they are silent but that no structure exists to hear them, and that speaking for them can repeat the silencing.","zh":"佳亚特里·查克拉沃蒂·斯皮瓦克（生于1942年）在加尔各答长大，1959年毕业于总统学院，随后赴美在康奈尔大学攻读比较文学，在保罗·德曼指导下完成关于叶芝的博士论文。她翻译的德里达《论文字学》（1976）连同长篇译序，把解构介绍给了许多英语读者。她自1991年起任教于哥伦比亚大学，2007年成为该校“大学教授”，并长期在西孟加拉农村办学；2025年她获得霍尔堡奖。\n\n《底层人能说话吗？》追问：殖民与后殖民社会最底层的人，尤其是贫穷的女性，能否让自己的声音被听见。她以寡妇殉夫（萨蒂）为例：英国官员声称是在把女人从她们的男人手中救出来，印度的传统派则声称寡妇是自愿赴死，在两种叙述中，女性自己的声音都消失了。她的回答“底层人不能说话”，并不是说她们沉默，而是说不存在能够听见她们的结构，而替她们发言也可能重复这种消音。"},"schools":["postcolonial"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Can the Subaltern Speak?","zh":"《底层人能说话吗？》"},"y":1988},{"t":{"en":"A Critique of Postcolonial Reason","zh":"《后殖民理性批判》"},"y":1999}],"wiki":"Gayatri Chakravorty Spivak"},
  {"id":"butler","tradition":"continental","born":1956,"died":null,"life":{"en":"born 1956","zh":"1956年生"},"name":{"en":"Judith Butler","zh":"朱迪斯·巴特勒"},"role":{"en":"American philosopher at Berkeley, theorist of gender performativity","zh":"任教于加州大学伯克利分校的美国哲学家，提出性别述行理论"},"idea":{"en":"Butler argued that gender is not an inner essence that behaviour expresses but a performance repeated under social norms, which produces the appearance of a natural sex; norms can be subverted by repeating them differently.","zh":"巴特勒认为，性别并不是由行为表达出来的内在本质，而是在社会规范下不断重复的表演，正是这种重复制造出“自然性别”的表象；规范也可以通过“有差异的重复”被颠覆。"},"detail":{"en":"Judith Butler (born 1956) grew up in a Jewish family in Cleveland, studied philosophy at Yale and wrote a doctorate on the reception of Hegel in twentieth-century France. Gender Trouble (1990) made Butler one of the most widely cited theorists of the late twentieth century. After teaching at Johns Hopkins, Butler moved to the University of California, Berkeley, and has also written on violence, mourning, Jewish ethics and the politics of protest.\n\nButler asks whether gender is something we are or something we do. Gender Trouble argues that there is no inner gender identity that our behaviour expresses; gender is performative, produced by repeated acts, gestures and styles under social norms, and the repetition creates the appearance of a natural core, even of a natural binary of sex. Because norms must be repeated constantly, they can be repeated differently: drag, for example, exposes gender as imitation. Bodies That Matter answered critics by asking how norms shape what counts as a body. The work became foundational for queer theory and remains contested.","zh":"朱迪斯·巴特勒（生于1956年）在克利夫兰一个犹太家庭中长大，在耶鲁大学学习哲学，博士论文研究黑格尔在二十世纪法国的接受。《性别麻烦》（1990）使巴特勒成为二十世纪末被引用最多的理论家之一。巴特勒曾任教于约翰斯·霍普金斯大学，后转到加州大学伯克利分校，还写过关于暴力、哀悼、犹太伦理与抗议政治的著作。\n\n巴特勒追问：性别是我们“是”的东西，还是我们“做”的东西？《性别麻烦》论证，并不存在一个由行为来表达的内在性别身份；性别是“述行”的，是在社会规范下反复重复的行动、姿态与风格所产生的，这种重复制造出一个自然内核的假象，甚至制造出“生理性别天然二分”的假象。正因为规范必须不断被重复，它们也可以被以不同的方式重复：比如变装就揭示了性别是一种模仿。《身体之重》回应批评者，追问规范如何塑造“什么算作身体”。这部作品成为酷儿理论的奠基之作，至今仍有争议。"},"schools":["feminism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Gender Trouble","zh":"《性别麻烦》"},"y":1990},{"t":{"en":"Bodies That Matter","zh":"《身体之重》"},"y":1993}],"wiki":"Judith Butler"},
  {"id":"peirce","tradition":"analytic","born":1839,"died":1914,"life":{"en":"1839–1914","zh":"1839–1914年"},"name":{"en":"Charles Sanders Peirce","zh":"查尔斯·桑德斯·皮尔士"},"role":{"en":"Logician and scientist who founded pragmatism and semiotics","zh":"创立实用主义与符号学的逻辑学家与科学家"},"idea":{"en":"To make an idea clear, ask what practical effects its object could conceivably have; truth is the opinion that endless inquiry by a community of investigators would finally agree on.","zh":"要弄清一个观念，就问它的对象可设想地会产生哪些实际效果；真理是研究者共同体经过无尽探究最终会一致认同的意见。"},"detail":{"en":"Charles Sanders Peirce (1839–1914) grew up in Cambridge, Massachusetts, the son of the Harvard mathematician Benjamin Peirce, and trained as a chemist. For about three decades he worked for the US Coast Survey, measuring gravity with pendulums, while writing on logic. He taught logic at Johns Hopkins from 1879 to 1884, lost the post, and never held another academic job; he died poor in Milford, Pennsylvania, leaving a vast mass of unpublished manuscripts.\n\nIn “How to Make Our Ideas Clear” (1878) he proposed a test for meaning: our idea of a thing is our idea of its practical effects. To call something “hard” is to say that many other things will not scratch it. Truth is what an unending community of inquirers would finally agree on, and inquiry starts from real doubt, not Descartes’ pretended doubt. He also founded a general theory of signs and, independently of Frege, a logic of quantifiers. When William James made “pragmatism” famous, Peirce renamed his own version “pragmaticism” to keep it apart.","zh":"查尔斯·桑德斯·皮尔士（1839–1914）在马萨诸塞州剑桥长大，父亲是哈佛数学家本杰明·皮尔士，他本人学的是化学。他为美国海岸测量局工作了约三十年，用摆测量重力，同时写作逻辑学。1879至1884年他在约翰斯·霍普金斯大学教逻辑，失去教职后再未获得学术职位，最终在宾夕法尼亚州米尔福德贫困离世，留下大量未刊手稿。\n\n在《如何使我们的观念清晰》（1878）中，他提出检验意义的办法：对一物的观念，就是对它可能产生的实际效果的观念——说某物“硬”，就是说许多别的东西划不动它。真理是无限延续的探究者共同体最终会同意的意见；探究始于真实的怀疑，而不是笛卡尔式的假装怀疑。他还创立了一般符号学，并独立于弗雷格发展出量词逻辑。詹姆士让“实用主义”一词风行之后，皮尔士把自己的学说改称“实效主义”，以示区别。"},"schools":["pragmatism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"The Fixation of Belief","zh":"《信念的确定》"},"y":1877},{"t":{"en":"How to Make Our Ideas Clear","zh":"《如何使我们的观念清晰》"},"y":1878}],"wiki":"Charles Sanders Peirce","c":"Charles Sanders Peirce.png","portrait":{"w":600,"h":900,"v":"f60e5e3100","author":"Charles_Sanders_Peirce_theb3558.jpg: NOAA Office of NOAA Corps Operations derivative work: Ori.livneh","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":null,"page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charles_Sanders_Peirce.png"}},
  {"id":"james","tradition":"analytic","born":1842,"died":1910,"life":{"en":"1842–1910","zh":"1842–1910年"},"name":{"en":"William James","zh":"威廉·詹姆斯"},"role":{"en":"Psychologist and philosopher who made pragmatism famous","zh":"让实用主义广为人知的心理学家与哲学家"},"idea":{"en":"Truth is what works in the long run of experience, and we may sometimes have the right to believe ahead of the evidence; consciousness is a flowing “stream”, not a chain of separate ideas.","zh":"真理是在长远经验中行得通的东西，人有时有权在证据之前就去相信；意识是流动的“意识流”，而不是一串彼此分离的观念。"},"detail":{"en":"William James (1842–1910), elder brother of the novelist Henry James, tried painting, then medicine, taking a Harvard MD in 1869 after a long depression. He began teaching physiology at Harvard, set up one of the first American laboratories of experimental psychology, and spent twelve years writing The Principles of Psychology (1890), which made him the leading American psychologist before he turned fully to philosophy.\n\nIn Pragmatism (1907) James argued that the meaning and truth of an idea lie in the difference it makes to experience: true ideas are those we can assimilate, validate and verify, the ones that “work” in the long run. In “The Will to Believe” (1896) he defended the right to adopt a belief, such as religious faith, when the choice is live, forced and momentous and evidence cannot settle it. His “radical empiricism” held that relations between things are experienced as directly as the things themselves. Russell and others accused him of making truth whatever is convenient; his pragmatism nevertheless shaped Dewey and, later, Putnam and Rorty.","zh":"威廉·詹姆士（1842–1910）是小说家亨利·詹姆斯的哥哥。他学过绘画，又学医，在经历长期抑郁后于1869年获哈佛医学博士。他先在哈佛教生理学，创办了美国最早的实验心理学实验室之一，并用十二年写成《心理学原理》（1890），成为美国首屈一指的心理学家，之后才全面转向哲学。\n\n在《实用主义》（1907）中，他主张一个观念的意义和真假，在于它对经验造成的差别：真观念是我们能够吸收、确证、证实的观念，是长远看来“行得通”的观念。在《信仰的意志》（1896）中，他为一种权利辩护：当一个选择是活的、被迫的、重大的，而证据又无法裁决时，人有权采纳某种信念，比如宗教信仰。他的“彻底经验主义”认为，事物之间的关系和事物本身一样直接地被经验到。罗素等人指责他把真理变成了方便；但他的实用主义深刻影响了杜威，后来又影响了普特南和罗蒂。"},"schools":["pragmatism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"The Principles of Psychology","zh":"《心理学原理》"},"y":1890},{"t":{"en":"The Varieties of Religious Experience","zh":"《宗教经验种种》"},"y":1902},{"t":{"en":"Pragmatism","zh":"《实用主义》"},"y":1907}],"wiki":"William James","c":"William James b1842c.jpg","portrait":{"w":600,"h":800,"v":"f4783a8a05","author":"Notman Studios (photographer)","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":null,"page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_James_b1842c.jpg"}},
  {"id":"frege","tradition":"analytic","born":1848,"died":1925,"life":{"en":"1848–1925","zh":"1848–1925年"},"name":{"en":"Gottlob Frege","zh":"戈特洛布·弗雷格"},"role":{"en":"Jena mathematician who invented modern logic","zh":"创立现代逻辑的耶拿大学数学家"},"idea":{"en":"Arithmetic can be derived from logic, and expressions have both a sense (a way of presenting something) and a reference (the thing itself): “the morning star” and “the evening star” differ in sense but both refer to Venus.","zh":"算术可以从逻辑中推导出来；语言表达式既有涵义（呈现对象的方式），也有指称（对象本身）：“晨星”和“昏星”涵义不同，指称的却都是金星。"},"detail":{"en":"Gottlob Frege (1848–1925) spent his whole career teaching mathematics at the University of Jena and was little noticed in his lifetime. In the Begriffsschrift (1879), a short book in an unusual two-dimensional notation, he created the first modern logic of quantifiers and variables, the system every logic student now learns in other symbols. His aim was to show that arithmetic is nothing but logic. In 1902, as the second volume of his Basic Laws of Arithmetic went to press, Russell wrote to him that one of his axioms led to a contradiction; Frege added an appendix admitting that his foundations had been shaken.\n\nIn “On Sense and Reference” (1892) he asked how “the morning star is the evening star” can tell us something, when both names refer to the same planet, Venus. His answer: an expression has a reference, the object it picks out, and a sense, the way it presents that object. The two names share a reference but differ in sense, so the sentence is informative. Philosophy of language, and much of analytic philosophy, starts from this distinction.","zh":"戈特洛布·弗雷格（1848–1925）一生在耶拿大学教数学，生前几乎无人注意。在《概念文字》（1879）这本用奇特二维记号写成的小书里，他创立了第一个现代的量词与变元逻辑——今天每个逻辑学学生用别的符号学到的，正是这个系统。他的目标是证明算术不过是逻辑。1902年，《算术基本规律》第二卷付印之际，罗素来信指出他的一条公理会导出矛盾；弗雷格在书后加了附录，承认自己的基础已被动摇。\n\n在《论意义与指称》（1892）中，他问：“晨星就是暮星”为什么能告诉我们新东西，既然两个名字指的都是金星？他的回答是：一个表达式既有指称，即它挑出的对象，也有意义，即它呈现该对象的方式。两个名字指称相同而意义不同，所以这句话有信息量。语言哲学乃至大部分分析哲学，都从这一区分出发。"},"schools":["early-analytic"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Begriffsschrift","zh":"《概念文字》"},"y":1879},{"t":{"en":"The Foundations of Arithmetic","zh":"《算术基础》"},"y":1884},{"t":{"en":"On Sense and Reference","zh":"《论涵义与指称》"},"y":1892}],"wiki":"Gottlob Frege","c":"Young frege.jpg","portrait":{"w":458,"h":619,"v":"8715113bd5","author":"Unknown author","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":null,"page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Young_frege.jpg"}},
  {"id":"dewey","tradition":"analytic","born":1859,"died":1952,"life":{"en":"1859–1952","zh":"1859–1952年"},"name":{"en":"John Dewey","zh":"约翰·杜威"},"role":{"en":"Philosopher of education and democracy; lectured in China 1919–21","zh":"教育与民主的哲学家，1919–1921年曾在中国讲学"},"idea":{"en":"Thinking is a tool for solving real problems, and democracy is not just a voting system but a way of life built on shared, experimental inquiry; schools should teach by doing.","zh":"思维是解决实际问题的工具；民主不只是投票制度，而是一种建立在共同、实验性探究之上的生活方式；学校应当“从做中学”。"},"detail":{"en":"John Dewey (1859–1952) was born in Burlington, Vermont, and took his doctorate at Johns Hopkins in 1884, where George Sylvester Morris drew him to Hegel. At the University of Chicago he founded a Laboratory School (1896) to test his ideas about teaching; from 1904 he taught at Columbia. For half a century a public intellectual, he lectured in Japan and for over two years in China (1919–21), was the first president of the American Association of University Professors, and in 1937 chaired the commission that examined Moscow’s charges against Trotsky.\n\nDewey treated thinking as the way living creatures cope with their surroundings (“instrumentalism”): inquiry begins in a troubled situation and ends when action resolves it, and ideas are hypotheses to be tested in practice. Schools should therefore teach through doing and shared projects rather than rote learning, and democracy is a way of life built on cooperative, experimental problem-solving, not just a voting system. His Columbia students included Hu Shi and B. R. Ambedkar, who carried his ideas to China and India.","zh":"约翰·杜威（1859–1952）生于佛蒙特州伯灵顿，1884年在约翰斯·霍普金斯大学获博士学位，在那里经莫里斯引导接触黑格尔。他在芝加哥大学创办实验学校（1896），检验自己的教育思想；1904年起任教于哥伦比亚大学。半个世纪里他都是活跃的公共知识分子：在日本讲学，又在中国讲学两年多（1919–21），是美国大学教授协会首任会长，1937年主持调查莫斯科对托洛茨基指控的委员会。\n\n杜威把思维看作生物应对环境的方式（“工具主义”）：探究始于一个令人困惑的情境，在行动解决问题时结束，观念则是有待实践检验的假设。因此学校应当“从做中学”、通过合作项目教学，而不是死记硬背；民主不只是投票制度，而是一种建立在合作的、实验式解决问题之上的生活方式。他在哥伦比亚的学生中有胡适和安贝德卡，两人把他的思想带回了中国和印度。"},"schools":["pragmatism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Democracy and Education","zh":"《民主与教育》"},"y":1916},{"t":{"en":"Experience and Nature","zh":"《经验与自然》"},"y":1925},{"t":{"en":"The Quest for Certainty","zh":"《确定性的寻求》"},"y":1929}],"wiki":"John Dewey","c":"John Dewey cph.3a51565.jpg","portrait":{"w":600,"h":820,"v":"0631512579","author":"Underwood & Underwood","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":null,"page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_Dewey_cph.3a51565.jpg"}},
  {"id":"russell","tradition":"analytic","born":1872,"died":1970,"life":{"en":"1872–1970","zh":"1872–1970年"},"name":{"en":"Bertrand Russell","zh":"伯特兰·罗素"},"role":{"en":"Logician, public intellectual and Nobel laureate; lectured in China 1920–21","zh":"逻辑学家、公共知识分子、诺贝尔文学奖得主，1920–1921年曾在中国讲学"},"idea":{"en":"Logical analysis reveals what our sentences really assert, as in his theory of descriptions; he tried to found mathematics on logic and discovered the paradox that undermined Frege’s system.","zh":"逻辑分析能揭示句子真正断言的内容，摹状词理论即是一例；他试图把数学建立在逻辑之上，并发现了动摇弗雷格体系的“罗素悖论”。"},"detail":{"en":"Bertrand Russell (1872–1970), grandson of a British prime minister, was orphaned young and raised by his grandmother. At Cambridge in 1898 he and G. E. Moore broke with the idealism then dominant, and with Alfred North Whitehead he wrote Principia Mathematica (1910–13), an attempt to derive mathematics from logic. He campaigned all his life: he was jailed in 1918 for opposing the First World War, lectured in China in 1920–21, won the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1950, and in his eighties led the campaign against nuclear weapons.\n\nRussell’s method was analysis: grammar can hide the logical form of what we say. In “On Denoting” (1905) he asked what “the present King of France is bald” is about, since there is no such king. His answer: the sentence really says that there is exactly one King of France and that he is bald, so it is simply false, and no strange non-existent object is needed. The paradox he found in Frege’s system, the set of all sets that are not members of themselves, reshaped the foundations of mathematics.","zh":"伯特兰·罗素（1872–1970）是一位英国首相的孙子，幼年父母双亡，由祖母抚养。1898年他在剑桥与摩尔一起同当时占主导的唯心论决裂，又与怀特海合著《数学原理》（1910–13），试图从逻辑推出数学。他一生投身社会运动：1918年因反对第一次世界大战入狱，1920–21年在中国讲学，1950年获诺贝尔文学奖，八十多岁时还领导反核武器运动。\n\n罗素的方法是分析：语法会掩盖我们所说的话的逻辑形式。在《论指称》（1905）中，他问“当今法国国王是秃头”说的是谁，既然并没有这样一位国王。他的回答是：这句话实际上是说，恰好存在一位法国国王，并且他是秃头——所以它只是假的，用不着设定什么奇怪的“不存在的对象”。他在弗雷格体系中发现的悖论——由一切不属于自身的集合构成的集合——改变了数学基础研究的面貌。"},"schools":["early-analytic"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"On Denoting","zh":"《论指称》"},"y":1905},{"t":{"en":"Principia Mathematica (with A. N. Whitehead)","zh":"《数学原理》（与怀特海合著）"},"y":1910},{"t":{"en":"The Problems of Philosophy","zh":"《哲学问题》"},"y":1912}],"wiki":"Bertrand Russell"},
  {"id":"moore","tradition":"analytic","born":1873,"died":1958,"life":{"en":"1873–1958","zh":"1873–1958年"},"name":{"en":"G. E. Moore","zh":"G. E. 摩尔"},"role":{"en":"Cambridge defender of common sense against idealism","zh":"以常识对抗唯心论的剑桥哲学家"},"idea":{"en":"Goodness is a simple, indefinable property, so defining it as pleasure or any natural property commits the “naturalistic fallacy”; and we know common-sense truths, such as “here is a hand”, better than any philosophical argument against them.","zh":"“善”是一种简单而不可定义的性质，把它定义为快乐或任何自然性质，就犯了“自然主义谬误”；我们对“这里有一只手”这类常识真理的把握，比任何反对它们的哲学论证都更可靠。"},"detail":{"en":"George Edward Moore (1873–1958) went up to Cambridge to study classics, and Russell persuaded him to take up philosophy. He spent nearly his whole career there and edited the journal Mind from 1921 to 1947; through the Apostles society his ethics deeply impressed the Bloomsbury circle, including Keynes. His papers of 1903 broke with British idealism, and with Russell he is counted a founder of analytic philosophy.\n\nIn Principia Ethica (1903) Moore argued that “good” names a simple quality that cannot be defined: whatever natural property you identify it with, such as pleasure, it remains an open question whether that property is good. Defining good in natural terms commits the “naturalistic fallacy”. In “Proof of an External World” (1939) he held up his hands and said, in effect: here is one hand, here is another, so external things exist. He was not being naive. His point was that we know such commonplace truths better than we could ever know the premises of a sceptical argument against them.","zh":"乔治·爱德华·摩尔（1873–1958）到剑桥学古典学，罗素说服他转学哲学。他几乎一生都在剑桥，1921至1947年主编《心》杂志；通过“使徒社”，他的伦理学深深打动了包括凯恩斯在内的布卢姆斯伯里圈子。他1903年的论文同英国唯心论决裂，他与罗素一起被视为分析哲学的奠基人。\n\n在《伦理学原理》（1903）中，摩尔主张“善”指一种无法定义的单纯性质：无论你把它等同于哪种自然性质，比如快乐，“这种性质是善的吗”始终是个开放的问题。用自然性质定义善，就犯了“自然主义谬误”。在《外部世界的证明》（1939）中，他举起双手，大意是说：这是一只手，这是另一只手，所以外部事物存在。这并不天真。他的意思是：我们对这类常识真理的把握，胜过对任何怀疑论论证前提的把握。"},"schools":["early-analytic"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Principia Ethica","zh":"《伦理学原理》"},"y":1903},{"t":{"en":"A Defence of Common Sense","zh":"《为常识辩护》"},"y":1925},{"t":{"en":"Proof of an External World","zh":"《外部世界的证明》"},"y":1939}],"wiki":"G. E. Moore"},
  {"id":"schlick","tradition":"analytic","born":1882,"died":1936,"life":{"en":"1882–1936","zh":"1882–1936年"},"name":{"en":"Moritz Schlick","zh":"莫里茨·石里克"},"role":{"en":"Physicist-philosopher who led the Vienna Circle","zh":"领导维也纳学派的物理学家兼哲学家"},"idea":{"en":"Philosophy is not a body of doctrines but the activity of clarifying meaning, and the meaning of a statement is the way it would be verified. He was shot dead by a former student at the University of Vienna in 1936.","zh":"哲学不是一套学说，而是澄清意义的活动；一个陈述的意义就在于它被证实的方式。1936年，他在维也纳大学被一名昔日学生枪杀。"},"detail":{"en":"Moritz Schlick (1882–1936) took his doctorate in physics under Max Planck in Berlin (1904) and wrote one of the first philosophical accounts of Einstein’s relativity (1917). Appointed to the chair of natural philosophy in Vienna in 1922, he gathered mathematicians, scientists and philosophers into a discussion group that in 1929 announced itself publicly as the Vienna Circle. On 22 June 1936 he was shot dead on the university stairs by a former student, Johann Nelböck; after the Nazi annexation of Austria, Nelböck was released early.\n\nSchlick held that philosophy is not a science with results of its own but the activity of making the meaning of statements clear, and that the meaning of a statement is the method of its verification: to understand a sentence is to know what observations would show it true. Questions that no possible experience could settle are not deep but empty. The Circle treated Wittgenstein’s Tractatus as a guiding text, and from 1927 Schlick met Wittgenstein regularly, though Wittgenstein never joined the group.","zh":"莫里茨·石里克（1882–1936）1904年在柏林师从普朗克获物理学博士，1917年写出最早从哲学上阐释爱因斯坦相对论的著作之一。1922年他出任维也纳大学自然哲学讲席，把数学家、科学家和哲学家聚成一个讨论小组，1929年以“维也纳学派”之名公开亮相。1936年6月22日，他在大学楼梯上被以前的学生内尔伯克枪杀；纳粹吞并奥地利后，内尔伯克被提前释放。\n\n石里克认为，哲学不是拥有自己结论的科学，而是澄清命题意义的活动；一个命题的意义就是它的证实方法——理解一句话，就是知道什么样的观察会表明它为真。任何可能的经验都无法裁决的问题，不是深刻，而是空洞。维也纳学派把维特根斯坦的《逻辑哲学论》奉为指导性文本；1927年起石里克与维特根斯坦定期会面，但维特根斯坦从未加入学派。"},"schools":["logical-positivism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"General Theory of Knowledge","zh":"《普通认识论》"},"y":1918},{"t":{"en":"The Turning Point in Philosophy","zh":"《哲学的转变》"},"y":1930}],"wiki":"Moritz Schlick"},
  {"id":"wittgenstein","tradition":"analytic","born":1889,"died":1951,"life":{"en":"1889–1951","zh":"1889–1951年"},"name":{"en":"Ludwig Wittgenstein","zh":"路德维希·维特根斯坦"},"role":{"en":"Viennese-born Cambridge philosopher who overturned his own first philosophy","zh":"生于维也纳、执教剑桥，推翻了自己早期哲学的哲学家"},"idea":{"en":"Early: a sentence pictures a possible fact, and what cannot be said must be passed over in silence. Late: meaning is use within “language-games” woven into forms of life, and philosophy is therapy for the bewitchment of our intelligence by language.","zh":"早期：命题是可能事实的图像，凡不可说的，必须保持沉默。后期：意义是词在交织于生活形式中的“语言游戏”里的用法，哲学是治疗语言对我们理智之蛊惑的疗法。"},"detail":{"en":"Ludwig Wittgenstein (1889–1951) was born into one of the richest families in Vienna and trained as an engineer before Frege sent him to Russell at Cambridge in 1911. He wrote the Tractatus Logico-Philosophicus during the First World War, partly as a soldier and prisoner of war; believing he had solved philosophy’s problems, he gave away his fortune and taught in Austrian village schools. He returned to Cambridge in 1929 and held its chair of philosophy from 1939 to 1947.\n\nThe Tractatus holds that a sentence is a picture of a possible fact; what cannot be pictured, including ethics and the meaning of life, cannot be said, only shown. His later work, published after his death as Philosophical Investigations (1953), rejects that picture. Words do not get their meaning by naming objects; they work like tools in “language-games” woven into human activities, and for most words the meaning is the use. Many philosophical problems arise when language “goes on holiday”; the task of philosophy is to show the fly the way out of the fly-bottle.","zh":"路德维希·维特根斯坦（1889–1951）出生于维也纳最富有的家族之一，学过工程，后经弗雷格建议于1911年到剑桥追随罗素。他在第一次世界大战期间写成《逻辑哲学论》，部分是在当兵和做战俘时完成的；他自认已解决了哲学问题，便散尽家财，到奥地利乡村小学教书。1929年他回到剑桥，1939至1947年任哲学教授。\n\n《逻辑哲学论》认为，句子是可能事实的图像；不能被描画的东西——包括伦理和人生的意义——不可说，只能显示。他身后出版的《哲学研究》（1953）推翻了这幅图景：词语不是靠命名对象获得意义，而是像工具一样在交织于人类活动的“语言游戏”中起作用；对大多数词来说，意义就是用法。许多哲学问题产生于语言“休假”的时候；哲学的任务，是给捕蝇瓶里的苍蝇指出飞出去的路。"},"schools":["early-analytic","ordinary-language"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Tractatus Logico-Philosophicus","zh":"《逻辑哲学论》"},"y":1921},{"t":{"en":"Philosophical Investigations (posthumous)","zh":"《哲学研究》（身后出版）"},"y":1953}],"wiki":"Ludwig Wittgenstein","c":"Ludwig Wittgenstein 1929.jpg","portrait":{"w":600,"h":812,"v":"82d2f37cce","author":"Moritz Nähr","license":"Public domain","licenseUrl":null,"page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ludwig_Wittgenstein_1929.jpg"}},
  {"id":"carnap","tradition":"analytic","born":1891,"died":1970,"life":{"en":"1891–1970","zh":"1891–1970年"},"name":{"en":"Rudolf Carnap","zh":"鲁道夫·卡尔纳普"},"role":{"en":"Vienna Circle’s great system-builder, later in Chicago and Los Angeles","zh":"维也纳学派的体系建构者，后执教芝加哥与洛杉矶"},"idea":{"en":"Knowledge of the world can be logically reconstructed from experience, and questions like “do numbers really exist?” are not factual questions but choices of a linguistic framework, to be judged by their usefulness.","zh":"关于世界的知识可以从经验出发进行逻辑重构；“数真的存在吗”这类问题不是事实问题，而是对语言框架的选择，应以其有用性来评判。"},"detail":{"en":"Rudolf Carnap (1891–1970) studied physics and philosophy at Jena, where he attended Frege’s logic lectures, and served in the First World War. He joined the Vienna Circle in 1926 and became its most systematic thinker, taught in Prague from 1931, and in 1935 emigrated to the United States, teaching at Chicago and later at UCLA.\n\nIn The Logical Structure of the World (1928) he tried to show how all our concepts of the world could be built up, by logic alone, from elementary experiences. He later argued that a question such as “do numbers really exist?” is not a question of fact. Asked inside a linguistic framework, such as arithmetic, it is trivial; asked from outside, it is a practical choice of which framework to use, to be judged by its usefulness. In a 1932 essay he held up Heidegger’s sentence “the Nothing itself nothings” as a model of a meaningless pseudo-statement. Quine, who admired him, attacked his distinction between analytic and synthetic truths, and their long exchange shaped mid-century analytic philosophy.","zh":"鲁道夫·卡尔纳普（1891–1970）在耶拿学物理和哲学，听过弗雷格的逻辑课，参加过第一次世界大战。1926年他加入维也纳学派，成为其中最有体系的思想家；1931年起在布拉格任教，1935年移居美国，先后执教于芝加哥大学和加州大学洛杉矶分校。\n\n在《世界的逻辑构造》（1928）中，他试图说明我们关于世界的一切概念，如何能仅凭逻辑从基本经验中构造出来。后来他主张，“数真的存在吗”这样的问题不是事实问题：在某个语言框架（比如算术）之内问，答案平凡无奇；在框架之外问，它是选用哪个框架的实际抉择，应以有用与否来评判。1932年的一篇文章里，他把海德格尔“无本身在无化”这句话当作无意义伪命题的样板。仰慕他的奎因批判了他的分析—综合之分，两人的长期交锋塑造了二十世纪中叶的分析哲学。"},"schools":["logical-positivism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"The Logical Structure of the World","zh":"《世界的逻辑构造》"},"y":1928},{"t":{"en":"The Logical Syntax of Language","zh":"《语言的逻辑句法》"},"y":1934},{"t":{"en":"Empiricism, Semantics, and Ontology","zh":"《经验主义、语义学和本体论》"},"y":1950}],"wiki":"Rudolf Carnap"},
  {"id":"ryle","tradition":"analytic","born":1900,"died":1976,"life":{"en":"1900–1976","zh":"1900–1976年"},"name":{"en":"Gilbert Ryle","zh":"吉尔伯特·赖尔"},"role":{"en":"Oxford philosopher who mocked the “ghost in the machine”","zh":"嘲讽“机器中的幽灵”的牛津哲学家"},"idea":{"en":"Treating the mind as a hidden inner thing is a “category mistake”, like looking for the university over and above its colleges; talk of minds is talk of abilities and dispositions to behave.","zh":"把心灵当作隐藏在内部的东西是一种“范畴错误”，就像在各个学院之外再去寻找“大学”本身；谈论心灵，就是谈论能力与行为倾向。"},"detail":{"en":"Gilbert Ryle (1900–1976) spent his whole career at Oxford, as a tutor at Christ Church and from 1945 as Waynflete Professor of Metaphysical Philosophy. He edited the journal Mind from 1947 to 1971, helped create the BPhil degree that drew graduate students from around the world, and more than anyone made postwar Oxford a centre of philosophy; Ayer and, later, Dennett were among his students.\n\nThe Concept of Mind (1949) attacks what Ryle called “the dogma of the Ghost in the Machine”: Descartes’ picture of a private inner mind steering a body. The picture rests on a category mistake, like that of a visitor shown Oxford’s colleges, libraries and laboratories who then asks, “But where is the University?” To say that someone is intelligent, vain or speaks French is not to report hidden inner events but to describe how they are disposed to act. Knowing how to do something is not a matter of knowing that certain facts are true. Critics called this behaviourism; Ryle said he was mapping the logic of our mental concepts.","zh":"吉尔伯特·赖尔（1900–1976）一生在牛津：先在基督教堂学院任导师，1945年起任韦恩弗利特形而上学教授。1947至1971年他主编《心》杂志，参与创设吸引世界各地研究生的BPhil学位，比任何人都更有力地把战后牛津建成哲学重镇；艾耶尔和后来的丹尼特都是他的学生。\n\n《心的概念》（1949）攻击他所谓“机器中的幽灵”教条，即笛卡尔那幅私密的内在心灵操纵身体的图景。这幅图景建立在“范畴错误”上，就像一位访客参观了牛津的学院、图书馆和实验室后问：“可大学在哪儿？”说某人聪明、虚荣或会说法语，不是报告隐藏的内心事件，而是描述他倾向于怎样行事；“知道怎么做”也不能归结为“知道某些事实”。批评者称之为行为主义，赖尔则说自己是在绘制心灵概念的逻辑地图。"},"schools":["ordinary-language"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"The Concept of Mind","zh":"《心的概念》"},"y":1949},{"t":{"en":"Dilemmas","zh":"《两难》"},"y":1954}],"wiki":"Gilbert Ryle"},
  {"id":"popper","tradition":"analytic","born":1902,"died":1994,"life":{"en":"1902–1994","zh":"1902–1994年"},"name":{"en":"Karl Popper","zh":"卡尔·波普尔"},"role":{"en":"Viennese-born philosopher of science and defender of the open society","zh":"生于维也纳的科学哲学家、开放社会的捍卫者"},"idea":{"en":"A theory is scientific only if it could in principle be refuted by observation; knowledge grows by conjectures and refutations, and societies, like science, flourish when they stay open to criticism.","zh":"一个理论只有在原则上可能被观察推翻时才是科学的；知识通过猜想与反驳而增长，社会也像科学一样，只有保持对批评的开放才能繁荣。"},"detail":{"en":"Karl Popper (1902–1994) grew up in Vienna in an assimilated family of Jewish origin, trained as a cabinet-maker and a schoolteacher, and moved on the edge of the Vienna Circle without joining it. His Logik der Forschung (1934) brought him a post in New Zealand, where he wrote The Open Society and Its Enemies during the Second World War; from 1946 he taught at the London School of Economics.\n\nPopper accepted Hume’s point that no number of white swans proves that all swans are white, while one black swan refutes it. Science therefore does not prove theories by induction: it proposes bold conjectures and tries hard to refute them. A theory is scientific if it forbids something observable and so could turn out false. Popper thought that Freudian psychoanalysis and Marx’s theory of history, as their followers defended them, could explain any outcome and so tested nothing. His politics follow the same logic: an open society corrects its mistakes by criticism and piecemeal reform, whereas Plato, Hegel and Marx promised certainty and pointed toward closed societies.","zh":"卡尔·波普尔（1902–1994）在维也纳一个犹太裔同化家庭长大，当过细木工学徒，受过教师训练，一直活动在维也纳学派的边缘而没有加入。《研究的逻辑》（1934）为他在新西兰谋得教职，第二次世界大战期间他在那里写成《开放社会及其敌人》；1946年起任教于伦敦政治经济学院。\n\n波普尔接受休谟的论点：看到再多白天鹅，也证明不了所有天鹅都是白的，而一只黑天鹅就能推翻这个结论。因此科学并非用归纳证明理论，而是提出大胆的猜想，再竭力去反驳它。一个理论之所以是科学的，在于它禁止某些可观察的事情发生，因而有可能被证明是错的。波普尔认为，按其信徒的辩护方式，弗洛伊德的精神分析和马克思的历史理论能解释任何结果，因而什么也没有检验。他的政治哲学遵循同样的逻辑：开放社会靠批评和渐进改良纠正错误，而柏拉图、黑格尔和马克思许诺确定性，指向封闭社会。"},"schools":["post-positivism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"The Logic of Scientific Discovery (Logik der Forschung)","zh":"《科学发现的逻辑》"},"y":1934},{"t":{"en":"The Open Society and Its Enemies","zh":"《开放社会及其敌人》"},"y":1945},{"t":{"en":"Conjectures and Refutations","zh":"《猜想与反驳》"},"y":1963}],"wiki":"Karl Popper"},
  {"id":"quine","tradition":"analytic","born":1908,"died":2000,"life":{"en":"1908–2000","zh":"1908–2000年"},"name":{"en":"W. V. O. Quine","zh":"W. V. O. 蒯因"},"role":{"en":"Harvard logician who attacked the analytic–synthetic distinction","zh":"抨击分析/综合二分的哈佛逻辑学家"},"idea":{"en":"There is no sharp line between truths of meaning and truths of fact; our beliefs form a single web that faces experience as a whole, and philosophy is continuous with natural science.","zh":"意义之真与事实之真之间没有截然的界线；我们的信念构成一张整体之网，作为整体面对经验，哲学与自然科学是连续的。"},"detail":{"en":"Willard Van Orman Quine (1908–2000) studied mathematics at Oberlin and wrote a Harvard doctorate on Principia Mathematica in two years. A travelling fellowship in 1932–33 took him to Vienna, Warsaw and Prague, where long discussions with Carnap began the most important philosophical relationship of his life. After serving in US naval intelligence in the Second World War, he taught at Harvard for the rest of his career.\n\nIn “Two Dogmas of Empiricism” (1951) Quine denied that there is a principled line between analytic truths, true by meaning alone, and synthetic truths, true because of the facts. Our beliefs form a single web that faces experience as a whole: when a prediction fails, we could revise anything, even logic, though we prefer to adjust the edges rather than the centre. Philosophy is therefore continuous with science, not a court above it. He also argued that translation is indeterminate: several incompatible ways of translating a stranger’s word “gavagai” could fit all the evidence of how the speaker behaves.","zh":"威拉德·范·奥曼·奎因（1908–2000）在奥柏林学院学数学，用两年时间在哈佛完成了关于《数学原理》的博士论文。1932–33年的游学让他到过维也纳、华沙和布拉格，与卡尔纳普的长谈开启了他一生中最重要的哲学关系。第二次世界大战中他在美国海军情报部门服役，此后一直执教于哈佛。\n\n在《经验论的两个教条》（1951）中，奎因否认分析真理（仅凭意义为真）与综合真理（因事实而真）之间有原则性的界线。我们的信念构成一张整体面对经验的网：预言落空时，我们可以修改任何东西，甚至逻辑，只是我们宁愿调整边缘而不动中心。因此哲学与科学是连续的，而不是凌驾其上的法庭。他还主张翻译具有不确定性：对一个陌生人所说的“gavagai”，几种彼此不相容的译法都可能与说话者的全部行为证据相符。"},"schools":["post-positivism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Two Dogmas of Empiricism","zh":"《经验论的两个教条》"},"y":1951},{"t":{"en":"Word and Object","zh":"《语词和对象》"},"y":1960}],"wiki":"Willard Van Orman Quine"},
  {"id":"ayer","tradition":"analytic","born":1910,"died":1989,"life":{"en":"1910–1989","zh":"1910–1989年"},"name":{"en":"A. J. Ayer","zh":"A. J. 艾耶尔"},"role":{"en":"Oxford philosopher who brought logical positivism to English readers","zh":"把逻辑实证主义带给英语读者的牛津哲学家"},"idea":{"en":"A statement is meaningful only if it is analytic or empirically verifiable; moral judgements are neither, so they express feelings of approval or disapproval rather than facts (emotivism).","zh":"陈述只有是分析的或可经验证实的才有意义；道德判断两者都不是，所以它表达的是赞成或反对的情感，而非事实（情感主义）。"},"detail":{"en":"Alfred Jules Ayer (1910–1989) was educated at Eton and Christ Church, Oxford, where Gilbert Ryle was one of his tutors. At Ryle’s urging he spent early 1933 attending the Vienna Circle, and at twenty-five he published Language, Truth and Logic (1936), a short, combative book that made logical positivism famous in the English-speaking world. After wartime intelligence work he taught at University College London and from 1959 held the Wykeham chair of logic at Oxford.\n\nAyer’s test: a statement means something only if it is true by definition or could in principle be checked by experience. The claims of metaphysics and theology, such as “God exists”, are neither, so they are not false but literally senseless. Moral judgements fail too: “stealing money is wrong” adds nothing to “you stole that money” except a tone of disapproval, as if spoken with horror. This is emotivism. Critics pointed out that the verification principle seems to fail its own test, and late in life Ayer admitted that most of the book was false.","zh":"A. J. 艾耶尔（1910–1989）在伊顿公学和牛津基督教堂学院受教育，赖尔是他的导师之一。在赖尔的鼓励下，他于1933年初去维也纳参加维也纳学派的聚会；二十五岁时出版《语言、真理与逻辑》（1936），这本短小犀利的书让逻辑实证主义在英语世界声名大噪。战时他从事情报工作，战后任教于伦敦大学学院，1959年起任牛津威克姆逻辑学教授。\n\n艾耶尔的检验标准是：一个陈述要有意义，要么依定义为真，要么原则上可由经验检验。形而上学和神学的主张（如“上帝存在”）两者都不是，因此它们不是假的，而是字面上无意义。道德判断也通不过检验：“偷钱是错的”比“你偷了那笔钱”多出的，只是一种不赞成的语气，仿佛带着惊恐说出。这就是情感主义。批评者指出，证实原则本身似乎也通不过它自己的检验；晚年的艾耶尔承认，这本书的大部分内容是错的。"},"schools":["logical-positivism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Language, Truth and Logic","zh":"《语言、真理与逻辑》"},"y":1936},{"t":{"en":"The Problem of Knowledge","zh":"《知识问题》"},"y":1956}],"wiki":"A. J. Ayer"},
  {"id":"austin","tradition":"analytic","born":1911,"died":1960,"life":{"en":"1911–1960","zh":"1911–1960年"},"name":{"en":"J. L. Austin","zh":"J. L. 奥斯汀"},"role":{"en":"Oxford philosopher of language who founded speech-act theory","zh":"开创言语行为理论的牛津语言哲学家"},"idea":{"en":"Many utterances do not describe anything but perform an act: saying “I promise” or “I do” at a wedding is making a promise or getting married. Careful study of ordinary words is the best starting point for philosophy.","zh":"许多话语并不描述什么，而是在做事：说“我保证”，或在婚礼上说“我愿意”，就是在许诺、在结婚。仔细研究日常词语，是哲学最好的起点。"},"detail":{"en":"John Langshaw Austin (1911–1960) studied classics at Oxford and spent his career there. In the Second World War he rose to lieutenant colonel in British military intelligence, helping prepare the D-Day landings. As White’s Professor of Moral Philosophy from 1952 he led the “Saturday mornings”, where Oxford philosophers took ordinary words apart with great care. He published little; his main books were edited from his lectures after he died of cancer at forty-eight.\n\nIn the 1955 William James Lectures at Harvard, published as How to Do Things with Words (1962), Austin noticed that many sentences describe nothing. Saying “I do” at a wedding, “I name this ship the Queen Elizabeth” or “I promise” does not report an act; it performs one. Such utterances are not true or false, but they can misfire: a promise is void if you are not in a position to make it. He went on to argue that every utterance does several things at once, and his student John Searle turned these lectures into a systematic theory of speech acts, now standard in linguistics.","zh":"J. L. 奥斯汀（1911–1960）在牛津学古典学，一生在牛津度过。第二次世界大战中他在英国军事情报部门升至中校，参与诺曼底登陆的准备工作。1952年起任怀特道德哲学教授，主持“周六上午”讨论会，带领牛津哲学家们细致拆解日常用语。他生前发表很少，主要著作是他四十八岁死于癌症之后根据讲稿整理出版的。\n\n在1955年哈佛的威廉·詹姆斯讲座（出版为《如何以言行事》，1962）中，奥斯汀注意到，许多句子并不描述什么。婚礼上说“我愿意”，说“我把这艘船命名为伊丽莎白女王号”，或说“我保证”，都不是在报告一个行为，而是在完成这个行为。这类话语无所谓真假，却可能“失效”：如果你没有资格许诺，许诺就不成立。他进一步主张，每句话都同时在做好几件事；他的学生塞尔把这些讲座发展成系统的言语行为理论，如今已是语言学的标准内容。"},"schools":["ordinary-language"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"How to Do Things with Words (lectures of 1955, posthumous)","zh":"《如何以言行事》（1955年讲稿，身后出版）"},"y":1962},{"t":{"en":"Sense and Sensibilia (posthumous)","zh":"《感觉与可感物》（身后出版）"},"y":1962}],"wiki":"J. L. Austin"},
  {"id":"anscombe","tradition":"analytic","born":1919,"died":2001,"life":{"en":"1919–2001","zh":"1919–2001年"},"name":{"en":"G. E. M. Anscombe","zh":"伊丽莎白·安斯康姆"},"role":{"en":"Wittgenstein’s student and translator who relaunched virtue ethics","zh":"维特根斯坦的学生与译者，重启美德伦理学"},"idea":{"en":"Modern moral philosophy uses a “moral ought” cut off from the divine-law framework that once gave it sense; we should first develop a philosophy of psychology, of action, intention and virtue.","zh":"现代道德哲学使用的“道德应当”，已经脱离了曾经赋予它意义的神圣律法框架；我们应当先发展一套关于行动、意向与美德的心理学哲学。"},"detail":{"en":"Elizabeth Anscombe (1919–2001) became a Catholic as a teenager, studied at Oxford and then at Cambridge, where she was one of Wittgenstein’s closest students. She translated his Philosophical Investigations and was one of his three literary executors. In 1956 she publicly opposed Oxford’s honorary degree for President Truman, calling the atomic bombing of Hiroshima and Nagasaki murder. Married to the philosopher Peter Geach, she held Wittgenstein’s old chair at Cambridge from 1970 to 1986.\n\nHer book Intention (1957) asked what makes an action intentional, and answered that it is one to which a certain sense of the question “Why?” applies, answered by giving reasons; it founded the modern philosophy of action. Her essay “Modern Moral Philosophy” (1958) argued that the idea of a special moral “ought” makes sense only within a framework of divine law that modern moral philosophers had abandoned. Until we have an adequate philosophy of psychology, she urged, we should set it aside and return to Aristotle’s ideas of virtue and flourishing. The essay launched the revival of virtue ethics and coined the word “consequentialism”.","zh":"伊丽莎白·安斯康姆（1919–2001）十几岁时皈依天主教，先后在牛津和剑桥求学，是维特根斯坦最亲近的学生之一。她翻译了《哲学研究》，并是维特根斯坦的三位遗稿执行人之一。1956年她公开反对牛津授予杜鲁门总统荣誉学位，称原子弹轰炸广岛和长崎是谋杀。她与哲学家吉奇结为夫妇，1970至1986年担任维特根斯坦曾经担任的剑桥哲学讲席。\n\n她的《意向》（1957）追问：是什么让一个行动成为有意的？她的回答是：对这个行动，可以在某种意义上问“为什么”，并以理由作答。此书奠定了现代行动哲学。她的论文《现代道德哲学》（1958）认为，一种特别的道德“应当”只有在神法框架中才讲得通，而现代道德哲学家早已抛弃了这个框架；在拥有恰当的心理哲学之前，我们应当把它放在一边，回到亚里士多德关于美德与人的兴盛的观念。这篇论文开启了美德伦理学的复兴，“后果主义”一词也出自此文。"},"schools":["virtue-ethics"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Intention","zh":"《意向》"},"y":1957},{"t":{"en":"Modern Moral Philosophy","zh":"《现代道德哲学》"},"y":1958}],"wiki":"G. E. M. Anscombe"},
  {"id":"foot","tradition":"analytic","born":1920,"died":2010,"life":{"en":"1920–2010","zh":"1920–2010年"},"name":{"en":"Philippa Foot","zh":"菲利帕·富特"},"role":{"en":"Oxford moral philosopher who devised the trolley problem","zh":"提出“电车难题”的牛津道德哲学家"},"idea":{"en":"Moral goodness is a kind of natural goodness: just as a plant or animal can be a good or defective specimen of its kind, human virtues are what human beings need to live well.","zh":"道德之善是一种自然之善：正如植物或动物可以是其种类中良好或有缺陷的个体，人的美德就是人要活得好所需要的东西。"},"detail":{"en":"Philippa Foot (1920–2010), a granddaughter of US President Grover Cleveland, studied at Somerville College, Oxford, and spent most of her career there; from 1976 to 1991 she also taught at UCLA. With Elizabeth Anscombe, Iris Murdoch and Mary Midgley she belonged to a remarkable group of women philosophers at wartime and postwar Oxford, and from 1948 she was active in the charity Oxfam for the rest of her life.\n\nFoot attacked the view, common after Hume and Ayer, that moral judgements only express feelings or choices: words like “good” or “rude” cannot be applied to anything at all regardless of facts about human life. In Natural Goodness (2001) she argued that we judge a human will the way we judge a plant’s roots or a wolf’s hunting, by what members of the species need in order to flourish, and human beings need virtues such as justice, courage and honesty. In a 1967 paper on abortion and the doctrine of double effect she imagined the driver of a runaway tram who can steer onto a track where it will kill one person instead of five: the origin of the “trolley problem”.","zh":"菲莉帕·富特（1920–2010）是美国总统格罗弗·克利夫兰的外孙女，在牛津萨默维尔学院求学，职业生涯大部分在那里度过；1976至1991年她也在加州大学洛杉矶分校任教。她与安斯康姆、默多克、米奇利同属战时和战后牛津一群出色的女性哲学家，从1948年起直到晚年，她一直积极参与慈善组织乐施会。\n\n在休谟和艾耶尔之后，流行一种看法：道德判断只是表达感受或选择。富特反驳说，“好”或“粗鲁”这样的词，不能不顾人类生活的事实而随意用在任何东西上。在《自然的善》（2001）中，她主张我们评判人的意志，就像评判植物的根或狼的捕猎一样，依据的是这个物种的成员为了兴盛需要什么；而人需要正义、勇敢、诚实等美德。在1967年一篇讨论堕胎与“双重效果原则”的论文里，她设想一辆失控电车的司机可以把车转到另一条轨道上，撞死一人而不是五人——这就是“电车难题”的起源。"},"schools":["virtue-ethics"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"The Problem of Abortion and the Doctrine of the Double Effect","zh":"《堕胎问题与双重效应原则》"},"y":1967},{"t":{"en":"Virtues and Vices","zh":"《美德与恶行》"},"y":1978},{"t":{"en":"Natural Goodness","zh":"《自然的善》"},"y":2001}],"wiki":"Philippa Foot"},
  {"id":"rawls","tradition":"analytic","born":1921,"died":2002,"life":{"en":"1921–2002","zh":"1921–2002年"},"name":{"en":"John Rawls","zh":"约翰·罗尔斯"},"role":{"en":"Harvard philosopher who revived political philosophy with justice as fairness","zh":"以“作为公平的正义”复兴政治哲学的哈佛哲学家"},"idea":{"en":"Principles of justice are those free and equal people would choose behind a veil of ignorance: equal basic liberties for all, and social and economic inequalities only if they benefit the least advantaged.","zh":"正义原则是自由平等的人在无知之幕后会选择的原则：人人享有平等的基本自由；社会和经济的不平等只有在有利于最不利者时才被允许。"},"detail":{"en":"John Rawls (1921–2002) grew up in Baltimore, where two of his younger brothers died of illnesses caught from him. He served as an infantryman in the Pacific in the Second World War, an experience that ended his plans for the priesthood, and after a doctorate at Princeton he taught at Harvard from 1962. A Theory of Justice (1971), twenty years in the making, revived political philosophy in the English-speaking world.\n\nRawls asks which principles of justice we would choose if we did not know our place in society: our talents, class, race or idea of the good life. In this “original position”, behind a “veil of ignorance”, he argued, we would choose, first, equal basic liberties for everyone, and second, that social and economic inequalities are acceptable only under fair equality of opportunity and only if they benefit the least advantaged (the “difference principle”). Against utilitarianism, no one’s basic rights may be sacrificed for a greater total good. Nozick replied from the right and Nussbaum from the left; Political Liberalism (1993) asked how people with deeply different beliefs can share one just society.","zh":"约翰·罗尔斯（1921–2002）在巴尔的摩长大，他的两个弟弟因被他传染疾病而夭折。第二次世界大战中他在太平洋战场当步兵，这段经历打消了他做神职人员的打算；在普林斯顿获博士学位后，他1962年起任教于哈佛。酝酿了二十年的《正义论》（1971）让政治哲学在英语世界重获生机。\n\n罗尔斯问：如果我们不知道自己在社会中的位置——自己的天赋、阶层、种族和人生理想——会选择什么样的正义原则？他认为，在这种“无知之幕”背后的“原初状态”中，我们会选择：第一，人人享有平等的基本自由；第二，社会和经济的不平等只有在公平的机会平等之下、并且有利于最不利者时才可接受（“差别原则”）。与功利主义相反，任何人的基本权利都不能为了更大的总体利益而被牺牲。诺齐克从右边回应他，努斯鲍姆从左边回应他；《政治自由主义》（1993）则追问：信念截然不同的人如何能共处于一个正义的社会。"},"schools":["liberal-egalitarianism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"A Theory of Justice","zh":"《正义论》"},"y":1971},{"t":{"en":"Political Liberalism","zh":"《政治自由主义》"},"y":1993}],"wiki":"John Rawls"},
  {"id":"kuhn","tradition":"analytic","born":1922,"died":1996,"life":{"en":"1922–1996","zh":"1922–1996年"},"name":{"en":"Thomas Kuhn","zh":"托马斯·库恩"},"role":{"en":"Physicist turned historian of science who coined “paradigm shift”","zh":"由物理学家转为科学史家，提出“范式转换”"},"idea":{"en":"Science mostly proceeds as “normal science” within a shared paradigm; when anomalies accumulate, a revolution installs a new paradigm that cannot be judged simply by the standards of the old.","zh":"科学大多以“常规科学”的形式在共享的范式内进行；当反常不断积累，一场革命便确立新范式，而新旧范式之间无法简单地用同一套标准衡量。"},"detail":{"en":"Thomas Kuhn (1922–1996) took a PhD in physics at Harvard, but while preparing a general-education course on the history of science he read Aristotle’s physics and realised that it was not bad science but a different, coherent way of seeing nature. He turned to history, taught at Berkeley, Princeton and MIT, and in 1962 published The Structure of Scientific Revolutions, one of the most cited books of the twentieth century.\n\nKuhn argued that most science is “normal science”: puzzle-solving within a paradigm, a shared set of exemplary achievements, methods and assumptions that tells scientists which problems matter. Anomalies are at first set aside; when they pile up, a crisis leads to a revolution, as from Ptolemy to Copernicus or from Newton to Einstein. Rival paradigms are partly “incommensurable”, so the change is not settled by logic and evidence alone. Popper and others objected that this made science irrational; Kuhn answered that scientists choose between theories by shared values such as accuracy, simplicity and fruitfulness. “Paradigm shift” entered everyday speech.","zh":"托马斯·库恩（1922–1996）在哈佛获物理学博士，但在准备一门科学史通识课时读了亚里士多德的物理学，意识到那不是糟糕的科学，而是另一种自洽的看待自然的方式。他由此转向科学史，先后执教于伯克利、普林斯顿和麻省理工学院，1962年出版《科学革命的结构》，成为二十世纪被引用最多的书之一。\n\n库恩认为，大部分科学是“常规科学”：在一个范式之内解谜。范式是一套共享的典范成就、方法和假设，告诉科学家哪些问题重要。反常起初会被搁置；反常积累到一定程度就引发危机，进而发生革命，比如从托勒密到哥白尼、从牛顿到爱因斯坦。相互竞争的范式在一定程度上“不可通约”，因此转变并不只由逻辑和证据决定。波普尔等人批评这让科学变得非理性；库恩回答说，科学家依据准确、简单、富有成果等共同价值在理论之间作选择。“范式转移”一词从此进入日常语言。"},"schools":["post-positivism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"The Copernican Revolution","zh":"《哥白尼革命》"},"y":1957},{"t":{"en":"The Structure of Scientific Revolutions","zh":"《科学革命的结构》"},"y":1962}],"wiki":"Thomas Kuhn"},
  {"id":"putnam","tradition":"analytic","born":1926,"died":2016,"life":{"en":"1926–2016","zh":"1926–2016年"},"name":{"en":"Hilary Putnam","zh":"希拉里·普特南"},"role":{"en":"Harvard philosopher who launched functionalism, then criticised it","zh":"提出功能主义、后来又批判它的哈佛哲学家"},"idea":{"en":"Mental states are functional states that could be realised in many materials; and “meanings just ain’t in the head”: what our words refer to depends on the world. He later turned against functionalism and toward pragmatism.","zh":"心理状态是可以由多种材料实现的功能状态；而且“意义不在头脑之中”：词语指称什么取决于世界本身。他后来转而反对功能主义，走向实用主义。"},"detail":{"en":"Hilary Putnam (1926–2016) studied with Hans Reichenbach at UCLA and first made his name in mathematical logic, contributing to the solution of Hilbert’s tenth problem. He taught at Princeton and MIT and from 1965 at Harvard. Politically active, he joined a Maoist group in the late 1960s, and he was famous for changing his mind in public when he thought his earlier arguments had failed.\n\nIn the 1960s Putnam proposed functionalism: a mental state such as pain is defined by its role, by what causes it and what it causes, not by what it is made of, so it could be realised in a human brain, an octopus or a computer. In “The Meaning of ‘Meaning’” (1975) he imagined Twin Earth, where a liquid exactly like water is chemically XYZ, not H₂O. A Twin Earthling’s word “water” refers to XYZ even though her thoughts feel the same as ours, so “meanings just ain’t in the head”. Later he criticised functionalism, rejected the idea of a “God’s-eye view” of reality, and ended close to the pragmatism of James and Dewey.","zh":"希拉里·普特南（1926–2016）在加州大学洛杉矶分校师从赖兴巴赫，最初以数理逻辑成名，参与解决了希尔伯特第十问题。他先后执教于普林斯顿和麻省理工，1965年起任教于哈佛。他积极参与政治，六十年代末加入过一个毛派团体；他也以公开改变观点著称——一旦认为自己先前的论证站不住，就会直说。\n\n六十年代，普特南提出功能主义：疼痛这样的心理状态由它的作用来界定，即由什么引起它、它又引起什么，而不在于它由什么构成，因此它可以实现在人脑中、章鱼身上或电脑里。在《“意义”的意义》（1975）中，他设想了一个“孪生地球”，那里有一种与水毫无二致的液体，化学成分却是XYZ而不是H₂O。孪生地球人说“水”时指的是XYZ，尽管她的想法和我们感觉一样——所以“意义根本不在头脑里”。后来他批评功能主义，拒绝“上帝之眼”式的实在观，最终走近詹姆士和杜威的实用主义。"},"schools":["philosophy-of-mind"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Psychological Predicates","zh":"《心理谓词》"},"y":1967},{"t":{"en":"The Meaning of “Meaning”","zh":"《“意义”的意义》"},"y":1975},{"t":{"en":"Reason, Truth and History","zh":"《理性、真理与历史》"},"y":1981}],"wiki":"Hilary Putnam"},
  {"id":"macintyre","tradition":"analytic","born":1929,"died":2025,"life":{"en":"1929–2025","zh":"1929–2025年"},"name":{"en":"Alasdair MacIntyre","zh":"阿拉斯戴尔·麦金太尔"},"role":{"en":"Scottish-born philosopher who moved from Marxism to Thomism","zh":"生于苏格兰、由马克思主义转向托马斯主义的哲学家"},"idea":{"en":"Modern moral debate is interminable because it uses fragments of older traditions without their context; virtues make sense only within practices, the narrative unity of a life, and a living tradition.","zh":"现代道德争论之所以永无止境，是因为它使用的是脱离了原有语境的旧传统碎片；美德只有在实践、生命的叙事统一性和活的传统之中才有意义。"},"detail":{"en":"Alasdair MacIntyre (1929–2025) was born in Glasgow and educated in London, Manchester and Oxford. As a young man he was a Marxist, a member of the Communist Party until 1956 and then of Trotskyist groups. He moved to the United States around 1970, taught at several universities and from the 1980s mainly at Notre Dame, with a spell at Duke, retiring in 2010. In the early 1980s he became a Catholic and a Thomist. He died in May 2025, aged 96.\n\nAfter Virtue (1981) opens with a parable: imagine that after a catastrophe people go on using the words of a lost natural science without understanding them. Modern moral language, he argues, is like that: we use fragments such as “rights”, “utility” and “duty” torn from the traditions that once gave them sense, so moral debates end in assertion against assertion. Virtues are the qualities needed to achieve the goods internal to practices such as chess, farming or medicine, to give a life the unity of a story, and to sustain a living tradition. The choice, he concluded, is “Nietzsche or Aristotle?”, and he chose Aristotle, and later Aquinas.","zh":"阿拉斯戴尔·麦金太尔（1929–2025）生于格拉斯哥，在伦敦、曼彻斯特和牛津受教育。青年时期他是马克思主义者，1956年前是英国共产党党员，此后参加过托洛茨基派团体。约1970年他移居美国，在多所大学任教，八十年代起主要在圣母大学（其间一度在杜克大学），2010年退休。八十年代初他皈依天主教，成为托马斯主义者。他于2025年5月去世，享年96岁。\n\n《追寻美德》（1981）以一则寓言开篇：设想一场灾难之后，人们仍在使用一门失落的自然科学的词汇，却不再懂它们的意思。他认为，现代道德语言正是如此：我们使用的“权利”“功用”“义务”等，都是从曾赋予它们意义的传统中撕下的碎片，因此道德争论只能以断言对断言收场。美德是这样一些品质：它们让人得以实现诸如下棋、务农、行医等实践的内在之善，让一生具有故事般的统一性，并维系一个活的传统。他的结论是，选择在于“尼采还是亚里士多德”，而他选择了亚里士多德，后来又经由阿奎那。"},"schools":["virtue-ethics"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"After Virtue","zh":"《追寻美德》"},"y":1981},{"t":{"en":"Whose Justice? Which Rationality?","zh":"《谁之正义？何种合理性？》"},"y":1988}],"wiki":"Alasdair MacIntyre"},
  {"id":"rorty","tradition":"analytic","born":1931,"died":2007,"life":{"en":"1931–2007","zh":"1931–2007年"},"name":{"en":"Richard Rorty","zh":"理查德·罗蒂"},"role":{"en":"Neopragmatist who challenged philosophy’s claim to mirror reality","zh":"质疑哲学能“映照”实在的新实用主义者"},"idea":{"en":"The mind is not a mirror of nature and philosophy cannot ground knowledge from outside our practices; we should aim at solidarity and less cruelty rather than at certainty.","zh":"心灵不是自然之镜，哲学也无法从我们的实践之外为知识奠基；我们应当追求团结、减少残忍，而不是追求确定性。"},"detail":{"en":"Richard Rorty (1931–2007) grew up in New York among anti-Stalinist leftists, entered the University of Chicago at fifteen and took his PhD at Yale. Trained in analytic philosophy, he taught at Princeton until Philosophy and the Mirror of Nature (1979) set him against much of his profession; he left philosophy departments for posts in the humanities at Virginia and in comparative literature at Stanford.\n\nRorty argued that the picture of the mind as a mirror that represents reality, inherited from Descartes, Locke and Kant, has outlived its use. We cannot step outside our language and practices to check them against the world, so truth is not a matching of words to things but what our peers let us get away with saying, and philosophy should stop acting as the judge of all culture and join the conversation. In Contingency, Irony, and Solidarity (1989) he described the “liberal ironist”, who knows her deepest beliefs are contingent yet holds that cruelty is the worst thing we do. Critics called this relativism; he called it Dewey’s pragmatism brought up to date.","zh":"理查德·罗蒂（1931–2007）在纽约反斯大林主义左派的圈子里长大，十五岁进入芝加哥大学，后在耶鲁获博士学位。他受分析哲学训练，在普林斯顿任教，直到《哲学和自然之镜》（1979）让他与本行的许多同事对立；此后他离开哲学系，先后在弗吉尼亚大学任人文学科教授、在斯坦福任比较文学教授。\n\n罗蒂认为，从笛卡尔、洛克、康德继承下来的“心灵如镜、映照实在”的图景已经过时。我们无法跳出自己的语言和实践，去把它们同世界对照，因此真理不是言辞与事物的符合，而是同行允许我们说的话；哲学应当放弃充当整个文化的裁判，加入“对话”。在《偶然、反讽与团结》（1989）中，他描绘了“自由主义反讽者”：她知道自己最深的信念都是偶然的，却仍坚持残忍是我们所做的最坏的事。批评者称之为相对主义，他则称之为更新了的杜威实用主义。"},"schools":["pragmatism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Philosophy and the Mirror of Nature","zh":"《哲学和自然之镜》"},"y":1979},{"t":{"en":"Contingency, Irony, and Solidarity","zh":"《偶然、反讽与团结》"},"y":1989}],"wiki":"Richard Rorty"},
  {"id":"searle","tradition":"analytic","born":1932,"died":2025,"life":{"en":"1932–2025","zh":"1932–2025年"},"name":{"en":"John Searle","zh":"约翰·塞尔"},"role":{"en":"Berkeley philosopher of language and mind, author of the Chinese Room","zh":"伯克利的语言与心灵哲学家，“中文屋”论证的提出者"},"idea":{"en":"A person who follows rules to shuffle Chinese symbols could answer questions in Chinese without understanding a word, so running a program is not the same as having a mind; understanding depends on the causal powers of brains.","zh":"一个按规则摆弄中文符号的人，可以用中文回答问题却一个字也不懂；所以运行程序并不等于拥有心灵，理解取决于大脑的因果能力。"},"detail":{"en":"John Searle (1932–2025) was born in Denver and went to Oxford as a Rhodes Scholar, writing his doctorate there under J. L. Austin and Peter Strawson. From 1959 he taught at the University of California, Berkeley, where in 1964 he was the first tenured professor to back the Free Speech Movement. In 2019 the university revoked his emeritus status after finding that he had violated its sexual-harassment policies. He died in September 2025, aged 93.\n\nSearle turned Austin’s lectures into a systematic theory of speech acts (1969). His best-known argument, the Chinese Room (1980), asks you to imagine sitting in a room with an English rulebook for matching Chinese symbols to other Chinese symbols. People outside receive fluent answers to their questions in Chinese, yet you understand nothing. A computer running a program does only what you do, manipulating symbols by their shapes, so a program alone cannot produce understanding. Minds, he held, are biological features caused by brain processes (“biological naturalism”). Critics, Dennett among them, replied that the whole system understands even if the man inside does not.","zh":"约翰·塞尔（1932–2025）生于丹佛，以罗德学者身份到牛津，在奥斯汀和斯特劳森指导下完成博士论文。1959年起他任教于加州大学伯克利分校，1964年成为第一位支持“言论自由运动”的终身教授。2019年，加州大学认定他违反了性骚扰相关规定，撤销了他的荣休身份。他于2025年9月去世，享年93岁。\n\n塞尔把奥斯汀的讲座发展成系统的言语行为理论（1969）。他最著名的论证是“中文屋”（1980）：设想你坐在一个房间里，手里有一本英文规则手册，教你把中文符号与别的中文符号配对。屋外的人收到流利的中文答复，而你一个字也不懂。运行程序的电脑做的正是你做的事——按形状摆弄符号——所以单靠程序产生不了理解。他认为心灵是由大脑过程引起的生物学特征（“生物自然主义”）。丹尼特等批评者回应说：即使屋里的人不懂，整个系统是懂的。"},"schools":["philosophy-of-mind"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Speech Acts","zh":"《言语行为》"},"y":1969},{"t":{"en":"Minds, Brains, and Programs","zh":"《心灵、大脑与程序》"},"y":1980},{"t":{"en":"The Rediscovery of the Mind","zh":"《心灵的再发现》"},"y":1992}],"wiki":"John Searle"},
  {"id":"nagel","tradition":"analytic","born":1937,"died":null,"life":{"en":"born 1937","zh":"1937年生"},"name":{"en":"Thomas Nagel","zh":"托马斯·内格尔"},"role":{"en":"NYU philosopher who asked what it is like to be a bat","zh":"追问“成为一只蝙蝠是什么样子”的纽约大学哲学家"},"idea":{"en":"An organism is conscious if there is something it is like to be it; this subjective point of view cannot be captured by any objective, physical description, the “view from nowhere”.","zh":"如果“成为某个生物”是某种样子，那么它就有意识；这种主观视角无法被任何客观的物理描述——“本然的观点”——所捕捉。"},"detail":{"en":"Thomas Nagel (born 1937 in Belgrade) grew up in the United States, studied at Cornell and at Oxford, and wrote his doctorate at Harvard under John Rawls. After teaching at Berkeley and Princeton he moved in 1980 to New York University, where he became University Professor. He writes in a clear, unspecialised style, and his short book What Does It All Mean? (1987) is a classic introduction to philosophy.\n\nIn “What Is It Like to Be a Bat?” (1974) Nagel points out that bats perceive the world by echolocation. We could learn every physical fact about a bat’s brain and still not know what it is like, for the bat, to experience the world that way. Experience is tied to a point of view, and physical science works by abstracting from points of view, so it leaves something out. The tension between the subjective and the objective, the “view from nowhere”, runs through his work on ethics, free will and the meaning of life. In Mind and Cosmos (2012) he argued that materialist neo-Darwinism cannot explain consciousness, a claim many scientists rejected.","zh":"托马斯·内格尔（1937年生于贝尔格莱德）在美国长大，先后就读于康奈尔和牛津，在哈佛师从罗尔斯完成博士论文。他在伯克利和普林斯顿任教后，1980年转到纽约大学，后任校级教授。他的文字清晰、不卖弄术语，小书《这一切意味着什么？》（1987）是经典的哲学入门读物。\n\n在《成为一只蝙蝠是什么样子？》（1974）中，内格尔指出蝙蝠靠回声定位感知世界。我们可以掌握蝙蝠大脑的全部物理事实，却仍然不知道：对蝙蝠自己来说，那样体验世界是什么感觉。经验总是系于某个视角，而物理科学恰恰靠抽离视角来运作，所以它遗漏了什么。主观与客观之间的张力——“本然的观点”——贯穿了他关于伦理、自由意志和人生意义的著作。在《心灵与宇宙》（2012）中，他主张唯物主义的新达尔文主义解释不了意识，这一主张遭到许多科学家反对。"},"schools":["philosophy-of-mind"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"What Is It Like to Be a Bat?","zh":"《成为一只蝙蝠可能是什么样子？》"},"y":1974},{"t":{"en":"The View from Nowhere","zh":"《本然的观点》"},"y":1986},{"t":{"en":"Mind and Cosmos","zh":"《心灵与宇宙》"},"y":2012}],"wiki":"Thomas Nagel"},
  {"id":"nozick","tradition":"analytic","born":1938,"died":2002,"life":{"en":"1938–2002","zh":"1938–2002年"},"name":{"en":"Robert Nozick","zh":"罗伯特·诺齐克"},"role":{"en":"Harvard libertarian who answered Rawls","zh":"回应罗尔斯的哈佛自由至上主义者"},"idea":{"en":"Individuals have rights that set strict limits on what the state may do; a distribution is just if it arose from just acquisition and free exchange, whatever pattern results, so only a minimal state is justified.","zh":"个人拥有严格限定国家行为的权利；只要一种分配源于正当的获取和自由的交换，不论结果呈现什么样的模式，它就是正义的，因此只有“最小国家”才是正当的。"},"detail":{"en":"Robert Nozick (1938–2002) was born in Brooklyn to a Jewish family from Russia, was a socialist student activist at Columbia, and took his doctorate at Princeton. He joined Harvard’s philosophy department in 1969, became a colleague of Rawls and stayed there for the rest of his life. Anarchy, State, and Utopia (1974), written in a brilliant, playful style, won a National Book Award; afterwards he moved restlessly to other subjects, from knowledge and free will to the meaning of life.\n\nNozick starts from rights: individuals have rights that no one, the state included, may violate. A distribution of goods is just if it arose from just acquisition and voluntary transfer, whatever pattern results. His Wilt Chamberlain example: begin from any distribution you think fair; if a million fans each freely pay to watch the basketball star play, he grows rich and nobody’s rights are violated, so keeping any fixed pattern would mean constantly interfering with free choices. Only a minimal state that protects against force, theft and fraud is justified, and he argued that redistributive taxation is on a par with forced labour.","zh":"罗伯特·诺齐克（1938–2002）出生于布鲁克林一个来自俄国的犹太家庭，在哥伦比亚大学读书时是社会主义学生活动分子，后在普林斯顿获博士学位。1969年他加入哈佛哲学系，成为罗尔斯的同事，终生没有离开。文笔精彩俏皮的《无政府、国家和乌托邦》（1974）获美国国家图书奖；此后他兴趣不断转移，从知识论、自由意志一直写到人生的意义。\n\n诺齐克从权利出发：个人拥有任何人——包括国家——都不得侵犯的权利。一种财富分配只要源于正当的获取和自愿的转让，无论最后呈现什么格局，都是正义的。他举过“张伯伦”的例子：从任何你认为公平的分配开始；如果一百万球迷各自自愿付钱看这位篮球明星打球，他变富了，而谁的权利也没有受到侵犯——所以要维持任何固定的分配格局，就得不断干涉人们的自由选择。只有防范暴力、盗窃和欺诈的“最低限度国家”是正当的；他认为再分配的税收无异于强迫劳动。"},"schools":["liberal-egalitarianism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Anarchy, State, and Utopia","zh":"《无政府、国家和乌托邦》"},"y":1974},{"t":{"en":"Philosophical Explanations","zh":"《哲学解释》"},"y":1981}],"wiki":"Robert Nozick"},
  {"id":"dennett","tradition":"analytic","born":1942,"died":2024,"life":{"en":"1942–2024","zh":"1942–2024年"},"name":{"en":"Daniel Dennett","zh":"丹尼尔·丹尼特"},"role":{"en":"Tufts philosopher who explained consciousness as the brain’s work","zh":"试图用大脑的运作解释意识的塔夫茨大学哲学家"},"idea":{"en":"There is no inner theatre where experience comes together; consciousness is many parallel processes in the brain, and the sense of an extra “hard problem” is a powerful illusion. Evolution by natural selection explains design without a designer.","zh":"大脑中并没有一个汇聚经验的“内在剧场”；意识是大脑中许多并行的过程，而觉得还剩下一个“难问题”，是一种强有力的错觉。自然选择的演化能够解释没有设计者的设计。"},"detail":{"en":"Daniel Dennett (1942–2024) was drawn to philosophy by reading Quine as a first-year student at Wesleyan, transferred to Harvard to study with him, and wrote his doctorate at Oxford under Gilbert Ryle. From 1971 he taught at Tufts University, co-directing its Center for Cognitive Studies, and he worked closely with researchers in artificial intelligence, psychology and biology. A tireless writer for general readers and one of the “Four Horsemen” of the new atheism, he died in April 2024.\n\nDennett argued that there is no “Cartesian theatre” in the brain where experience comes together for an inner viewer. Instead, many parallel processes produce “multiple drafts”, and what we call consciousness is whichever drafts win influence over memory and speech. The feeling that something more remains to be explained, the “hard problem”, is in his view a kind of user illusion. He also explained talk of beliefs and desires as an “intentional stance”, a strategy for predicting behaviour, and in Darwin’s Dangerous Idea (1995) he described natural selection as a mindless algorithm that produces design without a designer.","zh":"丹尼尔·丹尼特（1942–2024）在卫斯理安大学读一年级时因读奎因而走上哲学之路，随即转学哈佛追随奎因；后在牛津师从赖尔完成博士论文。1971年起他任教于塔夫茨大学，并共同主持认知研究中心，与人工智能、心理学和生物学的研究者密切合作。他为普通读者笔耕不辍，是“新无神论四骑士”之一，于2024年4月去世。\n\n丹尼特认为，大脑里并没有一个“笛卡尔剧场”，让经验汇聚起来供内在的观众观看。实际上，许多并行过程产生出“多重草稿”，我们所谓的意识，就是那些赢得了对记忆和言语影响力的草稿。觉得还有更多东西有待解释——所谓“困难问题”——在他看来是一种“用户错觉”。他还把关于信念和欲望的谈论解释为一种“意向立场”，即预测行为的策略；在《达尔文的危险观念》（1995）中，他把自然选择描述为一种无心的算法，它不需要设计者就能产生设计。"},"schools":["philosophy-of-mind"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Consciousness Explained","zh":"《意识的解释》"},"y":1991},{"t":{"en":"Darwin’s Dangerous Idea","zh":"《达尔文的危险观念》"},"y":1995}],"wiki":"Daniel Dennett"},
  {"id":"singer","tradition":"analytic","born":1946,"died":null,"life":{"en":"born 1946","zh":"1946年生"},"name":{"en":"Peter Singer","zh":"彼得·辛格"},"role":{"en":"Australian utilitarian of animal liberation and effective giving","zh":"倡导动物解放与有效利他的澳大利亚功利主义者"},"idea":{"en":"Equal interests deserve equal consideration whoever has them, so the suffering of animals counts, and if we can prevent something very bad without sacrificing anything of comparable importance, we ought to do it.","zh":"同等的利益应得到同等的考虑，不论它属于谁，所以动物的痛苦同样重要；如果我们能在不牺牲同等重要之物的前提下阻止非常糟糕的事，就应当去做。"},"detail":{"en":"Peter Singer (born 1946 in Melbourne) is the son of Viennese Jews who fled to Australia in 1938; his grandparents were among the victims of the Holocaust. He studied in Melbourne and at Oxford under R. M. Hare, founded the Centre for Human Bioethics at Monash University, and from 1999 taught at Princeton, where he is now emeritus. He co-founded The Life You Can Save and helped inspire the effective-altruism movement.\n\nSinger is a utilitarian: what matters morally is the interests of every being that can suffer or enjoy, counted equally. In “Famine, Affluence, and Morality” (1972) he argued that if you would wade into a pond to save a drowning child at the cost of ruining your clothes, you ought equally to give money that would save a child far away, because distance makes no moral difference. Animal Liberation (1975) extended equal consideration to animals and, using Richard Ryder’s term, condemned “speciesism”, helping to launch the modern animal-rights movement. His views on euthanasia and on severely disabled newborns have provoked fierce protests.","zh":"彼得·辛格（1946年生于墨尔本）的父母是1938年逃往澳大利亚的维也纳犹太人，他的祖辈死于大屠杀。他在墨尔本求学，后在牛津师从黑尔，在莫纳什大学创办人类生命伦理学中心，1999年起任教于普林斯顿，现为荣休教授。他参与创办“你能拯救的生命”组织，也是有效利他主义运动的思想先驱之一。\n\n辛格是功利主义者：道德上要紧的，是一切能感受痛苦或快乐的存在者的利益，而且同等计算。在《饥荒、富裕与道德》（1972）中他论证：如果你愿意弄脏衣服、蹚进池塘去救一个落水儿童，那么你同样应当捐钱去救远方的孩子，因为距离在道德上无关紧要。《动物解放》（1975）把平等考虑扩展到动物，借用理查德·赖德的说法谴责“物种歧视”，推动了现代动物权利运动的兴起。他关于安乐死和重度残障新生儿的观点曾引发激烈抗议。"},"schools":["utilitarianism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Famine, Affluence, and Morality","zh":"《饥荒、富裕与道德》"},"y":1972},{"t":{"en":"Animal Liberation","zh":"《动物解放》"},"y":1975},{"t":{"en":"Practical Ethics","zh":"《实践伦理学》"},"y":1979}],"wiki":"Peter Singer"},
  {"id":"nussbaum","tradition":"analytic","born":1947,"died":null,"life":{"en":"born 1947","zh":"1947年生"},"name":{"en":"Martha Nussbaum","zh":"玛莎·努斯鲍姆"},"role":{"en":"Chicago philosopher of the capabilities approach and the emotions","zh":"芝加哥大学哲学家，倡导能力进路、研究情感"},"idea":{"en":"Justice should secure for every person a threshold of central capabilities, such as health, education, play and political voice; drawing on Aristotle and the Greek tragedians, she argues that emotions and vulnerability belong to ethics.","zh":"正义应当保障每个人都达到一系列核心能力的门槛，如健康、教育、游戏与政治发言权；她借鉴亚里士多德和希腊悲剧，主张情感与脆弱性本就属于伦理学。"},"detail":{"en":"Martha Nussbaum (born 1947 in New York) studied theatre and classics before writing a Harvard doctorate on Aristotle. She taught at Harvard and Brown and since 1995 has been at the University of Chicago, where she holds a chair in law and ethics. Her collaboration with the economist Amartya Sen at the UN’s development research institute in Helsinki shaped the “capabilities approach”, and in 2026 the Library of Congress awarded her the Kluge Prize.\n\nThe Fragility of Goodness (1986) draws on Greek tragedy and philosophy to argue that a good human life is exposed to luck, and that emotions are not blind forces but judgements about what matters. In politics she asks not how much wealth people have but what they are actually able to do and to be. A just society must secure for every person a threshold of ten central capabilities, among them life, health, bodily integrity, practical reason, play, affiliation and some control over one’s environment. Frontiers of Justice (2006) argues that social-contract theories like Rawls’s leave out people with disabilities, citizens of other nations and animals.","zh":"玛莎·努斯鲍姆（1947年生于纽约）先学戏剧和古典学，后在哈佛以亚里士多德为题完成博士论文。她曾任教于哈佛和布朗大学，1995年起执教于芝加哥大学，任法律与伦理学讲席教授。她与经济学家阿马蒂亚·森在联合国位于赫尔辛基的发展研究机构合作，塑造了“可行能力进路”；2026年美国国会图书馆授予她克鲁格奖。\n\n《善的脆弱性》（1986）借助希腊悲剧和哲学论证：美好的人生暴露在运气之下，情感也不是盲目的力量，而是关于什么重要的判断。在政治上，她问的不是人们拥有多少财富，而是他们实际上能够做什么、成为什么。正义的社会必须保证每个人达到十项核心能力的门槛，其中包括生命、健康、身体完整、实践理性、游戏、归属以及对自身环境的一定掌控。《正义的前沿》（2006）指出，像罗尔斯那样的社会契约论遗漏了残障人士、他国公民和动物。"},"schools":["liberal-egalitarianism"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"The Fragility of Goodness","zh":"《善的脆弱性》"},"y":1986},{"t":{"en":"Frontiers of Justice","zh":"《正义的前沿》"},"y":2006},{"t":{"en":"Creating Capabilities","zh":"《寻求有尊严的生活：正义的能力理论》"},"y":2011}],"wiki":"Martha Nussbaum"},
  {"id":"chalmers","tradition":"analytic","born":1966,"died":null,"life":{"en":"born 1966","zh":"1966年生"},"name":{"en":"David Chalmers","zh":"大卫·查默斯"},"role":{"en":"Australian philosopher who named the “hard problem” of consciousness","zh":"提出意识“难问题”的澳大利亚哲学家"},"idea":{"en":"Explaining the brain’s functions (the “easy problems”) leaves open why any of it is accompanied by experience at all; since a physical duplicate without experience seems conceivable, consciousness may need new fundamental laws.","zh":"解释大脑的种种功能（“易问题”）之后，仍然留下一个问题：为什么这一切会伴随着经验？既然一个没有经验的物理复制品似乎是可设想的，意识或许需要新的基本定律来解释。"},"detail":{"en":"David Chalmers (born 1966 in Sydney) studied mathematics in Adelaide and at Oxford before turning to the philosophy of mind, taking his PhD at Indiana University in 1993 in Douglas Hofstadter’s research group. At a conference in Tucson in 1994 he named “the hard problem of consciousness”. He has taught at the Australian National University and is now University Professor at New York University, where he co-directs the Center for Mind, Brain, and Consciousness.\n\nChalmers separates the “easy problems” of consciousness, explaining how the brain discriminates stimuli, integrates information and reports its states, from the hard problem: why any of this is accompanied by felt experience at all. His zombie argument runs: a being physically identical to you but with no inner experience seems conceivable; if so, the physical facts do not settle the facts of experience, and we may need new fundamental laws linking the two. With Andy Clark he proposed the “extended mind”, and in Reality+ (2022) he argues that virtual worlds can be genuinely real. Dennett called the hard problem an illusion; the debate is unresolved.","zh":"大卫·查默斯（1966年生于悉尼）先在阿德莱德和牛津学数学，后转向心灵哲学，1993年在印第安纳大学侯世达的研究组获博士学位。1994年在图森的一次会议上，他提出了“意识的困难问题”这一说法。他曾任教于澳大利亚国立大学，现为纽约大学校级教授，并共同主持该校心灵、大脑与意识中心。\n\n查默斯把意识的“容易问题”——解释大脑如何分辨刺激、整合信息、报告自身状态——同“困难问题”区分开：为什么这一切会伴随着被感受到的经验？他的“僵尸论证”是：一个与你物理上完全相同、却没有任何内在经验的存在者似乎是可以设想的；如果是这样，物理事实就决定不了经验事实，我们或许需要新的基本定律把两者联系起来。他还与安迪·克拉克提出“延展心灵”，并在《现实+》（2022）中论证虚拟世界可以是真正的实在。丹尼特称困难问题是一种错觉，这场争论至今未决。"},"schools":["philosophy-of-mind"],"works":[{"t":{"en":"Facing Up to the Problem of Consciousness","zh":"《直面意识问题》"},"y":1995},{"t":{"en":"The Conscious Mind","zh":"《有意识的心灵》"},"y":1996},{"t":{"en":"Reality+","zh":"《现实+：每个虚拟世界都是一个新的现实》"},"y":2022}],"wiki":"David Chalmers"}
];
